/* Greek Knowledge Check practice library: 30 fact-checked questions per lesson. Generated; do not edit by hand. */
window.GREEK_PRACTICE={"history":[{"q":"After whose conquests did Greek spread across a massive empire?","kind":"concept","options":["Alexander the Great","Julius Caesar","Caesar Augustus","Herod the Great"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson explains that Greek spread across a huge empire after the conquests of Alexander the Great, which set the stage for Koine."},{"q":"In which direction is Greek read?","kind":"concept","options":["Right to left","Top to bottom","Left to right","Bottom to top"],"answer":2,"explain":"Even though the alphabet is different, Greek is read left to right, just like English, so the reading direction will feel natural."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the New Testament was written as a hidden religious code for elites.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson stresses that the New Testament was written to communicate, not as a hidden code for an elite few."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what did a person need in order to understand Koine Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["Training as a philosopher","Nothing special; it was ordinary language","Membership in a religious order","A university degree in rhetoric"],"answer":1,"explain":"Koine was the language of ordinary communication. You did not have to be a philosopher, scholar, or religious expert to understand it."},{"q":"The lesson compares Greek in the New Testament era to the global role of which modern language?","kind":"concept","options":["French","Latin","Spanish","English"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek was the global language of its day, the way English is widely used around the world today."},{"q":"Instead of relying mainly on word order like English, what does Greek use to show meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Endings","Accents","Capital letters","Punctuation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek uses word endings to show meaning, which is why the lesson says endings matter."},{"q":"Which language does the lesson name as an example of a right-to-left language, unlike Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["Latin","English","Hebrew","German"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says Greek is closer to a familiar Western-style language than to a right-to-left language like Hebrew or Arabic."},{"q":"What did later copies of Greek manuscripts add to make reading easier?","kind":"concept","options":["Pictures and diagrams","Spacing, punctuation, accents, and lowercase letters","Chapter titles and footnotes","Latin translations between the lines"],"answer":1,"explain":"The earliest copies had none of these helps. Later copies added spacing, punctuation, accents, and lowercase letters."},{"q":"This line shows how the earliest manuscripts looked. Which feature does it show?","kind":"concept","options":["Lowercase letters with accents","All capital letters with no spaces","Spaces between every word","A period after each word"],"answer":1,"explain":"The earliest manuscripts were written in all capital letters, with no spaces or punctuation, just like this example.","greek":"ΕΝΑΡΧΗΗΝΟΛΟΓΟΣ"},{"q":"With no spaces in the earliest manuscripts, how did readers recognize words?","kind":"concept","options":["By colored ink for each word","By numbered lines","By small marks between words","By familiarity, context, and repeated exposure"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without spaces, readers had to rely on familiarity, context, and repeated exposure to tell where words began and ended."},{"q":"Which set of features does the lesson list as making Koine Greek unique?","kind":"concept","options":["Very structured, more precise than English, flexible word order","Very simple, less precise than English, fixed word order","Poetic, rhyming, and read right to left","Spoken only, never written, and regional"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says Koine is very structured (endings matter), more precise than English, and has more flexible word order."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson lists merchants and government officials among the people who used Koine Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson's answer to \"Who used it?\" is everyone: merchants, government officials, writers, and ordinary people."},{"q":"What does the lesson say translations sometimes do with details of the Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["They add hidden meanings","They copy the Greek word order exactly","They smooth them out so the sentence sounds natural in English","They leave out entire sentences"],"answer":2,"explain":"Translations are good and useful, but they sometimes smooth out details so the English reads naturally. Greek helps you notice what was actually written."},{"q":"Which of these is listed as something learning Greek helps you notice?","kind":"concept","options":["Hidden number codes","Emphasis in word order","Secret meanings","The author's handwriting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists emphasis in word order, along with repeated words, connections between ideas, and grammatical patterns."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what is this app training first?","kind":"concept","options":["Your handwriting","Your speaking speed","Your translation of whole books","Your eyes"],"answer":3,"explain":"The app is meant to train your eyes to recognize patterns before overwhelming your brain."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the goals listed under \"Your goal\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Recognize common words","Memorize every chart overnight","Become a professional scholar","Translate the whole New Testament quickly"],"answer":0,"explain":"The goals are about recognition: recognize common words, notice patterns, understand basic structure, and read simple Greek with growing confidence."},{"q":"What kind of study does the lesson say Greek rewards?","kind":"concept","options":["Marathon cramming sessions","Silent study of charts only","Short, repeated contact","Learning everything in one sitting"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek rewards short, repeated contact. Returning to the same patterns often is what makes them stick."},{"q":"Why does the lesson suggest saying Greek forms out loud?","kind":"concept","options":["It replaces the need for review","Sound strengthens recognition","It is the only way to learn accents","It lets you skip the charts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reading Greek words with your voice helps, because sound strengthens recognition."},{"q":"The lesson says to learn this noun with its article rather than alone. Why?","kind":"concept","options":["The article changes the word's meaning","The article makes the noun plural","The article tells you the verb","The article carries gender"],"answer":3,"explain":"Learning ὁ λόγος instead of λόγος alone helps because the article carries the noun's gender.","greek":"ὁ λόγος"},{"q":"For this 3rd declension noun, the genitive shows the stem. What is the stem?","kind":"form","options":["σαρκ-","σαρξ-","σαρ-","σαρκο-"],"answer":0,"explain":"Use the genitive σαρκός and drop the ending to find the stem σαρκ-.","greek":"σάρξ, σαρκός"},{"q":"What does the lesson recommend doing before starting a new lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Skip review to save time","Rewrite every earlier chart","Two minutes of review","Take a full practice exam"],"answer":2,"explain":"A couple of minutes of review before new content keeps the new material from floating loose."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson describes a good Greek student as someone who never forgets.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A good Greek student is someone who keeps returning to the patterns until recognition becomes normal, not someone who never forgets."},{"q":"What does the lesson say about writing tiny paradigms?","kind":"concept","options":["Wait until you know every form first","Copying a short chart slowly helps the pattern settle in","It only helps with verbs, not nouns","It should replace saying forms aloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"Copying a short chart by hand, or typing it slowly, helps the pattern settle in."},{"q":"Which statement about New Testament vocabulary appears in the quick facts?","kind":"concept","options":["Most words appear only once","Every word is equally common","Common words are rarely repeated","A small number of common words appear very frequently"],"answer":3,"explain":"The quick facts note that a small number of common words appear very frequently in the New Testament."},{"q":"What happened to Greek as different regions adopted it?","kind":"concept","options":["It became simplified and standardized","It split into unrelated languages","It became more elite and complex","It stopped being written down"],"answer":0,"explain":"As more regions adopted Greek, it became simplified and standardized, forming what we now call Koine Greek."},{"q":"Which uses of Koine Greek does the lesson mention?","kind":"concept","options":["Temple ceremonies only","Poetry and drama only","Communication, travel, trade, letters, and public life","Private records kept by rulers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Koine was a real historical language used for communication, travel, trade, letters, and public life."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says every part of the New Testament is easy to understand.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says not every part is easy, but the language itself was not designed to be unreachable."},{"q":"True or false: before Koine, different forms of Greek were used in different regions.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Koine developed as a shared form of Greek that people from many regions could understand."},{"q":"According to the Big Takeaway, what are you NOT learning Greek to do?","kind":"concept","options":["Find secret meanings","Notice repeated words","See sentence structure","Better see what is already there"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says you are not learning Greek to find secret meanings, but to better see what is already there."},{"q":"In \"What this means for YOU,\" what does the lesson say the purpose of learning Greek is?","kind":"concept","options":["To become a scholar","To replace all translations","To decode a private language","To recognize meaning the authors intentionally put there"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aim is not to become a scholar, but to recognize meaning the authors intentionally put in the text."}],"alphabet":[{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Alpha","Delta","Lambda","Omega"],"answer":0,"explain":"α is Alpha, the first letter. It sounds like the \"a\" in father.","greek":"α"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Delta","Beta","Zeta","Theta"],"answer":1,"explain":"β is Beta. It makes a \"b\" sound, as in Bible.","greek":"β"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Nu","Upsilon","Gamma","Psi"],"answer":2,"explain":"γ is Gamma. It makes a \"g\" sound, as in God.","greek":"γ"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Sigma","Omicron","Alpha","Delta"],"answer":3,"explain":"δ is Delta. It makes a \"d\" sound, as in day.","greek":"δ"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“x” as in ax","“z” as in zoo","“ch/kh” sound","“ps” as in lips"],"answer":0,"explain":"ξ is Xi, which sounds like the \"x\" in ax.","greek":"ξ"},{"q":"This letter is one of the \"letters that can trick you.\" What is its name?","kind":"letter","options":["Nu","Eta","Mu","Iota"],"answer":1,"explain":"η is Eta. Despite its shape, it makes an \"ay\" sound, as in obey.","greek":"η"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“n” as in no","“y” as in yes","“u/oo” sound","“v” as in very"],"answer":2,"explain":"υ is Upsilon, which makes a \"u/oo\" sound. Do not trust its English-looking shape.","greek":"υ"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Xi","Kappa","Psi","Chi"],"answer":3,"explain":"χ is Chi. It looks like an English x but makes a \"ch/kh\" sound.","greek":"χ"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["short “o” as in not","long “o” as in tone","“w” as in water","“u/oo” sound"],"answer":1,"explain":"ω is Omega, which makes a long \"o\" sound, as in tone.","greek":"ω"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Upsilon","Gamma","Mu","Nu"],"answer":3,"explain":"ν is Nu. It makes an \"n\" sound, as in no.","greek":"ν"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“ph/f” as in phone","“p” as in peace","“ps” as in lips","“th” as in thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"φ is Phi, which makes a \"ph/f\" sound, as in phone.","greek":"φ"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“x” as in ax","“s” as in see","“ps” as in lips","“ph/f” as in phone"],"answer":2,"explain":"ψ is Psi, which makes a \"ps\" sound, like the end of lips.","greek":"ψ"},{"q":"Which of these letters is Zeta?","kind":"letter","options":["ξ","ζ","ς","γ"],"answer":1,"explain":"ζ is Zeta, which makes a \"z\" sound, as in zoo."},{"q":"Which letter makes the \"p\" sound, as in peace?","kind":"letter","options":["ρ","φ","ψ","π"],"answer":3,"explain":"π is Pi, the \"p\" sound. Watch out: ρ looks like a p but is Rho."},{"q":"Which of these letters is Omicron?","kind":"letter","options":["ο","ω","σ","θ"],"answer":0,"explain":"ο is Omicron, the \"o\" sound as in not. ω is Omega, a different letter."},{"q":"True or false: the Greek alphabet has 24 letters.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson's quick facts say the Greek alphabet has 24 letters."},{"q":"True or false: ς is a separate letter, not a form of sigma.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ς is the final form of sigma, not a separate letter.","greek":"ς"},{"q":"True or false: this lesson says you should perfect your pronunciation before moving on.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Recognition comes first. The lesson tells you not to worry about perfect pronunciation yet."},{"q":"For each Greek letter, which three things does the lesson want you to connect?","kind":"concept","options":["Shape, number, and color","Name, accent, and meaning","Shape, name, and sound","Sound, spelling, and gender"],"answer":2,"explain":"The goal is to connect what the letter looks like, what it is called, and what sound it usually makes."},{"q":"In which group does the lesson place this letter?","kind":"concept","options":["Letters that feel familiar","Letters that can trick you","Letters that look new","Final letter forms"],"answer":1,"explain":"η looks like an English letter but makes an \"ay\" sound, so it is one of the letters that can trick you.","greek":"η"},{"q":"Which letters spell this word?","kind":"letter","options":["theta + epsilon + omicron + sigma","theta + eta + omicron + sigma","tau + epsilon + omega + sigma","phi + epsilon + omicron + sigma"],"answer":0,"explain":"θεός is theta, epsilon, omicron, and sigma (in its final form ς).","greek":"θεός"},{"q":"Which letters spell this word?","kind":"letter","options":["zeta + omicron + eta","xi + omega + eta","zeta + omega + eta","zeta + omega + epsilon"],"answer":2,"explain":"ζωή is zeta, omega, and eta. Notice the long ω and the η at the end.","greek":"ζωή"},{"q":"What is the last letter of this word?","kind":"letter","options":["Omicron","Gamma","Lambda","Sigma"],"answer":3,"explain":"λόγος ends with sigma, written in its final form ς because it comes at the end of the word.","greek":"λόγος"},{"q":"Which letter appears twice in this word?","kind":"letter","options":["Omicron","Lambda","Gamma","Sigma"],"answer":0,"explain":"λόγος is lambda + omicron + gamma + omicron + sigma, so omicron appears twice.","greek":"λόγος"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“g” as in God","“ch/kh” sound","“k” as in king","“x” as in ax"],"answer":2,"explain":"κ is Kappa, the \"k\" sound, as in king.","greek":"κ"},{"q":"Which of these letters is NOT in the lesson's \"letters that feel familiar\" group?","kind":"concept","options":["α","κ","τ","ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"α, κ, and τ are in the familiar group. ω (Omega) is listed with the letters that can trick you."},{"q":"Which letter is Iota, with the \"i\" sound as in machine?","kind":"letter","options":["η","ι","υ","ε"],"answer":1,"explain":"ι is Iota. It sounds like the \"i\" in machine."},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["“ay” as in obey","“i” as in machine","long “o” as in tone","“e” as in met"],"answer":3,"explain":"ε is Epsilon, which sounds like the \"e\" in met.","greek":"ε"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what makes the Greek letters stick?","kind":"concept","options":["Repeated exposure","Memorizing everything in one sitting","Studying only the hardest letters","Skipping the letter sounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"You do not need to memorize everything at once. Repeated exposure is what makes the letters stick."},{"q":"True or false: δ (Delta) is in the lesson's group of letters that look new.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. δ is listed with γ, ζ, θ, and others as letters that may look strange at first but become familiar through repetition.","greek":"δ"}],"pronunciation":[{"q":"What sound does a double gamma usually make?","kind":"sound","options":["“ng” sound","“gg” as in egg","“g” as in God","“k” as in king"],"answer":0,"explain":"When two gammas sit together, they usually make an \"ng\" sound.","greek":"γγ"},{"q":"Roughly how is this word pronounced?","kind":"sound","options":["“ag-gelos”","“AN-ge-los”","“AH-he-los”","“AK-ke-los”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Because γγ makes an \"ng\" sound, ἄγγελος sounds something like \"AN-ge-los,\" not \"ag-gelos.\"","greek":"ἄγγελος"},{"q":"Where in a word is this form of sigma used?","kind":"letter","options":["Only at the end of a word","Only before vowels","At the beginning or middle of a word","Only in capital letters"],"answer":2,"explain":"σ is used at the beginning or in the middle of a word. At the end, sigma is written ς.","greek":"σ"},{"q":"What sits at the center of every Greek syllable?","kind":"concept","options":["Two separate vowels","No vowel at all","At least three letters","One vowel or diphthong"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each syllable is one beat of sound, built around one vowel or one diphthong."},{"q":"How does this word break into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["λό-γος","λόγ-ος","λ-όγος","λόγο-ς"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single consonant usually goes forward with the next vowel, so the γ starts the second syllable: λό-γος.","greek":"λόγος"},{"q":"How does this word break into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγ-ά-πη","ἀ-γά-πη","ἀγά-πη","ἀ-γάπ-η"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three vowels mean three beats, and each single consonant goes forward: ἀ-γά-πη.","greek":"ἀγάπη"},{"q":"How does this word break into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["κύρ-ι-ος","κύ-ριο-ς","κύ-ρι-ος","κύ-ριος"],"answer":2,"explain":"κύριος has three vowel sounds (υ, ι, ο), so it has three beats: κύ-ρι-ος.","greek":"κύριος"},{"q":"How does this word break into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["ἄ-νθρω-πος","ἄνθ-ρω-πος","ἄν-θρωπ-ος","ἄν-θρω-πος"],"answer":3,"explain":"When two consonants make the break feel heavy, divide between them: ἄν-θρω-πος.","greek":"ἄνθρωπος"},{"q":"True or false: when breaking a word into syllables, a diphthong like ου is split between two syllables.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Diphthongs stay together because they count as one vowel sound."},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “eye”","like “eight”","like “oil”","like “oo” in food"],"answer":0,"explain":"αι is one blended sound, like \"eye,\" not \"ah-ih.\"","greek":"αι"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “eye”","like “eight”","like “ow” in cow","like “wee”"],"answer":1,"explain":"ει blends into one sound, like the \"ei\" in eight.","greek":"ει"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “ow” in cow","like “oil”","like “oo” in food","like “eight”"],"answer":2,"explain":"ου always sounds like \"oo\" in food. It shows up in words like λόγου and θεοῦ.","greek":"ου"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “oo” in food","like “eye”","like “eh-oo” blended","like “ow” in cow"],"answer":3,"explain":"αυ sounds like the \"ow\" in cow.","greek":"αυ"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “wee”","like “oil”","like “eye”","like “ow” in cow"],"answer":0,"explain":"υι blends into one sound, like \"wee.\"","greek":"υι"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["like “oo” in food","like “eh-oo” blended","like “eight”","like “wee”"],"answer":1,"explain":"ευ is a blended \"eh-oo\" sound, said as one vowel sound.","greek":"ευ"},{"q":"What is a diphthong?","kind":"concept","options":["A pair of consonants that splits","A mark written above a vowel","Two vowels that blend into one sound","A letter used only at the end of a word"],"answer":2,"explain":"A diphthong is two vowels sitting together that blend into a single sound, like \"oi\" in oil."},{"q":"Over which letter does the breathing mark sit in this word?","kind":"concept","options":["α, the first vowel","τ, the first consonant","ς, the last letter","υ, the second vowel"],"answer":3,"explain":"αὐτός begins with the diphthong αυ, so the breathing mark sits over υ, the second vowel.","greek":"αὐτός"},{"q":"Why is the breathing mark in this word on ι instead of ο?","kind":"concept","options":["The word begins with a diphthong, so the mark goes on the second vowel","Iota always carries the breathing mark in every word","Omicron can never carry a breathing mark","The mark shows that the word ends a question"],"answer":0,"explain":"οἶκος starts with the diphthong οι. When a word begins with a diphthong, the breathing mark goes on the second vowel.","greek":"οἶκος"},{"q":"What does the smooth breathing mark on this letter tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["Add an “h” sound","No “h” sound","Stress this syllable","The sentence is a question"],"answer":1,"explain":"Smooth breathing means no \"h\" sound. Rough breathing is the one that adds an \"h.\"","greek":"ἀ"},{"q":"The rho in this word has a rough breathing. How does the lesson transliterate the word?","kind":"sound","options":["abbi","habbi","rhabbi","pabbi"],"answer":2,"explain":"A rough breathing on rho gives \"rh,\" so ῥαββί is rhabbi.","greek":"ῥαββί"},{"q":"Breathing marks appear on words that begin with what?","kind":"concept","options":["Any consonant","Sigma only","Double gamma","A vowel or rho"],"answer":3,"explain":"Breathing marks appear on words that begin with a vowel or with rho (ρ)."},{"q":"These two words differ only in their breathing mark. What does each mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["ἐν = in, ἕν = one","ἐν = one, ἕν = in","ἐν = and, ἕν = but","ἐν = into, ἕν = out of"],"answer":0,"explain":"Breathing marks mainly affect pronunciation, but here they tell two words apart: ἐν (smooth) means \"in\" and ἕν (rough) means \"one.\"","greek":"ἐν / ἕν"},{"q":"What kind of accent mark is on this letter?","kind":"concept","options":["grave","acute","circumflex","rough breathing"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is the acute accent, a rising or stressed accent.","greek":"ά"},{"q":"What kind of accent mark is on this letter?","kind":"concept","options":["acute","grave","circumflex","smooth breathing"],"answer":2,"explain":"This curved mark is the circumflex, a rising and falling accent.","greek":"ᾶ"},{"q":"True or false: this lesson says accent marks change the basic meaning of a vocabulary word.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. For now, accents mark where the voice is stressed. They do not change the basic meaning of the vocabulary word."},{"q":"What does this Greek punctuation mark, the raised dot, often work like?","kind":"concept","options":["A question mark","An exclamation point","A quotation mark","A semicolon or colon"],"answer":3,"explain":"The raised dot often works like an English semicolon or colon.","greek":"·"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says nobody alive has heard Koine Greek spoken natively.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. That is why scholars still argue about how it sounded, and why a consistent system matters more than perfection."},{"q":"True or false: ο and ω both make an \"o\" sound, but ω is the longer one.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Omicron is a short \"o\" as in not, and omega is a long \"o\" as in tone.","greek":"ο / ω"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what matters most about your own pronunciation?","kind":"concept","options":["Having a consistent system","Sounding like a native speaker","Perfect accent placement","Using modern Greek sounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"You do not need perfect pronunciation. What matters is a consistent system so that words become recognizable."},{"q":"How does the lesson sound out this word?","kind":"sound","options":["zeta-oh","zo-ay","zo-ee","so-eh"],"answer":1,"explain":"ζωή is zeta, omega, eta, so it sounds like \"zo-ay.\" Remember that η says \"ay.\"","greek":"ζωή"}],"nouns":[{"q":"What is a lexicon?","kind":"concept","options":["A Greek dictionary","A chart of verb endings","An ancient manuscript","A list of Greek articles"],"answer":0,"explain":"A lexicon is simply a Greek dictionary. You look words up in it by their lexical form."},{"q":"For nouns, which form is the lexical (dictionary) form?","kind":"concept","options":["The accusative singular","The nominative singular","The genitive plural","The object form"],"answer":1,"explain":"A noun's lexical form is its nominative singular, the form listed in the dictionary, such as λόγος."},{"q":"You find this form in the text. Under which lexical form would you look it up?","kind":"form","options":["λόγον","λόγου","λόγος","λόγῳ"],"answer":2,"explain":"λόγῳ is one of the forms of λόγος, and λόγος is the lexical form you look up in a lexicon.","greek":"λόγῳ"},{"q":"What does this lexical form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["word","life","God","love"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγάπη is the lexical form meaning \"love.\"","greek":"ἀγάπη"},{"q":"The lesson groups nouns as person, place, or thing/idea. Where does \"kingdom\" go?","kind":"concept","options":["Place","Person","Thing / Idea","None; it is not a noun"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists kingdom with world, house, and city as nouns that name a place."},{"q":"In the English sentence \"The apostle sees the city,\" which word is the object?","kind":"concept","options":["apostle","city","sees","the"],"answer":1,"explain":"The object is the one receiving the action. The city is what is being seen, so \"city\" is the object."},{"q":"True or false: a noun is a word that names an action, like \"sees.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A noun names a person, place, or thing. \"Sees\" is the action, not a noun."},{"q":"What role does the ending of this form usually point to?","kind":"form","options":["Subject","The action","Object","The article"],"answer":2,"explain":"κύριον ends in -ον, and -ον usually points to the object.","greek":"κύριον"},{"q":"In this phrase, which form does the article τὸν match?","kind":"form","options":["The subject form","A feminine noun","A neuter noun","The object form"],"answer":3,"explain":"τὸν goes with the object form, so τὸν δοῦλον is \"the servant\" as the object. Article and ending give two clues.","greek":"τὸν δοῦλον"},{"q":"What does this sentence mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The master sees the servant.","The servant sees the master.","The servant sees the word.","The word sees the master."],"answer":0,"explain":"ὁ κύριος (ὁ + -ος) is the subject and τὸν δοῦλον (τὸν + -ον) is the object, wherever they sit. So the master sees the servant.","greek":"βλέπει τὸν δοῦλον ὁ κύριος"},{"q":"In the lesson's sentences, what job does this word do?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject","The action (sees)","The object","The article"],"answer":1,"explain":"βλέπει means \"sees.\" It is the action in the sentence.","greek":"βλέπει"},{"q":"True or false: changing δοῦλος to δοῦλον changes the word's basic dictionary meaning.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Both still mean servant. The ending shows how the word is functioning, not a new meaning.","greek":"δοῦλος → δοῦλον"},{"q":"How many grammatical genders does a Greek noun choose from?","kind":"concept","options":["One: neuter only","Two: masculine and feminine","Three: masculine, feminine, and neuter","Four, including a common gender"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every Greek noun has one of three genders: masculine, feminine, or neuter."},{"q":"What gender is this noun meaning \"sin\"?","kind":"form","options":["Masculine","Neuter","It has no gender","Feminine"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἁμαρτία is feminine. That is a grammatical category, not a statement that sin is a feminine concept.","greek":"ἁμαρτία"},{"q":"What gender is this noun meaning \"spirit\"?","kind":"form","options":["Neuter","Masculine","Feminine","It has no gender"],"answer":0,"explain":"πνεῦμα is neuter. The lesson stresses this is grammar, not a claim about meaning.","greek":"πνεῦμα"},{"q":"What gender is this noun meaning \"law\"?","kind":"form","options":["Feminine","Masculine","Neuter","It has no gender"],"answer":1,"explain":"νόμος is masculine. You learn the gender as part of the word, like its spelling.","greek":"νόμος"},{"q":"What does this noun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["law","spirit","work / deed","life"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔργον means \"work\" or \"deed.\" It is a neuter noun.","greek":"ἔργον"},{"q":"This word for \"child\" refers to a person. What is its grammatical gender?","kind":"form","options":["Masculine","Feminine","It changes with the child","Neuter"],"answer":3,"explain":"παιδίον is neuter even though it names a person. Greek gender is built into the word, not taken from meaning.","greek":"παιδίον"},{"q":"Which gender does the article ἡ show here?","kind":"form","options":["Feminine","Masculine","Neuter","No gender"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἡ is the feminine article, matching the feminine noun ζωή (life) as subject.","greek":"ἡ ζωή"},{"q":"These model words belong to the 2nd declension. Which genders are 2nd declension nouns mostly?","kind":"concept","options":["Feminine only","Masculine and neuter","Feminine and neuter","All three genders equally"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2nd declension (the o-stem family) is mostly masculine and neuter nouns, like λόγος (masculine) and ἔργον (neuter). The 1st declension is the mostly feminine family.","greek":"λόγος, ἔργον"},{"q":"According to the lesson, is a Greek noun's gender based on its meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Yes, it always follows biological sex","Yes, feminine nouns always name feminine ideas","No, it is a grammatical category built into the word","Only nouns that name people have gender"],"answer":2,"explain":"In Greek, gender is a built-in grammatical feature of the word, not a statement about what the word means."},{"q":"True or false: once you know a noun's gender, it never changes.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. A noun's gender stays fixed, and most dictionaries list it next to the word."},{"q":"In English, where does grammatical gender mainly show up?","kind":"concept","options":["In every noun ending","In verb endings","In the word \"the\"","Only in pronouns like he, she, it"],"answer":3,"explain":"English mostly lost grammatical gender. It only shows up in pronouns like he, she, and it."},{"q":"What is a declension?","kind":"concept","options":["A family of nouns that share the same set of endings","A change in a verb's tense","A mark that shows stress","A sentence's word order"],"answer":0,"explain":"A declension is a noun family. Learn the endings for one noun in the family and you have a template for the rest."},{"q":"Which declension is called the o-stem family?","kind":"concept","options":["1st declension","2nd declension","3rd declension","4th declension"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2nd declension is the o-stem family. You can spot the ο in endings like -ος, -ου, and -ον."},{"q":"Which declension family does this noun (heart) belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["2nd declension (o-stem)","3rd declension (consonant stem)","1st declension (alpha/eta stem)","No declension family"],"answer":2,"explain":"καρδία is a model word for the 1st declension, the mostly feminine alpha/eta-stem family.","greek":"καρδία"},{"q":"Which declension family does this noun (flesh) belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["1st declension (alpha/eta stem)","2nd declension (o-stem)","No declension family","3rd declension (consonant stem)"],"answer":3,"explain":"σάρξ is a 3rd declension noun. Its stem ends in a consonant rather than a vowel.","greek":"σάρξ"},{"q":"Which declension does the lesson call the largest family and the most common in the New Testament?","kind":"concept","options":["2nd declension","1st declension","3rd declension","All three are equally common"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says to start with the 2nd declension because it is the largest and most common in the NT."},{"q":"A study tool labels a noun \"2nd declension masculine.\" Which of these model words fits that label?","kind":"concept","options":["ἀγάπη","λόγος","καρδία","σάρξ"],"answer":1,"explain":"λόγος is a masculine model word for the 2nd declension. ἀγάπη and καρδία are 1st declension, and σάρξ is 3rd."},{"q":"True or false: every Greek noun belongs to one of three declension families.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Knowing a noun's family tells you which endings to expect across all its forms."}],"cases":[{"q":"True or false: in λόγος, λόγου, and λόγον, the stem λογ stays the same.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The stem λογ carries the basic idea \"word,\" while the endings change to show the word's job.","greek":"λόγος / λόγου / λόγον"},{"q":"Which case is usually the subject, the one doing the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Accusative","Genitive","Dative"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nominative is usually the subject. Think \"subject\" when you see a nominative form."},{"q":"Which case is often translated with the English word \"of\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Genitive","Accusative","Dative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The genitive often shows possession, source, or relationship, and is often translated with \"of.\""},{"q":"Which case often shows an indirect object, means, location, or association?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Accusative","Dative","Genitive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The dative covers these uses and is often translated with to, for, in, by, or with."},{"q":"In Greek study tools, what does the label N-GSM stand for?","kind":"concept","options":["Noun – Nominative Singular Masculine","Noun – Accusative Singular Masculine","Noun – Dative Singular Masculine","Noun – Genitive Singular Masculine"],"answer":3,"explain":"N-GSM means Noun, Genitive, Singular, Masculine. Tools like interlinears use these labels constantly."},{"q":"In this sentence from John 3:16, which phrase is in the accusative case?","kind":"reading","options":["τὸν κόσμον","ὁ θεὸς","ἠγάπησεν","οὕτως"],"answer":0,"explain":"τὸν κόσμον is accusative. It is the object, the one receiving the love.","greek":"οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον"},{"q":"In this sentence, what shows that God is the one loving, not the world?","kind":"reading","options":["ὁ θεὸς is the first word in the sentence","ὁ θεὸς is nominative, so it is the subject","τὸν κόσμον is genitive","The verb comes last"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὁ θεὸς is nominative (the subject) and τὸν κόσμον is accusative (the object). The cases settle it, not the word order.","greek":"οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον"},{"q":"Which case is this phrase?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive","Nominative","Dative","Accusative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both the article τῷ and the noun θεῷ are dative, so the phrase means \"to God\" or \"for God.\"","greek":"τῷ θεῷ"},{"q":"In this phrase from Eph 2:8, what does the genitive θεοῦ contribute?","kind":"reading","options":["to God","God (as the subject)","into God","of God"],"answer":3,"explain":"θεοῦ is genitive, which often means \"of.\" The phrase is \"the gift of God.\"","greek":"θεοῦ τὸ δῶρον"},{"q":"What is the tiny mark written under the ω in a dative ending like ῳ called?","kind":"concept","options":["Iota subscript","Rough breathing","Circumflex","Final sigma"],"answer":0,"explain":"That small mark is an iota subscript. On 1st and 2nd declension nouns it signals a dative singular.","greek":"ῳ"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["genitive singular neuter","dative singular neuter","accusative singular neuter","dative plural neuter"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔργον is neuter, and ἐργ + ῳ is the dative singular: \"to/for/in/with a work.\"","greek":"ἔργῳ"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["nominative plural neuter","dative singular neuter","genitive singular neuter","genitive plural neuter"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐργ + ου is the genitive singular of the neuter noun ἔργον: \"of a work.\"","greek":"ἔργου"},{"q":"What case is this form?","kind":"form","options":["genitive singular","accusative singular","nominative singular","dative singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"Spot the iota subscript under the α (ᾳ). On a 1st or 2nd declension noun, that means dative singular.","greek":"καρδίᾳ"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["accusative singular feminine","genitive singular feminine","dative singular feminine","nominative plural feminine"],"answer":0,"explain":"καρδι + αν is the accusative singular of the α-stem feminine noun καρδία (heart).","greek":"καρδίαν"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["dative plural feminine","nominative plural feminine","genitive singular feminine","accusative plural feminine"],"answer":1,"explain":"γραφ + αι is the feminine nominative plural: \"writings\" as the subject.","greek":"γραφαί"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["genitive singular feminine","nominative plural feminine","accusative plural feminine","dative plural feminine"],"answer":2,"explain":"γραφ + ας is the feminine accusative plural. Compare γραφῆς, the genitive singular, which has η.","greek":"γραφάς"},{"q":"What is the form of this noun?","kind":"form","options":["genitive plural neuter","accusative plural neuter","dative singular neuter","dative plural neuter"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐργ + οις is the neuter dative plural: \"to/for/in/with works.\"","greek":"ἔργοις"},{"q":"This form ends in -ων. How is it translated?","kind":"meaning","options":["of works","to works","works (as subject)","into works"],"answer":0,"explain":"In 1st and 2nd declension nouns, -ων is always genitive plural, so ἔργων means \"of works.\"","greek":"ἔργων"},{"q":"True or false: Greek has a separate word for \"a\" or \"an.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek has only a definite article. A noun without an article is often understood as \"a\" in English."},{"q":"What does this article form show?","kind":"form","options":["dative singular feminine","genitive singular feminine","genitive plural (all genders)","nominative singular feminine"],"answer":1,"explain":"τῆς is the feminine genitive singular article, part of the τοῦ / τῆς / τοῦ genitive singular row.","greek":"τῆς"},{"q":"What does this article form show?","kind":"form","options":["accusative singular masculine","genitive plural masculine","accusative plural masculine","dative plural masculine"],"answer":2,"explain":"τούς is the masculine accusative plural article, matching nouns like λόγους.","greek":"τούς"},{"q":"What does this article form show?","kind":"form","options":["nominative plural feminine","accusative plural feminine","genitive plural feminine","dative plural feminine"],"answer":3,"explain":"ταῖς is the feminine dative plural article, matching the -αις ending on nouns like γραφαῖς.","greek":"ταῖς"},{"q":"This article form can be two different cases. Which?","kind":"form","options":["nominative or accusative singular neuter","genitive or dative singular neuter","nominative or accusative plural neuter","nominative singular masculine only"],"answer":0,"explain":"τό is the neuter article for both nominative and accusative singular. The plural equivalent is τά.","greek":"τό"},{"q":"What does this article form show?","kind":"form","options":["nominative singular feminine","nominative plural feminine","genitive plural feminine","dative plural feminine"],"answer":1,"explain":"αἱ is the feminine nominative plural article. The masculine is οἱ and the neuter is τά.","greek":"αἱ"},{"q":"True or false: every Greek article must be translated as \"the\" in English.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The article always means \"the\" in Greek, but English often drops it. It helps you understand the Greek even when you do not translate it.","greek":"ὁ λόγος"},{"q":"Using the genitive, what is the stem of this 3rd declension noun?","kind":"form","options":["χαρ-","χαρις-","χαριτ-","χαριτο-"],"answer":2,"explain":"Drop the genitive ending -ος from χάριτος and you get the real stem χαριτ-.","greek":"χάρις, χάριτος"},{"q":"Why do lexicons list two forms for a 3rd declension noun?","kind":"concept","options":["The second form is the plural","The second form is the feminine","The second form shows only the accent","The nominative can hide the real stem, so the genitive shows it"],"answer":3,"explain":"3rd declension nouns have consonant stems that the nominative can hide. Lexicons give the genitive so you can find the stem."},{"q":"Which three things can a Greek noun ending tell you at once?","kind":"concept","options":["Case, number, and gender","Person, tense, and mood","Case, tense, and voice","Gender, accent, and breathing"],"answer":0,"explain":"A noun ending can show case (the job), number (singular or plural), and gender (masculine, feminine, or neuter)."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson's endings chart includes the 3rd declension.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The chart shows only the main 1st and 2nd declension endings. The 3rd declension gets only a preview."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["of the word","in the word","the word (as subject)","he sees the word"],"answer":1,"explain":"λόγῳ is dative and works with ἐν here, so the phrase means \"in the word.\"","greek":"ἐν τῷ λόγῳ"}],"prepositions":[{"q":"Which case does this preposition take?","kind":"form","options":["Accusative","Dative","Genitive","Nominative"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰς takes the accusative, which points you toward or into something.","greek":"εἰς"},{"q":"True or false: this preposition takes the dative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ἐκ takes the genitive, the case that takes you out from a source.","greek":"ἐκ"},{"q":"What does this preposition mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["into / toward","out of / from","in / within","according to"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐκ means \"out of\" or \"from,\" as in ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ, \"out of God.\"","greek":"ἐκ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["into the beginning","out of the beginning","in the beginning","because of the beginning"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐν with the dative ἀρχῇ means \"in the beginning.\"","greek":"ἐν ἀρχῇ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["into God","in God","according to God","out of God"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐκ (out of) plus the genitive τοῦ θεοῦ gives \"out of God.\"","greek":"ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["into eternal life","in eternal life","out of eternal life","because of eternal life"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰς with the accusative shows motion into something: \"into eternal life.\"","greek":"εἰς ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["into Christ","in Christ","out of Christ","according to Christ"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐν takes the dative, and Χριστῷ is dative, so the phrase means \"in Christ.\"","greek":"ἐν Χριστῷ"},{"q":"The lesson pictures each case as a kind of direction. What does the dative do, as with ἐν?","kind":"concept","options":["It points you toward something","It takes you out from a source","It locates you inside","It gives a reason"],"answer":2,"explain":"The dative locates you inside, the accusative points toward something, and the genitive takes you out from a source."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["with God","into God","according to God","from God"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀπό takes the genitive and means \"from\" or \"away from,\" so ἀπὸ θεοῦ is \"from God.\"","greek":"ἀπὸ θεοῦ"},{"q":"In John 1:1, what does this phrase mean?","kind":"reading","options":["with God","from God","out of God","because of God"],"answer":0,"explain":"πρός takes the accusative and can mean \"to,\" \"toward,\" or \"with.\" In John 1:1 the lesson gives \"with God.\"","greek":"πρὸς τὸν θεόν"},{"q":"When κατά means \"according to,\" which case does it take?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive","Accusative","Dative","Nominative"],"answer":1,"explain":"κατά meaning \"according to\" takes the accusative, as in κατὰ σάρκα, \"according to flesh.\"","greek":"κατά"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["because of him","into him","through him","according to him"],"answer":2,"explain":"διά with the genitive means \"through\" or \"by means of,\" so this is \"through him.\"","greek":"δι᾽ αὐτοῦ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["through this","into this","out of this","because of this"],"answer":3,"explain":"διά with the accusative means \"because of,\" so διὰ τοῦτο is \"because of this.\"","greek":"διὰ τοῦτο"},{"q":"διά can mean \"through\" or \"because of.\" What decides which meaning it has?","kind":"concept","options":["The case of the word that follows it","Where it sits in the sentence","The accent mark on διά","The tense of the main verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"With the genitive διά means \"through\"; with the accusative it means \"because of.\" The case that follows decides.","greek":"διά"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["through flesh","according to flesh","out of flesh","into flesh"],"answer":1,"explain":"κατά with the accusative means \"according to,\" so κατὰ σάρκα is \"according to flesh.\"","greek":"κατὰ σάρκα"},{"q":"True or false: ἐν, εἰς, and ἐκ each take one specific case.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. ἐν takes the dative, εἰς takes the accusative, and ἐκ takes the genitive."},{"q":"True or false: in ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ, the article τοῦ tells you the case is genitive even before you read the noun.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. τοῦ is a genitive article, which is why learning the articles pays off right away.","greek":"ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["in Christ","out of Christ","into Christ","with Christ"],"answer":2,"explain":"εἰς with the accusative Χριστόν shows direction: \"into Christ.\" Compare ἐν Χριστῷ, \"in Christ.\"","greek":"εἰς Χριστόν"},{"q":"What does this form of εἰμί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we are","you are","I am","he/she/it is"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐστίν is the 3rd person singular of εἰμί: \"he/she/it is,\" as in ὁ θεός ἐστιν ἀγάπη.","greek":"ἐστίν"},{"q":"What does this form of the verb \"to be\" mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I am","you are","he/she/it is","we are"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰμί is the 1st person singular: \"I am.\"","greek":"εἰμί"},{"q":"What does this form of εἰμί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all are","we are","they are","I am"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐσμέν is the 1st person plural: \"we are.\"","greek":"ἐσμέν"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form of εἰμί?","kind":"form","options":["1st singular: I am","3rd singular: he/she/it is","2nd singular: you are","2nd plural: you all are"],"answer":2,"explain":"εἶ is the 2nd person singular: \"you are,\" spoken to one person.","greek":"εἶ"},{"q":"What does this form of εἰμί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we are","you all are","he/she/it is","they are"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰσί(ν) is the 3rd person plural: \"they are.\"","greek":"εἰσί(ν)"},{"q":"What does this form of εἰμί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all are","you are (one person)","we are","they are"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐστέ is the 2nd person plural: \"you all are.\" The singular \"you are\" is εἶ.","greek":"ἐστέ"},{"q":"What does this sentence mean?","kind":"reading","options":["God sees love.","God is love.","God is the word.","They are love."],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐστιν means \"is\" and links ὁ θεός with ἀγάπη like an equals sign: \"God is love.\"","greek":"ὁ θεός ἐστιν ἀγάπη"},{"q":"The verb ending already shows the subject. What does adding ἐγώ do here?","kind":"concept","options":["It makes the verb plural","It changes the verb to past time","It adds emphasis: \"I myself am\"","It turns the sentence into a question"],"answer":2,"explain":"Since εἰμι already means \"I am,\" an explicit ἐγώ adds emphasis: \"I myself am.\"","greek":"ἐγώ εἰμι"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the way εἰμί often works between two ideas?","kind":"concept","options":["Like a question mark","Like a preposition","Like an article","Like an equals sign"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰμί often works like an equals sign, linking two ideas, as in \"God is love.\""},{"q":"True or false: εἰμί is a regular verb that rarely appears in Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. εἰμί is irregular and extremely common, so the lesson tells you to memorize it early.","greek":"εἰμί"},{"q":"What are the lesson's three steps for reading a preposition phrase quickly?","kind":"concept","options":["Spot the preposition, check the case that follows, lock in the meaning","Find the verb, find the subject, translate the sentence","Read the accent, find the stem, guess the meaning","Check the gender, count the syllables, translate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Spot the preposition, check the case of what follows (using the article or ending), then lock in the meaning."},{"q":"What does this sentence mean?","kind":"reading","options":["You are the good shepherd.","I am the good shepherd.","He is the good shepherd.","We are the good shepherds."],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐγώ εἰμι is \"I am,\" so the sentence means \"I am the good shepherd.\" The added ἐγώ gives emphasis.","greek":"ἐγώ εἰμι ὁ ποιμὴν ὁ καλός"}],"adjectives":[{"q":"What does an adjective do?","kind":"concept","options":["It describes a noun","It names an action","It connects two clauses","It replaces a noun"],"answer":0,"explain":"An adjective describes a noun, like \"good\" in \"the good word.\""},{"q":"A Greek adjective must agree with its noun in which three things?","kind":"concept","options":["Person, tense, and mood","Gender, case, and number","Case, accent, and breathing","Gender, tense, and voice"],"answer":1,"explain":"One rule, three checks: the adjective matches its noun in gender, case, and number."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["the word is good","the holy word","the good word","the faithful word"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαθός means \"good,\" and it sits inside the article-noun group, so it directly describes the noun: \"the good word.\"","greek":"ὁ ἀγαθὸς λόγος"},{"q":"What is the form of this adjective?","kind":"form","options":["genitive singular feminine","nominative singular feminine","dative plural feminine","dative singular feminine"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαθῇ, with the iota subscript, is the feminine dative singular of ἀγαθός.","greek":"ἀγαθῇ"},{"q":"What is the form of this adjective?","kind":"form","options":["accusative plural masculine","accusative singular masculine","genitive plural masculine","dative plural masculine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The -ους ending is masculine accusative plural, the same ending the noun λόγους uses.","greek":"ἀγαθούς"},{"q":"Which forms could this adjective be?","kind":"form","options":["feminine nominative singular","neuter nominative or accusative plural","masculine accusative plural","neuter genitive plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἀγαθά appears in both the nominative plural and accusative plural rows of the neuter column. Context decides which.","greek":"ἀγαθά"},{"q":"What is the form of this adjective?","kind":"form","options":["dative plural masculine","genitive singular masculine","genitive plural (all genders)","accusative plural feminine"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαθῶν is the genitive plural in all three genders.","greek":"ἀγαθῶν"},{"q":"Which form or forms could this adjective be?","kind":"form","options":["feminine genitive singular","masculine dative singular","neuter genitive plural","masculine or neuter genitive singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαθοῦ is the genitive singular in both the masculine and neuter columns. The feminine is ἀγαθῆς.","greek":"ἀγαθοῦ"},{"q":"In the 2-1-2 adjective pattern, which gender uses 1st-declension endings?","kind":"concept","options":["Feminine","Masculine","Neuter","Masculine and neuter"],"answer":0,"explain":"2-1-2 means masculine and neuter use 2nd-declension endings, while the feminine uses 1st-declension endings."},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["holy","all / every","eternal","great"],"answer":1,"explain":"πᾶς / πᾶσα / πᾶν means \"all\" or \"every.\"","greek":"πᾶς / πᾶσα / πᾶν"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["faithful","good","holy","great"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἅγιος means \"holy.\"","greek":"ἅγιος"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["eternal","all / every","holy","faithful"],"answer":3,"explain":"πιστός means \"faithful.\"","greek":"πιστός"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["great","good","holy","eternal"],"answer":0,"explain":"μέγας means \"great.\"","greek":"μέγας"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["faithful","eternal","beautiful","every"],"answer":1,"explain":"αἰώνιος means \"eternal,\" as in ζωὴν αἰώνιον, \"eternal life.\"","greek":"αἰώνιος"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["great","holy","good / beautiful","all / every"],"answer":2,"explain":"καλός means \"good\" or \"beautiful.\"","greek":"καλός"},{"q":"The adjective stands outside the article-noun group here. How does the lesson translate it?","kind":"reading","options":["the faithful God","faithful to God","the faith of God","God is faithful"],"answer":3,"explain":"πιστός sits outside ὁ θεός, so it makes a statement about the noun: \"God is faithful.\"","greek":"πιστὸς ὁ θεός"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"reading","options":["great is the joy","the great joy","the joy of the great one","joy to the great one"],"answer":0,"explain":"μεγάλη stands outside the article-noun group ἡ χαρά, so it makes a statement: \"great is the joy.\"","greek":"μεγάλη ἡ χαρά"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["the good word","the word is good","the words are good","the word of the good one"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἀγαθός sits outside the article-noun group ὁ λόγος, so it is predicative: \"the word is good.\"","greek":"ὁ λόγος ἀγαθός"},{"q":"What is the name for an adjective that sits inside the article-noun group?","kind":"concept","options":["Predicative","Genitive","Attributive","Relative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Inside the article-noun group, the adjective is attributive and directly describes the noun."},{"q":"True or false: an adjective in predicative position sits inside the article-noun group.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A predicative adjective sits outside the group and makes a statement about the noun."},{"q":"How should this phrase be translated?","kind":"meaning","options":["the shepherd is good","the shepherds are good","good for the shepherd","the good shepherd"],"answer":3,"explain":"καλός sits between the article and the noun, inside the group, so it is attributive: \"the good shepherd.\"","greek":"ὁ καλὸς ποιμήν"},{"q":"What form do the article, adjective, and noun share in this phrase?","kind":"form","options":["accusative feminine singular","nominative feminine singular","genitive feminine singular","accusative masculine plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"τήν, ἁγίαν, and ἐκκλησίαν are all accusative feminine singular: \"the holy church.\"","greek":"τὴν ἁγίαν ἐκκλησίαν"},{"q":"πᾶσα agrees with ἡ γῆ in this phrase. What form do they share?","kind":"form","options":["accusative feminine singular","nominative feminine singular","genitive feminine singular","nominative neuter plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"πᾶσα and ἡ γῆ are both nominative feminine singular: \"all the earth.\"","greek":"πᾶσα ἡ γῆ"},{"q":"True or false: a Greek adjective changes its endings to match the noun it describes.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. If the noun is masculine or accusative, the adjective takes a masculine or accusative ending too."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson calls μέγας the most common adjective in the New Testament.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson gives that title to πᾶς / πᾶσα / πᾶν (all, every). μέγας means \"great.\"","greek":"μέγας"},{"q":"True or false: an adjective's ending can tell you about its noun even if you do not recognize the noun yet.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Because the adjective matches its noun, its ending gives you information about the noun it is attached to."},{"q":"What is the form of this adjective?","kind":"form","options":["dative singular feminine","nominative plural feminine","dative plural feminine","accusative plural feminine"],"answer":2,"explain":"The -αις ending is feminine dative plural, the same ending as the noun γραφαῖς.","greek":"ἀγαθαῖς"},{"q":"What is the form of this adjective?","kind":"form","options":["nominative singular masculine","accusative singular feminine","genitive singular feminine","nominative singular feminine"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαθή is the feminine nominative singular. The masculine is ἀγαθός and the neuter is ἀγαθόν.","greek":"ἀγαθή"},{"q":"Where do 2-1-2 adjectives like ἀγαθός get their endings?","kind":"concept","options":["They borrow the noun endings you already learned","They use endings unrelated to nouns","They use verb endings","They never change their endings"],"answer":0,"explain":"You do not need to memorize random forms. The adjective borrows the 1st and 2nd declension noun endings you already know.","greek":"ἀγαθός"},{"q":"A word has familiar case endings but is not the main noun. What does the lesson suggest?","kind":"concept","options":["It must be the verb","It may be an adjective, so check for agreement","It is always a pronoun","It can be ignored"],"answer":1,"explain":"If a word with familiar case endings is not the main noun, it may be an adjective. Check whether it agrees in gender, case, and number."}],"pronouns":[{"q":"What is a pronoun's antecedent?","kind":"concept","options":["The noun it stands in for","The verb it follows","The case it takes","The article before it"],"answer":0,"explain":"A pronoun stands in for a noun already mentioned. That earlier noun is its antecedent."},{"q":"Which pronoun does the lesson call the most common in the New Testament?","kind":"concept","options":["οὗτος","αὐτός","ἐκεῖνος","ὅς"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτός (he, she, it) is the most common pronoun in the NT. You will see it in almost every chapter."},{"q":"What does this masculine form of αὐτός mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["he","him","his","to him"],"answer":2,"explain":"αὐτοῦ is genitive, so it means \"his\" (or \"its\" in the neuter).","greek":"αὐτοῦ"},{"q":"What does this form of αὐτός mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["she","of her","to her","her (as the object)"],"answer":3,"explain":"αὐτήν is the feminine accusative, so it means \"her\" as the object of the action.","greek":"αὐτήν"},{"q":"What case is this form, and what does it mean?","kind":"form","options":["genitive: her / of her","dative: to her","accusative: her","nominative: she"],"answer":0,"explain":"αὐτῆς is the feminine genitive. Its ending matches the noun ending -ης you already learned.","greek":"αὐτῆς"},{"q":"This neuter form appears in two rows of the αὐτός chart. Which cases could it be?","kind":"form","options":["genitive or dative","nominative or accusative","dative only","genitive only"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτό appears in both the nominative and accusative rows of the neuter column, just like neuter nouns.","greek":"αὐτό"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["him","his","he","to him"],"answer":2,"explain":"αὐτός is the masculine nominative, so it means \"he.\"","greek":"αὐτός"},{"q":"What does this phrase from John 3:16 mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["from him","his","to her","into him"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰς means \"into,\" and αὐτόν is masculine accusative \"him,\" so the phrase is \"into him.\"","greek":"εἰς αὐτόν"},{"q":"What does this pronoun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["this","that","who","I"],"answer":0,"explain":"οὗτος means \"this.\" It points to something near, immediate, or just mentioned.","greek":"οὗτος"},{"q":"This is a form of \"this.\" What gender is it?","kind":"form","options":["masculine","feminine","neuter","plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὕτη is the feminine form of \"this.\" The masculine is οὗτος and the neuter is τοῦτο.","greek":"αὕτη"},{"q":"This is a form of \"this.\" What gender is it?","kind":"form","options":["masculine","feminine","neuter","plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"τοῦτο is the neuter form of \"this.\"","greek":"τοῦτο"},{"q":"In this sentence (Matthew 3:17), what does μου mean?","kind":"reading","options":["your","his","our","my"],"answer":3,"explain":"μου is the short genitive form of \"I,\" meaning \"of me\" or \"my\": \"My beloved Son.\"","greek":"οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ υἱός μου ὁ ἀγαπητός."},{"q":"What does this short pronoun form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to/for me","my","me (as the object)","I"],"answer":0,"explain":"μοι is the dative of \"I,\" so it means \"to me\" or \"for me.\"","greek":"μοι"},{"q":"What does this pronoun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all","we","they","I"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἡμεῖς means \"we.\" The lesson notes it is common in Paul's letters.","greek":"ἡμεῖς"},{"q":"What does this pronoun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we","you (singular)","you all","they"],"answer":2,"explain":"ὑμεῖς means \"you all,\" the plural \"you.\" The singular is σύ.","greek":"ὑμεῖς"},{"q":"What case is this short form of \"I / me\"?","kind":"form","options":["genitive","dative","nominative","accusative"],"answer":3,"explain":"με (also ἐμέ) is the accusative, \"me\" as the object.","greek":"με"},{"q":"What does this short pronoun form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to/for you (singular)","of you / your","you (as the object)","you all"],"answer":0,"explain":"σοι is the dative of σύ, so it means \"to you\" or \"for you,\" speaking to one person.","greek":"σοι"},{"q":"What does this pronoun form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to us","of us / our","us (as the object)","we"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἡμῶν is the genitive of ἡμεῖς, so it means \"of us\" or \"our.\" Notice the -ων genitive plural ending.","greek":"ἡμῶν"},{"q":"What does this pronoun form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["of you all","to you all","you all (as the object)","you (singular)"],"answer":2,"explain":"ὑμᾶς is the accusative of ὑμεῖς, so it is \"you all\" as the object.","greek":"ὑμᾶς"},{"q":"What does this pronoun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["this one","who / which","I myself","that one"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐκεῖνος means \"that one.\" It points farther away or back to a known person or thing.","greek":"ἐκεῖνος"},{"q":"What does this pronoun do?","kind":"concept","options":["It introduces a relative clause: who / which / that","It points to something nearby: this","It means I or me","It means that one, farther away"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὅς / ἥ / ὅ is the relative pronoun. It means who, which, or that, and connects clauses.","greek":"ὅς / ἥ / ὅ"},{"q":"What does this phrase from John 14:24 mean?","kind":"reading","options":["this word you hear","the word that you hear","that one will hear the word","you hear his word"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὃν is a relative pronoun (\"that\"), connecting ὁ λόγος to the clause \"you hear.\"","greek":"ὁ λόγος ὃν ἀκούετε"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"reading","options":["this one testifies","who testifies","that one / he will testify","I will testify"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐκεῖνος is \"that one,\" so the phrase is \"that one (he) will testify.\"","greek":"ἐκεῖνος μαρτυρήσει"},{"q":"True or false: Greek pronouns change their endings like nouns, showing case, gender, and number.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Pronouns use the same case system as nouns, which helps you see their role and what they point back to."},{"q":"True or false: a pronoun's case tells you which noun it came from.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Gender and number point to the antecedent. Case tells you the pronoun's own job in its sentence."},{"q":"True or false: Greek often does not need a separate subject pronoun, because the verb ending already shows the subject.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Personal pronouns still appear for emphasis, contrast, possession, and objects."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says you need to memorize the plural forms of αὐτός right now.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Plurals like αὐτοί and αὐτῶν exist, but you don't need to memorize them yet. They follow the same ending patterns."},{"q":"When you see a pronoun, what question does the lesson tell you to ask?","kind":"concept","options":["What tense is the verb?","Which preposition comes next?","How many syllables does it have?","What noun was just introduced that matches this gender and number?"],"answer":3,"explain":"A pronoun's gender and number match its antecedent, so look back for a noun that fits."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"reading","options":["you are the Christ","I am the Christ","he is the Christ","we are the Christ"],"answer":0,"explain":"σύ is \"you\" (singular); before εἶ it is written σὺ. The phrase means \"you are the Christ.\"","greek":"σὺ εἶ ὁ Χριστός"},{"q":"This is a dative form of αὐτός. Which genders could it be?","kind":"form","options":["feminine only","masculine or neuter","masculine only","neuter only"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτῷ is the dative in both the masculine and neuter columns. The feminine dative is αὐτῇ.","greek":"αὐτῷ"}],"conjunctions":[{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["and / also / even","but (strong contrast)","for / because","therefore / so"],"answer":0,"explain":"καί means \"and,\" but it can also mean \"also\" or \"even.\"","greek":"καί"},{"q":"καί is the second most common word in the New Testament. Which word is more common?","kind":"concept","options":["δέ","the article ὁ","αὐτός","ἐν"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says only the article ὁ appears more often than καί.","greek":"καί"},{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["in order that","therefore / so","but / and / now","that / because"],"answer":2,"explain":"δέ means \"but,\" \"and,\" or \"now.\" It marks a soft transition or mild contrast.","greek":"δέ"},{"q":"Which three conjunctions does the lesson say always come second in their clause, never first?","kind":"concept","options":["καί, ἀλλά, and ἵνα","ἀλλά, ἵνα, and ὅτι","γάρ, ἵνα, and καί","δέ, γάρ, and οὖν"],"answer":3,"explain":"δέ, γάρ, and οὖν are tucked behind the first word of their clause, as in ὁ δὲ Ἰησοῦς. They never stand first."},{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["but (strong contrast)","and / also / even","for / because","so that"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀλλά means \"but\" and marks a strong contrast.","greek":"ἀλλά"},{"q":"How does ἀλλά differ from δέ?","kind":"concept","options":["ἀλλά is gentle and δέ is sharp","ἀλλά is a hard pivot and δέ is a gentle turn","Both mean \"therefore\"","ἀλλά gives a reason and δέ gives a purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both can be translated \"but,\" but δέ is a gentle turn while ἀλλά is a hard pivot."},{"q":"ἀλλά often follows οὐ (\"not\"). What pattern do they form together?","kind":"concept","options":["X, therefore Y","X, because Y","not X, but rather Y","X, so that Y"],"answer":2,"explain":"οὐ ... ἀλλά gives the pattern \"not X, but rather Y.\"","greek":"οὐ ... ἀλλά"},{"q":"In this line from Matt 5:17, which word marks the sharp contrast?","kind":"reading","options":["οὐκ","ἦλθον","πληρῶσαι","ἀλλὰ"],"answer":3,"explain":"οὐκ sets up the \"not\" and ἀλλά supplies the strong \"but,\" forming the \"not X, but rather Y\" pattern.","greek":"οὐκ ἦλθον καταλῦσαι ἀλλὰ πληρῶσαι"},{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["for / because / since","therefore / so","in order that","but (strong)"],"answer":0,"explain":"γάρ means \"for,\" \"because,\" or \"since.\" It gives the reason for what was just said.","greek":"γάρ"},{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["that / because","in order that / so that","therefore / so","and / also"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἵνα means \"in order that\" or \"so that.\" It introduces purpose.","greek":"ἵνα"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does reading conjunctions carefully tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["The tense of every verb","The gender of each noun","How the author is thinking, not just what they're saying","Where each sentence begins and ends"],"answer":2,"explain":"Conjunctions show whether the author is adding, contrasting, explaining, or concluding. That is how the author is thinking, not just what they say."},{"q":"In this clause from John 3:16, what does ἵνα introduce?","kind":"reading","options":["A reason","A conclusion","A soft transition","A purpose"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἵνα introduces the purpose of an action: \"so that ...\"","greek":"ἵνα μὴ ἀπόληται ἀλλ' ἔχῃ ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"The lesson says this conjunction has two jobs. What are they?","kind":"concept","options":["It introduces a reason (\"because\") or content (\"that\")","It introduces a purpose or a command","It marks a conclusion or a transition","It adds an idea or a sharp contrast"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὅτι either gives a reason (\"because\") or introduces the content of a statement (\"that\"). Context tells you which.","greek":"ὅτι"},{"q":"What does ὅτι do in this sentence?","kind":"reading","options":["It gives a purpose","It introduces what is known","It draws a conclusion","It marks a sharp contrast"],"answer":1,"explain":"Here ὅτι means \"that\" and introduces the content of what is known: \"we know that he is true.\"","greek":"οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἀληθής ἐστιν"},{"q":"What does this conjunction mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["for / because","but / now","therefore / so / then","in order that"],"answer":2,"explain":"οὖν means \"therefore,\" \"so,\" or \"then.\"","greek":"οὖν"},{"q":"What does οὖν signal about the sentence it appears in?","kind":"concept","options":["It explains the reason for what came before","It introduces a purpose","It starts a strong contrast","It draws a conclusion from what came before"],"answer":3,"explain":"οὖν says, in effect, \"given all that, here is the takeaway.\" γάρ is the one that gives a reason.","greek":"οὖν"},{"q":"True or false: in the verb preview, the perfect tense describes an action done with lasting results.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The preview describes present as ongoing, aorist as a single completed event, and perfect as done with lasting results."},{"q":"True or false: where English says \"and,\" Greek might mean \"also\" or even \"even.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. That is why καί is glossed \"and / also / even.\""},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says Greek conjunctions are less precise than English ones.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek conjunctions are more precise. For example, Greek separates a gentle transition (δέ) from a sharp contrast (ἀλλά)."},{"q":"Nouns have declensions. What is the matching concept for verbs called?","kind":"concept","options":["Conjugation","Parsing","Agreement","Declension"],"answer":0,"explain":"Verb ending patterns are called conjugation, the verb counterpart to noun declensions."},{"q":"According to the verb preview, how many things does a verb ending pack into one word?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Five","Two","Seven"],"answer":1,"explain":"Verb endings pack five things into one word: person, number, tense, voice, and mood."},{"q":"Which verb category tells you who is doing the action: I, you, he/she/it, we, you all, or they?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Mood","Person","Tense"],"answer":2,"explain":"Person tells you who is doing the action."},{"q":"Which tense does the preview describe as a single completed event?","kind":"concept","options":["Present","Perfect","Indicative","Aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist is described as a single completed event. Present is ongoing, and perfect is done with lasting results."},{"q":"In which voice does the subject do the action for itself?","kind":"concept","options":["Middle","Active","Passive","Imperative"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the middle voice, you do the action for yourself. Active means you do it; passive means it is done to you."},{"q":"What mood is the verb that follows ἵνα in a purpose clause?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Subjunctive","Imperative","Active"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἵνα introduces a purpose clause, and the verb after it is in the subjunctive mood.","greek":"ἵνα"},{"q":"A verb is labeled \"present active indicative.\" Which three categories do these words name?","kind":"concept","options":["Person, number, and tense","Case, number, and gender","Tense, voice, and mood","Voice, mood, and person"],"answer":2,"explain":"Present is the tense, active is the voice, and indicative is the mood."},{"q":"Which mood states a fact?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Subjunctive","Passive","Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The indicative states a fact. The imperative gives a command, and the subjunctive expresses purpose or possibility."},{"q":"True or false: in the passive voice, the subject does the action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. In the passive voice the action is done to the subject. In the active voice the subject does it."},{"q":"How can this phrase be translated?","kind":"meaning","options":["but Jesus / now Jesus","for Jesus","therefore Jesus","so that Jesus"],"answer":0,"explain":"δέ (tucked after ὁ) means \"but\" or \"now,\" so the phrase is \"but Jesus\" or \"now Jesus.\"","greek":"ὁ δὲ Ἰησοῦς"},{"q":"In this line from John 1:1, which word is the conjunction \"and\"?","kind":"reading","options":["ὁ","καὶ","ἦν","πρὸς"],"answer":1,"explain":"καί (written καὶ here) is the conjunction \"and.\"","greek":"καὶ ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεόν"}],"howToRead":[{"q":"What is the lesson's \"big rule\" for reading a Greek sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["Ask “what pieces do I recognize?”","Ask “what does it all mean?”","Translate every word in order","Skip any word you don't know"],"answer":0,"explain":"Don't try to grasp the whole sentence at once. Look for the pieces you recognize, then build from there."},{"q":"In the complete reading process, what is step 0?","kind":"concept","options":["Find the verb","Read the conjunctions","Find the subject","Build the meaning"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 0 is reading the conjunctions, so you know the sentence's direction before you read a noun."},{"q":"Why does the lesson have you glance at conjunctions before finding the verb?","kind":"concept","options":["They show the verb's tense","They show the subject's gender","They tell you how the sentence relates to what came before","They mark where the sentence ends"],"answer":2,"explain":"Conjunctions tell you how this sentence connects to what came before, and that context shapes the rest of your reading."},{"q":"You spot γάρ in second position. What does that tell you about the sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["It draws a conclusion","It states a purpose","It makes a strong contrast","It explains what came before"],"answer":3,"explain":"γάρ (\"for / because\") signals that this sentence explains what came before.","greek":"γάρ"},{"q":"You spot οὖν in second position. What does that tell you about the sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["It draws a conclusion","It explains what came before","It states a purpose","It makes a strong contrast"],"answer":0,"explain":"οὖν (\"therefore / so\") signals that this sentence draws a conclusion.","greek":"οὖν"},{"q":"What does the lesson compare conjunctions to?","kind":"concept","options":["Building blocks","Road signs","Anchors","Labels"],"answer":1,"explain":"Conjunctions are road signs: read them first and you know which direction the sentence is heading."},{"q":"Which word is the verb in this sentence?","kind":"form","options":["ζωὴν","αἰώνιον","ἔχομεν","ζωὴν αἰώνιον"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔχομεν (\"we have\") is the verb. ζωὴν αἰώνιον is the noun group.","greek":"ζωὴν αἰώνιον ἔχομεν."},{"q":"What does this sentence mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Eternal life has us.","They have eternal life.","We are eternal life.","We have eternal life."],"answer":3,"explain":"ζωὴν αἰώνιον (eternal life) is one group, and ἔχομεν means \"we have.\"","greek":"ζωὴν αἰώνιον ἔχομεν."},{"q":"Why do ζωὴν and αἰώνιον travel together as one unit?","kind":"concept","options":["The adjective agrees with the noun; both are accusative","Both words are verbs","Both words follow an article","Both words begin with a vowel"],"answer":0,"explain":"αἰώνιον is an adjective agreeing with the noun ζωήν. Both are accusative, so they form one phrase.","greek":"ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"When you see a preposition, what does the lesson tell you to look for?","kind":"concept","options":["The verb","The phrase attached to it","The subject","The conjunction"],"answer":1,"explain":"A preposition brings a small phrase with it, so look for that phrase."},{"q":"In the complete reading process, what clue helps you find the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["The genitive ending -ου","The preposition ἐν","The nominative article (ὁ/ἡ/οἱ/αἱ)","The conjunction γάρ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Look for the nominative article ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, or αἱ. That is your subject."},{"q":"When you see this article, what does it often point to?","kind":"form","options":["A feminine plural noun","A neuter noun","A verb","A masculine singular noun"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὁ means \"the\" and often points to a masculine singular noun.","greek":"ὁ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they believed in him","he believed in them","they believed in her","we believe in him"],"answer":0,"explain":"αὐτόν is masculine accusative (\"him\"), so the phrase is \"they believed in him.\"","greek":"ἐπίστευσαν εἰς αὐτόν"},{"q":"In John 3:16, which noun does the lesson say αὐτόν points back to?","kind":"concept","options":["τὸν κόσμον","τὸν υἱόν","ὁ θεός","ζωήν"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτόν is masculine accusative. The lesson traces it back to τὸν υἱόν (the Son).","greek":"αὐτόν"},{"q":"What does a pronoun share with the noun it refers to?","kind":"concept","options":["Case and tense","Accent and breathing","Gender and number","Person and mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"A pronoun's gender and number match the noun it refers to. That is how you track references."},{"q":"True or false: the goal of this lesson is to translate perfectly.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The goal is to teach your eyes what to look for first, not perfect translation."},{"q":"True or false: these four forms are related to the same basic word.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. All four are forms of λόγος (word). Their endings show different roles in the sentence.","greek":"λόγος / λόγον / λόγῳ / λόγου"},{"q":"In Step 5, which role is described as \"of/from something\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject","Object","Location/means","Possession/source"],"answer":3,"explain":"Possession/source covers \"of\" or \"from\" something. Location/means covers \"in/by/with.\""},{"q":"When grouping words, what tells you where a group begins?","kind":"concept","options":["The article","The verb","The conjunction","The final sigma"],"answer":0,"explain":"The article marks where a group begins, and adjectives join that group."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe this verb?","kind":"form","options":["present active indicative, 1st person plural","aorist active indicative, 3rd person singular","perfect passive indicative, 3rd person singular","aorist active subjunctive, 3rd person plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἠγάπησεν, \"he loved,\" is aorist (a completed event), active, indicative (a fact), 3rd person singular.","greek":"ἠγάπησεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe this verb?","kind":"form","options":["aorist active indicative, 3rd person singular","present passive indicative, 1st person plural","present active indicative, 1st person plural","present active imperative, 2nd person plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔχομεν, \"we have,\" is present (ongoing), active, indicative, 1st person plural.","greek":"ἔχομεν"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we have","is","sees","loved"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἠγάπησεν means \"loved\" (\"he loved\").","greek":"ἠγάπησεν"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["sees","is","loved","we have"],"answer":0,"explain":"βλέπει means \"sees.\"","greek":"βλέπει"},{"q":"Which tense means the action happened and the result still stands?","kind":"concept","options":["Present","Perfect","Aorist","Subjunctive"],"answer":1,"explain":"The perfect tense means it happened and the result still stands. Present is ongoing; aorist is a single completed event."},{"q":"In which voice does the subject receive the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Active","Middle","Passive","Indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Passive means the subject receives the action. Active means the subject does it."},{"q":"Which mood gives a command?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Subjunctive","Aorist","Imperative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The imperative gives a command. The indicative states a fact, and the subjunctive expresses purpose or possibility."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what question do verb endings answer?","kind":"concept","options":["Who is acting, how, when, and with what kind of action?","What role is this noun playing in the sentence?","What gender is the noun this word describes?","Which preposition comes next?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Noun endings answer \"what role am I playing?\" Verb endings answer who is acting, how, when, and with what kind of action."},{"q":"True or false: prepositions add location, direction, or source to a sentence.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Endings show case, and prepositions add relationships like location, direction, and source."},{"q":"True or false: you should settle on the meaning of a sentence before recognizing its pieces.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Recognize the pieces first. Meaning comes from putting them together."},{"q":"When you see ἵνα, what does it tell you about what follows?","kind":"concept","options":["It is a conclusion","It is the purpose","It is a reason","It is a contrast"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἵνα (\"in order that\") tells you that what follows is the purpose.","greek":"ἵνα"}],"adv_history":[{"q":"During roughly which span of time did Koine Greek develop?","kind":"concept","options":["800–500 BC","500–300 BC","300–1453 AD","300 BC–300 AD"],"answer":3,"explain":"Koine developed between about 300 BC and 300 AD, as Greek spread far beyond its homeland. That is the NT period.","greek":"κοινή"},{"q":"Plato, Aristotle, Thucydides and Sophocles wrote in which stage of Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["Epic Greek","Koine Greek","Classical (Attic) Greek","Byzantine Greek"],"answer":2,"explain":"Classical (Attic) Greek, about 500–300 BC, was the golden age of Greek philosophy and drama. It is the dialect most university Greek courses focus on."},{"q":"Modern Greek is recognizably descended from Koine Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Modern Greek comes from Koine, though it has changed a lot in vocabulary, pronunciation and grammar."},{"q":"Which philosopher educated Alexander the Great?","kind":"concept","options":["Plato","Homer","Sophocles","Aristotle"],"answer":3,"explain":"Alexander, king of Macedon, was educated by the philosopher Aristotle and was enthusiastic about Greek culture."},{"q":"After Alexander's empire broke apart, which dynasty of his generals ruled Egypt?","kind":"concept","options":["The Ptolemies","The Seleucids","The Antigonids","The Herodians"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Ptolemies ruled Egypt. The Seleucids held Syria and Persia, and the Antigonids held Macedonia."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what was the daily language of Jesus and his disciples?","kind":"concept","options":["Hebrew","Aramaic","Latin","Greek"],"answer":1,"explain":"Jesus and his disciples spoke Aramaic day to day. The Jewish scriptures were written mostly in Hebrew."},{"q":"The name \"Septuagint\" comes from a Latin word. What does that word mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Seventy","Seven","Seventy-two","Translation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Latin septuaginta means \"seventy,\" which is why the Septuagint is abbreviated LXX, the Roman numeral for 70."},{"q":"According to the Letter of Aristeas, which king wanted a Greek copy of the Jewish law for his library?","kind":"concept","options":["Alexander the Great","Ptolemy II Philadelphus","Aristotle","Constantin von Tischendorf"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ptolemy II Philadelphus of Egypt wanted the Jewish law for the Library of Alexandria, so Jewish scholars came to translate it."},{"q":"Which part of the Old Testament was translated into Greek first, around 250 BC?","kind":"concept","options":["The Psalms","The Prophets","Daniel and Ezra","The Torah (Genesis through Deuteronomy)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The translation began with the Torah, the first five books, around 250 BC in Alexandria. The rest followed over the next century or two."},{"q":"What does this NT term mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Faith","Grace","Righteousness","Flesh"],"answer":1,"explain":"χάρις means grace. The lesson lists it among terms whose meaning was shaped by how the LXX used them.","greek":"χάρις"},{"q":"What does this NT term mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Grace","Word","Faith","Love"],"answer":2,"explain":"πίστις means faith. It is one of the terms the lesson says carries meaning shaped by the LXX.","greek":"πίστις"},{"q":"What does this NT term mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Righteousness","Grace","Faith","Word"],"answer":0,"explain":"δικαιοσύνη is usually translated \"righteousness,\" though the lesson notes it has legal, relational and covenantal dimensions.","greek":"δικαιοσύνη"},{"q":"How many documents make up the New Testament?","kind":"concept","options":["22","24","27","39"],"answer":2,"explain":"The NT has 27 documents, all written in Koine Greek over roughly 50 years."},{"q":"Which Gospel is widely considered the earliest?","kind":"concept","options":["Matthew","Mark","Luke","John"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mark, dated around 65–70 AD, is widely considered the earliest Gospel, with Luke and Matthew likely following."},{"q":"Which group of letters does the lesson call the Prison Epistles?","kind":"concept","options":["Romans, 1–2 Corinthians, Galatians","1–2 Thessalonians, James, Jude","Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians, Philemon","Hebrews, 1–3 John, Revelation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians and Philemon are the Prison Epistles, dated around 62–63 AD during Paul's Roman imprisonment."},{"q":"The lesson presents Revelation as one of the earliest NT documents.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. John's writings, including Revelation, are dated around 85–95 AD and are the latest NT documents."},{"q":"Where were most of the papyrus manuscripts discovered?","kind":"concept","options":["Greece","Rome","Asia Minor","Egypt"],"answer":3,"explain":"Papyrus survives best in dry conditions, and most papyri were found in Egypt's dry climate."},{"q":"About what share of the NT's textual variants are simple spelling differences?","kind":"concept","options":["About 1%","About 25%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":3,"explain":"About 75% of variants are just spelling differences, like \"favor\" vs. \"favour.\" Only about 1% are meaningful and viable."},{"q":"What does the abbreviation NA28 refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["The Nestle-Aland Greek New Testament, 28th edition","The United Bible Societies text, 28th edition","The 28th printing of the Septuagint","A list of 28 key manuscripts"],"answer":0,"explain":"NA28 is the 28th edition of the Nestle-Aland Greek New Testament, the text most Greek NT scholars use today."},{"q":"The UBS5 Greek New Testament uses a different base text from NA28.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. UBS5 uses the same base text as NA28. It differs by having a more detailed apparatus for translators."},{"q":"What does this reordered version of the lesson's example sentence mean?","kind":"reading","options":["The world loves God.","God loves the world.","The world is God.","God is loved by the world."],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending on κόσμον marks it as the object wherever it stands, so the sentence still means \"God loves the world.\"","greek":"τὸν κόσμον ἀγαπᾷ θεός"},{"q":"How does the aorist tense portray an action?","kind":"concept","options":["As ongoing, continuous action","As a simple whole, without commenting on duration","As habitual, repeated action","As action that has not happened yet"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist views the action as a simple whole. The present tense, by contrast, typically shows ongoing action."},{"q":"In the lesson's John 21 discussion, which Greek word is described as the word for friendship-love?","kind":"meaning","options":["ἀγαπάω","φιλέω","ἀγάπη","λόγος"],"answer":1,"explain":"φιλέω is the word for friendship-love. ἀγαπάω is described as the word for deep, sacrificial love."},{"q":"What is the basic meaning of this word?","kind":"meaning","options":["Word","Grace","Flesh","Faith"],"answer":2,"explain":"σάρξ means \"flesh.\" The lesson notes Paul also uses it for the fallen human nature, not just physical tissue.","greek":"σάρξ"},{"q":"This form appears in the Greek NT. What is its lexical form?","kind":"form","options":["λόγον","λόγου","λόγοι","λόγος"],"answer":3,"explain":"λόγον, λόγου and λόγῳ are all forms of one lexical entry, λόγος, the nominative singular you look up in a lexicon.","greek":"λόγῳ"},{"q":"λύω (\"I loose\") is the lexical form of a verb. Which form does a lexicon list for verbs?","kind":"form","options":["First person singular present active indicative","Third person singular aorist indicative","The present infinitive","Second person plural present imperative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Verbs are listed in the first person singular present active indicative, like λύω, \"I loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"A lexicon lists the adjective ἀγαθός in which form?","kind":"form","options":["Feminine nominative singular","Neuter nominative singular","Masculine genitive singular","Masculine nominative singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"Adjectives are listed in the masculine nominative singular, so ἀγαθός is the form you look up.","greek":"ἀγαθός"},{"q":"Which lexicon does the lesson call the standard one in NT scholarship?","kind":"concept","options":["Strong's","Thayer's","BDAG","NA28"],"answer":2,"explain":"BDAG (Bauer, Danker, Arndt, Gingrich) is the standard NT lexicon. Thayer's is older, and Strong's is a numbered concordance."},{"q":"The lesson says this word means \"love,\" but specifically what kind?","kind":"meaning","options":["Unconditional, self-giving love","Friendship-love","Any kind of affection","Fallen human nature"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀγάπη is love, but specifically unconditional, self-giving love, not just any affection.","greek":"ἀγάπη"},{"q":"Mounce's 320 required words each occur over 50 times in the NT. Together they cover about what share of word occurrences?","kind":"concept","options":["Over 50%","Over 65%","Over 80%","Over 95%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Those 320 words cover over 80% of all word occurrences, so a small vocabulary gives broad coverage of the text."}],"adv_alphabet":[{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Phi","Theta","Psi","Omicron"],"answer":1,"explain":"θ is Theta. It sounds like the \"th\" in think.","greek":"θ"},{"q":"What is the name of this letter?","kind":"letter","options":["Phi","Chi","Upsilon","Psi"],"answer":3,"explain":"ψ is Psi. It sounds like the \"ps\" in lips.","greek":"ψ"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["\"p\" as in peace","\"ph/f\" as in phone","\"ps\" as in lips","\"th\" as in think"],"answer":1,"explain":"φ (Phi) is the aspirated labial, a breathy \"p\" that sounds like \"ph/f\" in phone.","greek":"φ"},{"q":"What sound does this letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["\"ch\" as in Bach","\"ks\" as in axe","\"x\" as in box","\"h\" as in hat"],"answer":0,"explain":"χ (Chi) is a guttural sound from the back of the throat, like the \"ch\" in Bach or loch. It is never an English \"x.\"","greek":"χ"},{"q":"This capital letter looks like an English letter. What sound does it actually make?","kind":"sound","options":["\"h\" as in hat","Short \"e\" as in met","Long \"e\" as in they","\"i\" as in machine"],"answer":2,"explain":"Η is Eta, a vowel that sounds like the long \"e\" in they. It is never an \"h\" consonant.","greek":"Η"},{"q":"What does the breathing mark over the first letter of this word tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["Start cleanly on the vowel, with no \"h\" sound","Add an \"h\" sound before the vowel","The vowel is pronounced long","The syllable is stressed"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is a smooth breathing. It means no \"h\" sound, so you start directly on the \"ah.\"","greek":"ἀγάπη"},{"q":"What does this word mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Sin","Church, assembly","Gospel","Peace"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐκκλησία (ek-klay-SEE-a) means church or assembly. It gives English \"ecclesiastical.\"","greek":"ἐκκλησία"},{"q":"This word is the lesson's example of the οι diphthong. What does it mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Son","Heaven","House","Peace"],"answer":2,"explain":"οἰκία means house. Its οι diphthong sounds like the \"oy\" in boy.","greek":"οἰκία"},{"q":"What sound does this lowercase letter make?","kind":"sound","options":["\"v\" as in vine","\"u\" as in French tu","\"m\" as in mother","\"n\" as in now"],"answer":3,"explain":"Lowercase ν is Nu. It looks like a \"v\" in many fonts, but it is always \"n.\"","greek":"ν"},{"q":"Which vowels can be either short or long, depending on the word form?","kind":"concept","options":["ε, ο","η, ω","α, ι, υ","ε, η, ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"α, ι and υ can be short or long. ε and ο are always short, and η and ω are always long."},{"q":"The letter names carry the vowel-length idea. What does \"omicron\" literally mean?","kind":"letter","options":["\"Small o\"","\"Great o\"","\"Bare e\"","\"Two sounds\""],"answer":0,"explain":"Omicron means \"small o,\" the always-short o. Omega, \"great o,\" is its always-long partner.","greek":"ο"},{"q":"How does this diphthong sound?","kind":"sound","options":["\"ow\" as in cow","\"oy\" as in boy","\"oo\" as in food","\"eu\" as in feud"],"answer":2,"explain":"ου sounds like the \"oo\" in food, as in οὐρανός (heaven).","greek":"ου"},{"q":"How is the double γγ in this word pronounced?","kind":"sound","options":["\"g-g,\" two hard g sounds","\"ng\" as in sing","\"j\" as in jet","\"gh,\" a breathy g"],"answer":1,"explain":"γ before κ, γ, χ or ξ sounds like \"ng,\" so ἄγγελος is \"AN-ge-los,\" the source of English \"angel.\"","greek":"ἄγγελος"},{"q":"Which consonant pairing does this double letter stand for?","kind":"letter","options":["κ + σ","π + σ","τ + θ","δ + σ (or σ + δ)"],"answer":3,"explain":"ζ (Zeta) packs δ and σ together, which is why it was historically pronounced \"dz.\"","greek":"ζ"},{"q":"This word begins with a diphthong. Where does its breathing mark go?","kind":"concept","options":["Over the first vowel","Over the second vowel","To the left of the word","Under the first vowel"],"answer":1,"explain":"On a diphthong, the breathing mark sits over the second vowel, as in αὐτός and εἰρήνη.","greek":"αὐτός"},{"q":"The subscript iota changes this form's grammar. Which case is it?","kind":"form","options":["Nominative","Genitive","Accusative","Dative"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγάπῃ, with the iota subscript, is dative: love as the indirect object, \"to/for love.\" ἀγάπη without it is nominative.","greek":"ἀγάπῃ"},{"q":"In this verse, which word is the definite article \"the\"?","kind":"reading","options":["ὁ","Ἐν","ἀρχῇ","ἦν"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὁ (ho) is the masculine definite article \"the.\" Its rough breathing adds the \"h.\"","greek":"Ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λόγος"},{"q":"What was the Greeks' key change when they adapted the Phoenician script?","kind":"concept","options":["They added letters for numbers","They repurposed unneeded consonant letters as vowels","They introduced spaces between words","They invented lowercase letters"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Phoenician script wrote no vowels. The Greeks turned consonant letters they did not need into vowel letters."},{"q":"The acute accent, as in this word, may appear on any of the last how many syllables?","kind":"concept","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":2,"explain":"The acute can stand on any of the last three syllables. In ἄνθρωπος it is on the third-to-last.","greek":"ἄνθρωπος"},{"q":"In γράφω (I write) becoming γράψω (I will write), the stem's φ meets the σ of the future ending. What do they become?","kind":"form","options":["ξ","ζ","σ","ψ"],"answer":3,"explain":"φ is a labial, and any labial plus σ combines into ψ.","greek":"γράψω"},{"q":"This verb means \"I lead,\" and its stem ends in a velar. What is its future form, \"I will lead\"?","kind":"form","options":["ἄξω","ἄψω","ἄγσω","ἄζω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem ends in γ, a velar. Velar + σ becomes ξ, so the future is ἄξω.","greek":"ἄγω"},{"q":"What happens when a dental (τ, δ, θ) meets σ?","kind":"concept","options":["The dental and σ combine into ξ","The dental and σ combine into ψ","The dental drops out and σ remains","The σ drops out and the dental remains"],"answer":2,"explain":"A dental disappears before σ, which is why some noun stems seem to lose a letter in certain forms."},{"q":"In ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον, why is θεός written with this accent?","kind":"concept","options":["Because it is in the dative case","Because another word follows it, so its acute becomes a grave","Because its final vowel is long","Because it is the first word of a sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"A word that normally ends in an acute switches to a grave when another word follows it mid-sentence.","greek":"θεὸς"},{"q":"This word has a circumflex. Where is a circumflex allowed to appear?","kind":"concept","options":["On any vowel in the last three syllables","Only on the first syllable","Only on a long vowel or diphthong in one of the last two syllables","Only on a vowel with rough breathing"],"answer":2,"explain":"The circumflex is the most restricted accent: last two syllables only, and only on a long vowel or diphthong, like the ω in ἡμῶν.","greek":"ἡμῶν"},{"q":"What do the two dots over the υ in this name tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["The υ is silent","Add an \"h\" sound before the υ","The υ is always long","Pronounce υ as its own syllable, not a diphthong with ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"That mark is a diaeresis. It tells you not to blend ω + υ into a diphthong, so the υ gets its own syllable.","greek":"Μωϋσῆς"},{"q":"Who is credited with first introducing accent marks as a teaching tool?","kind":"concept","options":["Aristophanes of Byzantium","Erasmus of Rotterdam","Bill Mounce","Aristotle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Alexandrian grammarian Aristophanes of Byzantium (ca. 257–180 BC) introduced accent marks for students of Greek literature."},{"q":"In Modern Greek pronunciation, η, ι, ει, οι and υ all converge on which single sound?","kind":"sound","options":["\"ee\"","\"ay\"","\"oo\"","\"oy\""],"answer":0,"explain":"Modern Greek merges these into \"ee.\" Erasmian keeps them distinct, so you can hear the endings."},{"q":"A Greek word that begins with ρ always takes a rough breathing.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Initial rho always has a rough breathing (ῥ). That is why English words from Greek, like rhetoric and rhythm, are spelled with \"rh.\"","greek":"ῥῆμα"},{"q":"On a capital letter, the breathing mark is written above the letter, just as on lowercase.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. On a capital it goes to the left of the letter (Ἀ, Ἡ, Ἰ). On lowercase it sits above (ἀ, ἡ, ἰ).","greek":"Ἀ"},{"q":"The original NT manuscripts had spaces between words and accent marks.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. They were written in scriptio continua: all capitals, no spaces, no punctuation, and no accent or breathing marks.","greek":"ΕΝΑΡΧΗΗΝΟΛΟΓΟΣ"}],"adv_pronunciation":[{"q":"According to the lesson's syllable rule, every Greek syllable contains exactly what?","kind":"concept","options":["One consonant cluster","One vowel or one diphthong","Two vowels","One accent mark"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each syllable has exactly one vowel or one diphthong, so counting vowels and diphthongs counts the syllables."},{"q":"How is this word divided into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["λόγ-ος","λ-όγος","λό-γος","λόγο-ς"],"answer":2,"explain":"A single consonant between vowels goes with the following vowel, so the γ starts the second syllable: λό-γος.","greek":"λόγος"},{"q":"What does the term \"antepenult\" refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["The last syllable","The second-to-last syllable","The first syllable","The third-to-last syllable"],"answer":3,"explain":"The antepenult is the third-to-last syllable. The penult is second-to-last, and the ultima is last."},{"q":"In Erasmian pronunciation, how is β pronounced?","kind":"sound","options":["\"b\"","\"v\" as in vine","\"ph\" as in phone","\"m\" as in mother"],"answer":0,"explain":"Erasmian keeps β as \"b.\" In Modern Greek it has shifted to \"v.\"","greek":"β"},{"q":"This phrase shows elision. What does it mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["From me","And I","But I","To me"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀλλά + ἐγώ becomes ἀλλ' ἐγώ, \"but I.\" The final short α drops before the vowel, and the meaning is unchanged.","greek":"ἀλλ' ἐγώ"},{"q":"In Erasmian pronunciation, how do η and ι sound?","kind":"sound","options":["Identical, both \"ee\"","Different: \"ey\" vs. \"ee\"","Identical, both \"ay\"","Different: \"ah\" vs. \"oo\""],"answer":1,"explain":"Erasmian keeps η (\"ey\") and ι (\"ee\") distinct, unlike Modern Greek, where both are \"ee.\""},{"q":"Which accent mark may appear only on the ultima?","kind":"concept","options":["Acute","Grave","Circumflex","Diaeresis"],"answer":1,"explain":"The grave slants down and appears only on the last syllable. The acute can be on any of the last three, and the circumflex on the last two.","greek":"ὰ"},{"q":"How many syllables does this word have?","kind":"form","options":["3","4","6","5"],"answer":3,"explain":"Count the vowels and diphthongs: ευ, α, ε, ι, ο. That makes 5 syllables: εὐ-αγ-γέ-λι-ον.","greek":"εὐαγγέλιον"},{"q":"How is this word divided into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["ἐσ-τίν","ἐστ-ίν","ἐ-στίν","ἐστί-ν"],"answer":2,"explain":"στ can begin a Greek word, so the cluster stays together and starts the next syllable: ἐ-στίν.","greek":"ἐστίν"},{"q":"How is this word (\"near\") divided into syllables?","kind":"form","options":["ἐγ-γύς","ἐ-γγύς","ἐγγ-ύς","ἐγγύ-ς"],"answer":0,"explain":"Double letters like γγ always split between syllables: ἐγ-γύς.","greek":"ἐγγύς"},{"q":"This is the Greek name of the acute accent. What does it mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"Heavy\"","\"Circumflexed\"","\"Long\"","\"Sharp\""],"answer":3,"explain":"ὀξεῖα means \"sharp,\" for the sharp rise in pitch. βαρεῖα, the grave, means \"heavy.\"","greek":"ὀξεῖα"},{"q":"Which accent mark has this Greek name?","kind":"concept","options":["Acute","Grave","Circumflex","Rough breathing"],"answer":2,"explain":"περισπωμένη is the circumflex, the curved mark that appears only on the last two syllables.","greek":"περισπωμένη"},{"q":"Short words such as ἐν, εἰς and ὁ usually have no accent of their own. What are they called?","kind":"concept","options":["Enclitics","Proclitics","Diphthongs","Elisions"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐν, εἰς and ὁ are proclitics. Enclitics, like μου and ἐστιν, also lean on a neighboring word."},{"q":"What happened to the Greek accent by the Byzantine period (roughly AD 300–600)?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek became a tonal language like Mandarin","Accent marks stopped being written","The pitch distinction collapsed and Greek became a stress-accent language","The grave accent replaced the acute everywhere"],"answer":2,"explain":"The pitch system collapsed into stress, which is why modern Greek speakers treat accents like English stress marks."},{"q":"A circumflex on the ultima of a noun, as in this form, often signals which form?","kind":"form","options":["Nominative singular","Dative singular","Accusative plural","Genitive plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"θεῶν means \"of gods.\" A circumflex on the ultima ending -ῶν often marks the genitive plural.","greek":"θεῶν"},{"q":"This phrase shows elision. What is the full form of the first word?","kind":"form","options":["ἀλλά","ἀπό","ἐκ","ἐν"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἀπό + αὐτοῦ becomes ἀπ' αὐτοῦ, \"from him.\" The apostrophe shows where the short ο dropped.","greek":"ἀπ' αὐτοῦ"},{"q":"Why does ἐστίν end with ν in this phrase?","kind":"concept","options":["Because the next word begins with a vowel","Because the verb is plural","Because the sentence is a question","Because ἐστίν is always spelled with ν"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is moveable nu. It appears before a word beginning with a vowel, and drops before a consonant, as in ἐστί ποιμήν.","greek":"ἐστίν ἀγαθός"},{"q":"For learning Koine, which interpretation of the accent do most teachers, including Mounce, use?","kind":"concept","options":["Stress: the accented syllable is spoken more prominently","Pitch: sing the accented syllable higher","Length: hold the accented vowel longer","Silence: ignore accents when reading aloud"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most teachers use the stress interpretation, speaking the accented syllable louder. The pitch background explains why the rules are complex."},{"q":"The lesson divides this word as ἐκ-κλησία. Which syllable rule does it use to explain that split?","kind":"concept","options":["Compound words split at the seam between their original parts","A single consonant goes with the preceding vowel","Diphthongs are split between syllables","The accent decides where the word splits"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐκκλησία comes from ἐκ + καλέω, so it splits at the prefix boundary: ἐκ-κλησία.","greek":"ἐκκλησία"},{"q":"ἄνθρωπος is accented on the antepenult, but this genitive form is accented on the penult. Why?","kind":"form","options":["The genitive always accents the ultima","The ending -ου is long, so the antepenult is off-limits","The ending -ου is short, so the accent moves forward","Nouns follow the recessive accent rule"],"answer":1,"explain":"When the ultima is long, the acute cannot stand on the antepenult. The long diphthong -ου pushes it to the penult.","greek":"ἀνθρώπου"},{"q":"When does the circumflex appear on the penult?","kind":"concept","options":["When the penult is short and the ultima is long","When both the penult and the ultima are long","When the penult and the ultima are both short","When the penult is long and the ultima is short"],"answer":3,"explain":"A circumflex on the penult needs a long penult and a short ultima. If the ultima is long, the acute is used instead."},{"q":"What does the circumflex on this verb form (\"he does\") signal?","kind":"form","options":["It marks a genitive plural","The word is an enclitic","Two vowels were compressed into one long sound","The next word begins with a vowel"],"answer":2,"explain":"ποιεῖ is a contracted form. Its circumflex shows that two vowels were compressed into one long sound.","greek":"ποιεῖ"},{"q":"In this verb form (\"we loose\"), why does the accent sit on the antepenult?","kind":"form","options":["Its ultima -μεν is long, forcing the accent back","The persistent accent keeps it where the lexical form has it","The accent always falls on the verb stem's first vowel","Its ultima -μεν is short, so the accent recedes to the antepenult"],"answer":3,"explain":"Verbs take a recessive accent. With a short ultima like -μεν, the accent goes back to the antepenult: λύ-ο-μεν.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"How is the genitive plural of λόγος (\"of words\") accented?","kind":"form","options":["λόγων","λογῶν","λογών","λόγον"],"answer":0,"explain":"λόγος keeps its accent on the penult as an acute, so the genitive plural is λόγων, unlike θεῶν."},{"q":"What does the term \"iotacism\" describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Writing an iota under a long vowel","Adding a moveable ν before a vowel","Modern Greek's merging of η, ι, υ, ει and οι into one \"ee\" sound","Dropping a short vowel before another vowel"],"answer":2,"explain":"Iotacism is the Modern Greek sound merger in which η, ι, υ, ει and οι all come to sound like \"ee.\""},{"q":"In Modern Greek pronunciation, how does γ sound before front vowels?","kind":"sound","options":["\"ng\" as in sing","\"j\" as in jet","A hard \"g\" as in go","\"y\" or \"gh\""],"answer":3,"explain":"In Modern Greek, γ before front vowels has become \"y\" or \"gh,\" one of the sound changes Erasmian avoids.","greek":"γ"},{"q":"Nouns follow the recessive accent rule, just like finite verbs.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Nouns follow the persistent accent rule: they try to keep the accent where the lexical form has it."},{"q":"Adding a moveable nu changes the meaning of the word.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Moveable nu is purely phonetic smoothing, like English \"a\" vs. \"an.\"","greek":"ἐστί(ν)"},{"q":"A diphthong such as ου always counts as a single syllable unit and cannot be split.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Two vowels that merge into one sound count as one syllable unit."},{"q":"In this verb, the accent is on the penult because the ultima -ω is long.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. A long ultima forces a verb's recessive accent to the penult: λύ-ω.","greek":"λύω"}],"adv_nouns":[{"q":"In the English sentence \"He gave Mary the book,\" what is \"Mary\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject","Direct object","Indirect object","Possessive"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mary is the recipient, the one to whom the book is given. That makes her the indirect object."},{"q":"In the English sentence \"She read the letter,\" what is \"the letter\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Direct object","Subject","Indirect object","Direct address"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ask \"What did she read?\" The answer, the letter, directly receives the action, so it is the direct object."},{"q":"Which Greek case is used for direct address, as when calling out to someone by name?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Genitive","Dative","Vocative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The vocative is the case of direct address. In Greek it has its own ending, while the English word does not change."},{"q":"The article in this phrase shows the noun's gender. Which gender is it?","kind":"form","options":["Masculine","Feminine","Neuter","It has no gender"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἡ is the feminine nominative article, so ζωή (life) is feminine. Learn it as ἡ ζωή.","greek":"ἡ ζωή"},{"q":"According to the lesson's gender table, what grammatical gender is this noun (law)?","kind":"form","options":["Feminine","Neuter","Masculine","Common"],"answer":2,"explain":"νόμος is masculine. There is no biological reason; it is simply the word's grammatical class.","greek":"νόμος"},{"q":"What does this noun form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The word","Of words","To the words","Words (as object)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending -ων almost always signals a genitive plural, so λόγων means \"of words.\"","greek":"λόγων"},{"q":"In this clause from John 1:1, which word is the subject in the nominative case?","kind":"reading","options":["θεόν","ἦν","πρὸς","λόγος"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὁ λόγος is nominative, the subject. τὸν θεόν has the accusative article and ending.","greek":"ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεόν"},{"q":"In English, \"God's word\" and \"the word of God\" both express what?","kind":"concept","options":["Direct address","Indirect object","Possession","Direct object"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both patterns, apostrophe-s and \"of,\" show possession. Greek shows it with a case ending instead."},{"q":"This noun means \"little child\" and refers to a person, yet it is neuter. According to the lesson, what drives its gender?","kind":"concept","options":["The diminutive suffix","The child's age","The meaning of the word","The article placed before it"],"answer":0,"explain":"For παιδίον, the diminutive suffix sets the grammatical gender, not the person the word refers to.","greek":"παιδίον"},{"q":"In this clause from 1 John 4:8, what case is ἀγάπη?","kind":"reading","options":["Genitive","Accusative","Nominative (predicate nominative)","Dative"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγάπη is a predicate nominative. With a verb like ἐστίν, the noun that renames the subject is also nominative.","greek":"ὁ θεὸς ἀγάπη ἐστίν"},{"q":"In this phrase meaning \"into the house,\" what case is οἶκον?","kind":"form","options":["Accusative","Genitive","Dative","Nominative"],"answer":0,"explain":"The accusative is used with many prepositions to express motion toward, as here after εἰς.","greek":"εἰς τὸν οἶκον"},{"q":"In this phrase meaning \"out of the world,\" what case is κόσμου?","kind":"form","options":["Dative","Genitive","Accusative","Vocative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The genitive often follows prepositions like ἐκ, ἀπό and διά. Both τοῦ and the -ου ending mark it.","greek":"ἐκ τοῦ κόσμου"},{"q":"This dative phrase means \"in the heart.\" Which dative function does it show?","kind":"form","options":["Indirect object","Means or instrument","Association","Locative: sphere or location"],"answer":3,"explain":"τῇ καρδίᾳ describes where something occurs, which is the locative function of the dative.","greek":"τῇ καρδίᾳ"},{"q":"In this clause from John 1:12, what function does the dative αὐτοῖς have?","kind":"reading","options":["Indirect object (to them)","Direct object","Subject","Possession (their)"],"answer":0,"explain":"αὐτοῖς is the dative proper, the indirect object: authority was given to them.","greek":"ἔδωκεν αὐτοῖς ἐξουσίαν"},{"q":"Parse this form of the article.","kind":"form","options":["Genitive feminine singular","Nominative feminine plural","Dative feminine plural","Accusative feminine plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"ταῖς is the dative feminine plural article. The masculine and neuter dative plural is τοῖς.","greek":"ταῖς"},{"q":"Parse this form of the article.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative masculine singular","Nominative masculine plural","Dative masculine plural","Accusative masculine plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"τούς is the accusative masculine plural. The singular is τόν, and the nominative plural is οἱ.","greek":"τούς"},{"q":"In the pattern καρδία, καρδίας, καρδίᾳ, καρδίαν, which form is the dative singular?","kind":"form","options":["καρδίᾳ","καρδία","καρδίας","καρδίαν"],"answer":0,"explain":"The forms run nominative, genitive, dative, accusative. καρδίᾳ, with its iota subscript, is the dative."},{"q":"Which phrase correctly agrees with a neuter accusative plural noun?","kind":"form","options":["τοὺς ἀγαθούς","τὰ ἀγαθά","τὰς ἀγαθάς","τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ"],"answer":1,"explain":"A neuter accusative plural noun needs the neuter accusative plural article and adjective: τὰ ἀγαθά (the article τά takes a grave before the next word). τοὺς ἀγαθούς and τὰς ἀγαθάς are masculine and feminine."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five things you identify when parsing a Greek noun?","kind":"concept","options":["Gender","Tense","Declension","Lexical form"],"answer":1,"explain":"Noun parsing covers gender, case, number, declension and lexical form. Tense belongs to verbs."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what kind of genitive is τῆς ἁμαρτίας in this phrase meaning \"the body of sin\" (sinful body)?","kind":"concept","options":["Possessive","Subjective","Descriptive","Genitive absolute"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists this as a descriptive genitive: the genitive describes the body as sinful.","greek":"σῶμα τῆς ἁμαρτίας"},{"q":"A participle and a noun both in the genitive can form a clause independent of the main sentence. Where does the lesson say this genitive absolute is common?","kind":"concept","options":["Revelation","Hebrews","James","Luke and Acts"],"answer":3,"explain":"The genitive absolute is common in Luke and Acts."},{"q":"In this clause from Luke 15:15 (he hired himself out to a citizen), which dative function does the lesson identify?","kind":"reading","options":["Dative of association","Dative of means","Locative dative","Indirect object"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson gives this as its example of the dative of association, which expresses accompaniment or a personal relationship.","greek":"ἐκολλήθη ἑνὶ τῶν πολιτῶν"},{"q":"Parse this form of the article.","kind":"form","options":["Dative singular, feminine","Dative singular, masculine or neuter","Genitive singular, masculine or neuter","Dative plural, masculine or neuter"],"answer":1,"explain":"τῷ is dative singular. Masculine and neuter share the same genitive and dative forms, so it can be either.","greek":"τῷ"},{"q":"This noun means \"disciple\" and belongs to the first declension. What makes it notable?","kind":"concept","options":["It is a neuter noun","It belongs to the third declension","It has no plural forms","It is masculine, though the first declension is mostly feminine"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first declension is mostly feminine, but some nouns in it, like μαθητής, are masculine.","greek":"μαθητής"},{"q":"This third-declension noun has the genitive σαρκός. What is its stem?","kind":"form","options":["σαρ-","σαρκ-","σαρξ-","σαρκο-"],"answer":1,"explain":"Remove the -ος from the genitive singular σαρκός and you get the stem σαρκ-.","greek":"σάρξ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The good works","To the good works","Of the good work","Of the good works"],"answer":3,"explain":"All three words are neuter genitive plural and agree, giving \"of the good works.\"","greek":"τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἔργων"},{"q":"Which older case functions did the dative absorb long before the Koine period?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative and vocative","Genitive and accusative","Locative and instrumental","Vocative and locative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The dative took over the locative (location, sphere) and instrumental (means) functions, so one form can carry several meanings."},{"q":"Greek has an indefinite article meaning \"a\" or \"an.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek has only a definite article. To say \"a word,\" it uses the noun without an article: λόγος."},{"q":"When parsing a noun phrase, the lesson recommends starting with the article if there is one.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The article is the most reliable case indicator, so check it first, then the noun ending, then agreement."},{"q":"The neuter nominative and accusative forms of the article are always identical.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. τό is both nominative and accusative singular, and τά is both in the plural.","greek":"τό"}],"adv_cases":[{"q":"Which parsing fits this 2nd declension masculine form?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive singular","Nominative plural","Dative plural","Accusative plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending -οι is the nominative plural of the λόγος pattern. The vocative plural looks the same.","greek":"λόγοι"},{"q":"Parse this 2nd declension masculine form.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative plural","Nominative plural","Genitive plural","Accusative singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ους is the accusative plural ending. The accusative singular is -ον (λόγον).","greek":"λόγους"},{"q":"Parse this 1st declension form.","kind":"form","options":["Nominative singular","Dative singular","Genitive singular","Accusative singular"],"answer":2,"explain":"-ης is the genitive singular of the ἀγάπη pattern. The nominative -η has no sigma.","greek":"ἀγάπης"},{"q":"What does this form of the article tell you about its noun?","kind":"form","options":["Accusative masculine singular","Genitive feminine singular","Nominative feminine singular","Accusative feminine singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"τήν is accusative feminine singular. It ends in -ν like masculine τόν, but has the long η.","greek":"τήν"},{"q":"What does this form of the article tell you about its noun?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive masculine or neuter singular","Genitive feminine singular","Dative masculine or neuter singular","Genitive plural, any gender"],"answer":0,"explain":"τοῦ is the genitive singular for masculine or neuter nouns. The feminine is τῆς, and the plural for all genders is τῶν.","greek":"τοῦ"},{"q":"In this clause from John 3:16a, which word is the main verb?","kind":"reading","options":["Οὕτως","ἠγάπησεν","θεὸς","κόσμον"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἠγάπησεν (\"loved\") is the main action. The first parsing step is to find the verb.","greek":"Οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον"},{"q":"In this word, which part is the stem that carries the core meaning?","kind":"form","options":["λ-","λόγ-","λο-","-ος"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem is λόγ-, which carries the meaning \"word.\" The ending -ος is the grammatical signal.","greek":"λόγος"},{"q":"Which of these nouns follows the λόγος (2nd declension masculine) pattern?","kind":"form","options":["χαρά","τέκνον","στρατιώτης","κύριος"],"answer":3,"explain":"κύριος (Lord) follows the λόγος paradigm, like ἄνθρωπος, νόμος, ἀδελφός and θεός."},{"q":"λόγος (nominative) and λόγον (accusative) differ by only one letter. Which letters are they?","kind":"form","options":["ο vs. ω","γ vs. κ","ς vs. ν","λ vs. ν"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only the final letter changes: sigma in the nominative -ος, nu in the accusative -ον. Context and the article help keep them straight."},{"q":"Parse this 2nd declension masculine form.","kind":"form","options":["Genitive plural","Dative plural","Nominative plural","Accusative plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"-οις is the dative plural. Compare the nominative plural -οι and the genitive plural -ων.","greek":"λόγοις"},{"q":"Parse this 2nd declension masculine form.","kind":"form","options":["Nominative singular","Genitive singular","Accusative singular","Vocative singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"-ε is the vocative singular ending of the λόγος pattern, used for direct address.","greek":"λόγε"},{"q":"Which of these is a 2nd declension neuter noun?","kind":"form","options":["ἀδελφός","ζωή","πλοῖον","προφήτης"],"answer":2,"explain":"πλοῖον (boat) is 2nd declension neuter, like ἔργον and τέκνον. ἀδελφός is masculine, ζωή is 1st declension feminine, and προφήτης is 1st declension masculine."},{"q":"Parse this 1st declension form.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative singular","Genitive singular","Nominative plural","Dative singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ην is the accusative singular of the η pattern, matching the article τήν.","greek":"ἀγάπην"},{"q":"Parse this 1st declension form.","kind":"form","options":["Dative plural","Accusative plural","Genitive plural","Nominative plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"-αι is the nominative plural feminine ending. The dative plural adds a sigma: -αις.","greek":"ἀγάπαι"},{"q":"Which noun follows the alpha pattern of καρδία rather than the η pattern of ἀγάπη?","kind":"form","options":["γραφή","ἡμέρα","ζωή","εἰρήνη"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἡμέρα (day) is in the lesson's list of words following the καρδία pattern. γραφή, ζωή and εἰρήνη are η-stem words."},{"q":"Which of these is a 1st declension noun that is masculine in gender?","kind":"form","options":["προφήτης","γραφή","καρδία","ἔργον"],"answer":0,"explain":"προφήτης (prophet), like μαθητής and στρατιώτης, is 1st declension in form but masculine in gender."},{"q":"In John 3:16a, what is γάρ and what does it do?","kind":"reading","options":["An adverb meaning \"so\"","A verb meaning \"loved\"","A conjunction meaning \"for,\" giving a reason","An article marking the subject"],"answer":2,"explain":"γάρ is a conjunction meaning \"for.\" It gives a reason. Οὕτως is the adverb \"so.\"","greek":"γάρ"},{"q":"According to the lesson, which word in this clause is emphatic because it comes first?","kind":"reading","options":["Οὕτως","ἠγάπησεν","θεὸς","κόσμον"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek word order signals emphasis. Οὕτως (so, in this way) comes first, so it is the emphatic element.","greek":"Οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον"},{"q":"What does this 2nd declension neuter noun mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Boat","Temple","Demon","Gospel"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἱερόν means temple. πλοῖον is boat, δαιμόνιον is demon, and εὐαγγέλιον is gospel.","greek":"ἱερόν"},{"q":"This form of καρδία is ambiguous. Which two parsings are possible?","kind":"form","options":["Nominative singular or dative singular","Genitive plural or dative plural","Accusative singular or nominative plural","Genitive singular or accusative plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"In the alpha pattern, the genitive singular and accusative plural are both -ας. Only context, often the article, decides.","greek":"καρδίας"},{"q":"In the 1st declension, after which letters does the η change to α throughout the paradigm?","kind":"concept","options":["After σ, τ, or ν","After λ, μ, or ν","After ε, ι, or ρ","After any consonant"],"answer":2,"explain":"After ε, ι or ρ, the 1st declension uses α throughout, as in καρδία."},{"q":"Which parsing fits this form of μαθητής?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive singular","Nominative plural","Dative singular","Accusative plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"-αί is the nominative plural in the μαθητής table. The vocative plural looks the same.","greek":"μαθηταί"},{"q":"Parse this form of μαθητής.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative singular","Genitive plural","Nominative singular","Dative singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ήν is the accusative singular, the same ending the feminine η pattern uses.","greek":"μαθητήν"},{"q":"How does the lesson parse this verb from John 3:16a?","kind":"form","options":["Present active, 1st person singular","Aorist active, 3rd person plural","Imperfect passive, 3rd person singular","Aorist active, 3rd person singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἠγάπησεν is aorist active 3rd person singular, \"he/she/it loved.\" Here its subject is ὁ θεός.","greek":"ἠγάπησεν"},{"q":"This 3rd declension noun is listed as χάρις, χάριτος. What is its stem?","kind":"form","options":["χαρ-","χάρις-","χαριτ-","χαριτο-"],"answer":2,"explain":"Drop the -ος from the genitive χάριτος and you get the stem χαριτ-.","greek":"χάρις"},{"q":"What kind of stems do 3rd declension nouns have?","kind":"concept","options":["Consonant stems","α/η stems","ο stems","No stems at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 1st and 2nd declensions have vowel stems (α/η and ο). The 3rd declension has consonant stems, so its forms look very different."},{"q":"Which form is the dative plural of ἔργον?","kind":"form","options":["ἔργων","ἔργα","ἔργῳ","ἔργοις"],"answer":3,"explain":"The neuter dative is identical to the masculine: -ῳ in the singular, -οις in the plural, so ἔργοις."},{"q":"In τὰ τέκνα ἔρχεται, using a singular verb with a neuter plural subject is a grammatical error.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A neuter plural subject normally takes a singular verb in Greek. The plural is treated as a collective.","greek":"τὰ τέκνα ἔρχεται"},{"q":"The lesson's practical parsing sequence starts by finding the article.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Find the article, read its case, gender and number, then check that the noun ending matches."},{"q":"For λόγος, the vocative plural has the same form as the nominative plural.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Both are λόγοι (-οι), and context tells them apart.","greek":"λόγοι"}],"adv_prepositions":[{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Into the beginning","In the beginning","From the beginning","After the beginning"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐν with the dative expresses location or sphere, so ἐν ἀρχῇ is \"in the beginning.\"","greek":"ἐν ἀρχῇ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Into the world","Out of the world","In the world","Against the world"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰς with the accusative shows motion toward or into: \"into the world.\"","greek":"εἰς τὸν κόσμον"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["With God","Under God","From God","Toward God"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀπό with the genitive expresses separation or origin: \"from God.\"","greek":"ἀπὸ θεοῦ"},{"q":"Which case always follows this preposition?","kind":"concept","options":["Genitive","Dative","Nominative","Accusative"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰς is a single-case preposition. It always takes the accusative.","greek":"εἰς"},{"q":"Which case always follows this preposition?","kind":"concept","options":["Accusative","Genitive","Dative","Nominative"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐκ (ἐξ) always takes the genitive and expresses source or separation.","greek":"ἐκ"},{"q":"What does the lesson call a preposition that can take either of two cases, with the case changing its meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Single-case preposition","Elided preposition","Dual-case preposition","Article preposition"],"answer":2,"explain":"A dual-case preposition, like διά, takes either of two cases, and the case changes the meaning entirely."},{"q":"Which of these is a dual-case preposition?","kind":"concept","options":["ἐν","εἰς","ἐκ","κατά"],"answer":3,"explain":"κατά takes the genitive (against) or the accusative (according to). ἐν, εἰς and ἐκ each take only one case."},{"q":"When checking the case after a preposition, what does the lesson say to look at first?","kind":"concept","options":["The article","The verb","The accent","The last word of the sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"The article is the fastest case signal. Read it before the noun and you already know the case."},{"q":"In the lesson's quick check, what case does this article signal after a preposition?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive","Dative","Accusative","Nominative"],"answer":1,"explain":"τῷ is a dative article. τοῦ signals genitive, and τόν or τήν signal accusative.","greek":"τῷ"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["From you","Under you","To you","Because of you"],"answer":2,"explain":"πρός with the accusative expresses direction toward, so πρὸς ὑμᾶς is \"to you.\"","greek":"πρὸς ὑμᾶς"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Through the love","Because of the love","With the love","Under the love"],"answer":1,"explain":"The article τήν shows an accusative, and διά + accusative means \"because of.\"","greek":"διὰ τὴν ἀγάπην"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["With these things","Against these things","Around these things","After these things"],"answer":3,"explain":"μετά with the accusative expresses time or sequence: \"after these things.\"","greek":"μετὰ ταῦτα"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Against us","According to us","With us","After us"],"answer":0,"explain":"καθ᾽ is κατά before a word with rough breathing. With the genitive ἡμῶν, it means \"against us.\"","greek":"καθ᾽ ἡμῶν"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["By the law","Concerning law","Under law","Through law"],"answer":2,"explain":"νόμον is accusative, and ὑπό + accusative means \"under\" (location or authority).","greek":"ὑπὸ νόμον"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Around sin","Concerning sin","Because of sin","Out of sin"],"answer":1,"explain":"περί with the genitive means \"concerning\" or \"about.\" With the accusative it means \"around.\"","greek":"περὶ ἁμαρτίας"},{"q":"When does ἐκ appear in the form ἐξ?","kind":"form","options":["Before vowels","Before consonants","At the end of a sentence","With the accusative"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐκ becomes ἐξ before a word that begins with a vowel. It still takes the genitive.","greek":"ἐξ"},{"q":"About how often does the phrase ἐν Χριστῷ appear in Paul's letters?","kind":"concept","options":["About 10 times","Over 30 times","Over 80 times","Over 300 times"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐν Χριστῷ, with ἐν plus the dative, appears over 80 times in Paul.","greek":"ἐν Χριστῷ"},{"q":"When you see ὑπό with the genitive, what question does the lesson tell you to ask?","kind":"concept","options":["Is there a passive verb nearby?","Is the noun plural?","Is there an elided vowel?","Is the sentence a question?"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὑπό + genitive often names the agent of a passive verb, so look for a passive verb nearby."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Against flesh","With flesh","Out of flesh","According to flesh"],"answer":3,"explain":"σάρκα is accusative, and κατά + accusative means \"according to.\"","greek":"κατὰ σάρκα"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Against the city","Out of the city","City by city, in every city","With the city"],"answer":2,"explain":"κατά + accusative can mean \"throughout,\" so κατὰ πόλιν is \"city by city\" or \"in every city.\"","greek":"κατὰ πόλιν"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Under the Spirit","By the Spirit","Concerning the Spirit","Around the Spirit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The article τοῦ shows a genitive, and ὑπό + genitive marks the agent: \"by the Spirit.\"","greek":"ὑπὸ τοῦ πνεύματος"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Concerning the place","From the place","Because of the place","Around the place"],"answer":3,"explain":"The article τόν shows an accusative, and περί + accusative means \"around\" or \"approximately.\"","greek":"περὶ τὸν τόπον"},{"q":"Both ἐκ and ἀπό take the genitive. How does the lesson distinguish them?","kind":"concept","options":["ἀπό takes the dative and ἐκ takes the genitive","ἀπό means \"into\" and ἐκ means \"out of\"","ἀπό is used only before vowels and ἐκ before consonants","ἀπό stresses distance or point of origin; ἐκ stresses emergence from within"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both express separation or origin. ἀπό is more general and stresses distance, while ἐκ stresses coming out from within."},{"q":"In this phrase, what tells you that μετά means \"with\" rather than \"after\"?","kind":"concept","options":["The genitive plural article τῶν","The accent on μετά","The word order of the phrase","A plural verb nearby"],"answer":0,"explain":"τῶν is a genitive plural article, and μετά + genitive means \"with\" (accompaniment). With the accusative, as in μετὰ ταῦτα, it means \"after.\"","greek":"μετὰ τῶν μαθητῶν"},{"q":"In this phrase from Romans 1:17, what case is πίστιν?","kind":"reading","options":["Genitive","Dative","Accusative","Nominative"],"answer":2,"explain":"πίστιν follows εἰς, which always takes the accusative. πίστεως, after ἐκ, is genitive.","greek":"ἐκ πίστεως εἰς πίστιν"},{"q":"Besides motion toward or into, what else can εἰς + accusative express, as in this phrase?","kind":"concept","options":["Purpose","The agent of a passive verb","Accompaniment","Opposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰς can also express purpose, so εἰς ζωὴν αἰώνιον can be \"into\" or \"for\" eternal life.","greek":"εἰς ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"In this phrase from 1 Cor 8:6, what does δι᾽ mean?","kind":"reading","options":["Because of","Through","After","Against"],"answer":1,"explain":"δι᾽ is διά with its vowel elided. Here it is followed by the genitive οὗ, so it means \"through.\"","greek":"δι᾽ οὗ τὰ πάντα"},{"q":"English prepositions are more precise than Greek prepositions.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. English \"for\" can mean many things, but each Greek preposition governs specific cases, and the case sets the meaning."},{"q":"In ὑπὸ χάριν (Romans 6:14), ὑπό takes the accusative and means \"under.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. χάριν is accusative, and ὑπό + accusative means \"under\" (location or authority).","greek":"ὑπὸ χάριν"},{"q":"In the lesson's three-step process, the first step is to check the case of the word that follows.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. First you spot the preposition, then check the case of what follows, then lock in the meaning."}],"adv_adjectives":[{"q":"Which form of ἀγαθός would modify a neuter nominative singular noun such as ἔργον?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαθός","ἀγαθή","ἀγαθοί","ἀγαθόν"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαθόν is the neuter form, used with neuter nouns like ἔργον. ἀγαθός is masculine and ἀγαθή is feminine."},{"q":"What is the key marker that an adjective is in attributive position?","kind":"concept","options":["An article immediately before the adjective","No article anywhere in the phrase","The adjective comes first in the sentence","A form of εἰμί after the adjective"],"answer":0,"explain":"If an article stands directly before the adjective, the adjective is inside the noun phrase, describing the noun."},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Faithful","Holy, set apart","Righteous","Blessed"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἅγιος means holy or set apart. Its forms are ἅγιος, ἁγία, ἅγιον.","greek":"ἅγιος"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["First","Only","Dead","Eternal"],"answer":2,"explain":"νεκρός means dead. Its feminine is νεκρά, with α after ρ.","greek":"νεκρός"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["First","Last","Great","Holy"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔσχατος means last. Its partner πρῶτος means first.","greek":"ἔσχατος"},{"q":"What does this adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Many","Last","Righteous","Only, alone"],"answer":3,"explain":"μόνος means only or alone, and follows the regular ἀγαθός pattern: μόνος, μόνη, μόνον.","greek":"μόνος"},{"q":"What does this irregular adjective mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Much, many","Great, large","All, every","First"],"answer":0,"explain":"πολύς, πολλή, πολύ means much or many. μέγας means great, and πᾶς means all or every.","greek":"πολύς"},{"q":"This is a substantive adjective. What does it mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The good man","The good woman","The good things","The good word"],"answer":2,"explain":"The neuter plural article with no noun implies things: \"the good things.\"","greek":"τὰ ἀγαθά"},{"q":"Parse this form of ἀγαθός.","kind":"form","options":["Genitive feminine singular","Nominative feminine singular","Dative feminine singular","Accusative feminine plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ῆς is the feminine genitive singular, matching 1st declension nouns like ἀγάπης.","greek":"ἀγαθῆς"},{"q":"Parse this form of ἀγαθός.","kind":"form","options":["Genitive plural, masculine or neuter","Dative plural, masculine or neuter","Dative plural, feminine","Nominative plural, masculine"],"answer":1,"explain":"-οῖς is the dative plural for masculine and neuter. The feminine dative plural is ἀγαθαῖς.","greek":"ἀγαθοῖς"},{"q":"Parse this form of ἀγαθός.","kind":"form","options":["Nominative feminine singular","Genitive neuter plural","Nominative or accusative neuter plural","Accusative feminine plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"-ά is the neuter plural ending for both nominative and accusative. The feminine accusative plural is ἀγαθάς.","greek":"ἀγαθά"},{"q":"In this phrase, what gender, case and number is ἁγίου?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive masculine plural","Dative neuter singular","Nominative neuter singular","Genitive neuter singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"The article τοῦ is genitive neuter singular here, and ἁγίου matches it, agreeing with πνεύματος.","greek":"τοῦ ἁγίου πνεύματος"},{"q":"What position is the adjective in this phrase from Revelation 3:14?","kind":"reading","options":["First attributive","Second attributive","Predicate","Substantive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Article, noun, article, adjective is the second attributive position. The adjective describes the noun.","greek":"ὁ μάρτυς ὁ πιστός"},{"q":"What position is the adjective in this clause from 1 Cor 1:9?","kind":"reading","options":["Predicate","First attributive","Second attributive","Substantive"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὁ θεός has the article but πιστός does not, so πιστός is a predicate adjective with εἰμί implied.","greek":"πιστὸς ὁ θεός"},{"q":"In this phrase from Revelation 1:17, how are πρῶτος and ἔσχατος being used?","kind":"reading","options":["As predicate adjectives","As attributive adjectives describing a noun","As substantive adjectives","As adverbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each has an article but no noun, so each is substantive: \"the First One\" and \"the Last One.\"","greek":"ὁ πρῶτος καὶ ὁ ἔσχατος"},{"q":"What does this form of πᾶς mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Every one","Of all","All things","To all"],"answer":1,"explain":"πάντων is the genitive plural of πᾶς, \"of all.\"","greek":"πάντων"},{"q":"In Ephesians 1:1, what case is this substantive phrase, and what role does it play?","kind":"reading","options":["Nominative plural: the saints as subject","Genitive plural: of the saints","Accusative plural: the saints as object","Dative plural: to the saints, the recipients"],"answer":3,"explain":"τοῖς ἁγίοις is dative plural, the indirect object: the saints are the recipients of the letter.","greek":"τοῖς ἁγίοις"},{"q":"In the phrase ἐκ νεκρῶν (from the dead), what case is νεκρῶν?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive plural","Dative plural","Accusative plural","Nominative plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ῶν is the genitive plural ending for all three genders in the 2-1-2 pattern.","greek":"ἐκ νεκρῶν"},{"q":"This form of ἀγαθός is ambiguous on its own. Which parsings fit it?","kind":"form","options":["Nominative masculine singular only","Genitive plural of any gender","Accusative masculine singular, or nominative/accusative neuter singular","Accusative feminine singular"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαθόν appears in the masculine accusative singular and in both neuter nominative and accusative singular. The article settles which.","greek":"ἀγαθόν"},{"q":"δίκαιος has a stem ending in ι. Which is its feminine nominative singular form?","kind":"form","options":["δικαίας","δίκαιον","δικαίους","δικαία"],"answer":3,"explain":"Stems ending in ε, ι or ρ use α instead of η in the feminine singular, so the nominative is δικαία. δικαίας is the feminine genitive singular.","greek":"δίκαιος"},{"q":"In this clause from 1 John 3:7, what is δίκαιος?","kind":"reading","options":["The subject","The predicate adjective","An attributive adjective","A substantive adjective"],"answer":1,"explain":"δίκαιος makes a statement about the subject ἐκεῖνος (\"that one\"), so it is the predicate adjective.","greek":"ἐκεῖνος δίκαιός ἐστιν"},{"q":"This Beatitude (Matthew 5:3) has no verb written. Which verb does the lesson say is implied?","kind":"reading","options":["εἰσίν (they are)","ἐστιν (he is)","ἦν (was)","ἠγάπησεν (loved)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The subject οἱ πτωχοί is plural, so the implied linking verb is εἰσίν, \"they are.\"","greek":"μακάριοι οἱ πτωχοί"},{"q":"You meet the form μεγάλης. Which adjective is it from?","kind":"form","options":["μέγας","πολύς","πᾶς","μόνος"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem μεγάλ- appears in most oblique cases of μέγας, even though the nominative is μέγας.","greek":"μεγάλης"},{"q":"αἰώνιος uses the same form for masculine and feminine. How is its gender shown?","kind":"concept","options":["By a special feminine ending","By its accent","By the article","By word order"],"answer":2,"explain":"αἰώνιος and μονογενής are 2-termination adjectives, so only the article shows masculine or feminine gender.","greek":"αἰώνιος"},{"q":"If neither the noun nor the adjective has an article, as in πνεῦμα ἅγιον, how does the lesson say to decide the adjective's position?","kind":"concept","options":["It is always predicate","It is always substantive","It must be the subject of the sentence","Decide from context; it is usually attributive"],"answer":3,"explain":"With no article on either word, context decides, and the adjective is usually attributive.","greek":"πνεῦμα ἅγιον"},{"q":"In the phrase ἐν ταῖς ἐσχάταις ἡμέραις (in the last days), what is the form of ἐσχάταις?","kind":"form","options":["Genitive feminine plural","Dative feminine plural","Accusative feminine plural","Dative masculine plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"-αις is the feminine dative plural, agreeing with the article ταῖς and the noun ἡμέραις.","greek":"ἐσχάταις"},{"q":"In English, adjectives change form to agree with the nouns they describe.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. English \"good\" never changes. Greek adjectives change to match gender, case and number."},{"q":"The first and second attributive positions differ in meaning.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ὁ ἀγαθὸς λόγος and ὁ λόγος ὁ ἀγαθός both mean \"the good word.\" The second is somewhat more emphatic."},{"q":"In predicate position, the linking verb εἰμί is often not written.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The structure is subject + \"is\" + predicate adjective, and the \"is\" is often only implied."},{"q":"If an article and an adjective do not match in gender, case and number, the adjective belongs to a different noun in the sentence.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The article and its adjective always tell the same story, so a mismatch points to another noun."}],"adv_pronouns":[{"q":"In this clause from Matthew 5:14, what does ὑμεῖς mean?","kind":"reading","options":["I","We","You (one person)","You all"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὑμεῖς is the 2nd person plural nominative, \"you all.\" The singular is σύ.","greek":"ὑμεῖς ἐστε τὸ φῶς τοῦ κόσμου"},{"q":"In the phrase πατὴρ ἡμῶν, what does ἡμῶν mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Our (of us)","Your (of you all)","To us","We"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἡμῶν is the genitive of ἡμεῖς, \"of us\" or \"our,\" so πατὴρ ἡμῶν is \"our Father.\"","greek":"ἡμῶν"},{"q":"Parse this form of αὐτός.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative masculine singular","Accusative feminine singular","Genitive feminine singular","Nominative feminine singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτήν is accusative feminine singular, \"her.\" The masculine is αὐτόν.","greek":"αὐτήν"},{"q":"What does this form of οὗτος mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["This one","That thing","These things","Those people"],"answer":2,"explain":"ταῦτα is the neuter plural of οὗτος, \"these things.\"","greek":"ταῦτα"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["In this day","In that day","On the same day","On which day?"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐκεῖνος points far, \"that,\" so ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ is \"in that day.\"","greek":"ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ"},{"q":"In τί ποιήσω; (Luke 12:17), what does τί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Who?","What?","Someone","Something"],"answer":1,"explain":"Accented τί is the neuter interrogative, \"what?\" Masculine or feminine τίς means \"who?\"","greek":"τί"},{"q":"What is a pronoun's antecedent?","kind":"concept","options":["The noun the pronoun refers back to","The verb that follows the pronoun","The case ending of the pronoun","The first word in the sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"The antecedent is the noun the pronoun stands in for. The pronoun takes its gender and number from it."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["You are","He is","I am","We are"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐγώ is \"I,\" so ἐγώ εἰμι is \"I am.\"","greek":"ἐγώ εἰμι"},{"q":"What does this pronoun form mean?","kind":"form","options":["Of us","Us (direct object)","We","To/for us"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἡμῖν is the dative of ἡμεῖς, \"to/for us.\" The accusative is ἡμᾶς and the genitive is ἡμῶν.","greek":"ἡμῖν"},{"q":"Parse this pronoun form.","kind":"form","options":["You (singular, direct object)","Of you (singular)","You all","To you all"],"answer":0,"explain":"σε is the short, unemphatic accusative singular of σύ, \"you\" as an object. The emphatic form is σέ.","greek":"σε"},{"q":"μου and ἐμοῦ both mean \"of me.\" What does the longer form ἐμοῦ add?","kind":"concept","options":["Plural number","Emphasis on the pronoun","Feminine gender","A question"],"answer":1,"explain":"The long forms are emphatic and stress the pronoun. The short forms like μου are unemphatic.","greek":"ἐμοῦ"},{"q":"Which is the neuter nominative/accusative singular of αὐτός?","kind":"form","options":["αὐτόν","αὐτά","αὐτοῦ","αὐτό"],"answer":3,"explain":"αὐτός follows ἀγαθός except that the neuter singular nominative/accusative is αὐτό, with no -ν."},{"q":"Parse this form of αὐτός.","kind":"form","options":["Dative plural, feminine","Genitive plural, any gender","Dative plural, masculine or neuter","Accusative plural, masculine"],"answer":2,"explain":"αὐτοῖς is dative plural for masculine or neuter, \"to/for them.\" The feminine is αὐταῖς.","greek":"αὐτοῖς"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["He himself, the word","The word itself","The same word","His word"],"answer":2,"explain":"With an article directly before it (attributive position), αὐτός means \"the same.\"","greek":"ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The Lord himself","The same Lord","His Lord","He saw the Lord"],"answer":0,"explain":"αὐτός with no article before it, beside a noun, is intensive: \"the Lord himself.\"","greek":"αὐτὸς ὁ κύριος"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Because of this","In this / by this","After these things","In that day"],"answer":1,"explain":"τούτῳ is the dative singular of οὗτος, so ἐν τούτῳ is \"in this\" or \"by this.\"","greek":"ἐν τούτῳ"},{"q":"How do you tell the relative pronoun ὅ from the article ὁ?","kind":"concept","options":["The relative has smooth breathing","The article always has an accent","The relative begins with τ","The relative has an accent; the article does not"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both have rough breathing. The relative forms ὅ, ἥ, οἵ, αἵ are accented, while the article forms ὁ, ἡ, οἱ, αἱ are not.","greek":"ὅ"},{"q":"In this phrase from Luke 10:30, what does τις add to ἄνθρωπος?","kind":"meaning","options":["Who is the man?","The same man","A certain man","That man"],"answer":2,"explain":"Unaccented τις is indefinite. ἄνθρωπός τις means \"a certain man,\" introducing a character.","greek":"ἄνθρωπός τις"},{"q":"In ἐν ᾧ κράζομεν (Romans 8:15), what case is ᾧ, and why?","kind":"reading","options":["Genitive, to show possession","Dative, governed by ἐν","Accusative, the object of κράζομεν","Nominative, the subject"],"answer":1,"explain":"ᾧ is a dative relative pronoun because ἐν takes the dative. Its neuter gender comes from πνεῦμα.","greek":"ἐν ᾧ"},{"q":"When does αὐτός mean \"the same\"?","kind":"concept","options":["When it has an article directly before it (attributive position)","When it has no article and stands beside a noun","Whenever it is in the genitive","When it comes at the end of the clause"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article first (attributive) means \"the same.\" No article before it means intensive or personal, and context decides between those."},{"q":"In this clause from John 1:4, why is αὐτῷ dative?","kind":"reading","options":["Because its antecedent λόγος is dative","Because it is governed by ἐν + dative","Because it is the subject","Because it shows possession"],"answer":1,"explain":"αὐτῷ is masculine singular to track λόγος, but its case comes from its own role, here after ἐν.","greek":"ἐν αὐτῷ ζωὴ ἦν"},{"q":"Parse this form.","kind":"form","options":["Accusative feminine singular of οὗτος","Genitive feminine singular of ἐκεῖνος","Nominative feminine plural of οὗτος","Genitive feminine singular of οὗτος"],"answer":3,"explain":"ταύτης is the feminine genitive singular of οὗτος. Like most of its forms, it begins with τ-.","greek":"ταύτης"},{"q":"Which forms of οὗτος begin with οὑ-/αὑ- (rough breathing) rather than τ-?","kind":"form","options":["All genitive and dative forms","Only the neuter forms","The masculine and feminine nominative, singular and plural","Every plural form"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only οὗτος, αὕτη, οὗτοι and αὗται begin with a rough-breathing vowel. All the others, including τοῦτο and ταῦτα, start with τ-.","greek":"οὗτος"},{"q":"In this clause from Romans 8:32, what case is ὅς, and why?","kind":"reading","options":["Nominative, as the subject of ἐφείσατο","Accusative, as the object of ἐφείσατο","Genitive, to match υἱοῦ","Dative, to match its antecedent"],"answer":0,"explain":"ὅς is nominative because it is the subject of ἐφείσατο inside its own clause. Its masculine singular comes from ὁ θεός.","greek":"ὅς γε τοῦ ἰδίου υἱοῦ οὐκ ἐφείσατο"},{"q":"In this phrase from John 1:33, what case is the relative pronoun ὅν?","kind":"reading","options":["Nominative","Genitive","Dative","Accusative"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὅν is masculine singular accusative, the object of ἐπί (here elided to ἐφ').","greek":"ἐφ' ὅν"},{"q":"What does this accented form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["To whom?","Whom?","Of whom?","Of someone"],"answer":2,"explain":"τίνος is the genitive of interrogative τίς, \"of whom?\" The indefinite τινός means \"of someone.\"","greek":"τίνος"},{"q":"ἐγώ and σύ have separate masculine and feminine forms.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. 1st and 2nd person pronouns have no gender, only case and number."},{"q":"Because the verb ending already shows the subject, ἐγώ and σύ are often left out as subjects.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. When they do appear as subjects, it is usually for emphasis or contrast."},{"q":"The relative pronoun never begins with τ.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Compare the article forms τό, τοῦ, τόν with the relative forms ὅ, οὗ, ὅν."},{"q":"τίς loses its accent when it appears in an indirect question.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Interrogative τίς keeps its accent in indirect questions too, as in \"he asked τίς ἦν.\"","greek":"τίς ἦν"}],"adv_conjunctions":[{"q":"καί appears about 9,000 times in the Greek NT, making it the second most common word after the article.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Only the article is more common than καί.","greek":"καί"},{"q":"What do you call a conjunction that attaches a dependent clause to a main clause?","kind":"concept","options":["Coordinating conjunction","Subordinating conjunction","Postpositive particle","Negative particle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Subordinating conjunctions attach a dependent clause. Coordinating conjunctions connect clauses of equal weight."},{"q":"What does ἀλλά become before a vowel?","kind":"form","options":["ἀλλ'","ἀλ","ἀλλάν","ἀλλή"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀλλά drops its final vowel before a vowel (elision) and becomes ἀλλ'.","greek":"ἀλλά"},{"q":"What does this particle mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Therefore","Because","Not","Behold! Look!"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἰδού means \"behold!\" or \"look!\" It draws attention and is very common in Matthew, Luke and Revelation.","greek":"ἰδού"},{"q":"What does this command mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Do not be afraid","He is not afraid","So that he might not fear","Certainly not"],"answer":0,"explain":"μή with an imperative gives a negative command: \"do not be afraid.\"","greek":"μὴ φοβοῦ"},{"q":"What does this mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Do not come","He is not coming","So that he might not come","The one not coming"],"answer":1,"explain":"οὐκ negates an indicative, a statement of fact: \"he is not coming.\"","greek":"οὐκ ἔρχεται"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Word, even deed","Not in word but in deed","In word and deed","In word, therefore in deed"],"answer":2,"explain":"Here καί is connective, joining two nouns: \"in word and deed.\"","greek":"ἐν λόγῳ καὶ ἔργῳ"},{"q":"In the verb framework, which mood presents an action as a fact?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Imperative","Optative","Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The indicative states things as fact. The subjunctive expresses possibility or purpose, and the imperative gives commands."},{"q":"Which form of the negative οὐ is used before a rough breathing?","kind":"form","options":["οὐ","οὐκ","μή","οὐχ"],"answer":3,"explain":"οὐ becomes οὐκ before vowels and οὐχ before a rough breathing."},{"q":"What kind of clause does ὥστε introduce?","kind":"concept","options":["Condition: \"if\"","Explanation: \"for, because\"","Result: \"so that, with the result that\"","Indirect statement: \"that\""],"answer":2,"explain":"ὥστε introduces result clauses, telling you what actually resulted.","greek":"ὥστε"},{"q":"In John's narrative, how is οὖν often used in a phrase like this one?","kind":"reading","options":["As a negative: \"Jesus did not...\"","As a narrative particle: \"So Jesus...\" or \"Jesus then...\"","As a sharp correction: \"Rather, Jesus...\"","As a question: \"Who is Jesus?\""],"answer":1,"explain":"In John, οὖν often works as a narrative particle meaning \"then\" or \"so\" rather than a strong \"therefore.\"","greek":"ὁ οὖν Ἰησοῦς"},{"q":"How is καί used in this phrase?","kind":"concept","options":["Connective: \"and\"","Contrastive: \"but you\"","Conditional: \"if you\"","Adverbial: \"you also\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Adverbial καί means \"also\" or \"even\" and adds emphasis to the word that follows.","greek":"καὶ ὑμεῖς"},{"q":"John's Gospel strings clauses together with καί in a way that echoes what?","kind":"concept","options":["Hebrew narrative","Latin poetry","Classical Attic drama","Paul's letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"John's καί-heavy style echoes Hebrew narrative. John 1:1–5 uses καί six times in five verses."},{"q":"What pattern does this clause from Matthew 5:17 show?","kind":"reading","options":["A μέν...δέ balance","The οὐ...ἀλλά pattern: \"not X, but rather Y\"","A γάρ explanation","An ἐάν condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"οὐ rules one thing out and ἀλλά substitutes another. This is the standard Greek way to correct or replace.","greek":"οὐκ ἦλθον καταλῦσαι ἀλλὰ πληρῶσαι"},{"q":"How is ὅτι used in this clause from John 11:27?","kind":"reading","options":["\"Because,\" giving a reason","\"That,\" introducing indirect statement after a verb of believing","\"So that,\" giving purpose","\"If,\" giving a condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὅτι follows πεπίστευκα (\"I have believed\"), a verb of believing, so it means \"that.\"","greek":"πεπίστευκα ὅτι σὺ εἶ ὁ Χριστός"},{"q":"Which mood follows ἐάν in a condition?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Imperative","Subjunctive","Infinitive"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐάν + subjunctive expresses an uncertain or general condition. εἰ + indicative states a real or assumed one.","greek":"ἐάν"},{"q":"The lesson says Acts often pairs τε with καί. What does τε...καί mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"Not...but\"","\"On the one hand...on the other\"","\"Both...and\"","\"If...then\""],"answer":2,"explain":"τε closely binds two items that belong together, and τε...καί means \"both...and.\"","greek":"τε...καί"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["The one not loving","He does not love","Do not love","So that he might not love"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀγαπῶν is a participle, so it is negated with μή: \"the one not loving.\"","greek":"ὁ μὴ ἀγαπῶν"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five categories used to parse a Greek verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Voice","Case","Mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"Verbs are parsed by person, number, tense, voice and mood. Case belongs to nouns."},{"q":"Which verb category has the options active, passive and middle?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense","Mood","Number","Voice"],"answer":3,"explain":"Voice shows how the subject relates to the action: it acts (active), is acted on (passive), or acts for itself (middle)."},{"q":"The lesson says ἄρα is a weaker conclusion marker than οὖν.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ἄρα means \"then, therefore,\" like οὖν, but signals a slightly stronger logical consequence.","greek":"ἄρα"},{"q":"In English translation, μέν is often left untranslated.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Usually only δέ gets the \"but.\" When you see μέν, expect a δέ soon.","greek":"μέν"},{"q":"In John 3:17, what does this phrase add after the first ἵνα clause?","kind":"reading","options":["A corrected purpose: \"but in order that\"","A conclusion: \"therefore\"","A condition: \"but if\"","A result expressed with an infinitive"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀλλ' sets up a contrast and ἵνα gives purpose, so together they give a corrected purpose.","greek":"ἀλλ' ἵνα σωθῇ ὁ κόσμος δι' αὐτοῦ"},{"q":"How is ὅτι used in this verse from 1 John 4:8?","kind":"reading","options":["\"That,\" after a verb of saying","\"Because,\" giving the reason for the previous statement","\"So that,\" expressing purpose","\"If,\" expressing a condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"This ὅτι does not report what someone said or knew. It explains the previous statement, so it is causal: \"because.\"","greek":"ὁ μὴ ἀγαπῶν οὐκ ἔγνω τὸν θεόν, ὅτι ὁ θεὸς ἀγάπη ἐστίν"},{"q":"In this clause from Matthew 12:22, which verb form follows ὥστε?","kind":"reading","options":["The infinitive","The subjunctive","The imperative","The participle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unlike ἵνα, ὥστε often takes the indicative or, as here with λαλεῖν and βλέπειν, an infinitive.","greek":"ὥστε τὸν τυφλὸν καὶ κωφὸν καὶ λαλεῖν καὶ βλέπειν"},{"q":"The particle ἰδού is an imperative form of which verb?","kind":"concept","options":["ἔρχομαι","λύω","ὁράω (to see)","λέγω"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἰδού comes from ὁράω, \"to see,\" and is used as an attention-drawing particle.","greek":"ἰδού"},{"q":"How does the lesson parse this verb form?","kind":"form","options":["Present active indicative, 3rd singular","Aorist active indicative, 3rd singular","Present active subjunctive, 1st singular","Perfect passive indicative, 3rd singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύει is present active indicative 3rd singular, \"he/she looses.\" The ending -ει encodes all five categories at once.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the perfect tense, as in ἐγήγερται?","kind":"concept","options":["A simple completed event","Ongoing action in the past","A past act whose result still stands","An action that will happen later"],"answer":2,"explain":"The perfect presents a past act with a present result. The aorist presents a simple completed event.","greek":"ἐγήγερται"},{"q":"Besides purpose and result, what other use of ἵνα does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Conditional: \"if\"","Negative: \"not\"","Causal: \"because\"","Epexegetical: \"namely that\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Epexegetical ἵνα introduces content, such as the content of a command or wish: \"namely that.\"","greek":"ἵνα"},{"q":"In Romans 7:17, νυνὶ δέ refers to time, meaning \"at this moment.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says this νῦν is logical, not temporal: \"as it is\" or \"as things actually stand.\"","greek":"νυνὶ δὲ οὐκέτι ἐγὼ κατεργάζομαι αὐτό"}],"adv_howToRead":[{"q":"What does this common verb form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["He/she says","He/she said","He/she was","It happened"],"answer":1,"explain":"εἶπεν means \"he/she said.\" It is the most common dialogue marker in the NT. λέγει is \"he/she says.\"","greek":"εἶπεν"},{"q":"What does this common verb form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["It stands written","He gave","It happened / it came to pass","He was raised"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐγένετο means \"it happened\" or \"it came to pass,\" and is extremely common in Luke.","greek":"ἐγένετο"},{"q":"What does this verb form from John 3:16 mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["He gave","He loved","He has","He said"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔδωκεν means \"he gave.\" ἠγάπησεν is \"he loved.\"","greek":"ἔδωκεν"},{"q":"Using article-first reading, what does this phrase mean?","kind":"reading","options":["The love of God","God loves","To the love of God","God of love"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἡ ἀγάπη is nominative (\"love\"), and τοῦ θεοῦ is genitive (\"of God\"). Together: \"the love of God,\" with no verb needed.","greek":"ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ θεοῦ"},{"q":"What do these article forms tell you about the nouns they go with?","kind":"form","options":["Dative plural","Genitive plural","Accusative plural","Nominative plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"τοῖς and ταῖς are dative plural articles, often marking plural indirect objects.","greek":"τοῖς / ταῖς"},{"q":"In Romans 5:1, what does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["Because of the Lord","Through the Lord","Against the Lord","After the Lord"],"answer":1,"explain":"τοῦ marks a genitive, and διά + genitive means \"through.\"","greek":"διὰ τοῦ κυρίου"},{"q":"Which mood gives a command, as in μὴ φοβοῦ?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Subjunctive","Infinitive","Imperative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The imperative gives a command and is negated with μή, as in μὴ φοβοῦ, \"do not be afraid.\"","greek":"μὴ φοβοῦ"},{"q":"Which tool does the lesson say lets you click a \"G\" (Greek) icon next to any verse?","kind":"concept","options":["Bible Gateway","Logos Bible Software","BDAG","Blue Letter Bible"],"answer":3,"explain":"In Blue Letter Bible, the \"G\" icon shows every word fully parsed, with Strong's and BDAG references."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what is the single highest-leverage activity for Greek reading ability?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing accent rules","Building vocabulary","Learning the verb series first","Reading English commentaries"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vocabulary has the most leverage. Every word you recognize automatically is one less load while reading."},{"q":"Greek tense primarily encodes the time of an action rather than its kind.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek tense primarily encodes aspect, the kind of action, and not just its time."},{"q":"What does this verb form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["He was raised","He gave","He loved","It happened"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἠγέρθη means \"he was raised.\" It appears in resurrection passages throughout the NT.","greek":"ἠγέρθη"},{"q":"Which set of endings does the lesson describe as distinctively verbal?","kind":"form","options":["-ος, -ου, -ῳ","-η, -ης, -ῃ","-ει, -εν, -σεν, -θη","-αι, -αις, -ας"],"answer":2,"explain":"Verbs have their own endings like -ει, -εν, -σεν and -θη. The others are noun endings from the 1st and 2nd declensions."},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["After them","With them","Against them","Through them"],"answer":1,"explain":"μετά + genitive means \"with,\" and αὐτῶν is genitive plural, so: \"with them.\"","greek":"μετ' αὐτῶν"},{"q":"The lesson calls ἐν vs. εἰς a common trap. What is the difference?","kind":"concept","options":["They are interchangeable","ἐν + accusative is direction; εἰς + dative is location","ἐν means \"from\"; εἰς means \"out of\"","ἐν + dative is location (\"in\"); εἰς + accusative is direction (\"into\")"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both can look like \"in\" in English, but ἐν + dative is location and εἰς + accusative is direction."},{"q":"In the nine-step reading strategy, what is step 3, right after scanning for articles?","kind":"concept","options":["Check adjective agreement","Spot the verb","Find the nominative, your subject","Handle prepositions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 3 is to find the nominative (ὁ, ἡ, τό, οἱ, αἱ), which gives you the subject. Step 4 finds the accusative."},{"q":"In John 3:16, why is this phrase in the accusative?","kind":"reading","options":["It is the subject of the clause","It means \"of eternal life\"","It means \"by eternal life\"","It is the direct object of the verb \"have\""],"answer":3,"explain":"ζωήν and the agreeing adjective αἰώνιον are accusative because they are the direct object of ἔχῃ, \"have.\"","greek":"ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"In Romans 5:1, how does the lesson parse εἰρήνην?","kind":"reading","options":["Accusative feminine singular, the direct object","Nominative feminine singular, the subject","Genitive feminine singular","Dative feminine singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἰρήνην has no article here, but its -ην ending marks accusative feminine singular. It is what \"we have.\"","greek":"εἰρήνην ἔχομεν"},{"q":"In εἰς τὴν χάριν ταύτην (Romans 5:2), what is ταύτην?","kind":"form","options":["Nominative feminine singular of ἐκεῖνος","Accusative feminine singular of οὗτος","Genitive feminine singular of οὗτος","Accusative feminine singular of αὐτός"],"answer":1,"explain":"ταύτην is the accusative feminine singular of οὗτος, \"this,\" agreeing with τὴν χάριν.","greek":"ταύτην"},{"q":"How does the lesson parse this verb from Romans 5:1?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist active indicative, 3rd person singular","Present active subjunctive, 3rd person singular","Present active indicative, 1st person plural","Perfect passive indicative, 1st person plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔχομεν is present active indicative 1st plural, \"we have.\" One word, five facts.","greek":"ἔχομεν"},{"q":"What does the middle voice express?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject does the action","The subject receives the action","The action is a command","The subject acts for itself or its own benefit"],"answer":3,"explain":"In the middle voice the subject acts for itself or its own benefit. English has no direct equivalent."},{"q":"The aorist is the most common tense in NT narrative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The aorist presents an action as a single complete event, and it dominates NT narrative."},{"q":"Indicative verbs, such as ἔχομεν, are negated with μή.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Indicative verbs are negated with οὐ. The subjunctive and imperative take μή."},{"q":"In John 3:16, what does the article ὁ before πιστεύων mark?","kind":"reading","options":["The direct object of ἔδωκεν","The subject of the purpose clause","A genitive of possession","The object of εἰς"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὁ is nominative masculine singular, so ὁ πιστεύων (\"the believing one\") is the subject of the ἵνα clause.","greek":"πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων"},{"q":"In εἰς αὐτόν (John 3:16), what does the pronoun αὐτόν refer back to?","kind":"reading","options":["τὸν κόσμον","ὁ θεός","τὸν υἱόν","ζωὴν αἰώνιον"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says αὐτόν, accusative masculine singular of αὐτός, refers back to τὸν υἱόν, the Son.","greek":"εἰς αὐτόν"},{"q":"The ἵνα clause in John 3:16 contains two subjunctives. Which word joins them?","kind":"reading","options":["γάρ","ὥστε","καί","ἀλλά"],"answer":3,"explain":"The two subjunctives, ἀπόληται and ἔχῃ, are joined by ἀλλ' (ἀλλά), the strong-contrast conjunction.","greek":"ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτὸν μὴ ἀπόληται ἀλλ' ἔχῃ ζωὴν αἰώνιον"},{"q":"In δι' οὗ (Romans 5:2), how does the grammar show that οὗ refers to κυρίου and not πίστεως?","kind":"reading","options":["Its masculine gender matches κυρίου, not feminine πίστεως","Its position directly after πίστεως","Its plural number","Its accusative case"],"answer":0,"explain":"οὗ is genitive masculine singular. Masculine points to κυρίου (Lord) rather than feminine πίστεως (faith).","greek":"δι' οὗ"},{"q":"In Romans 5:2, which noun does the relative pronoun in ἐν ᾗ refer to?","kind":"reading","options":["τὸν θεόν","τοῦ κυρίου","Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ","τὴν χάριν"],"answer":3,"explain":"ᾗ is dative feminine singular, and its gender matches χάριν (grace, feminine). The other options are masculine.","greek":"ἐν ᾗ ἑστήκαμεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson parse this verb form?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist active indicative, 3rd person singular","Perfect passive indicative, 3rd person singular","Present middle indicative, 3rd person singular","Perfect active subjunctive, 3rd person singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐγήγερται is perfect passive indicative 3rd singular, \"he has been raised.\" The perfect shows a completed act with a lasting result.","greek":"ἐγήγερται"},{"q":"Which step of the nine-step strategy does the lesson say the verb series will complete as a full skill?","kind":"concept","options":["Step 1, reading the conjunctions","Step 5, checking adjective agreement","Step 8, spotting the verb","Step 9, connecting the pieces"],"answer":2,"explain":"For now, step 8 means spotting the verb and looking it up. The verb series will let you parse it yourself."},{"q":"This verb means \"we were saved\" (Romans 8:24). Which voice is it?","kind":"form","options":["Passive","Active","Middle","Subjunctive"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the passive voice the subject receives the action. Here \"we\" are the ones acted on.","greek":"ἐσώθημεν"}],"vb_01":[{"q":"The lesson sorts verbs by what they communicate. Which kind of verb is \"run\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Existence","Action","Condition","Process"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Run\" is listed with speak and write as a verb of action."},{"q":"Which category does the lesson put \"will be\" in?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Experience","Existence","Process"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Will be\" appears with is and was under existence: it says that something exists."},{"q":"Which kind of verb is \"becomes\" in the lesson's list?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Experience","Condition","Existence"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Becomes\" is grouped with seems and appears as a verb of condition."},{"q":"Which category does the verb \"know\" belong to in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Condition","Action","Experience","Process"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Know\" is listed with believe and feel under experience."},{"q":"The lesson lists \"increase\" as a verb of which kind?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Existence","Condition","Process"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Increase\" is grouped with grow and change as a verb of process."},{"q":"Which of these verbs does the lesson list under Process?","kind":"concept","options":["change","seems","feel","speak"],"answer":0,"explain":"Change is listed with grow and increase as process verbs. Speak is action, feel is experience, and seems is condition."},{"q":"How many kinds of things does the lesson say a verb can communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Seven","Five","Three","Two"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists five: action, existence, condition, experience and process."},{"q":"What is the verb in the sentence \"He believes the message\"?","kind":"concept","options":["message","believes","the","He"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Believes\" is the verb. It tells what he is doing with the message."},{"q":"The lesson's example \"The teacher speaks\" uses which kind of verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Existence","Process","Action","Condition"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Speak\" is one of the lesson's action verbs, alongside run and write."},{"q":"What does the lesson say happens if you remove the verb from \"The disciples walked along the road\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Only a fragment is left","It becomes a command","It is still a whole sentence","It becomes a question"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without its verb the sentence falls apart into a fragment. The verb is what makes it whole."},{"q":"A sentence can still have movement, action and meaning without a verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says that without a verb a sentence has no movement, no action and no meaning."},{"q":"The lesson says the same foundational idea lies underneath all the Greek endings and grammar labels.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Underneath the endings and labels is one idea: verbs communicate what is happening, who does it, when, and how."},{"q":"The lesson describes verbs as the ___ of communication.","kind":"concept","options":["frame","engine","decoration","label"],"answer":1,"explain":"Verbs are called the engine of communication because they drive the sentence forward."},{"q":"Why can Greek verbs seem intimidating at first, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["They always stand at the end of a sentence","They have no connection to English verbs","They carry a lot of information packed into one word","They never change their form"],"answer":2,"explain":"A single Greek verb packs in a great deal of information, which can feel like a lot at first."},{"q":"Verbs communicate what is happening. What else does the lesson say they communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["What it costs and who sees it","Where, why, and to whom","How loud, how long, and how often","Who does it, when, and how"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's foundational idea is that verbs show what is happening: who does it, when, and how."},{"q":"Before worrying about Greek endings, what does the lesson call the most important step?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every Greek ending","Translating whole Greek books","Recognizing verbs naturally in English","Learning Greek accent marks"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first step is to spot verbs confidently in your own language."},{"q":"In the sentence \"The crowd grows larger,\" which word is the verb, and which kind does the lesson's list make it?","kind":"concept","options":["larger: Condition","crowd: Existence","grows: Process","crowd: Action"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Grows\" is the verb. The lesson lists grow with change and increase as verbs of process."},{"q":"The lesson says verbs are not just what?","kind":"concept","options":["Parts of sentences","Ways to communicate","Action words","Grammar labels"],"answer":3,"explain":"Verbs are more than grammar labels: they are the engine of communication."},{"q":"Which sentence from the lesson uses a verb of existence?","kind":"concept","options":["The teacher speaks.","The disciples walked along the road.","He believes the message.","God is faithful."],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Is\" is listed under existence. Speaks is action, believes is experience, and walked describes movement."},{"q":"Which sentence from the lesson uses a verb of experience?","kind":"concept","options":["He believes the message.","God is faithful.","The teacher speaks.","The disciples walked along the road."],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Believe\" is in the lesson's experience list, along with know and feel."},{"q":"The lesson lists \"feel\" as an action verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. \"Feel\" is listed under experience, with believe and know. The action verbs are run, speak and write."},{"q":"Using the lesson's lists, what kind of verb is the verb in \"The answer appears simple\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Process","Existence","Condition"],"answer":3,"explain":"The verb is \"appears,\" which the lesson lists with seems and becomes as a verb of condition."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the kinds of things the lesson says a verb can communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Location","Existence","Process"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's five are action, existence, condition, experience and process. Location is not on the list."},{"q":"Which pair of verbs does the lesson place in the same category?","kind":"concept","options":["grow and feel","seems and becomes","run and know","is and write"],"answer":1,"explain":"Seems and becomes are both condition verbs. Each of the other pairs mixes two different categories."},{"q":"What benefit does the lesson promise once you can identify verbs confidently in English?","kind":"concept","options":["You will find it much easier to spot them in Greek","You will know every Greek word's meaning","You will no longer need to learn Greek endings","You will be able to skip the rest of the track"],"answer":0,"explain":"Skill with English verbs carries over and makes Greek verbs easier to spot."},{"q":"Which two verbs from the lesson's lists are both action verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["speak and feel","speak and write","run and seems","write and grow"],"answer":1,"explain":"Run, speak and write form the action list. Feel is experience, grow is process, and seems is condition."},{"q":"Before learning Greek verbs, what does the lesson ask you to notice?","kind":"concept","options":["What verbs actually do in normal language","How many words are in a Greek sentence","Where Greek accents fall","How Greek letters are pronounced"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson starts by looking at what verbs do in everyday language."},{"q":"Which of these verbs does the lesson list under Existence?","kind":"concept","options":["was","feel","grow","seems"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Was\" is listed with is and will be as a verb of existence."},{"q":"Which of these is a complete sentence from the lesson, not a fragment?","kind":"concept","options":["The disciples along the road.","The teacher.","God faithful.","He believes the message."],"answer":3,"explain":"Only \"He believes the message\" keeps its verb. The others have lost their verbs and are fragments."},{"q":"Which of these verbs is NOT in the lesson's Experience list?","kind":"concept","options":["believe","know","feel","grow"],"answer":3,"explain":"Grow is a process verb. The experience verbs are believe, know and feel."}],"vb_02":[{"q":"In the lesson's sentence \"The student learns,\" what is the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["student","learns","the","the -s ending"],"answer":0,"explain":"The subject is who or what the verb is about. Here the verb \"learns\" is about the student."},{"q":"What is the verb in the lesson's sentence \"The crowd listened\"?","kind":"concept","options":["listened","the","The crowd","crowd"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Listened\" is the verb. It tells what the crowd did."},{"q":"What is the subject of the sentence \"Jesus spoke\"?","kind":"concept","options":["the -e ending","spoke","There is no subject","Jesus"],"answer":3,"explain":"The verb \"spoke\" is about Jesus, so Jesus is the subject."},{"q":"What does the lesson say shapes everything about how Greek sentences work?","kind":"concept","options":["How often a verb is repeated","The difference between relying on word order and relying on endings","The number of capital letters in a sentence","Placing the verb last in every sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"English relies on word order and Greek relies on endings, and the lesson says that difference shapes everything about how Greek sentences work."},{"q":"The lesson says a subject can be \"someone or something.\" What is the subject of \"The message reached the crowd\"?","kind":"concept","options":["crowd","reached","message","the"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Reached\" is about the message: the message performs the action and comes before the verb. A subject does not have to be a person."},{"q":"According to the lesson, a Greek sentence never has a separate subject word.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the verb ending often makes a separate subject word unnecessary. Often is not the same as always."},{"q":"How does the lesson define the subject of a sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["A word that describes the verb","The word that tells when the action happened","The last word of the sentence","Who or what the verb is about"],"answer":3,"explain":"The subject is the person or thing the verb is about."},{"q":"In English, what do we depend on to identify the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["Verb endings","Accent marks","Word order","Capital letters"],"answer":2,"explain":"English speakers find the subject mainly by its position in the sentence."},{"q":"Where does the subject usually come in an English sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["At the very end","Before the verb","After the verb","Inside the verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"In English the subject usually comes before the verb."},{"q":"The lesson labels \"The disciple writes\" as \"subject first, verb second.\" What does this example illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek uses endings to show the subject","English has no subjects","English uses word order to show the subject","English verbs always come first"],"answer":2,"explain":"The example shows English depending on word order: the subject comes first, then the verb."},{"q":"The lesson says English speakers do what with their dependence on word order?","kind":"concept","options":["Ignore it in writing","Avoid it whenever possible","Learn it from Greek","Take it for granted"],"answer":3,"explain":"English speakers rely on word order so naturally that they rarely notice it."},{"q":"Greek identifies the subject of a sentence in exactly the same way English does.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the Greek system works differently: it relies on endings rather than word order."},{"q":"The lesson says the difference between Greek and English is what makes Greek both challenging and fascinating.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Greek's different way of marking the subject is described as both challenging and fascinating."},{"q":"According to the lesson, a subject can be someone or something that experiences an action, not only one that performs it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson describes the subject as someone or something performing or experiencing the action."},{"q":"Which English phrase does the lesson say Greek can express with a single word?","kind":"concept","options":["\"The disciple writes\"","\"The student learns\"","\"I write\"","\"The crowd listened\""],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek can say \"I write\" as one word, because the verb ending tells you the subject is \"I.\""},{"q":"Why can Greek say \"I write\" in one word?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending tells you the subject is \"I\"","An accent mark shows the subject","Greek has no subjects","Word order shows the subject"],"answer":0,"explain":"The verb ending itself carries the subject, so no separate word for \"I\" is needed."},{"q":"In Greek, what does the verb ending often make unnecessary?","kind":"concept","options":["The verb stem","A separate subject word","The end of the sentence","The verb itself"],"answer":1,"explain":"Because the ending already shows the subject, Greek often does not need a separate subject word."},{"q":"Which later topics does the lesson say will make the role of Greek endings much clearer?","kind":"concept","options":["Prepositions and particles","Person and number","Accents and breathing marks","Spelling and punctuation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson points ahead to person and number, which show how endings mark the subject."},{"q":"Which statement matches the lesson's key comparison?","kind":"concept","options":["English relies on word order; Greek relies on endings","English relies on endings; Greek relies on word order","Greek relies on punctuation; English relies on endings","English and Greek both rely mainly on word order"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the central contrast of the lesson: English finds the subject by position, Greek by the verb ending."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the core of every sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject + adjective","Subject + verb","Verb + object","Article + noun"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every sentence is built on a subject and a verb."},{"q":"A Greek sentence has a verb but no separate subject word. Where should a reader look to find who is acting?","kind":"concept","options":["At the word after the verb","At the punctuation","At the first word of the sentence","At the verb ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"In Greek the verb ending itself can tell you who the subject is."},{"q":"The lesson says Greek builds its sentences on a completely different foundation from subject + verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek marks the subject differently, but it builds on the same subject + verb foundation."},{"q":"According to the lesson, every verb connects to what?","kind":"concept","options":["A subject","An article","A question","A preposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"In every sentence, the verb connects to a subject."},{"q":"Why does the lesson say understanding the subject–verb connection matters?","kind":"concept","options":["It is a key step toward reading Greek well","It explains Greek accent marks","It replaces the need to learn vocabulary","It matters only for English"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing how verbs connect to subjects prepares you to read Greek well."},{"q":"Which feature of Greek does the lesson call \"remarkable\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek has no verbs","Greek sentences always start with the verb","The verb ending can communicate who the subject is","Greek uses English word order"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson highlights that a Greek verb ending can carry the subject by itself."},{"q":"If the words of an English sentence were scrambled, which tool for finding the subject would be lost?","kind":"concept","options":["Word order","Spelling","Verb endings","Vocabulary"],"answer":0,"explain":"English depends on word order to identify the subject, so scrambling the words removes that signal."},{"q":"Using the lesson's definition, what is the subject of \"The teacher writes the message\"?","kind":"concept","options":["message","writes","teacher","the"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Writes\" is about the teacher: the teacher performs the action and comes before the verb. \"Message\" comes after the verb, so it is not the subject."},{"q":"In \"The disciples listened,\" what tells an English reader that \"disciples\" is the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["It comes after the verb","It comes before the verb","It has a special ending","It is capitalized"],"answer":1,"explain":"English depends on word order, and the subject usually comes before the verb."},{"q":"Which of these is a complete subject + verb sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["The disciple.","The crowd.","Jesus.","The crowd listened."],"answer":3,"explain":"Only \"The crowd listened\" has both a subject and a verb, the core of every sentence."},{"q":"Which sentence does the lesson use to show \"subject first, verb second\"?","kind":"concept","options":["The crowd listened.","Jesus spoke.","The disciple writes.","The student learns."],"answer":2,"explain":"\"The disciple writes\" is the lesson's example of English word order."}],"vb_03":[{"q":"In the lesson's examples, which time does \"He wrote\" show?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect","Past","Present","Future"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"He wrote\" is the lesson's past-tense example."},{"q":"According to the lesson, who commonly makes the mistake of reducing Greek tense to time alone?","kind":"concept","options":["Ancient Greek authors","Early students","Experienced scholars only","Dictionary editors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson calls this one of the most common mistakes early students make."},{"q":"Following the lesson's pattern (He writes, He wrote, He will write), which form shows future time for the verb speak?","kind":"concept","options":["He spoke","He speaks","He has spoken","He will speak"],"answer":3,"explain":"Like \"He will write,\" the future uses will: \"He will speak.\" None of the other forms shows future time."},{"q":"What term does the lesson use for the author's perspective on how an action is viewed?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect","Number","Voice","Mood"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aspect is the author's perspective on how the action is being viewed."},{"q":"Whose perspective does aspect describe, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The translator's","The author's","The listener's","The reader's"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aspect is the author's choice about how to view the action."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the three ways the lesson says an action can be viewed?","kind":"concept","options":["As completed with a result that continues","As a complete whole","As ongoing and unfolding","As happening in a particular place"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's three viewpoints are ongoing, a complete whole, and completed with a continuing result. Place is not one of them."},{"q":"An author presents an action as ongoing and unfolding. Is that a matter of time or of aspect?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect","Mood","Person","Time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ongoing versus whole is about how the action is viewed, which is aspect, not when it happened."},{"q":"The lesson says time is central to how English handles verbs.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. English changes its verb forms mainly to show when an action happens."},{"q":"According to the lesson, aspect is the same thing as time.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the viewpoints of aspect are not the same as time."},{"q":"The lesson says an author can describe a past event while viewing it as ongoing.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Time and viewpoint are separate, so a past event can still be viewed as ongoing."},{"q":"The lesson asks you to master every detail of aspect right away.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. For now you are told to hold the idea loosely. Later lessons on specific tenses will build it out."},{"q":"What combination does the lesson say makes Greek so precise?","kind":"concept","options":["Word order and punctuation together","Time and viewpoint together","Spelling and accents together","Subjects and objects together"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek tense carries both time and viewpoint, and that combination gives it precision."},{"q":"A student translates every Greek verb by asking only \"When did this happen?\" What mistake does the lesson warn about?","kind":"concept","options":["Reducing Greek tense to time alone","Ignoring the subject of the verb","Reading Greek word order like English","Confusing commands with statements"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's common mistake is treating Greek tense as only time, which misses viewpoint."},{"q":"How important is viewpoint compared with simple time in Greek, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Often as much as, or more than, time","Never as important as time","Only important in the future tense","Only important in English"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek often communicates viewpoint as much as, or more than, simple past, present or future time."},{"q":"What does the lesson say about time in Greek verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek verbs show time and nothing else","Greek uses verb forms for time, but time is not the whole story","Greek verbs never show time","Greek shows time only with separate words"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek forms do show time, but they also show how the action is viewed."},{"q":"An author describes a repeated action as if it were a single whole. What is shaping that description?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject","Word order","Aspect","The time of the action"],"answer":2,"explain":"Choosing to view a repeated action as one whole is a matter of viewpoint, which is aspect."},{"q":"Which of the lesson's three viewpoints includes a result that lasts?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing and unfolding","Located in the present moment","Completed, with a result that continues","Viewed as a complete whole"],"answer":2,"explain":"One viewpoint sees the action as completed, with its result continuing."},{"q":"Which viewpoint looks at an action all at once, as a single unit?","kind":"concept","options":["Viewed as a complete whole","Completed with a continuing result","Seen as future time","Ongoing and unfolding"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing an action as a complete whole treats it as one unit rather than something unfolding."},{"q":"An author wants to show an action still in progress. Which of the lesson's viewpoints fits?","kind":"concept","options":["Future time","Completed with a continuing result","Ongoing and unfolding","Viewed as a complete whole"],"answer":2,"explain":"An action in progress is viewed as ongoing and unfolding."},{"q":"Which English verb does the lesson use for all three of its time examples?","kind":"concept","options":["believe","run","speak","write"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson uses writes, wrote and will write to show present, past and future."},{"q":"The lesson shows English verb forms for which three times?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing, whole and completed","Present, command and future","Present, past and future","Past, ongoing and repeated"],"answer":2,"explain":"English time is shown through present, past and future forms."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe English speakers' use of time in verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["They find it very hard to learn","They borrowed it from Greek","It comes so naturally they rarely think about it","They avoid it in writing"],"answer":2,"explain":"Marking time in verbs comes so naturally to English speakers that they rarely stop to think about it."},{"q":"Which part of the Greek verb does the lesson say communicates both time and viewpoint?","kind":"concept","options":["Its tense","Its subject","Its word order","Its spelling"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says Greek tense communicates both time and viewpoint (aspect)."},{"q":"Which question does aspect answer?","kind":"concept","options":["Where did the action happen?","When did the action happen?","How is the author viewing the action?","Who did the action?"],"answer":2,"explain":"Aspect is about viewpoint: how the action is being viewed."},{"q":"Where does the lesson say the idea of aspect will be built out carefully?","kind":"concept","options":["In a lesson on the alphabet","Nowhere; it is not needed","Only in advanced commentaries","In future lessons on specific tenses"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson introduces aspect and promises later lessons on specific tenses."},{"q":"Comparing \"He writes\" with \"He wrote,\" what has changed?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject","The verb form, to show a different time","The person writing","The aspect only"],"answer":1,"explain":"In English the form of the verb shifts to show when the action takes place."},{"q":"An author narrates a past event as if watching it unfold. In that description, which part is the time and which is the viewpoint?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing is the time; past is the viewpoint","Past is the time; ongoing is the viewpoint","Both past and ongoing are times","Both past and ongoing are viewpoints"],"answer":1,"explain":"Past tells when the event happened, which is time. Watching it unfold is how the author views it, which is viewpoint (aspect)."},{"q":"Two authors describe the same past event, one as ongoing and one as a complete whole. What differs between their verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject","The time, not the aspect","The language","The aspect, not the time"],"answer":3,"explain":"The event is past in both, so the time is the same. What differs is how each author views it."},{"q":"Which of these is a viewpoint rather than a time?","kind":"concept","options":["Future","Present","Past","A complete whole"],"answer":3,"explain":"Past, present and future are times. Viewing an action as a complete whole is aspect."},{"q":"How many ways of viewing an action does the lesson list when it introduces aspect?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Two","Five","Six"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists three: ongoing and unfolding, a complete whole, and completed with a result that continues."}],"vb_04":[{"q":"Which of these sentences is a command?","kind":"concept","options":["He learns.","Does he learn?","Learn!","If he learns…"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Learn!\" directs someone to act, so it is a command. The others are a statement, a question and a condition."},{"q":"What kind of sentence is \"If he speaks…\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A condition","A question","A statement","A command"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"If he speaks…\" raises a possibility, which makes it a condition."},{"q":"What kind of sentence is \"Does he speak?\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A command","A question","A statement","A condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Does he speak?\" seeks information, so it is a question."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does a statement do?","kind":"concept","options":["Directs someone to act","Presents something as real","Seeks information","Raises a possibility"],"answer":1,"explain":"A statement such as \"He speaks\" presents something as real."},{"q":"What does the lesson say a command does?","kind":"concept","options":["Expresses a wish","Raises a possibility","Directs someone to act","Presents something as real"],"answer":2,"explain":"A command directs someone to act, as in \"Speak!\""},{"q":"What idea does the lesson say it is simply introducing?","kind":"concept","options":["That every Greek verb is a command","That sentence type is part of what a Greek verb communicates","That English has no way to mark questions","That Greek has no conditions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson only introduces the idea that a Greek verb also tells you what kind of sentence it is."},{"q":"What is a question doing, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Presenting something as real","Raising a possibility","Seeking information","Directing someone to act"],"answer":2,"explain":"A question like \"Does he speak?\" seeks information."},{"q":"What does Greek call the verb category that marks these kinds of sentences?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Number","Aspect","Mood"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek builds sentence type into the verb through a category called mood."},{"q":"Which Greek mood is used for giving a command?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Indicative","Optative","Subjunctive"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperative is the mood of commands."},{"q":"What does the indicative mood do, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["States or asserts something as real","Expresses a wish","Gives a command","Expresses contingency"],"answer":0,"explain":"The indicative states or asserts something as real."},{"q":"What does the subjunctive mood express?","kind":"concept","options":["A statement of fact","A request for information","A command","Possibility or contingency"],"answer":3,"explain":"The subjunctive expresses possibility or contingency."},{"q":"What does the optative mood express?","kind":"concept","options":["A direct command","Wish or remote possibility","A question","A plain statement of fact"],"answer":1,"explain":"The optative expresses a wish or a remote possibility."},{"q":"Which two moods from the lesson's list both deal with possibility?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative and subjunctive","Subjunctive and optative","Imperative and optative","Indicative and imperative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The subjunctive expresses possibility or contingency; the optative expresses wish or remote possibility."},{"q":"How many major moods does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Five","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":3,"explain":"The four are indicative, imperative, subjunctive and optative."},{"q":"How does English signal the difference between kinds of sentences?","kind":"concept","options":["Capital letters only","Different verb endings only","Accent marks","Different punctuation and word order"],"answer":3,"explain":"English uses punctuation and word order to show what kind of sentence it is."},{"q":"Where does Greek build the difference between statements, commands and conditions?","kind":"concept","options":["Directly into the verb form","Into a separate helper word","Only into punctuation","Only into word order"],"answer":0,"explain":"In Greek, sentence type is built directly into the verb form through mood."},{"q":"The lesson says upcoming lessons will focus on the indicative and imperative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Those two moods are the focus of the next lessons."},{"q":"The lesson expects you to master all four moods right now.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. This lesson only introduces the idea; you do not need to master the moods yet."},{"q":"According to the lesson, mood tells you when an action happens.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Mood tells you what kind of communication is happening, such as a statement or a command, not when the action happens."},{"q":"The optative is the mood the lesson uses for giving a command.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Commands use the imperative. The optative expresses wish or remote possibility."},{"q":"\"If he speaks…\" raises a possibility. Which mood's description in the lesson best matches that idea?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Imperative","Subjunctive","Interrogative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Of these options, the subjunctive's description, \"possibility or contingency,\" fits a condition best. Greek conditions are not limited to one mood, though."},{"q":"What does each Greek mood give the reader, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A different spelling","A different subject","A different kind of communication signal","A different time"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each mood signals a different kind of communication."},{"q":"Mood tells you what kind of communication is happening, not just what?","kind":"concept","options":["What is happening","When it happened","Where it happened","Who is speaking"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood goes beyond the action itself to show the kind of communication."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the major moods the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Optative","Interrogative","Subjunctive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's four moods are indicative, imperative, subjunctive and optative. Interrogative is not among them."},{"q":"Which pairing of mood and function matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative: wish or remote possibility","Subjunctive: possibility or contingency","Indicative: giving a command","Optative: stating something as real"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the subjunctive pairing is right. Indicative states as real, imperative commands, and optative expresses wish."},{"q":"Which pairing of sentence type and example matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Condition: \"If he speaks…\"","Question: \"He speaks.\"","Command: \"Does he speak?\"","Statement: \"Speak!\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"If he speaks…\" is the lesson's condition. The other examples are paired with the wrong types."},{"q":"Which of these sentences is a statement?","kind":"concept","options":["The disciple writes.","Write!","Does the disciple write?","If the disciple writes…"],"answer":0,"explain":"A statement presents something as real. The others are a command, a question and a condition."},{"q":"How many kinds of sentences does the lesson list in its first section?","kind":"concept","options":["Six","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":3,"explain":"It lists statement, command, condition and question."},{"q":"What does the lesson say Greek has for describing each kind of sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["Only punctuation marks","No system at all","A formal grammatical system","A separate word before each verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek has a formal grammatical system, mood, to describe each sentence type."},{"q":"Which statement best describes how English and Greek mark sentence types?","kind":"concept","options":["Neither language marks sentence types","English uses punctuation and word order; Greek builds it into the verb form","Greek uses punctuation and word order; English builds it into the verb form","Both languages mark it only with capital letters"],"answer":1,"explain":"English signals sentence type with punctuation and word order, while Greek builds it into the verb through mood."}],"vb_05":[{"q":"How many categories of information does the lesson say a Greek verb often communicates at once?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Six","Five"],"answer":3,"explain":"A Greek verb often carries person, number, tense, voice and mood together."},{"q":"Which category tells who is performing the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Number","Voice","Mood"],"answer":0,"explain":"Person tells who is performing the action: I, you, he/she/it, we, they."},{"q":"Which category tells whether a verb is singular or plural?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense","Voice","Number","Person"],"answer":2,"explain":"Number is the category for singular or plural."},{"q":"What does the category tense cover, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Singular or plural","Who performs the action","The type of communication","Time and aspect of the action"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tense covers the time and aspect of the action."},{"q":"What does the category voice describe?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject's relationship to the action","Whether it is a statement or command","Whether the verb is singular or plural","The time of the action"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voice describes how the subject relates to the action."},{"q":"What does the category mood describe?","kind":"concept","options":["The type of communication","Who performs the action","The time of the action","The number of people acting"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood describes the type of communication, such as a statement or a command."},{"q":"Which person is this verb?","kind":"form","options":["No person","Third person","Second person","First person"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφω is first person: the subject is \"I.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What is the number of this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Plural","Singular","No number","Dual"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφω is singular: one person, \"I,\" is writing.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which tense does the lesson give for this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Future","Perfect","Present","Past"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson parses γράφω as present tense.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What voice is this verb, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["Passive","Imperative","Active","Middle"],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφω is active voice.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which mood does the lesson give for this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Indicative","Optative","Imperative","Subjunctive"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφω is in the indicative mood.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"The lesson gives two English translations for this word. Which pair is it?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"We write\" and \"we are writing\"","\"You write\" and \"you are writing\"","\"I write\" and \"I am writing\"","\"I wrote\" and \"I will write\""],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφω can be translated \"I write\" or, more fully, \"I am writing.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What term do scholars use for identifying all five categories of a verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Spelling","Glossing","Phrasing","Parsing"],"answer":3,"explain":"Identifying person, number, tense, voice and mood is called parsing."},{"q":"Which full description matches the lesson's parsing of this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Second person singular present active indicative","First person singular present active indicative","First person plural present active indicative","First person singular future active indicative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists γράφω as first person, singular, present, active, indicative.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"According to the lesson, a single Greek verb can do the work of an entire English phrase.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Because one verb carries so many categories, it can do the work of a whole English phrase."},{"q":"According to the lesson, voice tells whether a verb is a statement or a command.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. That is mood, the type of communication. Voice is the subject's relationship to the action."},{"q":"Which word does the lesson's introduction use to describe Greek verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Random","Compact","Decorative","Rare"],"answer":1,"explain":"The intro calls Greek verbs remarkably compact: one word can carry five categories of information."},{"q":"Parsing means identifying only the person and number of a verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Parsing means identifying all five categories for a verb."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five categories in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Person","Voice","Gender"],"answer":3,"explain":"The five are person, number, tense, voice and mood. Gender is not on the list."},{"q":"Which set of English words does the lesson use to illustrate person?","kind":"concept","options":["I, you, he/she/it, we, they","statement, command","present, past, future","singular, plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"Person is illustrated with the subjects I, you, he/she/it, we and they."},{"q":"According to the lesson, this verb needs a separate Greek word for \"I\" to show who is writing.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The single word γράφω already communicates first person singular: \"I.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What does the lesson say each of the five categories will receive later in the track?","kind":"concept","options":["A separate dictionary","Its own lesson","No further discussion","A single shared chart"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each category will be covered in its own lesson."},{"q":"Which pairing of category and description matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood: singular or plural","Voice: the subject's relationship to the action","Number: who is performing the action","Person: time and aspect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voice is the subject's relationship to the action. The other pairings mix up the categories."},{"q":"By the end of the track, what does the lesson say you will be able to do?","kind":"concept","options":["Write Greek without any endings","Skip parsing altogether","Read a Greek verb and understand what each part communicates","Speak modern Greek fluently"],"answer":2,"explain":"The goal is to read a Greek verb and understand what each part is telling you."},{"q":"For now, what does the lesson want you to recognize about Greek verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["They always need helper words","They carry a lot of information efficiently","They carry very little information","They never change form"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first step is simply to notice how much a Greek verb carries."},{"q":"According to the lesson, understanding the five categories is the foundation of what?","kind":"concept","options":["Pronouncing Greek letters","Reading Greek verbs well","Memorizing Greek nouns","Writing Greek accents"],"answer":1,"explain":"These five categories are the foundation of reading Greek verbs well."},{"q":"In the lesson's parsing of this verb, \"first person (I)\" belongs to which category?","kind":"form","options":["Mood","Number","Person","Tense"],"answer":2,"explain":"First person is a value of the category person, which tells who performs the action.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which examples does the lesson give, in parentheses, of the types of communication that mood covers?","kind":"concept","options":["Statement, command","Time, aspect","Singular, plural","I, you, we"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood covers the type of communication, illustrated with statement and command."},{"q":"In the lesson's parsing of this verb, \"active\" fills which category?","kind":"form","options":["Voice","Number","Mood","Tense"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active is a voice, describing the subject's relationship to the action.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which of the five categories does the lesson describe as covering two ideas at once?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Number","Person","Tense"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tense covers both time and aspect."}],"vb_06":[{"q":"What person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["2nd person plural","1st person singular","1st person plural","3rd person plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ending -ομεν marks 1st person plural: \"we.\"","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"What person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person plural","3rd person singular","2nd person singular","1st person singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending -ει marks 3rd person singular: \"he/she/it.\"","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"Identify the person and number of this form.","kind":"form","options":["2nd person plural","1st person plural","3rd person singular","2nd person singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ending -ετε marks 2nd person plural: \"you all.\"","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"What person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person singular","2nd person plural","1st person plural","3rd person plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"The ending -ουσι(ν) marks 3rd person plural: \"they.\"","greek":"λύουσι(ν)"},{"q":"Which person and number does this ending mark in the present active indicative?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person singular","1st person singular","2nd person singular","1st person plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"-ω is the 1st singular ending, with \"I\" built in, as in λύω.","greek":"-ω"},{"q":"Which subject is built into this ending?","kind":"form","options":["you (one person)","they","we","you all"],"answer":3,"explain":"-ετε is the 2nd plural ending, so its built-in subject is \"you all.\"","greek":"-ετε"},{"q":"Using the endings in the lesson's table, what does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they write","you all write","I write","we write"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφομεν is the stem γράφ- plus the 1st plural ending -ομεν, so it means \"we write.\"","greek":"γράφομεν"},{"q":"Using the stem γράφ- and the lesson's endings, which form means \"they write\"?","kind":"form","options":["γράφει","γράφετε","γράφουσι(ν)","γράφομεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 3rd plural ending is -ουσι(ν), so γράφ- + -ουσι(ν) gives γράφουσι(ν), \"they write.\""},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they write","he/she/it writes","you write","I write"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφει has the 3rd singular ending -ει: \"he/she/it writes.\"","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"Which form means \"I write\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["γράφει","γράφεις","λύω","γράφω"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφω has the 1st singular ending -ω. λύω also ends in -ω, but it is a different verb."},{"q":"In γράφω, γράφεις and γράφει, what does this part of the word represent?","kind":"concept","options":["The stem, which stays the same","The plural marker","The ending, which shows the subject","A separate pronoun"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφ- is the stem. It stays the same while the ending changes.","greek":"γράφ-"},{"q":"How many persons does Greek distinguish?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Six","Three","Four"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek has first, second and third person."},{"q":"According to the lesson, who is the second person?","kind":"concept","options":["The one addressed","The speaker","A group of speakers","The one spoken about"],"answer":0,"explain":"Second person is the one being addressed: \"you.\""},{"q":"Which description fits the third person?","kind":"concept","options":["The one addressed","The one spoken about","The speaker","The one listening"],"answer":1,"explain":"Third person is the one spoken about: he, she, it or they."},{"q":"Who is the first person, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The one spoken about","The reader","The speaker","The one addressed"],"answer":2,"explain":"First person is the speaker: I or we."},{"q":"How many basic verb endings does person combined with number give for any one tense/voice/mood combination?","kind":"concept","options":["Nine","Two","Six","Three"],"answer":2,"explain":"Three persons times two numbers gives six slots."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the endings used in the present active indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["The secondary passive endings","The pronoun endings","The connecting endings","The primary active endings"],"answer":3,"explain":"The present active indicative uses the primary active endings."},{"q":"Besides the present, where does the lesson say you will see the primary active endings over and over?","kind":"concept","options":["In future active forms","In imperative forms","In past passive forms","In noun endings"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says these endings appear in present and future active forms."},{"q":"In Greek, person is communicated through the verb ending, not a separate pronoun.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The ending itself tells you who the subject is, so no separate pronoun is needed (one can still be added for emphasis)."},{"q":"This ending marks the 2nd person singular.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. -ει is the 3rd person singular ending (he/she/it). The 2nd singular ending is -εις.","greek":"-ει"},{"q":"In the lesson's list of six slots, the English word \"you\" can stand for either 2nd person singular or 2nd person plural.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson lists 2nd singular as \"you\" and 2nd plural as \"you (all).\" Greek keeps them apart with different endings: -εις and -ετε."},{"q":"Which form in the λύω column has the built-in subject \"you\" (one person)?","kind":"form","options":["λύεις","λύει","λύετε","λύομεν"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 2nd singular ending is -εις, giving λύεις."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the verb endings really are?","kind":"concept","options":["A sign of the tense only","A separate word placed after the verb","The subject pronoun built into the verb","A spelling decoration"],"answer":2,"explain":"The endings carry the subject, like a pronoun built into the verb."},{"q":"Comparing γράφω, γράφεις and γράφει, what changes from form to form?","kind":"concept","options":["Both the stem and the ending","Only the ending","Nothing changes","Only the stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem γράφ- stays the same; only the ending changes to show who is writing."},{"q":"Which pair of forms has the same person but a different number?","kind":"form","options":["λύει and λύετε","λύεις and λύει","λύω and λύεις","λύω and λύομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύω (1st singular) and λύομεν (1st plural) are both first person; only the number differs."},{"q":"Which pair of forms has the same number but a different person?","kind":"form","options":["γράφεις and γράφει","λύω and λύομεν","λύει and λύουσι(ν)","λύεις and λύετε"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφεις (2nd singular) and γράφει (3rd singular) are both singular but differ in person."},{"q":"Which number does the lesson describe as \"one person\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Third person","Singular","Plural","First person"],"answer":1,"explain":"Singular means one person; plural means more than one. First and third are persons, not numbers."},{"q":"In Greek verbs, what does plural mean?","kind":"concept","options":["The one addressed","Exactly one","More than one","The speaker only"],"answer":2,"explain":"Plural means more than one; singular means one."},{"q":"Which English example in the lesson shows person marked by an ending?","kind":"concept","options":["\"I write\"","\"You write\"","\"We write\"","\"He writes,\" with its -s ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"In English, \"He writes\" adds -s to mark the third person."},{"q":"The six basic slots come from combining three persons with two numbers.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Three persons (1st, 2nd, 3rd) times two numbers (singular, plural) gives six slots."}],"vb_07":[{"q":"This verb is present tense. How does the lesson say the present tense views the action?","kind":"concept","options":["As a command","As finished in the past","As a future plan","As ongoing or in progress"],"answer":3,"explain":"The present tense views the action as ongoing or in progress.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"In the active voice, who performs the action?","kind":"concept","options":["The listener","The subject","No one in particular","The object"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active voice means the subject is the one doing the action."},{"q":"What does the indicative mood communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Something asserted or presented as real","A command to act","A possibility only","A wish"],"answer":0,"explain":"The indicative presents something as real."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the English form \"He is writing\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Future","Simple present","Present continuous","Simple past"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"He is writing\" is the present continuous."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the English form \"He writes\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Future","Simple present","Present continuous","Simple past"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"He writes\" is the simple present."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"The student reads,\" who is acting?","kind":"concept","options":["The teacher","The student","No one","The reader of the lesson"],"answer":1,"explain":"In the active voice the subject acts, and here the subject is the student."},{"q":"Which kind of sentences is the indicative mood used for?","kind":"concept","options":["Wishes","Normal statements and declarations","Commands","Questions only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indicative is the mood of ordinary statements and declarations."},{"q":"What does the lesson say \"They believe\" is doing?","kind":"concept","options":["Raising a possibility","Giving a command","Asking a question","Asserting that this is true"],"answer":3,"explain":"As an indicative statement, \"They believe\" asserts that something is true."},{"q":"What tense is this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Past","Perfect","Present","Future"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω is present: it views the action as ongoing.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"What voice is this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Passive","Indicative","Middle","Active"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύω is active: the subject is the one acting.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"What mood is this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Subjunctive","Indicative","Optative","Imperative"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύω is indicative: it is stated as real.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Besides \"I loose,\" what other meaning does the lesson give for this verb?","kind":"meaning","options":["I write","I believe","I destroy","I speak"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson glosses λύω as \"I loose\" or \"I destroy.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Using the lesson's definition of active voice, which sentence has a subject that performs the action?","kind":"concept","options":["The letter is written.","The message is heard.","The disciple writes the letter.","The road is built."],"answer":2,"explain":"In \"The disciple writes the letter,\" the subject (the disciple) does the writing. In the others, the subject does not perform the action."},{"q":"Which of these sentences is a normal statement presenting something as real, the job of the indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Teach!","He teaches.","Does he teach?","If he teaches…"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"He teaches.\" asserts that something is happening, like the lesson's \"He speaks.\" The others do not assert anything as real."},{"q":"The lesson's two English equivalents of the Greek present both use which verb?","kind":"concept","options":["speak","teach","write","believe"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"He writes\" (simple present) and \"He is writing\" (present continuous) both use write."},{"q":"Which of these labels does NOT apply to this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Indicative","Present","Future","Active"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω is present, active and indicative. It is not future.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe this verb?","kind":"concept","options":["As the classic textbook example","As a command form","As an irregular verb","As a rare, unusual form"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύω is the classic textbook example of the present active indicative.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Most Greek textbooks begin with the present active indicative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Textbooks start here because it is the clearest, most foundational form."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the present tense simply locates an action in the present moment.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The present often portrays action as in progress, not simply located in the present moment."},{"q":"In the active voice, the subject receives the action rather than performing it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. In the active voice the subject is the one performing the action."},{"q":"The lesson says the present active indicative establishes patterns that appear throughout the entire verb system.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. That is part of why it is learned first."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the present active indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["A form used only in commands","A form found only in poetry","The clearest, most foundational form of the Greek verb","The rarest form of the Greek verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"It is the clearest, most foundational form, which is why textbooks begin with it."},{"q":"The lesson describes this verb with three labels. Which pairing of label and meaning matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative: stated as real","Active: ongoing action","Present: the subject acts","Indicative: a command"],"answer":0,"explain":"Indicative means stated as real. Present is ongoing action, and active means the subject acts.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Which two English sentences does the lesson say can both represent the Greek present, depending on context?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He wrote\" and \"He was writing\"","\"He will write\" and \"He writes\"","\"He has written\" and \"He writes\"","\"He writes\" and \"He is writing\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Both the simple present and the present continuous can represent the Greek present."},{"q":"A translator renders a Greek present verb as \"he writes\" instead of \"he is writing.\" What does the lesson say about this?","kind":"concept","options":["The present does not always require a continuous translation","The present must always be continuous","Present verbs cannot be translated into English","\"He writes\" can only translate a past tense"],"answer":0,"explain":"Context decides. The Greek present does not always need a continuous translation."},{"q":"What does the lesson say \"He speaks\" is doing as an indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Giving an order","Seeking information","Expressing a wish","Asserting that this is happening"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"He speaks\" asserts that the action is happening."},{"q":"The lesson calls this verb \"present active indicative.\" How many labels is that?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Two","Four","Five"],"answer":0,"explain":"Present, active and indicative are three labels in one combination.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"What person and number is this verb, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["1st person plural","3rd person singular","1st person singular","2nd person singular"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω is 1st person singular: \"I loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Which voice does \"The teacher teaches\" show?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Present","Passive","Active"],"answer":3,"explain":"The teacher is the one acting, so the sentence is in the active voice."},{"q":"Which word does the lesson pair with \"ongoing\" to describe the idea often found in the present?","kind":"concept","options":["Unfolding","Commanded","Finished","Possible"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson speaks of ongoing or unfolding action."}],"vb_23":[{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they loose","you loose","I loose","he/she/it looses"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύει is the 3rd singular form: \"he/she/it looses.\"","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"The stem λυ- stays the same in every form. What ending does this form add to it?","kind":"form","options":["-ετε","-εις","-ουσι(ν)","-ομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν is λυ- plus -ομεν. That ending gives the meaning \"we loose.\"","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they loose","you all loose","we loose","he/she/it looses"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύουσι(ν) is the 3rd plural form: \"they loose.\"","greek":"λύουσι(ν)"},{"q":"Which slot of the paradigm does this form fill?","kind":"form","options":["1st singular","3rd singular","1st plural","2nd singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύω is the 1st singular form: \"I loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Which form fills the 1st plural slot of the paradigm?","kind":"form","options":["λύεις","λύετε","λύουσι(ν)","λύομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"The 1st plural slot holds λύομεν."},{"q":"This form is 2nd person plural.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. λύετε fills the 2nd plural slot: \"you all loose.\"","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"How does the lesson show this word is pronounced?","kind":"sound","options":["low-oh","lee-oh","loo-oh","loo-ee"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson gives the pronunciation of λύω as loo-oh.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"What core meaning does this stem carry?","kind":"meaning","options":["Writing","Believing","Loosing or releasing","Speaking"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem λυ- carries the core meaning of loosing or releasing.","greek":"λυ-"},{"q":"What does a paradigm show?","kind":"concept","options":["Only the dictionary form of a word","The history of a word","A list of words that rhyme","Every form of a word organized by grammatical category"],"answer":3,"explain":"A paradigm lays out every form of a word, organized by grammatical category."},{"q":"How many slots does the present active indicative paradigm have?","kind":"concept","options":["Four","Six","Eight","Three"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three persons and two numbers make six slots."},{"q":"Which two categories organize the six slots of this paradigm?","kind":"concept","options":["Stem and accent","Person and number","Voice and aspect","Tense and mood"],"answer":1,"explain":"The slots come from three persons and two numbers."},{"q":"What does the lesson say a paradigm is, instead of a chart to memorize?","kind":"concept","options":["A pronunciation guide","A window into how the language works","A list of exceptions","A replacement for vocabulary"],"answer":1,"explain":"A paradigm shows how the system of the language works."},{"q":"Besides \"I loose,\" what translation does this lesson give for this verb?","kind":"meaning","options":["I release","I speak","I believe","I write"],"answer":0,"explain":"Here λύω is glossed as \"I loose\" or \"I release.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"These two forms are both first person, and they have the same ending.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Both are first person, but λύω is singular and λύομεν is plural, so their endings differ.","greek":"λύω / λύομεν"},{"q":"A paradigm shows only the singular forms of a verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. It shows every form, singular and plural, in all three persons."},{"q":"Which two forms both have \"you\" as their subject but differ in number?","kind":"form","options":["λύω and λύομεν","λύεις and λύει","λύεις and λύετε","λύει and λύουσι(ν)"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύεις is \"you loose\" (singular) and λύετε is \"you all loose\" (plural)."},{"q":"Which of these forms is singular?","kind":"form","options":["λύει","λύομεν","λύετε","λύουσι(ν)"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύει is 3rd singular. The other three are plural forms."},{"q":"These two forms share the stem λυ-. What makes their meanings different?","kind":"concept","options":["Their stems","Their endings","Their word order","Their accents"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem stays the same; the different endings mark \"we\" and \"they.\"","greek":"λύομεν / λύουσι(ν)"},{"q":"Complete the lesson's summary: \"Six different endings —\"","kind":"concept","options":["three different meanings","six different stems","one single meaning","six different meanings"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each ending gives a different meaning: who is loosing."},{"q":"Which row of the paradigm is correct?","kind":"form","options":["2nd Plural: λύετε, you all loose","2nd Singular: λύει, you loose","3rd Singular: λύουσι(ν), they loose","1st Plural: λύετε, we loose"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύετε is 2nd plural, \"you all loose.\" Each other row mismatches its form and slot."},{"q":"Which paradigm of λύω does this lesson lay out?","kind":"concept","options":["Future passive indicative","Present active indicative","Past active subjunctive","Present active imperative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson shows the complete present active indicative paradigm of λύω."},{"q":"How many numbers does the paradigm use?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","One","Six","Two"],"answer":3,"explain":"The paradigm has two numbers: singular and plural."},{"q":"In the paradigm, which translation uses an English -s ending on the verb?","kind":"concept","options":["they loose","you all loose","he/she/it looses","we loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only the 3rd singular translation, \"he/she/it looses,\" adds -s in English."},{"q":"Which three columns does the lesson's paradigm table use?","kind":"concept","options":["Letter, Sound, Example","Person/Number, Greek Form, Translation","Stem, Accent, Meaning","Tense, Voice, Mood"],"answer":1,"explain":"The table lists each slot's person and number, its Greek form, and its translation."},{"q":"How many of the six forms in the paradigm are plural?","kind":"concept","options":["Four","Two","Six","Three"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν, λύετε and λύουσι(ν) are plural: one for each person."},{"q":"Which two forms are both third person?","kind":"form","options":["λύεις and λύετε","λύω and λύομεν","λύει and λύουσι(ν)","λύει and λύετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύει (he/she/it looses) and λύουσι(ν) (they loose) are both third person."},{"q":"The lesson sums up the paradigm as six combinations and six different forms making one what?","kind":"concept","options":["Accident","Dictionary","System","Exception"],"answer":2,"explain":"The six forms work together as one system."},{"q":"These two forms are the same person.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. λύεις is 2nd person singular (you), while λύει is 3rd person singular (he/she/it).","greek":"λύεις / λύει"},{"q":"What does the lesson call one of the most powerful tools in Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["An accent mark","A paradigm","A dictionary","A translation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Seeing every form laid out together, a paradigm, is called one of the most powerful tools in Greek."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a slot in the six-slot paradigm?","kind":"concept","options":["4th person singular","1st person plural","2nd person singular","3rd person plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"The paradigm has only three persons (1st, 2nd, 3rd), so there is no 4th person."}],"vb_24":[{"q":"How many parts is every thematic Greek verb built from?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Two","Six"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every thematic verb has a stem, a connecting vowel and an ending."},{"q":"Which building block carries the core meaning of the verb?","kind":"concept","options":["The connecting vowel","The stem","The ending","The accent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem carries the core meaning."},{"q":"Which building block communicates person and number?","kind":"concept","options":["The connecting vowel","The ending","The accent","The stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending tells you person and number."},{"q":"What job does the connecting vowel do?","kind":"concept","options":["Carries the core meaning","Shows person and number","Marks the end of a sentence","Smooths the junction between stem and ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"The connecting vowel smooths the join between the stem and the ending."},{"q":"In this lesson's breakdown of this form, what is the ending?","kind":"form","options":["μεν","ομεν","ο","λύ"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύομεν breaks down as λύ + ο + μεν, so the ending is μεν.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"What is the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ει","ις","ε","ο"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύεις is λύ + ε + ις, so the connecting vowel is ε.","greek":"λύεις"},{"q":"What is the ending in this form, according to the lesson's breakdown?","kind":"form","options":["λύ","ι","ει","ε"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύει is λύ + ε + ι, so the ending is ι.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown of this form, what is the ending?","kind":"form","options":["σι","λύ","ου","ουσι"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson splits λύουσι(ν) as λύ + ου + σι, so the ending is σι.","greek":"λύουσι(ν)"},{"q":"Why does the lesson break this form into only two pieces?","kind":"form","options":["It has two connecting vowels","Its ending replaces the stem","It has no personal ending; the connecting vowel ο lengthens to ω","It has no stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω has no personal ending. Its connecting vowel ο lengthens to ω.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown, these two forms have the same connecting vowel.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. λύεις is λύ + ε + ις and λύετε is λύ + ε + τε. Both use ε.","greek":"λύεις / λύετε"},{"q":"Which pairing matches the lesson's summary of the pieces?","kind":"concept","options":["Ending tells WHAT; connecting vowel tells WHO","Stem tells WHAT; ending tells WHO","Stem tells WHO; ending tells WHAT","Connecting vowel tells WHO; stem tells WHEN"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem gives the meaning (what), and the ending gives the person and number (who)."},{"q":"What is the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ε","ο","τε","ου"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύετε is λύ + ε + τε, so the connecting vowel is ε.","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"Which breakdown is correct?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + ο + ι = λύει","λύ + ε + ις = λύει","λύ + ου + ι = λύει","λύ + ε + ι = λύει"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύει is stem λύ, connecting vowel ε, ending ι. λύ + ε + ις would give λύεις."},{"q":"Which breakdown of λύουσι(ν) matches the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + ου + σι","λύ + ω + σι","λύ + ε + σι","λύ + ο + υσι"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson shows λύουσι(ν) as stem λύ, connecting vowel ου, ending σι."},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown, which ending marks 2nd person singular (\"you\")?","kind":"form","options":["ι","τε","ις","σι"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύεις is λύ + ε + ις, \"you loose,\" so the ending ις marks 2nd singular."},{"q":"The connecting vowel tells you who is performing the action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The ending tells you who. The connecting vowel holds the stem and ending together."},{"q":"The lesson describes Greek verbs as a system rather than a set of random forms.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Once you see the three pieces, the forms follow a system."},{"q":"Which form do the pieces λύ + ε + ις produce?","kind":"form","options":["λύομεν","λύει","λύεις","λύετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"Stem λύ, connecting vowel ε and ending ις make λύεις, \"you loose.\""},{"q":"Which form do the pieces λύ + ε + τε produce?","kind":"form","options":["λύετε","λύεις","λύει","λύομεν"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stem λύ, connecting vowel ε and ending τε make λύετε, \"you all loose.\""},{"q":"Comparing λύεις and λύει, which piece is different?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending","The stem","The connecting vowel","Nothing is different"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both have stem λύ and connecting vowel ε. Only the ending differs: ις versus ι."},{"q":"Which piece stays the same in all six forms of λύω?","kind":"concept","options":["The connecting vowel ε","The ending μεν","The ending ις","The stem λύ"],"answer":3,"explain":"Every form starts with the stem λύ; the other pieces change."},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown of λύομεν, which person and number does the ending μεν mark?","kind":"form","options":["1st person singular (I)","1st person plural (we)","3rd person plural (they)","2nd person plural (you all)"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύομεν means \"we loose,\" and the ending μεν carries the \"we.\"","greek":"μεν"},{"q":"Every ___ Greek verb is built from three parts. Which word fills the blank in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["single-part","foreign","thematic","random"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson speaks of every thematic Greek verb."},{"q":"What does this ending communicate?","kind":"form","options":["1st person plural (we)","3rd person plural (they)","2nd person singular (you)","3rd person singular (he/she/it)"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύ + ε + ι gives λύει, \"he/she/it looses,\" so the ending ι marks 3rd singular.","greek":"ι"},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdowns, which form uses the connecting vowel ο?","kind":"form","options":["λύει","λύεις","λύετε","λύομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν is λύ + ο + μεν. λύεις, λύει and λύετε all use ε."},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown of the six forms, how many show ε as the connecting vowel?","kind":"concept","options":["Six","Three","Two","One"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύεις, λύει and λύετε use ε. λύομεν uses ο, λύουσι(ν) shows ου, and λύω shows ω from ο."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all loose","I loose","we loose","they loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύομεν is \"we loose.\"","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we loose","he/she/it looses","you all loose","you loose"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύεις is \"you loose\" (one person).","greek":"λύεις"},{"q":"In what order do the three pieces appear in a verb like λύομεν?","kind":"concept","options":["Stem, ending, connecting vowel","Ending, stem, connecting vowel","Stem, connecting vowel, ending","Connecting vowel, ending, stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem comes first, then the connecting vowel, then the ending."},{"q":"Besides their endings, which piece differs between these two forms?","kind":"form","options":["The stem","The connecting vowel","The accent","No other piece"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύομεν is λύ + ο + μεν and λύετε is λύ + ε + τε. The stem is the same, but the connecting vowel differs: ο versus ε.","greek":"λύομεν / λύετε"}],"vb_25":[{"q":"The connecting vowel is always one of which two vowels?","kind":"concept","options":["ω or η","α or ι","υ or ε","ο or ε"],"answer":3,"explain":"The connecting vowel is always either ο or ε."},{"q":"Which connecting vowel goes before endings that start with μ or ν?","kind":"concept","options":["ο","α","ε","ι"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rule: ο before μ and ν endings."},{"q":"Which connecting vowel goes before all other endings (those not starting with μ or ν)?","kind":"concept","options":["ο","ου","ω","ε"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rule: ε before all other endings."},{"q":"What is the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ου","ε","ο","ι"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφεις is γράφ + ε + ις. The ending -ις does not start with μ or ν, so ε is used.","greek":"γράφεις"},{"q":"Which rule explains the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ε before all other endings, since -ι does not start with μ or ν","ο before μ and ν endings","ο lengthens to ω when there is no ending","ν drops out and ο lengthens to ου"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ending -ι does not begin with μ or ν, so the rule calls for ε.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"What happens to produce the ου in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο and ε combine into ου","ε lengthens to ου","The stem changes to λυου-","ν drops out and ο lengthens to ου"],"answer":3,"explain":"The ending begins with ν (-νσι), so ο is used; then ν drops out and ο lengthens to ου.","greek":"λύουσι(ν)"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what ending stands behind the 3rd plural -σι?","kind":"concept","options":["-ις","-τε","-νσι","-μεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson shows the 3rd plural ending as -νσι. Because it starts with ν, the connecting vowel is ο (which then lengthens to ου)."},{"q":"How does the lesson show this word is pronounced?","kind":"sound","options":["GREE-foh","GRAH-foh","GRAY-foo","GROH-fah"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson gives γράφω as GRAH-foh.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I read","I speak","I write","I loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφω means \"I write.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What is the stem of this verb?","kind":"form","options":["γράφ-","γρ-","γράφω-","-ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem of γράφω is γράφ-, which carries the meaning \"write.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What happens to the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο lengthens to ω","ο drops out","ε changes to ο","ε lengthens to ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύω has no ending, and its connecting vowel ο lengthens to ω.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"The lesson says the connecting vowel rule works the same for every thematic verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Every thematic verb follows the same ο/ε pattern."},{"q":"In the comparison table, λύω and γράφω use different connecting vowels.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The connecting vowels are identical; only the stems differ."},{"q":"The lesson says it is a coincidence that λύω and γράφω have the same connecting vowels.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the connecting vowel is the same in every thematic verb, and that this is not a coincidence."},{"q":"According to the lesson, which form of λύω has no ending at all?","kind":"form","options":["λύεις","λύω","λύει","λύετε"],"answer":1,"explain":"1st singular λύω has no ending. Its connecting vowel ο lengthens to ω."},{"q":"In the lesson's comparison of λύω and γράφω, what differs between the two columns?","kind":"concept","options":["The pattern","The connecting vowels","The stems","The endings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stems differ, but the connecting vowels and the pattern are the same."},{"q":"What connecting vowel does the comparison table show in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ε","ο","ω","ου"],"answer":3,"explain":"The table shows γράφ + ου + σι, the same as λύουσι(ν): the ending is really -νσι, so ο is used, then ν drops out and ο lengthens to ου.","greek":"γράφουσι(ν)"},{"q":"Which of these forms must have ε as its connecting vowel?","kind":"form","options":["λύουσι(ν)","γράφει","λύομεν","γράφομεν"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφει ends in -ι, which does not start with μ or ν, so it takes ε. The others take ο (or ου)."},{"q":"Which of these endings calls for the connecting vowel ο?","kind":"concept","options":["-ις","-ι","-νσι","-τε"],"answer":2,"explain":"-νσι starts with ν, so it takes ο. The others do not start with μ or ν, so they take ε."},{"q":"Once you know the connecting vowel rule, what does the lesson say six forms become?","kind":"concept","options":["Random forms","Six exceptions","Two separate systems","One pattern"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rule turns the six forms into one pattern."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the connecting vowel?","kind":"concept","options":["The glue between stem and ending","The core meaning of the verb","The ending of the verb","The subject of the verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"The connecting vowel is the glue that joins the stem to the ending."},{"q":"Which breakdown of γράφετε matches the lesson's table?","kind":"form","options":["γράφε + τ + ε","γράφ + ε + τε","γράφ + ο + τε","γρά + φε + τε"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφετε is stem γράφ, connecting vowel ε, ending τε."},{"q":"Which pair of forms has the same connecting vowel?","kind":"form","options":["λύομεν and γράφεις","λύετε and γράφετε","λύει and γράφομεν","λύεις and λύομεν"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύετε and γράφετε both use ε before -τε. Each other pair mixes ε and ο."},{"q":"In the comparison table, which form is the 3sg of γράφω?","kind":"form","options":["γράφεις","γράφετε","γράφομεν","γράφει"],"answer":3,"explain":"The 3sg row shows γράφ + ε + ι, which is γράφει. The ending -ι takes the connecting vowel ε."},{"q":"What decides whether the connecting vowel is ο or ε?","kind":"concept","options":["The first letter of the stem","Whether the verb is λύω or γράφω","The first letter of the ending","The meaning of the verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"The rule looks at the ending: ο before μ and ν, ε before everything else."},{"q":"In the rule table, how many of the forms take plain ε as the connecting vowel?","kind":"concept","options":["Five","Three","One","Two"],"answer":1,"explain":"ε appears before -ις, -ι and -τε, so three forms take it."},{"q":"Which of the three building blocks does this lesson focus on?","kind":"concept","options":["The connecting vowel","The ending","The accent","The stem"],"answer":0,"explain":"This lesson zooms in on the middle piece, the connecting vowel."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the connecting vowel rule unlock?","kind":"concept","options":["The entire present active indicative system","Greek accent rules","The Greek alphabet","The meaning of every Greek noun"],"answer":0,"explain":"One simple rule unlocks the whole present active indicative system."},{"q":"A learner incorrectly writes λύεμεν for \"we loose.\" What does the rule say is wrong with it?","kind":"form","options":["The ending should be -τε","The ending starts with μ, so the connecting vowel should be ο","The ending starts with μ, so the connecting vowel should be ου","The stem should be γράφ-"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύεμεν is not a correct form. The ending -μεν starts with μ, so the rule calls for ο, giving the correct form λύομεν."},{"q":"Which verb does the lesson compare with λύω to show the rule works the same way?","kind":"concept","options":["λύει","λύετε","γράφω","λύομεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson sets λύω beside γράφω, \"I write,\" and shows the same connecting vowels."}],"vb_26":[{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I write","I hear","I loose","I speak"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἀκούω is the new verb in this lesson, and it means \"I hear.\"","greek":"ἀκούω"},{"q":"In the lesson's pronunciation guide for this verb, which syllable is stressed?","kind":"sound","options":["The first (ah)","The third (oh)","The second (KOO)","No syllable is stressed"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson writes ah-KOO-oh, with the stressed middle syllable in capitals.","greek":"ἀκούω"},{"q":"Which part of this form carries the meaning \"hear\"?","kind":"form","options":["-τε","-ε-","ἀκ-","ἀκου-"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stem ἀκου- carries the meaning \"hear.\" The pieces after it add the grammar.","greek":"ἀκούετε"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form apart?","kind":"form","options":["ἀκου + ε + τε","ἀκ + ουε + τε","ἀκου + ετ + ε","ἀκουε + τ + ε"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson splits it as ἀκου + ε + τε: the stem, then ε, then the ending τε.","greek":"ἀκούετε"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we hear","they hear","I hear","you all hear"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀκούετε is the 2nd person plural form, so it means \"you all hear.\"","greek":"ἀκούετε"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says you can read ἀκούετε even though you have never seen the verb ἀκούω before.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Once you know the system, you can read a brand-new verb by breaking it into its parts."},{"q":"In the lesson's side-by-side comparison of λύω, γράφω, and ἀκούω, what changes from verb to verb?","kind":"concept","options":["The endings","The stems","The person labels","The number of forms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each verb has its own stem, but all three share the same endings."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says this pattern of endings works only for λύω, γράφω, and ἀκούω.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says to learn the system once and apply it to every thematic verb you meet."},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["1st person plural","2nd person plural","3rd person plural","1st person singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφομεν sits in the 1pl row of the table, alongside λύομεν and ἀκούομεν.","greek":"γράφομεν"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["1st person singular","3rd person singular","2nd person singular","2nd person plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύεις is in the 2sg row of the table, with γράφεις and ἀκούεις.","greek":"λύεις"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person plural","1st person plural","2nd person singular","3rd person singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀκούει is in the 3sg row of the table, just like λύει and γράφει.","greek":"ἀκούει"},{"q":"Which form of γράφω is 2nd person plural?","kind":"form","options":["γράφομεν","γράφετε","γράφεις","γράφουσι"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφετε is the 2pl form. It matches λύετε and ἀκούετε in the same row."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we write","you all write","they write","he writes"],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφουσι is the 3pl form of γράφω (write), so it means \"they write.\"","greek":"γράφουσι"},{"q":"ἀκούετε means \"you all hear.\" Following the same pattern, what does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all loose","we loose","they loose","I loose"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύετε has the same 2pl ending as ἀκούετε, but the stem of λύω (loose), so it means \"you all loose.\"","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"Which ending do λύει, γράφει, and ἀκούει all share?","kind":"form","options":["-εις","-ει","-ετε","-ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"All three 3sg forms end in -ει. Only the stem in front of it changes."},{"q":"Which ending marks the 1st person plural in all three verbs of the comparison?","kind":"form","options":["-ετε","-ουσι","-ομεν","-εις"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύομεν, γράφομεν, and ἀκούομεν all end in -ομεν, the 1pl ending."},{"q":"Which ending marks the 3rd person plural in all three verbs?","kind":"form","options":["-ομεν","-ει","-ετε","-ουσι"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύουσι, γράφουσι, and ἀκούουσι all end in -ουσι, the 3pl ending."},{"q":"Which ending appears on every 2nd person singular form in the table?","kind":"form","options":["-εις","-ει","-ομεν","-ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύεις, γράφεις, and ἀκούεις all end in -εις, the 2sg ending."},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person singular","1st person singular","1st person plural","2nd person singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφω is in the 1sg row, alongside λύω and ἀκούω.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a form of ἀκούω?","kind":"form","options":["ἀκούεις","ἀκούομεν","γράφεις","ἀκούουσι"],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφεις has the stem of γράφω, not ἀκου-. The other three all begin with the stem ἀκου-."},{"q":"Which pair shares the same person and number?","kind":"form","options":["λύεις / ἀκούει","γράφετε / γράφουσι","λύει / γράφεις","λύομεν / ἀκούομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν and ἀκούομεν are both 1pl: different stems, same -ομεν ending."},{"q":"Which form is the odd one out in person and number?","kind":"form","options":["ἀκούεις","λύετε","γράφετε","ἀκούετε"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύετε, γράφετε, and ἀκούετε are all 2pl. ἀκούεις is 2sg."},{"q":"Which form means \"we loose\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["λύετε","λύουσι","λύομεν","ἀκούομεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύομεν puts the 1pl ending -ομεν on the stem of λύω (loose). ἀκούομεν has the right ending but the wrong stem."},{"q":"What do these three forms have in common?","kind":"form","options":["They are all plural","They are all singular","They are all 2nd person","They all have different stems"],"answer":0,"explain":"All three come from the plural rows of the table (3pl, 2pl, 1pl), and all share the stem ἀκου-.","greek":"ἀκούουσι · ἀκούετε · ἀκούομεν"},{"q":"Why can you read ἀκούετε without ever having seen ἀκούω before?","kind":"concept","options":["It has no ending at all","Its stem is the same as the stem of λύω","It is an irregular verb","Its endings follow the same system as λύω and γράφω"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀκούω uses the same endings as λύω and γράφω. Once you know the system, only the stem is new."},{"q":"How many verbs does the lesson compare side by side?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lines up three verbs, λύω, γράφω, and ἀκούω, to show that their endings match."},{"q":"What meaning does the comparison table give for this verb?","kind":"meaning","options":["write","loose","hear","see"],"answer":1,"explain":"The table heads the λύω column with the meaning \"loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"How do these two forms differ?","kind":"form","options":["In their person","In their number","Only in their stems","Only in their endings"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both are 2pl with the ending -ετε. Only the stems (hear vs. write) are different.","greek":"ἀκούετε · γράφετε"},{"q":"Which form completes the pattern?","kind":"form","options":["ἀκούεις","ἀκούει","ἀκούετε","ἀκούω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pattern is the 2sg row, so the matching form of ἀκούω is ἀκούεις. ἀκούει, without the final ς, is 3sg.","greek":"λύεις · γράφεις · ___"},{"q":"True or false: in the table, the 2nd person singular of γράφω is γράφει.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The 2sg form is γράφεις. γράφει, without the final ς, is 3sg."}],"vb_08":[{"q":"According to the lesson, Greek words are built from which two parts?","kind":"concept","options":["A stem and an ending","A root and an article","A prefix and a suffix","A subject and an object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek words have a stem, which carries the core meaning, and an ending, which carries the grammar."},{"q":"Which part of a Greek verb carries its core meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending","The stem","The accent","The breathing mark"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem holds the core idea of the verb, such as write, speak or loose."},{"q":"Which part of a Greek verb carries grammatical information such as person and number?","kind":"concept","options":["The stem","The first letter","The ending","The accent"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ending carries the grammar: person, number, tense, voice and mood."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT carried by the ending, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Voice","Mood","Core meaning"],"answer":3,"explain":"Core meaning belongs to the stem. The ending carries person, number, tense, voice and mood."},{"q":"In these three forms, which part stays the same?","kind":"form","options":["-ω","γράφ-","-εις","-ει"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem γράφ- is the same in all three forms. Only the endings -ω, -εις and -ει change.","greek":"γράφω · γράφεις · γράφει"},{"q":"What idea does this stem carry?","kind":"meaning","options":["Loosing","Speaking","Writing","Hearing"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem γράφ- carries the idea of writing.","greek":"γράφ-"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I write","you write","he/she writes","they write"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem γράφ- means write, and the ending -εις tells you the subject is \"you.\"","greek":"γράφεις"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you write","I write","he/she writes","we write"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem γράφ- means write, and the ending -ει points to \"he\" or \"she.\"","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I hear","I write","I speak","I loose/destroy"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson gives λύω the meaning \"I loose/destroy.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"The lesson says the stem tells you \"what.\" What does the ending tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["Who, when, and how","Only the meaning","Only the spelling","Where the word came from"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem gives the basic idea, and the ending supplies who is acting, when, and how."},{"q":"Across most present active indicative forms, what happens to the stem?","kind":"concept","options":["It changes completely","It remains the same","It drops its last letter","It moves to the end of the word"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem stays steady in most present active indicative forms. Only the ending changes."},{"q":"In this series of forms, what does the changing ending communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["What is being written","Where the writing happens","Who is writing","How long the writing lasts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The meaning \"write\" stays the same. The ending shifts to show who is doing the writing.","greek":"γράφω · γράφεις · γράφει"},{"q":"Once you know a verb's stem and its meaning, what tells you everything else?","kind":"concept","options":["The accents","The word order","The article","The endings"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stem gives you the meaning, and the endings tell you everything else."},{"q":"How widely does the stem-and-ending pattern repeat, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Across thousands of Greek words","Only in the verb γράφω","In about a dozen verbs","Only in nouns"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the pattern repeats across thousands of Greek words, which is why it is worth learning well."},{"q":"What does the lesson name as one of the key habits of a good Greek reader?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every accent rule","Recognizing a stem quickly","Translating word for word","Reading only nouns first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spotting the stem quickly lets you grasp a verb's meaning right away."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson calls seeing the division between stem and ending one of the most useful skills in Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson opens by saying that learning to see this division is one of the most useful skills in Greek."},{"q":"Which is the correct stem-and-ending division of this form?","kind":"form","options":["γρά- + -φεις","γράφε- + -ις","γράφ- + -εις","γ- + -ράφεις"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem γράφ- stays the same, and -εις is the ending, just as the lesson writes it: γράφ-εις.","greek":"γράφεις"},{"q":"The lesson labels the ending -ω of γράφω as \"first person singular present active indicative.\" Which words in that label give the person and number?","kind":"concept","options":["present active","active indicative","present indicative","first person singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"First person\" is the person and \"singular\" is the number. The other words name other categories the ending carries."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the core meanings the lesson gives as an example of what a stem carries?","kind":"concept","options":["speak","hear","see","believe"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's examples of core meanings are write, speak and loose."},{"q":"True or false: in γράφω, the ending -ω is what carries the meaning \"write.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The idea of writing is in the stem γράφ-. The ending -ω carries grammatical information."},{"q":"A reader recognizes the stem γράφ- in a form but does not yet know its ending. What can the reader still tell?","kind":"concept","options":["Who is doing the action","When the action happens","That the verb is about writing","The mood of the verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem alone gives the meaning. Who, when and how come from the ending."},{"q":"Which pair shows the same stem with different endings?","kind":"form","options":["γράφω / λύω","λύω / γράφει","γράφεις / λύω","γράφω / γράφει"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφω and γράφει both have the stem γράφ-. Only their endings, -ω and -ει, differ."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson claims the stem stays exactly the same in every Greek verb form, without exception.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson is careful to say \"most present active indicative forms,\" not every form."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I write","you write","he/she writes","we write"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφ- means write, and the ending -ω marks \"I,\" so γράφω is \"I write.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"In the lesson's notation, what does the hyphen in this form show?","kind":"form","options":["Where the accent falls","Where the stem ends and the ending begins","A missing letter","A pause in pronunciation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson writes γράφ-ω to separate the stem γράφ- from the ending -ω.","greek":"γράφ-ω"},{"q":"Which change turns this form (\"I write\") into \"you write\"?","kind":"form","options":["Replace the stem γράφ- with λύ-","Add -ει after γράφω","Replace the ending -ω with -εις","Remove the stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"Keep the stem γράφ- and swap the ending: γράφ-ω becomes γράφ-εις, \"you write.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Which pair shows two different stems with the same ending?","kind":"form","options":["γράφω / γράφει","γράφεις / γράφει","γράφω / γράφεις","γράφω / λύω"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφω and λύω both end in -ω, but their stems differ: γράφ- (write) and λύ- (loose/destroy)."},{"q":"Which statement best matches the lesson's view of a Greek verb?","kind":"concept","options":["The stem gives the meaning; the ending gives person, number, tense, voice and mood","The ending gives the meaning; the stem gives person and number","Both parts give only the meaning","The stem changes to show each person"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is the lesson's division of labor: the stem carries the core meaning, and the ending carries the grammar."},{"q":"How many kinds of grammatical information does the lesson list for the ending?","kind":"concept","options":["Five","Two","Three","Seven"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists five: person, number, tense, voice and mood."},{"q":"True or false: in γράφει, the stem shows that the action is writing, and the ending shows that he or she is the one doing it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The stem γράφ- gives \"write,\" and the ending -ει tells you who: he or she."}],"vb_09":[{"q":"According to the lesson, what does voice describe?","kind":"concept","options":["The relationship between the subject and the action","The time when the action happens","How many people are involved","Whether the sentence is a question"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voice is about how the subject relates to the action, for example whether the subject is the one doing it."},{"q":"What does the lesson call a subject that is the one doing something?","kind":"concept","options":["The object","The agent","The receiver","The modifier"],"answer":1,"explain":"In active voice the subject is the agent: the one doing something."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"The author writes,\" what is the author doing?","kind":"concept","options":["Receiving the action","Watching the action","Performing the action","Stopping the action"],"answer":2,"explain":"The author is the subject and is the one doing the writing, so this is active voice."},{"q":"In the lesson's summary of active voice, what is the subject compared to?","kind":"concept","options":["A passenger","A road","A map","A driver"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson pictures the subject as the driver: it drives the action."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe active voice?","kind":"concept","options":["The rarest voice","The most straightforward voice","A voice used only in poetry","A voice with no subject"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active voice is the most straightforward because the subject simply performs the action."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, English uses active voice in most sentences.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. English defaults to active voice in most sentences, which is why it feels natural."},{"q":"How does Greek active voice compare with English active voice?","kind":"concept","options":["It works the same way","It works in reverse","It has no subject","It is used only in questions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says Greek active voice works the same way as English: the subject performs the action."},{"q":"This form comes from which verb?","kind":"form","options":["λύω","ἀκούω","γράφω","λέγω"],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφει is a form of γράφω, the verb for writing.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"What person and number does the lesson give for this form?","kind":"form","options":["1st person singular","3rd person singular","2nd person singular","3rd person plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson identifies γράφει as third person singular, which is why it means \"he\" or \"she\" writes.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"The lesson parses this form as \"third person singular present active indicative.\" Which word in that label names its voice?","kind":"form","options":["present","indicative","singular","active"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Active\" is the voice. It tells you the subject is performing the writing.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"What does the active voice of this form tell you about its subject?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject performs the writing","The subject receives the writing","The writing is only planned","No one is writing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because γράφει is active, its subject is the one doing the writing.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"Why does Greek not need a separate word like \"he\" or \"she\" with γράφει?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek has no words for he or she","The stem shows who is acting","The verb ending shows the subject is third person singular","Word order shows the subject"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ending of γράφει already tells you the subject is third person singular, so no extra word is required."},{"q":"Which English sentence has the same voice as γράφει?","kind":"concept","options":["The letter was written.","The message is heard.","The author writes.","The student was taught."],"answer":2,"explain":"γράφει is active, and in \"The author writes\" the subject performs the action, so it is active too."},{"q":"What do the lesson's three examples, \"The teacher teaches,\" \"The student learns,\" and \"The author writes,\" have in common?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject is the one doing something","The subject receives the action","Each one is a command","The sentences have no subject"],"answer":0,"explain":"In each sentence the subject is the agent, the one doing something. That is what makes them active."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the active voice of γράφει tells you exactly who the subject is by name.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Active voice tells you the subject, \"whoever he or she is,\" performs the writing. It does not name the person."},{"q":"In the sentence \"The student writes a letter,\" who is the agent?","kind":"concept","options":["The letter","The student","The writing","The reader"],"answer":1,"explain":"The student is the subject doing the writing, so the student is the agent. The letter is what gets written."},{"q":"The lesson sums up active voice as \"subject → action.\" What does the arrow show?","kind":"concept","options":["The action happens to the subject","The action has no subject","The subject performs the action","The action is only planned"],"answer":2,"explain":"The arrow runs from the subject to the action: the subject is the driver, the one performing the action."},{"q":"A learner translates γράφει as \"They write.\" What is wrong with that translation?","kind":"meaning","options":["Nothing is wrong with it","γράφει is first person, so it should be \"I write\"","γράφει is not a form of γράφω","γράφει is third person singular, so it should be \"He writes\" or \"She writes\""],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφει is singular, not plural. The lesson translates it \"He writes\" or \"She writes.\"","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson parses γράφει as a present tense form.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson calls it third person singular present active indicative."},{"q":"This lesson teaches you to recognize active voice in which language or languages?","kind":"concept","options":["Only English","Only Greek","Both English and Greek","Latin and Greek"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson starts with English examples and then shows active voice in the Greek form γράφει."},{"q":"Which phrase does the lesson use to describe how γράφει works without a separate subject word?","kind":"concept","options":["The word does all the work.","The word is incomplete.","The word needs a helper.","The word has lost its subject."],"answer":0,"explain":"The ending carries the subject information, so the single word does all the work.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"The lesson translates γράφει as \"He writes\" or \"She writes.\" Why can it be translated either way?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending shows a third person singular subject, which can be he or she","γράφει is plural, so it covers more than one person","The stem γράφ- changes for he and she","Greek has no third person forms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ending tells you only that the subject is third person singular. The lesson leaves it open as \"whoever he or she is,\" so \"He writes\" and \"She writes\" both fit.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson's parsing of γράφει includes the word \"indicative.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson parses γράφει as third person singular present active indicative of γράφω.","greek":"γράφει"},{"q":"Which verb does NOT appear in the lesson's three English examples of active voice?","kind":"concept","options":["teaches","learns","writes","speaks"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's examples are \"The teacher teaches,\" \"The student learns,\" and \"The author writes.\" \"Speaks\" is not one of them."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"The teacher teaches,\" which words are the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["teaches","The teacher","the lesson","the student"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"The teacher\" is the subject, and it is the one doing the teaching."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"The author writes,\" which word names the action?","kind":"concept","options":["author","writes","teacher","student"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Writes\" is the action. \"The author\" is the subject performing it."},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's example \"The student learns,\" the student receives the action instead of performing it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The student is the subject doing the learning, so the student performs the action. That is active voice."},{"q":"Which question helps you decide whether a verb is in active voice?","kind":"concept","options":["When does the action happen?","How many people are acting?","Is the sentence a question?","Is the subject performing the action?"],"answer":3,"explain":"Voice is about the relationship between the subject and the action. In active voice, the subject performs the action."},{"q":"Which Greek form does the lesson translate as \"She writes\"?","kind":"form","options":["γράφω","γράφει","γράφεις","γράφομεν"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφει is third person singular, so the lesson translates it \"He writes\" or \"She writes.\" The ending alone supplies the subject."},{"q":"Which description of γράφει matches the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["First person singular active: I perform the writing","Third person plural active: they perform the writing","Third person singular active: he or she receives the writing","Third person singular active: he or she performs the writing"],"answer":3,"explain":"γράφει is third person singular and active, so he or she is the one doing the writing.","greek":"γράφει"}],"vb_10":[{"q":"Besides active voice, which two voices does Greek have?","kind":"concept","options":["Passive and middle","Indicative and imperative","Present and future","Singular and plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek has three voices: active, passive and middle. Each describes a different relationship between the subject and the action."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"The letter was written,\" what is the letter doing?","kind":"concept","options":["Performing the writing","Receiving the writing","Watching the writing","Stopping the writing"],"answer":1,"explain":"The letter does not write anything. It receives the writing, which makes the sentence passive."},{"q":"In English, which helper words combine with a past participle to form the passive, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["will, shall, or may","do, does, or did","was, is, or were","has, have, or had"],"answer":2,"explain":"English builds the passive with \"was,\" \"is,\" or \"were\" plus a past participle, as in \"was written.\""},{"q":"Which sentence is in passive voice?","kind":"concept","options":["The student teaches.","The author writes.","The student learns.","The student was taught."],"answer":3,"explain":"In \"The student was taught,\" the student receives the teaching. In the others, the subject performs the action."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says passive voice is common throughout the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Greek passive voice expresses the same idea as English passive, and it is common throughout the NT."},{"q":"True or false: the middle voice has a direct English equivalent.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the middle voice does not have a direct English equivalent."},{"q":"Which of these is one way the lesson says the middle voice can be used?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject is left out of the sentence","The subject acts on or for themselves","The action is a command","The action is in the future"],"answer":1,"explain":"One use of the middle is the subject acting on or for themselves, though that is not its only use."},{"q":"Besides acting on or for oneself, what else can the middle voice indicate, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A completed past action","A command","Special interest or participation","A wish"],"answer":2,"explain":"The middle can show the subject's special interest or participation in the action."},{"q":"True or false: the middle voice always means \"the subject does the action to himself.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson warns against oversimplifying: the middle is not always \"subject does action to himself.\""},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the precise nuance of the middle voice sometimes depend on?","kind":"concept","options":["The context","The accent","The first letter","The length of the word"],"answer":0,"explain":"The middle has several uses, and the surrounding context often shows which nuance is meant."},{"q":"Where does the lesson say the middle voice will be treated in more depth?","kind":"concept","options":["In the very next lesson","Nowhere in the course","In the vocabulary lists","In the Advanced Track"],"answer":3,"explain":"This lesson only introduces the middle. It gets deeper treatment in the Advanced Track."},{"q":"These present forms can be read as middle or passive. What do the middle and passive share in the present tense?","kind":"concept","options":["Nothing; they look completely different","The same set of endings","The same meaning in every sentence","An augment at the front"],"answer":1,"explain":"In the present tense, middle and passive use one set of endings.","greek":"λύομαι · λύῃ · λύεται"},{"q":"This present form could be read as middle or passive. How does the lesson say you decide which?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending tells you","Middle forms are always singular","Context tells you","Passive forms are always plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"The endings are identical, so the surrounding context tells you whether to read the form as middle or passive.","greek":"λύομαι"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["1st singular","2nd singular","3rd singular","1st plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύομαι is the 1st singular present middle/passive of λύω.","greek":"λύομαι"},{"q":"What basic translation does the lesson give for this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["they are being loosed","we are being loosed","I am being loosed","he/she/it is being loosed"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύεται is 3rd singular, so the lesson translates it \"he/she/it is being loosed.\"","greek":"λύεται"},{"q":"What basic translation does the lesson give for this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all are being loosed","they are being loosed","he/she/it is being loosed","we are being loosed"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύονται is 3rd plural: \"they are being loosed.\"","greek":"λύονται"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["2nd plural","3rd plural","1st plural","1st singular"],"answer":2,"explain":"λυόμεθα is the 1st plural present middle/passive: \"we are being loosed.\"","greek":"λυόμεθα"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson translates this form \"you all are being loosed.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. λύεσθε is the 2nd plural present middle/passive of λύω.","greek":"λύεσθε"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd singular","1st singular","3rd plural","2nd singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύῃ is the 2nd singular present middle/passive: \"you are being loosed / you loose for yourself.\"","greek":"λύῃ"},{"q":"The lesson gives two translations for this form. Which pair is it?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose / I am loosing","I am being loosed / I loose for myself","I was loosed / I loosed","I will loose / I will be loosed"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύομαι can be passive (\"I am being loosed\") or middle (\"I loose for myself\"), since both share one set of endings.","greek":"λύομαι"},{"q":"Which of the lesson's translations of this form reads it as middle rather than passive?","kind":"meaning","options":["you are being loosed","you all are being loosed","you loose for yourself","they are being loosed"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"You loose for yourself\" shows the subject acting for themselves, a middle use. \"You are being loosed\" is the passive reading.","greek":"λύῃ"},{"q":"Which form is the odd one out in number?","kind":"form","options":["λύομαι","λύῃ","λύεται","λύονται"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομαι, λύῃ and λύεται are all singular. λύονται is 3rd plural."},{"q":"Besides λύομαι, which form in the lesson's table is given both a passive and a middle translation?","kind":"form","options":["λύῃ","λύεται","λυόμεθα","λύονται"],"answer":0,"explain":"The table gives λύῃ two translations, \"you are being loosed\" and \"you loose for yourself.\" The other rows list only the passive reading, though context can make any of these forms middle."},{"q":"Which is the correct spelling of the 2nd singular present middle/passive of λύω, as shown in the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["λύη","λύῃ","λυῃ","λυῇ"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson spells it λύῃ, with an accent on the υ and an iota subscript under the η."},{"q":"Which of the lesson's passive examples uses \"is\" rather than \"was\"?","kind":"concept","options":["The letter was written.","The student was taught.","The message is heard.","The teacher teaches."],"answer":2,"explain":"\"The message is heard\" uses \"is\" plus a past participle. The message receives the hearing."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does understanding even the basics of the middle and passive open up?","kind":"concept","options":["A large portion of the Greek New Testament","Only a few rare verbs","Only Classical poetry","Nothing new beyond active voice"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both voices are frequent, so even basic understanding opens up a large portion of the Greek NT."},{"q":"For now, what does the lesson ask you to do with the middle voice?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorize every one of its nuances","Recognize that it exists and shows a different kind of subject involvement","Always translate it as reflexive","Treat it exactly like the active"],"answer":1,"explain":"At this stage the goal is simply to recognize the middle and know it shows a different kind of subject involvement than the active."},{"q":"Which pair of forms are both 2nd person?","kind":"form","options":["λύῃ / λύεται","λύομαι / λύεσθε","λύονται / λυόμεθα","λύῃ / λύεσθε"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύῃ is 2nd singular and λύεσθε is 2nd plural."},{"q":"How does the passive differ from the middle, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["In the passive the subject receives the action; in the middle the subject is specially involved in it","In the passive the subject acts; in the middle no one acts","The passive is always past; the middle is always present","They differ only in English, not in Greek"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive means the subject is acted upon. Middle means the subject is involved in or connected to the action in a special way."},{"q":"Which sentence is passive, formed with \"were\" plus a past participle?","kind":"concept","options":["The teacher was teaching.","The students write letters.","The letters were written.","The authors will write."],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Were written\" is \"were\" plus a past participle, and the letters receive the action. \"Was teaching\" has a helper word, but the teacher is still the one doing the action."}],"vb_11":[{"q":"Which mood does the lesson call by far the most common in the New Testament?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Subjunctive","Imperative","Optative"],"answer":0,"explain":"The indicative is by far the most common mood in the NT, which is why it comes first."},{"q":"How is the action presented in the lesson's example \"They traveled\"?","kind":"concept","options":["As a command","As having happened","As a wish","As a remote possibility"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"They traveled\" asserts the journey as something that actually happened. That is the indicative."},{"q":"How is the statement presented in the lesson's example \"We believe\"?","kind":"concept","options":["As a command","As a question","As true","As a contingency"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"We believe\" is stated as true, so it is an indicative statement."},{"q":"Which mood communicates a command?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Subjunctive","Optative","Imperative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The imperative is the mood of command."},{"q":"Which mood does the lesson tell you to master first?","kind":"concept","options":["The optative","The indicative","The imperative","The subjunctive"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indicative is the mood of statements. Master it first, and the others are easier to grasp by contrast."},{"q":"Which mood communicates a wish?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Indicative","Optative","Subjunctive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The optative expresses a wish or a remote possibility."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the optative can communicate a remote possibility.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson describes the optative as expressing a wish or a remote possibility."},{"q":"When no special mood is signaled, which mood does Greek default to?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Optative","Imperative","Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The indicative is the default. Greek uses it unless a special mood is signaled."},{"q":"Why does the lesson call the indicative the natural starting point for learning verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Most verbs you meet in early Greek reading will be indicative","It has no endings to learn","It is the only mood found in the NT","It is used only for commands"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since most verbs in early reading are indicative, learning it first pays off right away."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how do the other moods become easier to understand?","kind":"concept","options":["By memorizing them before the indicative","By contrast with the indicative","By ignoring the context","By treating them all as commands"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once you master the indicative, you can see the other moods by how they differ from it."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["The mood of commands","The mood of wishes","The mood of statements","The mood of possibility"],"answer":2,"explain":"The indicative is the mood of statements. Commands, wishes and possibilities belong to the other moods."},{"q":"In each of the lesson's indicative examples, what is the speaker doing?","kind":"concept","options":["Giving an order","Expressing a wish","Asking permission","Putting forward a claim or presentation of reality"],"answer":3,"explain":"With the indicative, the speaker puts something forward as a claim about reality."},{"q":"Which meaning from the lesson's list of moods does the sentence \"Believe!\" express?","kind":"concept","options":["Command","Assertion","Wish","Contingency"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Believe!\" tells someone to do something, so it is a command, the job of the imperative."},{"q":"A sentence simply states \"They believe,\" with no special mood signaled. Which mood would Greek use for it?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Indicative","Optative","Subjunctive"],"answer":1,"explain":"A plain statement with no special mood signaled defaults to the indicative."},{"q":"Which pairing of mood and meaning is INCORRECT according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative: assertion","Optative: wish","Imperative: wish","Subjunctive: possibility or contingency"],"answer":2,"explain":"The imperative is for command, not wish. Wish belongs to the optative."},{"q":"True or false: all three of the lesson's English examples, \"He speaks,\" \"They traveled,\" and \"We believe,\" are indicative statements.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Each one asserts something as real: presented as real, as having happened, or as true."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, what does understanding the indicative give you?","kind":"concept","options":["Mastery of every Greek mood at once","The ability to skip the other moods","Knowledge of every NT verb","A strong foundation for reading Greek sentences"],"answer":3,"explain":"Knowing what the indicative communicates gives you a strong base for reading Greek sentences."},{"q":"Which two moods does the lesson connect with some kind of possibility?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive and optative","Indicative and imperative","Imperative and optative","Indicative and subjunctive"],"answer":0,"explain":"The subjunctive expresses possibility or contingency, and the optative expresses wish or remote possibility."},{"q":"Which meaning from the lesson's list of moods does the sentence \"Oh, that they would come!\" express?","kind":"concept","options":["Command","Wish","Assertion","Contingency"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Oh, that they would come!\" expresses a wish, which the lesson links to the optative."},{"q":"Which meaning from the lesson's list of moods does the sentence \"The student learns\" express?","kind":"concept","options":["Command","Wish","Assertion","Contingency"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"The student learns\" presents something as real, so it is an assertion, the job of the indicative."},{"q":"A writer wants to present an event as something that really happened. Which mood would the lesson lead you to expect?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Optative","Subjunctive","Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Presenting something as real is exactly what the indicative does."},{"q":"Which statement best captures the difference between the indicative and the imperative?","kind":"concept","options":["The indicative asserts; the imperative commands","The indicative commands; the imperative asserts","Both express wishes","Both express contingency"],"answer":0,"explain":"The indicative presents something as real, while the imperative gives a command."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the other moods the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Imperative","Infinitive","Optative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists the subjunctive, imperative and optative as the other moods. The infinitive is not on that list."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson describes the subjunctive as communicating a command.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The subjunctive communicates possibility or contingency. Command belongs to the imperative."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"He speaks,\" how is the action presented?","kind":"concept","options":["As a command","As real","As a wish","As a contingency"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"He speaks\" puts the action forward as real. That is what the indicative does."},{"q":"Which meaning from the lesson's list of moods does the sentence \"The crowd was gathering\" express?","kind":"concept","options":["Wish","Command","Contingency","Assertion"],"answer":3,"explain":"Even though it describes ongoing past action, it still presents something as real, so it is an assertion."},{"q":"True or false: besides the indicative, the lesson names only two other moods.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson names three other moods: the subjunctive, the imperative and the optative."},{"q":"What does the subjunctive communicate, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Command","Possibility or contingency","Assertion of fact","Wish"],"answer":1,"explain":"The subjunctive deals with possibility or contingency rather than plain statement."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the indicative is the default mood for every Greek statement, without exception.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the indicative is the default for most Greek statements, used when no special mood is signaled."},{"q":"Which statement matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The imperative is the default mood","All four moods communicate assertion","The optative is the most common mood in the NT","The indicative is the default, and the other moods communicate different things"],"answer":3,"explain":"The indicative is the default mood of statements, while the subjunctive, imperative and optative each communicate something different."}],"vb_12":[{"q":"In what kind of sections does the imperfect tend to appear?","kind":"concept","options":["Narrative sections","Lists of names","Greetings only","Commands to the reader"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfect is at home in narrative, where it shows action unfolding in the past."},{"q":"Which person and number is this imperfect form?","kind":"form","options":["1st singular","2nd singular","3rd singular","2nd plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυες is the 2nd singular imperfect of λύω: \"you were loosing.\"","greek":"ἔλυες"},{"q":"What does this imperfect form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all were loosing","they were loosing","we were loosing","I was loosing"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐλύομεν is the 1st plural imperfect: \"we were loosing.\"","greek":"ἐλύομεν"},{"q":"Which person and number is this imperfect form?","kind":"form","options":["1st plural","3rd plural","2nd singular","2nd plural"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐλύετε is the 2nd plural imperfect: \"you all were loosing.\"","greek":"ἐλύετε"},{"q":"What does this imperfect form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["he/she/it was loosing","I was loosing","you were loosing","we were loosing"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔλυε(ν) is the 3rd singular imperfect: \"he/she/it was loosing.\"","greek":"ἔλυε(ν)"},{"q":"Which prefix does the lesson call the augment?","kind":"form","options":["σ-","ε-","λυ-","-ον"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment is the ε- added to the front of the verb, as in ἔλυον."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, aorist indicative forms carry an augment too.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says every imperfect and aorist indicative form carries an augment."},{"q":"How does the imperfect portray an action at that point in the past?","kind":"concept","options":["As a simple whole","As a future event","As unfinished or in process","As a command"],"answer":2,"explain":"The imperfect shows the action as still in process at that point in the past."},{"q":"A writer wants to present an action simply as a whole rather than as unfolding. Which tense does the lesson say tends to do that?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfect","Present","Future","Aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist tends to present action as a simple whole, while the imperfect lingers on the action in motion."},{"q":"Why do Greek writers often use the imperfect in narrative, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["To create a sense of movement and scene development","To give commands","To list names","To mark events still to come"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfect helps a story feel like it is moving, building up the scene."},{"q":"In a story, what kind of detail is the imperfect often used to describe?","kind":"concept","options":["The title of the book","What was happening in the background","A command to the reader","An event still to come"],"answer":1,"explain":"Background activity, such as a crowd gathering, is often told with the imperfect."},{"q":"Which two persons share this same imperfect form in the lesson's table?","kind":"form","options":["1st singular and 1st plural","2nd singular and 3rd singular","1st singular and 3rd plural","2nd plural and 3rd plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυον appears twice in the table: as \"I was loosing\" (1st singular) and as \"they were loosing\" (3rd plural).","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"Why is this form NOT in the lesson's imperfect table?","kind":"form","options":["It has a different stem","It is singular","It ends in -ετε","It has no augment at the front"],"answer":3,"explain":"Every imperfect form starts with the augment ε-. λύομεν has the same stem and ending as ἐλύομεν, but no augment.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, only some imperfect forms start with the augment.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says every imperfect form starts with the augment ε-."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the imperfect in its introduction?","kind":"concept","options":["As one of the most vivid tenses in the Greek NT","As the rarest tense in the NT","As a tense used only for commands","As a kind of future tense"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls the imperfect one of the most vivid tenses, since it shows action in motion."},{"q":"Besides ongoing action, which other kind of past action does the introduction say the imperfect can communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Commanded action","Repeated action","Planned future action","Wished-for action"],"answer":1,"explain":"The introduction says the imperfect often shows past action as ongoing, repeated, or unfolding."},{"q":"According to the lesson, does the imperfect simply say that something happened?","kind":"concept","options":["Yes, that is all it does","Only in the plural","No, it shows the action unfolding","Only with the verb λύω"],"answer":2,"explain":"The imperfect goes beyond reporting an event. It shows the action unfolding."},{"q":"A narrator says people were coming and going while the main event took place. Which tense would the lesson lead you to expect for \"were coming and going\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Future","Present","Imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"Background action in a story, like people coming and going, is often told with the imperfect."},{"q":"What do all of these forms have at the front?","kind":"form","options":["The augment ε-","A σ before the stem","The ending -ομεν","No prefix at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each begins with the augment ε-, which marks them as past-time forms.","greek":"ἔλυον · ἔλυες · ἐλύετε"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the imperfect do that the aorist does not?","kind":"concept","options":["It presents the action as a simple whole","It lingers and shows the action in motion","It points to an action still to come","It gives a command"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist tends to present action as a simple whole, while the imperfect lingers and shows the action in motion."},{"q":"In the lesson's English examples, what does \"He was teaching\" show?","kind":"concept","options":["A command","A future plan","Ongoing action in the past","An action presented as a simple whole"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson labels \"He was teaching\" as ongoing in the past, like the Greek imperfect."},{"q":"Which verb does the lesson use to show the full imperfect paradigm?","kind":"concept","options":["γράφω","ἀκούω","λέγω","λύω"],"answer":3,"explain":"The table shows the imperfect of λύω (I loose), from ἔλυον to ἔλυον."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the difference between the imperfect and the aorist matters for reading narrative with precision.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Seeing whether an action is unfolding or presented whole helps you read a story precisely."},{"q":"Which set lists only plural forms from the imperfect table?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυον, ἔλυες, ἔλυε(ν)","ἐλύομεν, ἐλύετε, ἔλυον","ἐλύομεν, ἔλυες, ἔλυον","ἔλυε(ν), ἐλύετε, ἔλυον"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐλύομεν (we), ἐλύετε (you all) and ἔλυον (they) are the plural rows. ἔλυες and ἔλυε(ν) are singular."},{"q":"A story first says the crowd was gathering, then reports a single event as a simple whole. Which tenses fit, in that order?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist, then imperfect","Future, then imperfect","Imperfect, then aorist","Imperfect, then future"],"answer":2,"explain":"The background (\"was gathering\") suits the imperfect. An event presented as a whole suits the aorist."},{"q":"Which form completes the singular rows of the table?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύετε","ἐλύομεν","ἔλυον","ἔλυε(ν)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The singular rows run ἔλυον (I), ἔλυες (you), ἔλυε(ν) (he/she/it).","greek":"ἔλυον · ἔλυες · ___"},{"q":"Which form is also 2nd person, but plural?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύετε","ἔλυε(ν)","ἐλύομεν","ἔλυον"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔλυες is 2nd singular (\"you were loosing\"), and ἐλύετε is 2nd plural (\"you all were loosing\").","greek":"ἔλυες"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the ε- at the front of this form signal?","kind":"concept","options":["The person of the subject","Past time","A command","Future time"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment is the past-time signal at the front of the verb.","greek":"ἐλύομεν"},{"q":"True or false: the imperfect can only describe a single, one-time past action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The imperfect often shows past action as ongoing, repeated, or unfolding."},{"q":"Which spelling matches the 1st plural imperfect in the lesson's table, with the correct breathing and accent?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυομεν","ελύομεν","ἐλύομεν","ἐλυόμεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson writes ἐλύομεν: smooth breathing on the augment and the accent on the υ."}],"vb_27":[{"q":"What kind of addition is the augment?","kind":"concept","options":["An ending added to the back of a verb","A prefix added to the front of a verb","A letter inserted between stem and ending","An accent mark over the stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment is a prefix: it goes on the front of the verb."},{"q":"In which mood does the augment signal past time, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Subjunctive","Indicative","Optative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson defines the augment as a past-time signal in the indicative mood."},{"q":"For most verbs, what syllable is the augment?","kind":"form","options":["ε-","σ-","ο-","ν-"],"answer":0,"explain":"For most verbs the augment is ε-, added before the stem."},{"q":"What does this stem mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["write","hear","speak","loose/release"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stem λυ- carries the meaning \"loose/release.\"","greek":"λυ-"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form into pieces?","kind":"form","options":["λυ + ε + ο + ν","ἐ + λυ + ο + ν","ἐ + λυο + ν + ε","ἔλ + υ + ο + ν"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυον is the augment ἐ, the stem λυ, then ο and ν.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"What does this present form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I was loosing","they were loosing","I loose","we loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω, with no augment, is the present: \"I loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Which English pattern does the lesson use to translate every form in its imperfect table?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"will\" + verb, as in \"I will loose\"","A plain present, as in \"I loose\"","A command, as in \"Loose!\"","\"was/were\" + -ing, as in \"I was loosing\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Each imperfect form is translated with was/were plus an -ing verb, such as \"we were loosing.\""},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says that when you see ε- at the front of a verb, you should immediately think \"past.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. An ε- at the front is the augment, the signal of past time."},{"q":"According to the lesson's summary, what kind of endings does the imperfect use?","kind":"form","options":["Primary endings","Secondary endings","No endings at all","Future endings"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfect uses secondary endings, not the primary ones."},{"q":"True or false: in the imperfect, the stem λυ- is replaced by a new stem.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The imperfect keeps the same stem, λυ-, and adds the augment in front."},{"q":"Which three features does the lesson say make up the imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["New stem, no augment, primary endings","Same stem, added σ, primary endings","Same stem, added augment, secondary endings","New stem, added augment, no endings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The imperfect keeps the stem, adds the augment, and uses secondary endings."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["you all were loosing","we were loosing","you all loose","they were loosing"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐλύετε is the 2nd plural imperfect: \"you all were loosing.\" The augment makes it past.","greek":"ἐλύετε"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I was loosing","you were loosing","we were loosing","he/she/it was loosing"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔλυε(ν) is the 3rd singular imperfect: \"he/she/it was loosing.\"","greek":"ἔλυε(ν)"},{"q":"Which person and number is this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd singular","2nd singular","1st singular","2nd plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυες is the 2nd singular imperfect: \"you were loosing.\"","greek":"ἔλυες"},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, what happens once you recognize the augment?","kind":"concept","options":["You will know every verb's meaning","You will no longer need endings","You will never miss a past-tense verb again","You will recognize every future verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"The augment is a built-in past-time signal, so once you can spot it, past-tense verbs stand out."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, which forms carry the augment as a built-in signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Only future forms","Only commands","Only plural forms","Every past-tense form"],"answer":3,"explain":"Every past-tense form in Greek carries this built-in signal, the augment."},{"q":"In the lesson's imperfect table of λύω, how many of the six forms carry the augment?","kind":"form","options":["All six","Three","Only the plurals","Only the 1st person forms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The augment shows up in every form of the imperfect paradigm."},{"q":"Which form appears twice in the lesson's imperfect table?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυες","ἐλύομεν","ἐλύετε","ἔλυον"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔλυον is both the 1st singular (\"I was loosing\") and the 3rd plural (\"they were loosing\")."},{"q":"What does the augment signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Past time","Future time","Plural number","A command"],"answer":0,"explain":"The augment is the signal of past time."},{"q":"In ἐ + λυ + ο + ν, which piece is the stem?","kind":"form","options":["ἐ","λυ","ο","ν"],"answer":1,"explain":"λυ is the stem, meaning \"loose/release.\" The ἐ in front is the augment.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"Which of these forms has NO augment?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυον","ἔλυες","λύω","ἐλύετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω is the present, so it has no augment. The other three are imperfect forms with ἐ- in front."},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's table, this form means \"they were loosing.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ἐλύομεν is the 1st plural: \"we were loosing.\" The 3rd plural is ἔλυον.","greek":"ἐλύομεν"},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, an ε- at the front of an indicative verb can mark either an imperfect or an aorist form.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The augment signals past time, so it can point to an imperfect or an aorist."},{"q":"What translation does the lesson give for this form in its present-versus-imperfect comparison?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose","we loose","I was loosing","you were loosing"],"answer":2,"explain":"In the comparison, ἔλυον is the imperfect \"I was loosing,\" set against the present λύω \"I loose.\"","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"Which tense does the lesson compare with the present to show the augment?","kind":"concept","options":["Future","Imperfect","Perfect","Pluperfect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson sets the present λύω beside the imperfect ἔλυον."},{"q":"Which pair shows the same stem without and with the augment?","kind":"form","options":["λύω / ἔλυον","ἔλυες / ἐλύετε","ἐλύομεν / ἔλυον","ἔλυε(ν) / ἔλυες"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύω is the present with no augment. ἔλυον is the same stem λυ- with the augment ἐ- added."},{"q":"Comparing these two imperfect forms, which part differs?","kind":"form","options":["The augment","The stem","Nothing at all","The ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both forms keep the augment ἐ- and the stem λυ-. Only the endings differ.","greek":"ἔλυον · ἔλυες"},{"q":"How can a reader tell this form is past time even before looking at its ending?","kind":"concept","options":["It begins with the augment ε-","Its stem is λυ-","It ends in -ν","It has an accent mark"],"answer":0,"explain":"The augment at the front is the past-time signal. The stem λυ- is the same as in the present.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"A form has the stem λυ- and secondary endings but no augment. By the lesson's summary, is it an imperfect indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Yes, the secondary endings are enough","Yes, the stem λυ- is enough","Only if it is plural","No, the imperfect also needs the added augment"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's summary names three features: same stem, added augment, secondary endings. Without the augment, the form is not an imperfect indicative."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT something ἔλυον and ἐλύομεν have in common?","kind":"form","options":["The augment","The stem λυ-","The same person and number","Being past-time forms"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both have the augment and the stem λυ-, and both are past. But ἔλυον is 1st singular or 3rd plural, while ἐλύομεν is 1st plural."}],"vb_13":[{"q":"Which of these is one of the lesson's English examples of the future tense?","kind":"concept","options":["They came.","They will come.","They were coming.","Come!"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"They will come\" points to something that has not yet happened, so it is future."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the future tense for English speakers?","kind":"concept","options":["The hardest Greek tense","A tense English does not have","Generally the most straightforward Greek tense","A tense used only in poetry"],"answer":2,"explain":"The future is generally the most straightforward Greek tense for English speakers, though context can add more meaning."},{"q":"Which letter does Greek insert to signal the future?","kind":"form","options":["ε","ο","μ","σ"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek marks the future by inserting σ between the stem and the ending."},{"q":"What does the lesson call this letter when it marks the future?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense formative","Augment","Connecting vowel","Personal ending"],"answer":0,"explain":"The σ that marks the future is called the tense formative.","greek":"σ"},{"q":"Where is the σ placed in this form?","kind":"form","options":["At the front of the word","Between the stem and the ending","At the very end","Before the stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The σ sits between the stem λύ and the ending: λύ + σ + ο + μεν.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"Which tense is this form?","kind":"form","options":["Present","Imperfect","Future","Aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύσομεν has σ inserted between the stem and the ending, so it is future.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"Which tense does the lesson label this form?","kind":"form","options":["Future","Imperfect","Aorist","Present"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν has no σ, so the lesson labels it present.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form apart?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + σ + ο + μεν","λύ + ο + σ + μεν","σ + λύ + ο + μεν","λύσ + ομ + εν"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem λύ comes first, then the future σ, then ο and μεν.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"What is the only difference between these two forms?","kind":"form","options":["An augment on the second","The σ inserted in the second","A different stem","A different ending"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both have the same stem and ending pieces. The future λύσομεν simply adds σ.","greek":"λύομεν · λύσομεν"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the Greek future tense works similarly to the English future.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Like \"He will speak\" in English, the Greek future presents action as forward-looking."},{"q":"What signals that a Greek future action is forward-looking, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A separate word for \"will\"","The word order","The verb form itself","An augment at the front"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek needs no separate word for \"will.\" The verb form itself marks the action as forward-looking."},{"q":"In the lesson, what kind of future statement is \"You will not escape\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A promise","A prediction","A command","A warning"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson gives \"You will not escape\" as its example of a warning."},{"q":"Which sentence does the lesson give as its example of a promise?","kind":"concept","options":["\"I will be with you.\"","\"You will not escape.\"","\"The hour is coming.\"","\"They traveled.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"I will be with you\" is the promise example. The other future examples are a warning and a prediction."},{"q":"Which purpose does the lesson attach to the example \"The hour is coming\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A promise","A prediction","A warning","A command"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists \"The hour is coming\" as its example of a prediction."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, a future verb used as a promise has a different grammatical form from one used as a warning.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The form is the same. The surrounding context shows whether it is a promise, a warning or a prediction."},{"q":"In which kinds of NT sections does the lesson say recognizing future forms is especially useful?","kind":"concept","options":["Genealogies only","Greetings only","Prophetic or narrative sections","Lists of names"],"answer":2,"explain":"Future forms are especially useful to spot in prophetic or narrative sections."},{"q":"According to the introduction, what can context add to future verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["A new stem","An augment","A past meaning","Layers of meaning beyond simple time"],"answer":3,"explain":"The future points to time ahead, but context can add more, such as promise or warning."},{"q":"The introduction describes future action with three words. Which word is one of them?","kind":"concept","options":["predicted","repeated","commanded","completed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the future communicates action that is expected, anticipated, or predicted."},{"q":"Which of these forms carries the future's signature?","kind":"form","options":["λύομεν","λύσομεν","λύω","ἐλύομεν"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only λύσομεν has σ between the stem and the ending, which is the future's signature."},{"q":"If you removed the σ from this future form, what form from the lesson would you have?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύομεν","λύω","λύομεν","λύετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύ + σ + ο + μεν without the σ is λύ + ο + μεν, the present λύομεν.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's breakdown, the future λύσομεν begins with an augment.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson builds it as λύ + σ + ο + μεν: the stem comes first, and the future is marked by the σ after the stem, not by a prefix.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the purposes the lesson lists for a future verb in context?","kind":"concept","options":["Promise","Warning","Prediction","Command"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists promise, warning and prediction. Command is not on its list."},{"q":"According to the lesson's summary, which way does the future tense point?","kind":"concept","options":["Backward, to the past","Forward","To the present moment only","Toward a command"],"answer":1,"explain":"The future points forward, and context tells you its purpose."},{"q":"A learner says, \"This form must be a promise, because it is future.\" What would the lesson say?","kind":"concept","options":["The form marks the future; context decides whether it is a promise, warning or prediction","Correct: every future verb is a promise","Wrong: this is a present form","Wrong: future verbs never express promises"],"answer":0,"explain":"The σ tells you it is future. Whether it works as a promise, warning or prediction depends on the context.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"The lesson describes future action as \"anticipated, not yet completed.\" Which pair of sentences both fit that description?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He will speak.\" / \"He spoke.\"","\"They came.\" / \"They will come.\"","\"He will speak.\" / \"You will see.\"","\"You saw.\" / \"You will see.\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"He will speak\" and \"You will see\" both point ahead to something not yet done. Each other pair includes a past statement."},{"q":"Which pair shows a present form followed by its matching future form, as in the lesson's comparison?","kind":"form","options":["λύω / λύομεν","λύομεν / ἐλύομεν","ἐλύομεν / λύσομεν","λύομεν / λύσομεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύομεν (no σ) is the present and λύσομεν (σ inserted) is the future. λύω / λύομεν are both present, and ἐλύομεν begins with an augment."},{"q":"In \"They will come,\" English uses the word \"will\" to mark the future. What does that job in a Greek future like λύσομεν?","kind":"concept","options":["An augment at the front","The σ inside the verb form","A separate Greek word for \"will\"","The word order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek builds the future into the verb itself: the σ between stem and ending marks it."},{"q":"What is the stem in both of these forms?","kind":"form","options":["σ","μεν","λύ","ο"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύ is the stem in both. The future adds σ right after it.","greek":"λύομεν · λύσομεν"},{"q":"What does the lesson call the σ that sits between the stem and the ending?","kind":"concept","options":["The past-time signal","The plural marker","The mood sign","The future's signature"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson calls that σ the future's signature, because it is how you spot a future form.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"A learner says λύομεν must be future because it ends in -μεν. What is wrong with that reasoning?","kind":"form","options":["Both forms end in -μεν; only the σ marks the future, and λύομεν has none","Nothing: -μεν marks the future","λύομεν begins with an augment","λύομεν has a different stem from λύσομεν"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both forms end in ο + μεν. The future's signature is the σ between stem and ending, which λύομεν lacks, so it is present.","greek":"λύομεν"}],"vb_14":[{"q":"How does the lesson describe the aorist's place in the Greek New Testament?","kind":"concept","options":["A rare tense found in few books","One of the most common and most important verb systems","A tense used only for commands","A tense that replaced the future"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist is one of the most common and most important verb systems in the NT, so it is worth learning carefully."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"She came,\" how is the action presented?","kind":"concept","options":["As ongoing","As completed with lasting results","As a whole","As a command"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson labels \"She came\" as the action presented as a whole."},{"q":"Which tense does the lesson connect with focusing on action as ongoing?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Perfect","Future","Imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson contrasts the aorist with the imperfect, which focuses on action as ongoing."},{"q":"Which tense does the lesson connect with action completed with results?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfect","Imperfect","Aorist","Future"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson names the perfect as the tense that views action as completed with results."},{"q":"When the aorist presents an action, what does it often leave out?","kind":"concept","options":["The stem","Any comment on the action's duration or result","The augment","The ending"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist simply presents the action, often without saying how long it lasted or what resulted."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what has over-reading the aorist as \"once-for-all\" led to?","kind":"concept","options":["Better translations","New Greek grammar rules","Some significant interpretive errors in NT scholarship","The loss of the augment"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson warns that this over-reading has caused some significant interpretive errors in NT scholarship."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what determines whether an action is one-time or repeated?","kind":"concept","options":["The aorist tense alone","The augment","The σ","Context"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist tense by itself does not settle this. Context does."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the aorist tense communicates on its own?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect","Special theological force","The exact duration of the action","The number of times the action happened"],"answer":0,"explain":"The aorist tense communicates aspect, a way of viewing the action. It does not carry special theological force on its own."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the aorist means an action can never happen again.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The aorist does not mean the action can never happen again. It simply presents it as a complete event."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loosed","we loosed","I was loosing","I loose"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔλυσα is the 1st singular aorist of λύω: \"I loosed.\"","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loosed","we were loosing","we loosed","we loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐλύσαμεν is the 1st plural aorist of λύω: \"we loosed.\"","greek":"ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form apart?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + σ + α + ε","ἐ + λύ + α + σ","ἐλ + ύσ + α","ἐ + λύ + σ + α"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔλυσα is the augment ἐ, the stem λύ, the σ, and α.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"Compared with ἔλυσα, what extra piece does this form add at the end?","kind":"form","options":["μεν","ν","ε","σ"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson builds ἐλύσαμεν as ἐ + λύ + σ + α + μεν, so it adds μεν after the σα.","greek":"ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"In the first aorist, what does the augment signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Future time","Past time","Plural number","A command"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment is the past-time signal at the front of the aorist form."},{"q":"In the first aorist of λύω, what does the lesson call this letter?","kind":"concept","options":["The augment","The past-time signal","The aorist tense formative","The connecting vowel"],"answer":2,"explain":"In the first aorist, σ is the aorist tense formative. The augment is what signals past time.","greek":"σ"},{"q":"Which three signals does the first aorist of λύω combine, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["The stem, an ο, and μεν","A σ, an ο, and the ending","The augment, an ο, and ν","The augment, the stem, and the σα pattern"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first aorist puts the augment in front, then the stem, then the σα pattern."},{"q":"Which part of this form is the augment?","kind":"form","options":["ἐ-","λύ","σ","α"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ἐ- at the front is the augment, marking past time.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what do the augment and the σ mark when they appear together?","kind":"concept","options":["The imperfect","The first aorist","The present","The perfect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment (past time) plus the σ (aorist tense formative) together mark the first aorist."},{"q":"Which form has the augment but NOT the σ that marks the first aorist?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυσα","ἐλύσαμεν","ἔλυον","λύσομεν"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυον has the augment ἐ- but no σ, so it is not a first aorist. ἔλυσα and ἐλύσαμεν have both, and λύσομεν has a σ but no augment."},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown of this form, which pieces make up the σα pattern?","kind":"form","options":["ἐ + λύ","α + μεν","λύ + σ","σ + α"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson builds ἐλύσαμεν as ἐ + λύ + σ + α + μεν. The σα pattern is the σ and the α after the stem; ἐ is the augment, and μεν comes last.","greek":"ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"Which piece in these forms marks them specifically as aorist, not just as past?","kind":"form","options":["σ","ἐ","λύ","μεν"],"answer":0,"explain":"The augment ἐ only signals past time. The σ is the aorist tense formative.","greek":"ἔλυσα · ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"Which form is the 1st singular first aorist of λύω, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύσαμεν","ἔλυσα","ἔλυον","λύσομεν"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυσα has the augment, the stem and the σα pattern, and the lesson labels it 1sg. ἐλύσαμεν is the 1pl."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"They believed,\" what does the aorist present?","kind":"concept","options":["The act as still continuing","The act's lasting results","The act, without further detail","A command to believe"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson labels \"They believed\" as the act presented without further detail."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"He spoke,\" how is the event presented?","kind":"concept","options":["As ongoing","As a future plan","As a wish","Simply, as an event"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"He spoke\" presents the event simply, which is typical of the aorist."},{"q":"What popular label for the aorist does the lesson warn against?","kind":"concept","options":["\"once-for-all action\"","\"undefined event\"","\"action as a whole\"","\"past time\""],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls \"once-for-all action\" a common, simplistic mistake for describing the aorist."},{"q":"Instead of giving further detail about an action's nature or repetition, how does the aorist present it?","kind":"concept","options":["As an unfinished process","As a complete event","As something still to come","As a repeated habit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist presents the action as a complete event, without adding detail about its nature or repetition."},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's breakdowns, ἔλυσα and ἐλύσαμεν share the same stem, λύ.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Both are built on λύ: ἐ + λύ + σ + α and ἐ + λύ + σ + α + μεν."},{"q":"True or false: in this form, the σ signals past time and the augment is the aorist tense formative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The roles are reversed: the augment signals past time, and the σ is the aorist tense formative.","greek":"ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"What English translation style does the lesson use for these aorist forms?","kind":"meaning","options":["Past continuous, like \"I was loosing\"","Future, like \"I will loose\"","Simple past, like \"I loosed\"","Present, like \"I loose\""],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson translates ἔλυσα as \"I loosed\" and ἐλύσαμεν as \"we loosed,\" in the simple past.","greek":"ἔλυσα · ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"According to the introduction, what is essential for reading Greek accurately?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every aorist form first","Translating every aorist as once-for-all","Skipping the aorist until later","Understanding the aorist well and avoiding oversimplifications"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says understanding the aorist well, without oversimplifying it, is essential for accurate reading."}],"vb_15":[{"q":"What does this aorist form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I came","I saw","I said","I say"],"answer":2,"explain":"εἶπον is the aorist of λέγω and means \"I said.\" It is one of the most common second aorists.","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"What does this aorist form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I come","I came","I saw","I said"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἦλθον means \"I came.\" It is the second aorist of ἔρχομαι (I come).","greek":"ἦλθον"},{"q":"Which Greek form means \"I saw\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["εἶδον","εἶπον","ἦλθον","ὁράω"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἶδον means \"I saw.\" It is the second aorist of ὁράω (I see)."},{"q":"What does this present-tense verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I came","I come","I see","I say"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔρχομαι is the present form meaning \"I come.\" Its aorist, ἦλθον, looks very different.","greek":"ἔρχομαι"},{"q":"What does this present-tense verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I come","I saw","I say","I see"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὁράω means \"I see.\" Its second aorist is εἶδον, \"I saw.\"","greek":"ὁράω"},{"q":"What does this present-tense verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I come","I said","I see","I say"],"answer":3,"explain":"λέγω is the present form meaning \"I say.\" Its aorist uses a different stem.","greek":"λέγω"},{"q":"This aorist form comes from which present-tense verb?","kind":"form","options":["ἔρχομαι","λέγω","ὁράω","γράφω"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἦλθον (I came) is the second aorist of ἔρχομαι (I come). The stem changes completely, so it has to be learned.","greek":"ἦλθον"},{"q":"Which list gives the aorists of λέγω, ἔρχομαι, and ὁράω, in that order?","kind":"form","options":["ἦλθον, εἶπον, εἶδον","εἶπον, εἶδον, ἦλθον","εἶδον, ἦλθον, εἶπον","εἶπον, ἦλθον, εἶδον"],"answer":3,"explain":"λέγω → εἶπον (I said), ἔρχομαι → ἦλθον (I came), ὁράω → εἶδον (I saw). Each is a second aorist with its own stem."},{"q":"Which form is the aorist of this verb?","kind":"form","options":["ἦλθον","εἶπον","εἶδον","ἔλυσα"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist of ὁράω (I see) is εἶδον (I saw), a second aorist with a different stem.","greek":"ὁράω"},{"q":"Where does a first aorist typically insert its -σα- suffix?","kind":"concept","options":["Before the stem","Between the stem and the ending","After the ending","In place of the stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"First aorists typically insert -σα- between the stem and the ending. Second aorists do not use this pattern."},{"q":"What do these three forms have in common?","kind":"form","options":["They all come from λέγω","They are all present tense forms","They all use the -σα- suffix","They are all second aorists"],"answer":3,"explain":"All three are second aorists: they use a different stem from their present forms instead of the first aorist suffix.","greek":"εἶπον · ἦλθον · εἶδον"},{"q":"Which word does the lesson use to describe the first aorist pattern?","kind":"concept","options":["Predictable","Rare","Irregular","Unpredictable"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls the first aorist pattern predictable. Second aorists are the group that does not follow it."},{"q":"How does this form present the action of saying?","kind":"meaning","options":["As repeated: \"I keep saying\"","As ongoing: \"I was saying\"","As anticipated: \"I will say\"","As a whole event: \"I said\""],"answer":3,"explain":"A second aorist works like any aorist: the action is presented as a whole or undefined event. So εἶπον is simply \"I said.\"","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"ἦλθον is formed by adding the -σα- suffix to the stem of ἔρχομαι.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ἦλθον is a second aorist: instead of adding -σα-, it uses a different stem from ἔρχομαι.","greek":"ἦλθον"},{"q":"Which suffix typically marks a first aorist?","kind":"form","options":["-ομεν-","-κα-","-θη-","-σα-"],"answer":3,"explain":"First aorists typically insert -σα- between the stem and the ending. Second aorists do not follow this pattern."},{"q":"What makes a second aorist different from a first aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["It keeps the present stem unchanged","It adds -σα- after the present stem","It uses a different stem instead of the -σα- pattern","It changes only the accent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Second aorists skip the first aorist suffix pattern and use a different stem from the present tense form."},{"q":"A second aorist stem can be only slightly altered from the present form rather than completely different.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says the new stem is sometimes only slightly altered and sometimes completely different."},{"q":"Most aorist verbs follow the second aorist pattern.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Most aorist verbs follow the predictable first aorist pattern; second aorists are a significant group, not the majority."},{"q":"At this stage, what is the main goal with second aorists?","kind":"concept","options":["Producing them when writing Greek","Deriving each one from the present by a rule","Recognizing them when reading","Tracing the history of each stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's summary ends \"Focus on recognition\": know the common second aorists so you identify them when you meet them in reading."},{"q":"What is the most practical way to handle second aorists at this stage?","kind":"concept","options":["Learn only the first aorist pattern for now","Work out each one from the present by a rule","Memorize the most common second aorist stems","Translate them as present tense verbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Because the stems cannot always be predicted, the practical approach is to memorize the most common ones so you recognize them in reading."},{"q":"Why will you meet second aorists frequently in reading?","kind":"concept","options":["Second aorists replace the present tense","Every aorist verb is a second aorist","Common verbs such as \"say,\" \"come,\" and \"see\" are second aorists","They are the only past tense in Greek"],"answer":2,"explain":"Some of the most common verbs, like \"say,\" \"come,\" and \"see,\" are second aorists, so they show up often."},{"q":"Which statement about second aorists is FALSE?","kind":"concept","options":["They use a different stem from the present tense form","Common verbs such as \"say\" and \"come\" are among them","They mark the aorist by inserting -σα-, like first aorists","Their new stem is sometimes only slightly altered"],"answer":2,"explain":"Second aorists do not follow the first aorist -σα- pattern; they use a different stem instead. The other three statements match the lesson."},{"q":"When the lesson says a second aorist uses a \"different stem,\" different from what?","kind":"concept","options":["From the future tense form","From the present tense form","From the personal ending","From the first person singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second aorist stem is different from the present tense form of the same verb, as with λέγω and εἶπον."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the payoff of learning second aorists now?","kind":"concept","options":["It matters only in the Advanced Track","It pays off quickly","It rarely helps in actual reading","It is useful only for rare verbs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Since such common verbs are second aorists, investing in recognizing them pays off quickly."},{"q":"A learner knows λέγω means \"I say\" but does not recognize εἶπον. What best explains this?","kind":"concept","options":["εἶπον is a first aorist with an unusual -σα- suffix","The second aorist uses a different stem, so it must be learned separately","εἶπον has a different meaning from the verb λέγω","εἶπον is a present tense form of another verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"εἶπον is a second aorist with a completely different stem. Recognition comes from familiarity with the common forms, not from the present stem."},{"q":"How does the lesson's introduction describe learning to recognize second aorists?","kind":"concept","options":["A skill needed only for writing Greek","An important skill for Greek reading","An optional topic for advanced students","A skill that matters only for pronunciation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The introduction calls recognizing second aorists an important skill for Greek reading."},{"q":"Which summary of second aorists matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Same function as the first aorist, different stem","Same stem as the first aorist, different function","Different function and different stem","Same stem and same suffix as the first aorist"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson sums it up: same function as the first aorist, different stem, so focus on recognition."},{"q":"εἶπον and εἶδον differ by only one letter. Which pairing is correct?","kind":"meaning","options":["εἶπον = I saw; εἶδον = I said","εἶπον = I came; εἶδον = I saw","εἶπον = I said; εἶδον = I came","εἶπον = I said; εἶδον = I saw"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἶπον (with π) is the aorist of λέγω, \"I said.\" εἶδον (with δ) is the aorist of ὁράω, \"I saw.\"","greek":"εἶπον · εἶδον"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe second aorists as a group?","kind":"concept","options":["The majority of aorist verbs","A handful of rare verbs","A significant group","Only verbs of motion"],"answer":2,"explain":"Most aorists are first aorists, but second aorists are a significant group, and they include very common verbs."},{"q":"Which of these is a present tense form rather than an aorist?","kind":"form","options":["ἔρχομαι","ἦλθον","εἶδον","εἶπον"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔρχομαι (I come) is the present form. ἦλθον, εἶδον, and εἶπον are all second aorists."}],"vb_16":[{"q":"What does this perfect form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose","I loosed","I have loosed","I will loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"λέλυκα means \"I have loosed\": the action is done, and its result continues.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which tense is this form?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect","Aorist","Present","Imperfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"λέλυκα has reduplication at the front and κ before the ending, the two signatures of the perfect.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which piece of this form is the reduplication?","kind":"form","options":["α","λυ","κ","λε"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson breaks λέλυκα into λε + λυ + κ + α. The λε at the front is the reduplication.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which letter in this form is the perfect tense formative?","kind":"form","options":["υ","λ","κ","α"],"answer":2,"explain":"The κ just before the ending is the perfect tense formative.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"In the lesson's breakdown of this form, which piece is the ending?","kind":"form","options":["κ","α","λε","λυ"],"answer":1,"explain":"λε + λυ + κ + α: the κ comes before the ending, and the final α is the ending.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Where does reduplication appear in a perfect form?","kind":"form","options":["At the front","Right before the ending","After the ending","Only in the ending"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reduplication is one of the perfect's two signatures, and it sits at the front of the word."},{"q":"Where does the κ tense formative appear in a perfect form?","kind":"form","options":["In place of the stem","At the front of the word","After the ending","Before the ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"The perfect places the κ tense formative just before the ending, as in λέλυκα."},{"q":"What two signatures identify a perfect form?","kind":"concept","options":["The -σα- suffix and a new stem","Reduplication and the κ tense formative","A changed stem and a circumflex accent","The κ formative and the -σα- suffix"],"answer":1,"explain":"The perfect is marked by reduplication at the front and κ before the ending."},{"q":"Which tense is this NT form?","kind":"form","options":["Future","Aorist","Present","Perfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"γέγραπται is the lesson's classic NT example of the perfect: a perfect passive.","greek":"γέγραπται"},{"q":"How does the lesson label this form?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect active","Aorist passive","Present passive","Perfect passive"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson labels γέγραπται a perfect passive: \"it has been written\" or \"it stands written.\"","greek":"γέγραπται"},{"q":"Why is \"it was written\" an incomplete rendering of this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["It leaves out that the result still stands now","It turns the action into a future event","It changes the verb into a command","It makes the action ongoing in the past"],"answer":0,"explain":"As a perfect, γέγραπται means the writing happened and its result is still present: \"it stands written.\"","greek":"γέγραπται"},{"q":"Which English sentence from the lesson illustrates the perfect?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He is rising.\"","\"He rose early.\"","\"He has risen.\"","\"He will rise.\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"He has risen\" uses a \"have\" construction: the event happened, and the result continues."},{"q":"In \"It has been written,\" what does the perfect convey?","kind":"concept","options":["Going to be written later","Being written right at this moment","Written in the past and still standing written now","Written over and over in the past"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson explains this example as written in the past and still standing written now."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"I have believed,\" what does the perfect add beyond a past event?","kind":"concept","options":["I believed once but no longer do","I came to faith and I continue in it","I am about to believe","I believe only at this moment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The perfect joins the past event (coming to faith) with a continuing result (continuing in it)."},{"q":"Which English constructions sometimes capture the Greek perfect?","kind":"concept","options":["\"has been\" or \"have\" constructions","\"will\" or \"shall\" constructions","\"was ___ing\" constructions","\"to ___\" constructions"],"answer":0,"explain":"English often uses \"has been\" or \"have\" to show a past action with a present result."},{"q":"The perfect appears more frequently than the present, aorist, or imperfect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The perfect appears less frequently than those tenses, though it often carries significant weight."},{"q":"A door was opened earlier and still stands open now. Which tense best fits that idea, according to this lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Future","Perfect","Present","Imperfect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The perfect presents a completed action whose result continues into the present: the opening is done, and the door still stands open."},{"q":"What should a reader do when a perfect appears?","kind":"concept","options":["Skip it, since it is uncommon","Read it as a plain past with no lasting result","Pay attention, since it often signals a past event and its enduring significance","Read it as an action that has not happened yet"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson advises paying attention to the perfect because it often communicates both a past event and its enduring significance."},{"q":"Which English sentence best shows the idea of the Greek perfect?","kind":"concept","options":["\"She wrote letters every day.\"","\"The letter has been written and is ready to read.\"","\"He will write the letter tomorrow.\"","\"Write the letter now!\""],"answer":1,"explain":"\"The letter has been written and is ready to read\" shows a completed action (the writing) whose result (a finished letter) is still present."},{"q":"In λε + λυ + κ + α, the front piece λε repeats which letter?","kind":"form","options":["υ","κ","λ","α"],"answer":2,"explain":"The front piece λε copies λ, the first letter of λυ, and adds ε. That repeated piece at the front is the reduplication.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"λέλυκα means \"I have loosed.\" What does this imply about the loosing?","kind":"meaning","options":["It is still in progress","It is complete and its result continues","It has not happened yet","It happened but left no result"],"answer":1,"explain":"As a perfect, λέλυκα presents a completed action whose result continues into the present.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"English always uses a \"has\" or \"have\" construction to express the Greek perfect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says English sometimes captures the perfect with \"has been\" or \"have\" constructions, not always. γέγραπται, for example, can also be rendered \"it stands written.\""},{"q":"Why does the lesson say the perfect has \"a unique kind of weight\"?","kind":"concept","options":["It refers only to future events","It is the most common tense in the NT","It is used only for commands","It combines a past event with a result that is still present"],"answer":3,"explain":"The perfect joins two ideas, that something happened and that its result remains, and that combination gives it weight."},{"q":"γέγραπται is the lesson's classic NT example of the perfect tense.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson presents γέγραπται, \"it has been written,\" as a classic NT perfect.","greek":"γέγραπται"},{"q":"What form do these pieces build?","kind":"form","options":["λέλυκα","γέγραπται","ἔλυσα","λύω"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reduplication λε, then λυ, then the κ formative, then the ending α: together they make λέλυκα.","greek":"λε + λυ + κ + α"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what helps train the eye to recognize perfect forms quickly?","kind":"concept","options":["Avoiding perfect forms at first","Memorizing every perfect in the NT","A few key examples","Translating each word separately"],"answer":2,"explain":"Recognizing perfects takes practice, but a few key examples train the eye quickly."},{"q":"According to the lesson, recognizing perfect forms comes instantly, without practice.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Recognizing perfect forms takes practice, though a few key examples help train the eye quickly."},{"q":"Which form shows both signatures of the perfect?","kind":"form","options":["λύω","ἔλυσα","λέλυκα","λύσω"],"answer":2,"explain":"λέλυκα has reduplication (λε-) at the front and κ before the ending. The other forms have neither."},{"q":"You meet an unfamiliar verb form with reduplication at the front and κ before the ending. What should you conclude?","kind":"concept","options":["It is probably a perfect: a completed action whose result continues","It is probably a future form","It is probably a second aorist","It is probably a present form"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reduplication plus κ is the perfect's signature, and the perfect presents a completed action whose result continues into the present."},{"q":"Which perfect signature can you see at the front of this form?","kind":"form","options":["The κ tense formative","Reduplication (γε-)","The -σα- suffix","A second aorist stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"Like λε- in λέλυκα, the γε- at the front of γέγραπται is reduplication.","greek":"γέγραπται"}],"vb_17":[{"q":"Which term names a verb form that gives a command?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative","Imperative","Infinitive","Participle"],"answer":1,"explain":"An imperative is a verbal command. It directs someone to act."},{"q":"In the command \"Follow me,\" who is understood to be the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He\" or \"she\"","\"Me,\" the speaker","\"You,\" the person addressed","\"They,\" a group nearby"],"answer":2,"explain":"English imperatives leave the subject unstated, and \"you\" is understood."},{"q":"English imperatives must include a stated subject.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. English imperatives do not require a stated subject; \"you\" is implied."},{"q":"Greek imperatives, like English ones, work without a stated subject, with \"you\" understood.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says Greek imperatives work the same way as English ones in this respect."},{"q":"How many imperatives are in this list: \"Go!\", \"He listens.\", \"Write!\", \"She came.\"","kind":"concept","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Go!\" and \"Write!\" are commands directed at the person addressed. \"He listens.\" and \"She came.\" are statements."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a command?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He comes.\"","\"Come.\"","\"Go!\"","\"Follow me.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"He comes\" states a fact. \"Come,\" \"Go!\" and \"Follow me\" direct the person addressed to act."},{"q":"How are Greek imperative forms set apart from indicative forms?","kind":"form","options":["By a different alphabet","By different endings","By an added -σα- suffix","By word order alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek imperatives have their own endings, which signal that a command is being given rather than a statement made."},{"q":"In which two tenses does the lesson say Greek imperatives come?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist and perfect","Present and future","Present and aorist","Imperfect and future"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek imperatives come in present and aorist forms, and that difference can carry meaning."},{"q":"What kind of action does a present imperative often imply?","kind":"concept","options":["An action already finished","A single specific act","A completed action with a result","Ongoing or repeated action"],"answer":3,"explain":"A present imperative often implies ongoing or repeated action: \"keep on doing this.\""},{"q":"Which paraphrase fits what an aorist imperative often implies?","kind":"concept","options":["\"You will do this.\"","\"Keep on doing this.\"","\"You did this.\"","\"Do this (now).\""],"answer":3,"explain":"An aorist imperative often points to a specific act: \"do this (now).\""},{"q":"A teacher wants to say \"keep on reading every day.\" Which kind of imperative typically fits that idea?","kind":"concept","options":["A present indicative","An aorist imperative","A present imperative","An aorist indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Keep on\" describes ongoing or repeated action, which a present imperative often implies."},{"q":"A speaker wants one specific act done right away: \"Shut the door now.\" Which kind of imperative typically fits?","kind":"concept","options":["An aorist imperative","A present imperative","A present indicative","An aorist indicative"],"answer":0,"explain":"An aorist imperative often implies a specific act, \"do this (now).\" It is a command, so an indicative would not fit."},{"q":"What does the lesson say always matters when judging what an imperative's tense communicates?","kind":"concept","options":["Context","Word length","Accent marks","Letter shapes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tense can suggest urgency, repetition, or a specific act, but context always matters."},{"q":"Which three things can the tense of an imperative communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Cause, result, or purpose","Time, place, or manner","Person, number, or case","Urgency, repetition, or specific action"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists urgency, repetition, or specific action, while noting that context always matters."},{"q":"A present imperative always means repeated action, no matter the context.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A present imperative often implies ongoing or repeated action, but context always matters."},{"q":"Why does the lesson ask you, for now, to simply recognize imperatives as commands?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperatives look exactly like indicatives","Greek imperatives have no tense at all","The present/aorist distinction is developed further in the Advanced Track","Context never affects imperatives"],"answer":2,"explain":"The finer present/aorist distinction is covered in the Advanced Track, so at this stage the goal is recognition."},{"q":"What is the main goal with imperatives at this stage?","kind":"concept","options":["Set them aside until later","Master every imperative ending","Translate them as statements","Recognize them as commands"],"answer":3,"explain":"For now, the lesson asks you simply to recognize imperatives as commands."},{"q":"Imperatives are rare in the New Testament.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says imperatives are common throughout the New Testament."},{"q":"Which pair of words does the lesson use to describe imperatives?","kind":"concept","options":["Gentle and hidden","Indirect and vague","Rare and decorative","Direct and purposeful"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson calls imperatives direct, purposeful, and common throughout the NT."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how does recognizing imperatives help you?","kind":"concept","options":["You no longer need vocabulary","You read the NT with greater clarity and accuracy","You can ignore a verb's tense","You can skip the verb when reading"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spotting commands helps you read the NT with greater clarity and accuracy."},{"q":"The lesson's quick check speaks of the imperative ___. Which grammatical term fills the blank?","kind":"concept","options":["tense","voice","mood","case"],"answer":2,"explain":"The quick check asks, \"What does an imperative mood communicate?\" The imperative is a mood; present or aorist is its tense, a separate feature."},{"q":"Compare \"He goes.\" with \"Go!\" What is the key difference?","kind":"concept","options":["The first is past; the second is future","The first is a command; the second states a fact","The first states a fact; the second directs someone to act","The first has no subject; the second has one"],"answer":2,"explain":"An imperative such as \"Go!\" is a command, while \"He goes\" is a statement about someone."},{"q":"Two Greek commands use the same verb, one as a present imperative and one as an aorist imperative. What might differ?","kind":"concept","options":["Whether the sentence is a command at all","Whether the action is ongoing or repeated, or one specific act","Which alphabet the verb is written in","Whether the verb has any meaning"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present/aorist distinction can carry meaning: ongoing or repeated action versus a specific act."},{"q":"A present imperative and an aorist imperative are both commands.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Both are imperatives, so both direct someone to act. Their tense can add a nuance, such as ongoing action or a specific act."},{"q":"Which kind of form does the lesson contrast Greek imperatives with?","kind":"form","options":["Infinitive forms","Indicative forms","Participle forms","Noun forms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek imperative forms are distinct from indicative forms: the imperative gives a command, the indicative makes a statement."},{"q":"What do the lesson's examples \"Go!\", \"Listen!\" and \"Write!\" have in common?","kind":"concept","options":["Each directs the person addressed to act","Each states a past fact","Each asks a question","Each names its subject"],"answer":0,"explain":"All three are imperatives: commands directing the person addressed to act, with \"you\" implied."},{"q":"A parent says, \"Eat your vegetables!\" What kind of verb form is \"eat\" here?","kind":"concept","options":["An imperative","An indicative statement","A question","A condition"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Eat\" gives a command and directs the listener to act, with \"you\" implied. That makes it an imperative."},{"q":"Which statement about Greek imperatives is accurate?","kind":"concept","options":["They use the same endings as indicatives","They come only in the aorist","They always state their subject","They come in present and aorist forms, with endings distinct from the indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek imperatives come in present and aorist forms, and their endings differ from indicative endings. Like English commands, they do not need a stated subject."},{"q":"An imperative is a form of which part of speech?","kind":"concept","options":["A noun","A verb","An adjective","A preposition"],"answer":1,"explain":"An imperative is a verbal command: a form of the verb that directs someone to act."},{"q":"A Greek verb has indicative endings rather than imperative endings. What is the sentence most likely doing?","kind":"concept","options":["Giving a command","Making a statement","Directing the listener to act","Telling someone to keep on doing something"],"answer":1,"explain":"Imperative endings signal that a command is being given rather than a statement being made, so indicative endings point to a statement."}],"vb_18":[{"q":"How is an infinitive formed in English?","kind":"concept","options":["The verb + \"-ed\"","The verb + \"-ing\"","\"has\" + the verb","\"to\" + the verb"],"answer":3,"explain":"English builds infinitives with \"to\" plus the verb, as in \"to speak\" or \"to learn.\""},{"q":"How are Greek infinitives marked?","kind":"form","options":["By reduplication at the front","By a separate word meaning \"to\"","By their own distinctive endings","By a change in word order"],"answer":2,"explain":"Unlike English, which uses \"to,\" Greek infinitives have their own distinctive endings."},{"q":"Infinitives are rare in the New Testament.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says infinitives are extremely common in the New Testament."},{"q":"Which sentence uses an infinitive as the object?","kind":"concept","options":["\"To learn is good.\"","\"She came in order to learn.\"","\"She wanted to learn.\"","\"She learns every day.\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"To learn\" is what she wanted, so it is the object, like \"He wanted to go.\" The first uses the infinitive as subject, the second for purpose, and the last has no infinitive."},{"q":"In \"To believe is essential,\" what role does the infinitive play?","kind":"concept","options":["Result","Object","Purpose","Subject"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"To believe\" is what the sentence is about: it is the subject."},{"q":"In \"He wanted to go,\" what role does \"to go\" play?","kind":"concept","options":["Object","Subject","Purpose","Cause"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"To go\" is what he wanted, so the infinitive serves as the object."},{"q":"In \"He came in order to teach,\" what does the infinitive express?","kind":"concept","options":["Purpose","Subject","Object","Cause"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"To teach\" tells why he came, so it expresses purpose."},{"q":"Which infinitive function does the lesson describe only as \"various uses in Greek syntax\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject","Result or cause","Object","Purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists result or cause as a function and notes that it covers various uses in Greek syntax."},{"q":"What does an infinitive carry over from the verb it comes from?","kind":"concept","options":["A command to the listener","The verb's action or meaning","A stated subject","A fixed past time"],"answer":1,"explain":"An infinitive keeps the action or meaning of the verb, even though it plays a noun-like role."},{"q":"What does it mean to call infinitives \"verbal nouns\"?","kind":"concept","options":["They are nouns that describe other nouns","They are nouns with no verbal meaning","They are verbs that always give commands","They carry a verb's meaning but play a noun-like role"],"answer":3,"explain":"Infinitives come from verbs and keep their meaning, but they fill noun-like slots in the sentence."},{"q":"Greek infinitives are especially common alongside which kinds of words?","kind":"concept","options":["Certain verbs and prepositions","Certain articles and pronouns","Only proper names","Only conjunctions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Some verbs and prepositions regularly appear with infinitives, so infinitives are especially common with them."},{"q":"Which two clues help you recognize a Greek infinitive?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication and a κ before the ending","Its distinctive endings and the \"to ___\" pattern in translation","A -σα- suffix and a different stem","A stated subject and an exclamation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Look for the distinctive infinitive endings, and notice when your translation instinct gives \"to ___.\""},{"q":"Where are the more specific syntactic categories for infinitives covered?","kind":"concept","options":["In the Advanced Track","Earlier in this lesson","In the alphabet lessons","In the noun lessons"],"answer":0,"explain":"This lesson introduces infinitives; the detailed syntactic categories come in the Advanced Track."},{"q":"What should you focus on with infinitives for now?","kind":"concept","options":["Every ending in every tense","Every syntactic category","Basic recognition","Translating them without \"to\""],"answer":2,"explain":"At this stage, the goal is basic recognition of infinitives."},{"q":"According to the lesson, infinitives appear in a wide variety of sentence structures.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Infinitives are extremely common in the NT and show up in many kinds of sentence structures."},{"q":"Which sentence uses an infinitive as the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He came in order to speak.\"","\"He wanted to speak.\"","\"To speak is a gift.\"","\"She hoped to speak.\""],"answer":2,"explain":"In \"To speak is a gift,\" the infinitive is what the sentence is about, like \"To believe is essential.\" The others use it as an object or for purpose."},{"q":"Which sentence uses an infinitive to express purpose?","kind":"concept","options":["\"She hoped to write.\"","\"To write is hard.\"","\"He wanted to write.\"","\"He came in order to write.\""],"answer":3,"explain":"\"In order to write\" tells why he came, like \"He came in order to teach.\" The others use the infinitive as subject or object."},{"q":"In \"He wanted to go,\" why is \"to go\" said to play a noun-like role?","kind":"concept","options":["It fills the object slot: it is what he wanted","It names the person who went","It gives a command to go","It tells when he wanted something"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"To go\" is the thing wanted, the object of \"wanted,\" a slot a noun could fill."},{"q":"Which words in \"He came in order to teach\" make up the infinitive?","kind":"concept","options":["came in","He came","to teach","in order"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"To teach\" is \"to\" + the verb, the English infinitive pattern."},{"q":"How many infinitive functions does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists four: subject, object, purpose, and result or cause."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the infinitive functions listed in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Serving as subject","Giving a command","Serving as object","Expressing purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists subject, object, purpose, and result or cause. Giving commands is the job of imperatives."},{"q":"\"He came in order to teach\" and \"He wanted to go\" use the infinitive in the same function.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. In the first, the infinitive expresses purpose; in the second, it serves as the object."},{"q":"In \"To speak is essential,\" which single noun could take the place of \"To speak\" in the same role?","kind":"concept","options":["Quickly","Speech","Spoke","Very"],"answer":1,"explain":"The infinitive is playing a noun-like role as subject, so a noun such as \"speech\" fits the same slot."},{"q":"Which pair shows the same English verb first as an ordinary verb and then as an infinitive?","kind":"concept","options":["\"He wrote\" / \"he writes\"","\"He writes\" / \"to write\"","\"to write\" / \"to believe\"","\"He wanted\" / \"he came\""],"answer":1,"explain":"\"To write\" is \"to\" + the verb, the English infinitive. \"He writes\" is an ordinary verb form of the same verb."},{"q":"In \"To believe is essential,\" what does the infinitive still carry even though it is the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["The meaning of the verb \"believe\"","A command to believe","A completed past action","A plural subject"],"answer":0,"explain":"Even in a noun-like role, an infinitive carries the action or meaning of the verb."},{"q":"In \"She hoped to write,\" which word is the main verb?","kind":"concept","options":["She","hoped","to write","write"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Hoped\" is the main verb. \"To write\" is the infinitive: it carries the verb's meaning but plays a noun-like role as what she hoped."},{"q":"Which of these sentences contains an infinitive?","kind":"concept","options":["\"I wrote yesterday.\"","\"I write every day.\"","\"I hope to write soon.\"","\"Write it down!\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"To write\" is \"to\" + the verb. The other sentences use ordinary verb forms or a command."},{"q":"\"To believe is essential\" and \"He wanted to go\" both contain infinitives. How do their roles differ?","kind":"concept","options":["Cause in the first, purpose in the second","Object in the first, subject in the second","Purpose in the first, cause in the second","Subject in the first, object in the second"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"To believe\" is the subject of its sentence, while \"to go\" is what he wanted, the object."},{"q":"In \"He came in order to teach,\" which words signal purpose in English?","kind":"concept","options":["in order to","He came","came in","He"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"In order to\" introduces the purpose of his coming, with the infinitive \"to teach.\""},{"q":"Which summary of infinitives matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Nouns with no verbal meaning, rare in the NT","Verbal forms in noun-like roles, extremely common in the NT","Commands that always state their subject","Forms that exist in English but not in Greek"],"answer":1,"explain":"Infinitives carry a verb's meaning, play noun-like roles, and are extremely common in the NT."}],"vb_19":[{"q":"In \"The running man,\" what does \"running\" modify?","kind":"concept","options":["man","the","a later verb","nothing at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Running\" is a participle modifying \"man\": it describes the man by what he is doing."},{"q":"Which phrase does NOT contain a participle?","kind":"concept","options":["\"The running man\"","\"The written word\"","\"The tall man\"","\"Having spoken, he left\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Tall\" describes the man but does not come from a verb, so it has no verbal force. \"Running,\" \"written,\" and \"having spoken\" all come from verbs."},{"q":"In \"Having spoken, he left,\" what does \"having spoken\" do?","kind":"concept","options":["Names the person who left","Gives a command","Gives context for the main action","Serves as the main verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"The participle \"having spoken\" sets the scene for the main action, \"he left.\""},{"q":"In \"Having spoken, he left,\" which word is the main action?","kind":"concept","options":["having","spoken","left","he"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Left\" is the main action; the participle phrase \"having spoken\" gives context for it."},{"q":"What does a participle's \"verbal force\" refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["It names a person","It modifies a noun","It always gives a command","It comes from a verb and carries action or process"],"answer":3,"explain":"Verbal force means the participle comes from a verb and carries action or process."},{"q":"What does a participle's \"adjectival function\" refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["It modifies a noun or functions descriptively","It carries action or process","It gives a command","It replaces the main verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adjectival function means the participle modifies a noun or works descriptively, like an adjective."},{"q":"Participles are rare in the Greek New Testament.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Participles are exceptionally common; you will meet them on nearly every page of the NT."},{"q":"Which of these is a use of participles named in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Identifying or describing people through what they are doing","Numbering the chapters of a book","Marking the end of a sentence","Showing the order of the alphabet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek writers used participles to identify or describe people through what they are doing."},{"q":"Which list names things participles can communicate in a compact form?","kind":"concept","options":["Article, gender, or alphabet","Time, cause, manner, or condition","Only the time of the action","Questions and exclamations"],"answer":1,"explain":"Participles let writers express time, cause, manner, or condition compactly."},{"q":"Greek writers used participles to add detail about what?","kind":"concept","options":["The spelling of the main verb","Circumstances surrounding the main verb","The accents of nearby words","The order of the alphabet"],"answer":1,"explain":"One major use of participles is to add detail about the circumstances around the main verb."},{"q":"Which five features does the lesson say a participle has?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense, voice, mood, person, and number","Tense, voice, case, gender, and number","Case, gender, number, person, and accent","Tense, mood, person, case, and article"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists tense, voice, case, gender, and number for participles."},{"q":"The lesson describes recognizing and handling participles as optional for reading the NT well.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson calls learning to recognize and handle participles correctly essential for reading the NT well."},{"q":"Where are the specific participle types covered in detail?","kind":"concept","options":["Earlier in this lesson","In the Advanced Track","In the alphabet lessons","In the preposition lessons"],"answer":1,"explain":"This lesson lays the foundation; the Advanced Track covers specific participle types in detail."},{"q":"What does this lesson set out to do with participles?","kind":"concept","options":["Cover every participle type in detail","Introduce the concept and set the foundation","Teach all participle endings","Replace the Advanced Track"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson introduces the concept of participles and sets the foundation for later study."},{"q":"Which English phrase contains a participle modifying a noun?","kind":"concept","options":["\"the broken cup\"","\"Break it!\"","\"He breaks it.\"","\"She broke it.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Broken\" comes from the verb \"break\" and modifies \"cup,\" just as \"written\" modifies \"word.\""},{"q":"Why is \"running\" in \"The running man\" more than a plain adjective?","kind":"concept","options":["It comes from a verb and carries action","It is the subject of the sentence","It gives a command to the man","It names the man"],"answer":0,"explain":"A participle has verbal force: \"running\" comes from the verb \"run\" and carries action, not just a quality."},{"q":"Why is \"running\" in \"The running man\" more than a plain verb?","kind":"concept","options":["It is the main verb of a sentence","It modifies the noun \"man\"","It gives a command","It states a completed fact"],"answer":1,"explain":"A participle also has adjectival function: \"running\" describes the noun \"man.\""},{"q":"What does the lesson call the participle's mix of verbal meaning and modifying nouns?","kind":"concept","options":["A minor detail","A rare exception","A spelling rule","A powerful combination"],"answer":3,"explain":"Carrying verbal meaning while modifying nouns makes the participle a powerful combination."},{"q":"How does the lesson's introduction describe the participle as a grammatical tool?","kind":"concept","options":["Rich and flexible","Rare and rigid","Simple and limited","Old and unused"],"answer":0,"explain":"By combining the force of a verb with the function of an adjective, the participle becomes a rich and flexible tool."},{"q":"The lesson admits that a participle carries a lot of information.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. With tense, voice, case, gender, and number, a participle carries a lot of information, but each element follows recognizable patterns."},{"q":"Why does the lesson say a participle's many features need not be discouraging?","kind":"concept","options":["Participles appear only rarely","Each element follows recognizable patterns","Participles have no endings","The features never change"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tense, voice, case, gender, and number each follow recognizable patterns, so they can be learned."},{"q":"Which sentence uses a participle to give context for the main action?","kind":"concept","options":["\"Sleep now!\"","\"She ate, and she slept.\"","\"She slept well.\"","\"Having eaten, she slept.\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Like \"Having spoken, he left,\" the phrase \"having eaten\" sets the context for the main action, \"she slept.\""},{"q":"Phrases like \"the one teaching\" point to a person by an activity. Which participle use from the lesson does this illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Showing the main verb's person","Giving a direct command","Marking the end of a clause","Identifying people through what they are doing"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek writers used participles to identify or describe people through what they are doing."},{"q":"Which of these phrases uses a participle to give context for a main action rather than to modify a noun directly?","kind":"concept","options":["\"The written word\"","\"The running man\"","\"Having spoken, he left.\"","\"The broken cup\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Having spoken\" gives context for \"he left,\" while the other phrases use a participle to describe a noun."},{"q":"How does the lesson's introduction describe participles among NT verbal forms?","kind":"concept","options":["Important but seldom used","Among the most important and most frequently occurring","Frequent but of little importance","Rare and mostly decorative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The introduction calls participles one of the most important and most frequently occurring verbal forms in the Greek NT."},{"q":"A participle can only modify a noun; it cannot give context for a main action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. In \"Having spoken, he left,\" the participle gives context for the main action rather than modifying a noun."},{"q":"Which English sentence contains no participle?","kind":"concept","options":["\"The written word stands.\"","\"Having spoken, he left.\"","\"The running man left.\"","\"He left.\""],"answer":3,"explain":"\"He left\" has only a main verb. Each of the others includes a participle: having spoken, running, or written."},{"q":"In \"the singing child,\" what is \"singing\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A noun naming a song","A command to sing","The main verb of a sentence","A participle modifying \"child\""],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Singing\" comes from a verb and describes \"child,\" like \"running\" in \"the running man.\""},{"q":"\"Having spoken, he left\" best illustrates which use of participles listed in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Identifying people through what they are doing","Giving a command","Naming the main verb's subject","Adding detail about circumstances surrounding the main verb"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Having spoken\" adds a circumstance to the main verb \"left\": it sets the scene for his leaving."},{"q":"Which statement matches the lesson's view of participles?","kind":"concept","options":["Commands that always state their subject","Noun forms with no verbal meaning, found rarely","Verb-based forms that modify or describe, found on nearly every NT page","Forms used only in the Advanced Track"],"answer":2,"explain":"Participles carry verbal meaning, function descriptively, and are exceptionally common in the NT."}],"vb_20":[{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect Active Indicative","Present Active Indicative","Aorist Active Indicative","Future Active Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύσω is the Future Active Indicative, the second principal part of λύω.","greek":"λύσω"},{"q":"Which form is the Aorist Active Indicative principal part of λύω?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύθην","ἔλυσα","λύσω","λέλυκα"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Aorist Active Indicative of λύω is ἔλυσα. ἐλύθην is the aorist passive.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect Active Indicative","Perfect Middle/Passive Indicative","Aorist Active Indicative","Future Active Indicative"],"answer":0,"explain":"λέλυκα is the Perfect Active Indicative. The perfect middle/passive is λέλυμαι.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which form is the Perfect Middle/Passive Indicative principal part of λύω?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύθην","λέλυκα","λέλυμαι","ἔλυσα"],"answer":2,"explain":"λέλυμαι is the Perfect Middle/Passive Indicative. λέλυκα is the perfect active.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"A learner calls this a perfect form. What is it actually?","kind":"form","options":["The Present Active Indicative","The Perfect Middle/Passive Indicative","The Future Active Indicative","The Aorist Passive Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐλύθην is the sixth principal part, the Aorist Passive Indicative. The perfect forms are λέλυκα and λέλυμαι.","greek":"ἐλύθην"},{"q":"What distinguishes these two principal parts?","kind":"form","options":["Indicative versus imperative mood","Present versus future tense","Aorist versus perfect tense","Active versus middle/passive voice"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both are perfect indicative forms. λέλυκα is active; λέλυμαι is middle/passive.","greek":"λέλυκα · λέλυμαι"},{"q":"How are these two principal parts related?","kind":"form","options":["Same tense (perfect), different voice","Same voice (active), different tense","Same tense (aorist), different voice","Same voice (passive), different tense"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυσα is the Aorist Active Indicative and ἐλύθην is the Aorist Passive Indicative: same tense, different voice.","greek":"ἔλυσα · ἐλύθην"},{"q":"In the lesson's list of six principal parts, which one comes fourth?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect Active Indicative: λέλυκα","Aorist Active Indicative: ἔλυσα","Perfect Middle/Passive Indicative: λέλυμαι","Future Active Indicative: λύσω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The order is present, future, aorist active, perfect active, perfect middle/passive, aorist passive. Fourth is λέλυκα."},{"q":"Which list gives the principal parts of λύω in the lesson's order?","kind":"form","options":["λύω, λύσω, ἔλυσα, λέλυκα, λέλυμαι, ἐλύθην","λύω, ἔλυσα, λύσω, λέλυκα, ἐλύθην, λέλυμαι","λύω, λύσω, λέλυκα, ἔλυσα, λέλυμαι, ἐλύθην","λύω, λύσω, ἔλυσα, λέλυμαι, λέλυκα, ἐλύθην"],"answer":0,"explain":"Present, future, aorist active, perfect active, perfect middle/passive, aorist passive: λύω, λύσω, ἔλυσα, λέλυκα, λέλυμαι, ἐλύθην."},{"q":"This form is the aorist principal part of which verb?","kind":"form","options":["ἔρχομαι","λύω","λέγω","ὁράω"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's example: λέγω (I say) has the aorist εἶπον (I said).","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"What does this aorist form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I say","I said","I loosed","I will say"],"answer":1,"explain":"εἶπον means \"I said.\" Without knowing this principal part, you might not connect it with \"say.\"","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"Why can't you work out εἶπον from λέγω?","kind":"concept","options":["It adds -σα- just like ἔλυσα","It follows the λύω pattern exactly","Nothing in the present form predicts the stem change","It is a perfect form, not an aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"λέγω gives no hint of the new stem in εἶπον, so the aorist principal part has to be learned."},{"q":"What person and number is every principal part?","kind":"form","options":["Second person singular","Third person singular","First person plural","First person singular"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each principal part is the first person singular form of a major tense/voice combination."},{"q":"What is the main payoff of knowing a verb's principal parts?","kind":"concept","options":["You automatically know its English meaning","You no longer need to learn endings","You can recognize virtually every form it takes in the NT","You can identify every noun related to it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Knowing the principal parts lets you recognize virtually every form a verb takes across the entire NT."},{"q":"Which verbs have unexpected forms that you can recognize only if you have seen them before?","kind":"concept","options":["Second aorists and irregular verbs","Verbs that follow λύω","Only future active forms","Only first person forms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Many verbs follow the λύω model, but second aorists and irregular verbs have forms you must have seen to recognize."},{"q":"What role does λύω play in this lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["An irregular verb with unexpected forms","A model verb whose pattern many verbs follow","A second aorist verb","A verb with no principal parts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Many Greek verbs behave predictably and follow the principal part patterns of a model verb like λύω.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Each principal part represents a major tense/voice combination.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Each of the six is the first person singular of a major tense/voice combination."},{"q":"A Present Passive Indicative form is one of the six principal parts listed in the lesson.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The passive principal parts listed are the Perfect Middle/Passive (λέλυμαι) and the Aorist Passive (ἐλύθην)."},{"q":"How does the lesson's introduction describe memorizing principal parts?","kind":"concept","options":["Something only for advanced scholars","A minor exercise that can be skipped","One of the highest-leverage investments a student can make","Useful only for rare verbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Because principal parts unlock a verb's whole system, memorizing them gives a large return."},{"q":"What does the lesson say memorizing principal parts dramatically increases?","kind":"concept","options":["Your ability to read Greek with confidence and speed","The number of verbs you need to learn","Your knowledge of noun cases","The length of each verb form"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says it dramatically increases your ability to read Greek with confidence and speed."},{"q":"Every Greek verb follows the principal part pattern of λύω exactly.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Many verbs follow the λύω model, but some common verbs, especially second aorists and irregular verbs, have unexpected forms."},{"q":"How many of the six principal parts are active forms?","kind":"form","options":["Four","Three","Two","Five"],"answer":0,"explain":"Present, future, aorist, and perfect active (λύω, λύσω, ἔλυσα, λέλυκα) are active. λέλυμαι and ἐλύθην are not."},{"q":"How many of the six principal parts are perfect forms?","kind":"form","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two are perfect: λέλυκα (perfect active) and λέλυμαι (perfect middle/passive)."},{"q":"Which mood are all six principal parts?","kind":"form","options":["Imperative","Indicative","Infinitive","Participle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every principal part in the list is an indicative form, such as Present Active Indicative."},{"q":"Which tense does NOT appear among the six principal parts?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect","Present","Future","Imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"The principal parts cover present, future, aorist, and perfect forms. No imperfect form is in the list."},{"q":"What are principal parts, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A set of base verb forms","A list of a verb's English meanings","The endings of Greek nouns","The accent rules for verbs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Principal parts are a set of base verb forms. From them you can recognize the rest of a verb's forms."},{"q":"Which other principal part of λύω begins with the same λε- as this form?","kind":"form","options":["ἐλύθην","λέλυμαι","ἔλυσα","λύσω"],"answer":1,"explain":"λέλυκα and λέλυμαι are the two perfect principal parts, and both begin with λε-.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which principal part would you need to know to recognize εἶπον as a form of λέγω?","kind":"concept","options":["The future active","The aorist active","The perfect middle/passive","The aorist passive"],"answer":1,"explain":"εἶπον is the aorist of λέγω. Without knowing the aorist principal part, you might not recognize it as \"said.\"","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"Which pair of principal parts has the same voice and mood but a different tense?","kind":"form","options":["λέλυκα and ἐλύθην","λέλυκα and λέλυμαι","ἔλυσα and ἐλύθην","λύω and λύσω"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύω (present active indicative) and λύσω (future active indicative) differ only in tense. Each of the other pairs differs in voice."},{"q":"For which verbs does the lesson especially recommend memorizing principal parts?","kind":"concept","options":["Only verbs that follow λύω","Only rare verbs","The most common verbs","Only verbs with no second aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"Memorizing principal parts for the most common verbs dramatically increases your ability to read Greek with confidence and speed."}],"vb_28":[{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose","I fill","I love","I see"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπάω means \"I love.\" It is the lesson's example of an α-contract verb.","greek":"ἀγαπάω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I am fond of","I love","I fill / fulfill","I loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"πληρόω means \"I fill\" or \"I fulfill.\" It is the lesson's ο-contract example.","greek":"πληρόω"},{"q":"Which contract family does this verb belong to?","kind":"form","options":["α-contract","ε-contract","ο-contract","Not a contract verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem of φιλέω ends in ε, so it is an ε-contract.","greek":"φιλέω"},{"q":"Which contract family does this verb belong to?","kind":"form","options":["Not a contract verb","α-contract","ε-contract","ο-contract"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stem of πληρόω ends in ο, which makes it an ο-contract.","greek":"πληρόω"},{"q":"What uncontracted combination underlies this form?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπά + ω","ἀγαπά + ομεν","ἀγαπά + ουσι","ἀγαπά + ετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπῶσι (they love) comes from ἀγαπά + ουσι. Do not confuse it with ἀγαπῶμεν (we love), from ἀγαπά + ομεν.","greek":"ἀγαπῶσι"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["we love","you love","they love","you all love"],"answer":3,"explain":"φιλεῖτε comes from φιλέ + ετε and means \"you all love.\"","greek":"φιλεῖτε"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["they fill","we fill","you all fill","he/she fills"],"answer":0,"explain":"πληροῦσι comes from πληρό + ουσι and means \"they fill.\"","greek":"πληροῦσι"},{"q":"What uncontracted combination underlies this form?","kind":"form","options":["πληρό + εις","πληρό + ει","πληρό + ω","πληρό + ετε"],"answer":0,"explain":"πληροῖς (you fill) comes from πληρό + εις. Compare πληροῖ (he/she fills), from πληρό + ει.","greek":"πληροῖς"},{"q":"What is the contracted form of this combination?","kind":"form","options":["φιλεῖ","φιλεῖς","φιλῶ","φιλοῦσι"],"answer":0,"explain":"φιλέ + ει contracts to φιλεῖ, \"he/she loves.\"","greek":"φιλέ + ει"},{"q":"What is the contracted form of this combination?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπῶσι","ἀγαπῶμεν","ἀγαπᾷς","ἀγαπᾶτε"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαπά + ετε contracts to ἀγαπᾶτε, \"you all love.\"","greek":"ἀγαπά + ετε"},{"q":"What is the contracted form of this combination?","kind":"form","options":["πληροῖ","πληροῦμεν","πληροῦτε","πληρῶ"],"answer":1,"explain":"πληρό + ομεν contracts to πληροῦμεν, \"we fill.\"","greek":"πληρό + ομεν"},{"q":"In an ε-contract, what do ε + ε produce?","kind":"form","options":["ου","ει","οι","ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"ε + ε contracts to ει, as in φιλέ + ετε becoming φιλεῖτε."},{"q":"In an ε-contract, what do ε + ο produce?","kind":"form","options":["ει","ου","οι","ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"ε + ο contracts to ου, as in φιλέ + ομεν becoming φιλοῦμεν."},{"q":"Contract verb stems end in which vowels?","kind":"concept","options":["α, ε, or ο","ι, υ, or ω","α, η, or ω","ε, ι, or υ"],"answer":0,"explain":"The three contract families have stems ending in α, ε, or ο."},{"q":"What does \"contract\" mean in this lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The ending is dropped entirely","The first syllable is doubled","A κ is added before the ending","Two vowels merge into a single sound"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the stem vowel meets the connecting vowel of the ending, the two contract, or merge, into one sound."},{"q":"Which accent mark does the lesson call a clue in contract forms?","kind":"concept","options":["The circumflex","The acute","The grave","The rough breathing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Many present contract forms carry a circumflex, which signals that vowels have merged."},{"q":"Why do many present contract forms carry a circumflex?","kind":"concept","options":["The verb is in the perfect tense","Two vowels have collapsed into one long vowel or diphthong","The stem has been reduplicated","The ending has been removed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The circumflex often appears because two vowels collapsed into a single long vowel or diphthong."},{"q":"Contraction is random and cannot be predicted.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Contraction is not chaos; it follows predictable rules."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I love","you all love","they love","we love"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαπῶμεν comes from ἀγαπά + ομεν and means \"we love.\"","greek":"ἀγαπῶμεν"},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I fill","you fill","he/she fills","they fill"],"answer":2,"explain":"πληροῖ comes from πληρό + ει and means \"he/she fills.\"","greek":"πληροῖ"},{"q":"What are the person, number, and meaning of this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd person singular: he/she loves","1st person plural: we love","2nd person plural: you all love","3rd person plural: they love"],"answer":3,"explain":"φιλοῦσι comes from φιλέ + ουσι, the third person plural: \"they love.\"","greek":"φιλοῦσι"},{"q":"Which form of this verb means \"you love\" (one person)?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπᾶτε","ἀγαπᾷ","ἀγαπᾷς","ἀγαπῶ"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπά + εις contracts to ἀγαπᾷς, \"you love.\" ἀγαπᾷ is \"he/she loves\" and ἀγαπᾶτε is \"you all love.\"","greek":"ἀγαπάω"},{"q":"What is the stem of ἀγαπάω, which carries the meaning \"love\"?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπα-","φιλε-","πληρο-","ἀγαπ-"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem of ἀγαπάω is ἀγαπα-, ending in α, which makes it an α-contract.","greek":"ἀγαπάω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I fill / fulfill","I love / I am fond of","I loose","I see"],"answer":1,"explain":"φιλέω means \"I love\" or \"I am fond of.\" It is the lesson's ε-contract example.","greek":"φιλέω"},{"q":"This form means \"you all fill.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. πληρό + ετε contracts to πληροῦτε, \"you all fill.\"","greek":"πληροῦτε"},{"q":"This form is third person singular, meaning \"he/she loves.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. φιλεῖς comes from φιλέ + εις and means \"you love.\" The form for \"he/she loves\" is φιλεῖ.","greek":"φιλεῖς"},{"q":"What do these three forms have in common?","kind":"form","options":["Each means \"we ___\": first person plural","Each is third person singular","Each is an ε-contract","Each has the same stem vowel"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each comes from stem + ομεν: \"we love,\" \"we love,\" and \"we fill.\" They belong to three different contract families.","greek":"φιλοῦμεν · ἀγαπῶμεν · πληροῦμεν"},{"q":"In ἀγαπῶ, which two vowels merged into ῶ?","kind":"form","options":["ο and ω","ε and ω","α and ω","α and ε"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπά + ω: the stem vowel α and the ending's ω merge into ῶ.","greek":"ἀγαπῶ"},{"q":"Which verb is the lesson's example of an α-contract?","kind":"form","options":["πληρόω","φιλέω","ἀγαπάω","λύω"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπάω has the stem ἀγαπα-, ending in α, so it is the α-contract example."},{"q":"What is the contracted form of this combination?","kind":"form","options":["φιλεῖ","φιλοῦσι","φιλῶ","φιλεῖς"],"answer":2,"explain":"φιλέ + ω contracts to φιλῶ, \"I love.\"","greek":"φιλέ + ω"}],"vb_21":[{"q":"How many categories does a full parse of a Greek verb identify?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Six","Five"],"answer":3,"explain":"A full parse identifies five things: tense, voice, mood, person, and number."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five verb parsing categories?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Tense","Case","Mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"The five categories are tense, voice, mood, person, and number. Case is not one of them."},{"q":"Which of these is a voice?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfect","Middle","Optative","Plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The voices are active, middle, and passive. Imperfect is a tense, optative a mood, and plural a number."},{"q":"Which parsing category does \"optative\" belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["Number","Tense","Voice","Mood"],"answer":3,"explain":"Optative is listed under mood, along with indicative, imperative, subjunctive, infinitive, and participle."},{"q":"Which parsing category does \"pluperfect\" belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Tense","Voice","Person"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pluperfect is one of the six tenses the lesson lists."},{"q":"The lesson lists infinitive and participle under the category of mood.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The mood list includes indicative, imperative, subjunctive, optative, infinitive, and participle."},{"q":"What are the two options for number?","kind":"concept","options":["First and third","Singular and plural","Active and passive","Present and future"],"answer":1,"explain":"Number is either singular or plural."},{"q":"Which list gives the three persons?","kind":"concept","options":["active, middle, passive","singular, plural, dual","first, second, third","present, future, aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"Person is first, second, or third. The other lists mix in number, voice, and tense."},{"q":"How many tenses does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Four","Five","Six","Three"],"answer":2,"explain":"Present, imperfect, future, aorist, perfect, and pluperfect: six tenses."},{"q":"How many voices does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"answer":2,"explain":"There are three voices: active, middle, and passive."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I write","you write","he writes","I wrote"],"answer":0,"explain":"γράφω is Present Active Indicative, 1st person singular: \"I write.\"","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"What are the person and number of this form?","kind":"form","options":["1st person singular","2nd person singular","3rd person singular","1st person plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"The full parse of γράφω is Present Active Indicative, 1st person singular.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does parsing help you understand?","kind":"concept","options":["How to pronounce the word","What the verb is communicating","Which noun comes next","Where the word came from historically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Parsing is not an end in itself: it is a tool that helps you understand what the verb is communicating."},{"q":"What does this form mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["He/she loosed","I loosed","They loosed","He/she looses"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔλυσεν is aorist, 3rd person singular: \"he/she loosed.\"","greek":"ἔλυσεν"},{"q":"Which is the full parse of this form, written in the standard order?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist Active Indicative, 3rd person singular","Active Aorist Indicative, 3rd person singular","Aorist Active Imperative, 3rd person singular","Aorist Passive Indicative, 3rd person singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔλυσεν is aorist (tense), active (voice), indicative (mood), 3rd person singular, listed in T-V-M-P-N order.","greek":"ἔλυσεν"},{"q":"Besides \"he/she loosed,\" what other translation does the lesson give for this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["He/she came","He/she wrote","He/she said","He/she destroyed"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson translates ἔλυσεν as \"he/she loosed\" or \"he/she destroyed.\"","greek":"ἔλυσεν"},{"q":"This form comes from which verb?","kind":"form","options":["λέγω","γράφω","λύω","ἔρχομαι"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυσεν is a form of λύω: its aorist active indicative, 3rd person singular.","greek":"ἔλυσεν"},{"q":"A parse reads \"Present Active Indicative, 2nd person plural.\" Which word gives the mood?","kind":"concept","options":["Plural","Present","Active","Indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Indicative is a mood. Present is the tense, active the voice, and plural the number."},{"q":"A student parses γράφω as \"Present Passive Indicative, 1st person singular.\" Which category is wrong?","kind":"form","options":["Tense","Voice","Mood","Person"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφω is active: \"I write.\" The student's tense, mood, person, and number are all correct.","greek":"γράφω"},{"q":"Parsing is an end in itself.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Parsing is a tool that helps you understand what the verb is communicating."},{"q":"Where does the lesson say you will encounter full parse statements?","kind":"concept","options":["Only in this lesson","In lexicons, commentaries, and Greek tools","Only in the Greek alphabet chart","Only in English dictionaries"],"answer":1,"explain":"Full parse statements are the kind you will meet in lexicons, commentaries, and Greek tools."},{"q":"Every Greek verb you encounter can be described with these five categories.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Tense, voice, mood, person, and number describe every Greek verb."},{"q":"Person and number are separate categories in a full parse.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Person (first, second, third) and number (singular, plural) are two of the five categories, as in \"1st person singular.\""},{"q":"Which parsing categories have the same value in both of these forms?","kind":"form","options":["Voice, mood, and number","Tense, voice, and person","Tense and person","Mood and person"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both are active, indicative, and singular. They differ in tense (present vs. aorist) and person (1st vs. 3rd).","greek":"γράφω · ἔλυσεν"},{"q":"Which pair of terms belongs to the same parsing category?","kind":"concept","options":["Middle and optative","Imperfect and imperative","Aorist and pluperfect","Plural and third"],"answer":2,"explain":"Aorist and pluperfect are both tenses. Middle is a voice and optative a mood; imperfect is a tense and imperative a mood; plural is a number and third a person."},{"q":"Which of these is a tense in the lesson's list?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperative","Imperfect","Middle","Plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"Imperfect is a tense. Do not confuse it with imperative, which is a mood."},{"q":"γράφω means \"I write.\" Which full parse would fit \"they write\" in the same tense, voice, and mood?","kind":"concept","options":["Present Active Indicative, 3rd person plural","Present Active Indicative, 1st person plural","Aorist Active Indicative, 3rd person plural","Present Active Indicative, 3rd person singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only person and number change: \"they\" is 3rd person plural, while tense, voice, and mood stay present, active, indicative."},{"q":"Which of these is a complete parse, naming all five categories?","kind":"concept","options":["Present Active Indicative","Active, 1st person singular","Present Active Indicative, 1st person singular","Aorist, 3rd person"],"answer":2,"explain":"A full parse names tense, voice, mood, person, and number. Only \"Present Active Indicative, 1st person singular\" includes all five."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what becomes natural and fast with practice?","kind":"concept","options":["Parsing","Memorizing the alphabet","Pronunciation","Translating word by word"],"answer":0,"explain":"With practice, describing verbs by the five categories becomes natural and fast."},{"q":"Which part of the parse of ἔλυσεν tells you the subject is \"he/she\"?","kind":"form","options":["Active","Aorist","3rd person singular","Indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Person and number identify the subject: 3rd person singular gives \"he/she.\"","greek":"ἔλυσεν"}],"vb_22":[{"q":"Which tense does the lesson's recap describe as \"anticipated action\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Imperfect","Future","Perfect"],"answer":2,"explain":"The future presents action that is anticipated, something still to come."},{"q":"How does the lesson's recap describe the imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["Anticipated action","Past ongoing","Action as a whole","Completed with continuing result"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfect presents action as ongoing in the past."},{"q":"How does the lesson's recap describe the aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing action","Past ongoing","Anticipated action","Action as a whole"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist presents the action as a whole."},{"q":"Which tense does the recap describe as \"completed with continuing result\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfect","Aorist","Imperfect","Present"],"answer":0,"explain":"The perfect presents an action as completed, with a result that continues."},{"q":"How does the recap describe the Present Active Indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Past ongoing","Ongoing action","Anticipated action","Completed with continuing result"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Present Active Indicative presents ongoing action."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the verb's ending signals who is doing the action (its person and number).","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The verb tells you who is doing the action through its ending, which shows person and number."},{"q":"What does voice communicate, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject's relationship to the action","When the action happens","Who is doing the action","The type of communication"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voice shows how the subject relates to the action."},{"q":"What does mood communicate, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Who is doing the action","The subject's relationship to the action","The type of communication","How many people are involved"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mood tells you the type of communication the verb carries."},{"q":"In the lesson's list of what a verb communicates, what does tense tell you?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject's relationship to the action","Who is doing the action","When or how the action is viewed","The type of communication"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tense communicates when or how the action is viewed."},{"q":"You must be able to parse every form immediately before you begin reading the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says you are equipped to begin reading even if you cannot parse every form immediately."},{"q":"In the lesson's list, what does \"What is happening\" refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["The person","The action","The mood","The voice"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first thing a verb communicates is what is happening: the action."},{"q":"In the steps for following Greek sentence flow, what is the second step?","kind":"concept","options":["See how the other words relate to the verb","Locate the main verb","Identify what the verb communicates","Translate naturally"],"answer":2,"explain":"After locating the main verb, you identify what it communicates: tense, voice, mood, person, and number."},{"q":"Put the lesson's reading steps in order: (a) translate naturally, (b) locate the main verb, (c) see how the other words relate, (d) identify what the verb communicates.","kind":"concept","options":["b, d, a, c","b, c, d, a","d, b, c, a","b, d, c, a"],"answer":3,"explain":"Locate the main verb, identify what it communicates, see how the other words relate to it, then translate naturally."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe grammar?","kind":"concept","options":["A tool for reading, not the destination","The destination of all reading","A replacement for reading","An extra with little use in reading"],"answer":0,"explain":"Grammar is a tool: use it to hear what the author is saying."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how does recognition develop?","kind":"concept","options":["It is complete after one lesson","It comes only from memorizing charts","It stays the same over time","It builds with exposure"],"answer":3,"explain":"Recognition builds with exposure: every sentence you read trains your eye further."},{"q":"What happens to verb spotting as you read more Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["You no longer need to find the verb","It gets harder with each passage","Verb endings stop mattering","Endings grow familiar and recognition gets faster"],"answer":3,"explain":"Greek readers develop an instinct: the endings become familiar, and recognition speeds up with each passage."},{"q":"Which voices does the lesson's recap list?","kind":"concept","options":["Singular, Plural","Indicative, Imperative","Present, Future","Active, Middle, Passive"],"answer":3,"explain":"The three voices introduced are active, middle, and passive."},{"q":"Which two moods does the lesson's recap list?","kind":"concept","options":["Present and Aorist","Active and Passive","Indicative and Imperative","Singular and Plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"The recap lists the indicative and imperative moods."},{"q":"Besides tenses, voices, and moods, which verbal forms does the recap list?","kind":"concept","options":["Infinitives and Participles","Nouns and Adjectives","Articles and Pronouns","Prepositions and Conjunctions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The recap also lists infinitives and participles among the systems you have met."},{"q":"Which two tenses in the recap both involve ongoing action?","kind":"concept","options":["Future and aorist","Aorist and perfect","Present and imperfect","Perfect and future"],"answer":2,"explain":"The recap describes the Present Active Indicative as ongoing action and the imperfect as past ongoing. The imperfect places the ongoing action in the past."},{"q":"According to the lesson, reading Greek is NOT about what?","kind":"concept","options":["Following the flow of the author's communication","Locating the main verb","Translating word by word","Seeing how words relate to the verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reading Greek is not about translating word by word; it is about following the flow of the author's communication."},{"q":"When you identify what a verb communicates, which five things are you naming?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense, voice, mood, person, number","Case, gender, number, tense, voice","Subject, object, verb, noun, article","Present, future, aorist, perfect, imperfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step two of reading is identifying the verb's tense, voice, mood, person, and number."},{"q":"The lesson says that with these foundations you are equipped to begin reading the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. With the systems from this track, you can begin reading the NT and identifying what you see."},{"q":"Once you have found the verb, how does the rest of the sentence relate to it?","kind":"concept","options":["The other words replace the verb","The other words can be ignored","The other words must be parsed first","Everything else relates to the verb"],"answer":3,"explain":"Find the verb first; everything else in the sentence relates to it."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the systems learned in the Basic Verb Track form?","kind":"concept","options":["A minor supplement to vocabulary","The backbone of all Greek reading","An introduction to nouns only","The final stage of Greek study"],"answer":1,"explain":"The verb systems from this track form the backbone of all Greek reading."},{"q":"According to the lesson's closing, what can you go on to next?","kind":"concept","options":["The alphabet lessons again","The Advanced Track","Nothing further","A course on nouns only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Having completed the Basic Verb Track, you are ready for the Advanced Track whenever you choose."},{"q":"A student stops reading a passage because she cannot parse one form. What approach fits the lesson's advice?","kind":"concept","options":["Stop until every form can be parsed","Keep reading, since recognition builds with exposure","Switch to translating word by word","Skip the verbs and read only the nouns"],"answer":1,"explain":"You can begin reading before you can parse every form; each sentence you read trains your eye further."},{"q":"In the lesson's steps, what does it mean to \"translate naturally\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Let the grammar serve the meaning","Keep every word in Greek order","Leave the verb untranslated","Set the grammar aside entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"The last step is to translate naturally, letting the grammar serve the meaning."},{"q":"What does the lesson's introduction call \"the real goal of everything you have learned\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every paradigm","Following the flow of a Greek sentence","Parsing every word in a passage","Learning the alphabet perfectly"],"answer":1,"explain":"The real goal is following the flow of a Greek sentence: reading the author's communication, not translating word by word."},{"q":"The lesson says the nouns, not the verb, anchor a Greek sentence.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The verb anchors the sentence by communicating the action, who does it, how it is viewed, the subject's relationship to it, and the type of communication."}],"va_01":[{"q":"A reader asks whether the speaker views an action as complete, ongoing, or expected. Which communicative category is the reader exploring?","kind":"concept","options":["Perspective","Condition","Emphasis","Action"],"answer":0,"explain":"Perspective is how the speaker views the action: complete, ongoing, or expected."},{"q":"In the lesson's list, which category asks whether the action is contingent on something?","kind":"concept","options":["Emphasis","Progression","Condition","Expectation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Condition is about contingency: does the action depend on something else?"},{"q":"A reader wonders whether a verb is a promise, a prediction, or a command. Which category does that question belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["Perspective","Expectation","Action","Condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"Expectation covers whether the statement is a promise, a prediction, or a command."},{"q":"Under \"progression,\" the lesson asks whether an action is developing, static, or what?","kind":"concept","options":["Repeated","Passive","Punctiliar","Conditional"],"answer":2,"explain":"Progression asks whether the action is developing, static, or punctiliar."},{"q":"In the lesson, which question goes with the category \"Emphasis\"?","kind":"concept","options":["What is being done?","Is the action contingent on something?","How does the speaker view the action?","What does the writer want to highlight?"],"answer":3,"explain":"Emphasis is about what the writer wants to highlight."},{"q":"According to the lesson, which element of a Greek sentence carries the most communicative weight?","kind":"concept","options":["The noun","The article","The conjunction","The verb"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says no other single element of a Greek sentence carries as much communicative weight as the verb."},{"q":"Once you have parsed a verb as a present active indicative, your work on that verb is finished.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says that at that point you are not finished; you are just beginning."},{"q":"A student says, \"I parsed the verb, so I already know what it means here.\" Which line from the lesson best corrects this?","kind":"concept","options":["Each lesson builds on the last.","No other single element of a Greek sentence carries as much communicative weight as the verb.","Parsing tells you where to look. Context tells you what you find.","The goal is not to accumulate terminology."],"answer":2,"explain":"The parse points you in the right direction, but context is what tells you what the verb actually communicates."},{"q":"After identifying a verb as a present active indicative, which question does the lesson suggest asking next?","kind":"concept","options":["How many syllables does it have?","Which noun does it follow?","Is it borrowed from Hebrew?","What kind of present?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The parse opens questions such as: What kind of present? What does this active construction emphasize?"},{"q":"In the lesson's three-part summary, what does form give you?","kind":"concept","options":["The communicative weight","The grammatical signal","The meaning","The genre"],"answer":1,"explain":"Form gives you the grammatical signal; context and integration supply the rest."},{"q":"According to the intro, learning to parse Greek verbs is a tool for understanding what?","kind":"concept","options":["How manuscripts were copied","How words are spelled","How communication works","How accents shift"],"answer":2,"explain":"The intro says parsing is not an end in itself; it is a tool for understanding how communication works."},{"q":"According to the lesson's summary, what gives you the meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Integration","Form alone","Parsing alone","Terminology"],"answer":0,"explain":"Integration, holding form and context together, gives you the meaning."},{"q":"A present active indicative appears once in a letter and once in a narrative. What does the lesson say about the two?","kind":"concept","options":["The forms differ, so the meanings differ","The form is identical, but the communicative force differs","The form and the force are always identical","Only the narrative one is a real present"],"answer":1,"explain":"The form is the same in both places; what changes is the communicative force."},{"q":"Which three things does the lesson say shape meaning when an identical verb form appears in different settings?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense, voice, and mood","Person, number, and gender","Context, genre, and discourse","Augment, stem, and ending"],"answer":2,"explain":"Context, genre, and discourse shape the communicative force of the same form."},{"q":"A student analyzes a verb's voice carefully but never looks at the passage around it. Which warning from the lesson does this match?","kind":"concept","options":["Treating voice in isolation from context","Using too many explicit pronouns","Confusing a letter with a narrative","Relying too heavily on lexicons"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson warns that advanced students sometimes over-specialize, treating voice in isolation from context."},{"q":"This track encourages you to study each verb category separately rather than holding them together.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The track encourages you to hold all the categories together."},{"q":"Which of these topics does the lesson say this advanced track will work through?","kind":"concept","options":["Conditional structures","Hebrew vowel points","Latin declensions","English spelling rules"],"answer":0,"explain":"Conditional structures are on the list, along with aspect, voice, mood, participles, and extended NT reading."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the goal of this track is?","kind":"concept","options":["To accumulate terminology","To read Greek more naturally and accurately","To memorize every paradigm in isolation","To replace reading with parsing"],"answer":1,"explain":"The goal is not to pile up terms but to read Greek more naturally and accurately."},{"q":"The lesson says each lesson in this track builds on the last.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The track is cumulative: each lesson builds on the one before it."},{"q":"The intro says a verb form communicates what is happening, how the speaker perceives it, and what the speaker wants the listener to understand about what?","kind":"concept","options":["Its gender, case, and number","Its root, prefix, and suffix","Its progression, emphasis, and relationship to reality","Its spelling, accent, and breathing"],"answer":2,"explain":"The intro names progression, emphasis, and relationship to reality as part of what a verb form conveys."},{"q":"A reader asks the most basic question about a verb: what is being done? Which category from the lesson's list is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Action","Emphasis","Perspective","Condition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Action is the first category in the list: what is being done."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does a parse \"open\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A door to ask questions","A final translation","A dictionary entry","A list of synonyms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson pictures the parse as opening a door to further questions about the verb."},{"q":"Parsing exists to help you ask the right questions about a verb. According to the lesson, what is it NOT meant to do?","kind":"concept","options":["Identify the verb's tense","Identify the verb's person","Replace those questions","Identify the verb's number"],"answer":2,"explain":"Parsing helps you ask the right questions; it does not replace them."},{"q":"How many of the lesson's communicative categories can a single Greek verb form carry at once?","kind":"concept","options":["Only one at a time","Only two at a time","Only those that match the tense","All of them simultaneously"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says a single verb form can carry all of these categories simultaneously."},{"q":"How does the lesson's intro describe Greek verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Mainly markers of time","Far more than simple action words","Less important than nouns","Simple labels for actions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The intro opens by saying Greek verbs are far more than simple action words."},{"q":"Which pair of categories does the lesson name when it describes students treating one in isolation from the other?","kind":"concept","options":["Person and number","Tense and voice","Case and gender","Mood and accent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's example of over-specializing is treating tense in isolation from voice (or voice in isolation from context)."},{"q":"In the lesson's words, parsing is a means. What one-word label does the lesson give it?","kind":"concept","options":["A goal","A rule","A tool","A translation"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson calls parsing a tool: something you use, not the destination."},{"q":"According to the lesson, context tells you where to look, and parsing tells you what you find.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. It is the other way around: parsing tells you where to look, and context tells you what you find."},{"q":"The lesson says this advanced track includes extended NT reading.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Extended NT reading is the last item in the list of topics the track will cover."},{"q":"After the parse \"present active indicative,\" the lesson asks: \"What does this active construction emphasize?\" Which part of the parse does that question follow up on?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense","Voice","Mood","Person"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Active\" is the voice in the parse, so a question about the active construction follows up on voice."}],"va_02":[{"q":"What subject is embedded in this form?","kind":"form","options":["I","you (sg)","he / she / it","you (pl)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending -εις is 2nd person singular, so the embedded subject is \"you\" (one person).","greek":"λύεις"},{"q":"What subject is embedded in this form?","kind":"form","options":["they","you (pl)","we","I"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ending -ομεν is 1st person plural, so the subject \"we\" is built into the verb.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"What subject is embedded in this form?","kind":"form","options":["he / she / it","we","you (pl)","they"],"answer":3,"explain":"The ending -ουσι is 3rd person plural, so the embedded subject is \"they.\"","greek":"λύουσι"},{"q":"What are the person and number of this form?","kind":"form","options":["2nd singular","2nd plural","3rd plural","1st plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ending -ετε marks the 2nd person plural: \"you (pl).\"","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"What are the person and number of this form?","kind":"form","options":["3rd singular","2nd singular","1st singular","3rd plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ending -ει marks the 3rd person singular: he, she, or it.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"Which form has the same person as this one but is plural?","kind":"form","options":["λύουσι","λύομεν","λύετε","λύεις"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύει is 3rd singular (he/she/it); λύουσι is 3rd plural (they). Same person, different number.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"Changing λύω to λύομεν changes which feature of the embedded subject?","kind":"form","options":["Person only","Both person and number","Neither; only the tense changes","Number only"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both forms are 1st person. λύω is singular (\"I\") and λύομεν is plural (\"we\"), so only the number changes."},{"q":"Which phrase does the lesson give as its Sermon on the Mount example?","kind":"reading","options":["ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδός","ἐγὼ δὲ λέγω ὑμῖν","ζῶ δὲ οὐκέτι ἐγώ","ἐγὼ οὐκ εἰμὶ ὁ Χριστός"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἐγὼ δὲ λέγω ὑμῖν is the Sermon on the Mount example. The other phrases are the lesson's examples from John 14:6, Galatians 2:20 and John 1:20."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose","you loose","we loose","they loose"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύω is 1st person singular, \"I loose\"; the subject \"I\" is already in the ending.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"In this Sermon on the Mount example, the lesson says ἐγώ is needed for emphasis and for contrast with what?","kind":"reading","options":["What was just said","The verb's tense","The following chapter","The Greek article"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ἐγώ is not needed for grammar; it sets the speaker's words against what was just said.","greek":"ἐγὼ δὲ λέγω ὑμῖν"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the force of the pronoun in this phrase from John 1:20?","kind":"reading","options":["Emphatic denial","Identity emphasis","Contrastive","Clarification of an antecedent"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson labels John 1:20 an emphatic denial: the added ἐγώ stresses who is denying.","greek":"ἐγὼ οὐκ εἰμὶ ὁ Χριστός"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the force of the pronoun in this phrase from John 14:6?","kind":"reading","options":["Emphatic denial","Contrastive","Identity emphasis","Clarification of an antecedent"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson labels John 14:6 as identity emphasis: ἐγώ highlights who the subject is.","greek":"ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδός"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the force of the pronoun in this phrase from Galatians 2:20?","kind":"reading","options":["Identity emphasis","Emphatic denial","Clarification of an antecedent","Contrastive"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson labels Galatians 2:20 as contrastive: the pronoun sets one subject against another.","greek":"ζῶ δὲ οὐκέτι ἐγώ"},{"q":"According to the lesson, removing ἐγώ from its NT examples would still leave grammatically valid Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The verbs already carry the subject, so the Greek stays grammatical; only the communicative force changes."},{"q":"A paragraph mentions two people, and a third-person verb could refer to either one. A writer adds an explicit pronoun to make it clear. Which function is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Emphasis","Contrast","Clarification","Agreement"],"answer":2,"explain":"Clarification is when a pronoun disambiguates a third-person form that could point to more than one antecedent."},{"q":"A writer adds pronouns to two verbs to say, in effect, \"you do this, but I do that.\" Which function is the lesson describing?","kind":"concept","options":["Clarification","Contrast","Emphasis on identity alone","Agreement"],"answer":1,"explain":"Setting one person against another is contrast."},{"q":"Which explicit pronouns does the lesson say are grammatically redundant in most contexts?","kind":"form","options":["ὁ, ἡ, τό","καί, δέ, οὖν","ἐγώ, σύ, αὐτός","ἐν, εἰς, ἐκ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Because the ending carries the subject, pronouns like ἐγώ, σύ, and αὐτός are usually not needed for grammar."},{"q":"According to the lesson, explicit pronouns in Greek are mainly decorative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says explicit pronouns are not decorative; they are communicative choices."},{"q":"According to the lesson, attentiveness to what is part of what separates mechanical reading from interpretive reading?","kind":"concept","options":["Word length","Pronouns","Accent marks","Punctuation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Noticing explicit pronouns and asking why they are there moves you from mechanical to interpretive reading."},{"q":"When you notice an explicit pronoun, which question does the lesson suggest asking?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb in the future?","How many letters does it have?","Is the sentence a question?","Is there a contrast being drawn? With whom?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's reading questions include: Is this for emphasis? Is there a contrast? Is there ambiguity being resolved?"},{"q":"How many person/number slots are in the lesson's full paradigm?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Six","Eight"],"answer":2,"explain":"There are six slots: 1st, 2nd, and 3rd person, each in singular and plural."},{"q":"Which part of this form tells you the subject is \"they\"?","kind":"form","options":["The stem λύ-","The ending -ουσι","A separate pronoun","The word order"],"answer":1,"explain":"The personal ending -ουσι carries 3rd person plural, so \"they\" is embedded in the verb.","greek":"λύουσι"},{"q":"Which reference does the lesson give for ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδός?","kind":"reading","options":["John 1:20","John 14:6","Galatians 2:20","The Sermon on the Mount"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson cites John 14:6 for ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδός, its example of identity emphasis."},{"q":"Why do explicit pronouns stand out to an attentive reader of Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["Because the ending already carries the subject, so the pronoun is not needed for grammar","Because pronouns always come at the end of the sentence","Because pronouns appear only in questions","Because pronouns change the verb's tense"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the ending already identifies the subject, an added pronoun is a deliberate choice, and that is why it stands out."},{"q":"In the lesson's paradigm, the ending -ει marks the 2nd person singular.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. -ει marks the 3rd person singular (he/she/it). The 2nd singular ending is -εις."},{"q":"A writer adds a pronoun to stress that \"I am the one doing this.\" Which function does the lesson call this?","kind":"concept","options":["Emphasis","Clarification","Contrast","Agreement"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stressing the identity of the actor is emphasis."},{"q":"Which word in this phrase is the explicit pronoun?","kind":"reading","options":["ζῶ","δὲ","οὐκέτι","ἐγώ"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐγώ is the explicit pronoun; the verb ζῶ already carries a 1st singular subject.","greek":"ζῶ δὲ οὐκέτι ἐγώ"},{"q":"The lesson says the verb ending by itself is enough to identify the subject.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The ending is enough; an explicit pronoun adds something beyond identification."},{"q":"Which pair of forms are both plural and differ only in person?","kind":"form","options":["λύω / λύομεν","λύει / λύεις","λύομεν / λύετε","λύεις / λύετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύομεν (1st plural) and λύετε (2nd plural) are both plural; the other pairs differ in number, or are both singular."},{"q":"According to the lesson, clarification by an explicit pronoun applies especially to which kind of verb form?","kind":"concept","options":["First-person forms","Third-person forms","Second-person plural forms","Forms without endings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clarification helps when a third-person form could refer to more than one antecedent."}],"va_29":[{"q":"How many slots does the lesson's morphological template have?","kind":"concept","options":["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],"answer":2,"explain":"The template has six slots: augment, reduplication, stem, tense formative, connecting vowel, and personal ending."},{"q":"Which two slots does the template mark as always present?","kind":"concept","options":["Stem and personal ending","Augment and stem","Reduplication and personal ending","Tense formative and connecting vowel"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the stem and the personal ending are marked \"always\"; the other four slots are optional."},{"q":"Which slot signals past indicative time?","kind":"concept","options":["Reduplication","Augment","Tense formative","Connecting vowel"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment is the optional slot that signals past indicative time."},{"q":"Which slot signals the perfect tense system?","kind":"concept","options":["Augment","Stem","Reduplication","Personal ending"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reduplication is the optional slot that signals the perfect tense system."},{"q":"Which slot carries the verb's core lexical meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Personal ending","Connecting vowel","Augment","Stem"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stem is always present and carries the core lexical meaning of the verb."},{"q":"What does the tense formative signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Which tense-voice system the form belongs to","Person and number","Past indicative time","The verb's core meaning"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tense formative is an optional slot that signals which tense-voice system is in use."},{"q":"What is the job of the connecting vowel in the template?","kind":"concept","options":["It signals past time","It carries core meaning","It smooths the stem-to-ending junction","It marks the perfect system"],"answer":2,"explain":"The connecting vowel is optional; it smooths the junction between stem and ending."},{"q":"Which slot communicates person and number?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense formative","Personal ending","Stem","Reduplication"],"answer":1,"explain":"The personal ending is always present and communicates person and number."},{"q":"In the lesson's template, the augment and reduplication are both optional slots.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Both are marked optional: the augment appears when a form signals past indicative time, and reduplication when it belongs to the perfect system."},{"q":"What is the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο","ε","ου","μ"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύομεν breaks down as λύ + ο + μεν; the connecting vowel is ο before μ.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form into its parts?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + ε + ις","λύ + εις, with no connecting vowel","λύ + ο + ις","λύ + σ + εις"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύεις is stem λύ + connecting vowel ε + ending ις.","greek":"λύεις"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe what happens at the end of this form?","kind":"form","options":["The ending is fused, with no visible connecting vowel","The connecting vowel and ending are contracted","A tense formative is added before the ending","An augment is added before the stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"For λύουσι the lesson shows λύ + ου + σι and says the CV and ending are contracted.","greek":"λύουσι"},{"q":"How does the lesson analyze this form?","kind":"form","options":["Stem + connecting vowel ο + ending","Augment + stem + ending","Stem + fused ending, with no visible connecting vowel","Stem + tense formative + ending"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύω is stem λύ + ω, a fused ending with no visible connecting vowel.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"The lesson gives λυ- as the stem of this verb. What meaning does it give for that stem?","kind":"meaning","options":["run/hurry","loose/release","give","fill"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem λυ- carries the core meaning \"loose/release.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"How does the lesson break this form into its parts?","kind":"form","options":["λύ + ει, with no connecting vowel","λύ + ο + ι","λύ + ε + ι","λύ + ε + ις"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύει is stem λύ + connecting vowel ε + ending ι.","greek":"λύει"},{"q":"Why is the paradigm you have studied called \"thematic\"?","kind":"concept","options":["It adds a sigma to the stem","It uses a thematic vowel as its connecting vowel","It always takes an augment","It has no personal endings"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is called thematic because it uses a thematic vowel, the connecting vowel ο/ε."},{"q":"The lesson calls this verb the classic example of which class?","kind":"concept","options":["Thematic verbs","Contract verbs","Athematic (μι-) verbs","Second aorist verbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"δίδωμι is the classic example of an athematic or μι-verb.","greek":"δίδωμι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loose","I take","I say","I give"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson glosses δίδωμι as \"I give.\"","greek":"δίδωμι"},{"q":"How do athematic verbs connect the stem to the ending?","kind":"concept","options":["Directly, without a thematic vowel","With the vowel ο before μ","With an added sigma","With reduplication"],"answer":0,"explain":"Athematic verbs connect the stem directly to the ending, with no thematic vowel."},{"q":"According to the lesson, most Greek verbs are athematic.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Most Greek verbs are thematic; the athematic μι-verbs are a smaller, older class."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the μι-verbs as a group?","kind":"concept","options":["A smaller, older class","The largest, newest class","A class found only in poetry","A class used only in the passive"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls the athematic μι-verbs a smaller, older class."},{"q":"According to the lesson, μι-verbs follow a completely different underlying logic from the verb template.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. μι-verbs fill the slots differently, but their underlying logic is the same template."},{"q":"What is the connecting vowel in this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο","ου","ε","None; the ending is fused"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύετε breaks down as λύ + ε + τε, so the connecting vowel is ε.","greek":"λύετε"},{"q":"A verb form shows stem + connecting vowel + personal ending, with no augment and no tense formative. Which system does the lesson say this points to?","kind":"concept","options":["The perfect system","The present system","Past indicative time","The athematic system"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lack of a tense formative is part of what signals the present system."},{"q":"The lesson says that analyzing a Greek verb form means decoding chaos.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says you are not decoding chaos; you are reading a predictable structure."},{"q":"Which slots of the template are filled in this form?","kind":"form","options":["Augment, stem, personal ending","Stem, tense formative, personal ending","Reduplication, stem, personal ending","Stem, connecting vowel, personal ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύουσι is λύ + ου + σι: stem, connecting vowel, and personal ending (with the CV and ending contracted).","greek":"λύουσι"},{"q":"Besides lacking the connecting vowel, how else does the lesson say μι-verbs differ from thematic verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["They follow different ending patterns","They have no stem","They do not mark person or number","They always take reduplication"],"answer":0,"explain":"μι-verbs lack the connecting vowel and follow different ending patterns, but use the same stem-based system."},{"q":"In this form, the lesson says the connecting vowel is ο because it comes before which letter?","kind":"form","options":["τ","σ","ι","μ"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson notes for λύομεν: CV = ο before μ. The connecting vowel stands right before the μ of -μεν.","greek":"λύομεν"},{"q":"Which vowels serve as the thematic (connecting) vowel in thematic verbs?","kind":"letter","options":["α/η","ι/υ","ω/ου","ο/ε"],"answer":3,"explain":"Thematic verbs use ο or ε as the connecting vowel."},{"q":"The intro says that once you internalize the template, unfamiliar forms become decodable not by guessing but by what?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorization","Analysis","Translation","Context alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"Knowing the template lets you decode unfamiliar forms by analysis rather than guesswork."}],"va_30":[{"q":"Under the ο/ε rule, which connecting vowel is used before μ and ν endings?","kind":"concept","options":["ο","ε","α","ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The thematic vowel is ο before μ and ν endings and ε before all others."},{"q":"In the uncontracted φιλέ + ομεν, the ο at the start of the ending follows the ο/ε rule, because it stands before μ.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The connecting vowel is ο before μ and ν endings, so -ομεν has ο. That ο then meets the stem ε and the two contract."},{"q":"Which contraction rule produced this form?","kind":"form","options":["ε + ο → ου","ε + ε → ει","ε + ω → ω","α + ει → ᾳ"],"answer":1,"explain":"φιλεῖς comes from φιλέ + εις: stem ε meets ε and contracts to ει.","greek":"φιλεῖς"},{"q":"What is the uncontracted form behind this contracted form?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπά + ομεν","φιλέ + εις","ἀγαπά + εις","πληρό + εις"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀγαπᾷς comes from ἀγαπά + εις, where α + ει → ᾳ.","greek":"ἀγαπᾷς"},{"q":"Which contraction rule produced this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο + ο → ου","ο + ω → ω","ε + ε → ει","ο + ει → οι"],"answer":3,"explain":"πληροῖς comes from πληρό + εις: ο + ει contracts to οι.","greek":"πληροῖς"},{"q":"Which contraction rule produced this form?","kind":"form","options":["α + ει → ᾳ","α + ο → ω","ε + ο → ου","ο + ο → ου"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἀγαπῶμεν comes from ἀγαπά + ομεν: α + ο contracts to ω.","greek":"ἀγαπῶμεν"},{"q":"Which contraction rule produced this form?","kind":"form","options":["ο + ω → ω","ο + ει → οι","ο + ο → ου","α + ο → ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"πληροῦμεν comes from πληρό + ομεν: ο + ο contracts to ου.","greek":"πληροῦμεν"},{"q":"What does φιλέ + ω contract to?","kind":"form","options":["φιλῶ","φιλεῖς","φιλοῦμεν","πληρῶ"],"answer":0,"explain":"ε + ω → ω, so φιλέ + ω becomes φιλῶ."},{"q":"How many families of contract verbs does the lesson present?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Two","Four","Five"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson presents three families: ε-contract (φιλέω), α-contract (ἀγαπάω) and ο-contract (πληρόω)."},{"q":"What does this verb mean, and which contract family is it in?","kind":"meaning","options":["I love; ε-contract","I call; ε-contract","I do/make; α-contract","I fill/fulfill; ο-contract"],"answer":3,"explain":"πληρόω means \"I fill/fulfill\" and is the lesson's ο-contract example.","greek":"πληρόω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I call","I do/make","I fill","I love"],"answer":0,"explain":"καλέω means \"I call\"; it is an ε-contract verb.","greek":"καλέω"},{"q":"Which contract family does this verb belong to?","kind":"form","options":["α-contract","ο-contract","ε-contract","Not a contract verb"],"answer":2,"explain":"ποιέω (I do/make) has a stem ending in ε, so it is an ε-contract verb.","greek":"ποιέω"},{"q":"The lesson cites this form from John 3:16. Which lexical verb does it list it under?","kind":"reading","options":["ἀγαπάω","φιλέω","ποιέω","καλέω"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists ἠγάπησεν (John 3:16) under ἀγαπάω, its α-contract verb meaning \"I love.\"","greek":"ἠγάπησεν"},{"q":"Which accent does the lesson name as a clue that a form may be contracted?","kind":"concept","options":["Acute","Grave","Circumflex","Rough breathing"],"answer":2,"explain":"A circumflex accent is one sign that a form may be a compressed, contracted form."},{"q":"The lesson says contraction is a kind of irregularity in the verb system.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Contraction is not irregularity; it is the connecting vowel rule meeting a vowel-final stem."},{"q":"What happens when a stem-final vowel meets the connecting vowel?","kind":"concept","options":["A sigma is inserted between them","The two vowels contract into one","An augment is added to the stem","The ending is dropped"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem vowel and connecting vowel collide, and Greek resolves this by contracting them into one vowel."},{"q":"The lesson describes the connecting vowel as a bridge, not an accident. What need does it say this bridge meets?","kind":"concept","options":["A smooth transition between stem and ending","Marking past time","Showing person and number","Making words longer for poetry"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greek phonology prefers a smooth transition between stem and ending, and the connecting vowel supplies it."},{"q":"According to the lesson, contraction tables are lists to be memorized as unrelated facts.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The tables are the predictable output of systematic vowel rules."},{"q":"In the lesson's tables, ε + ω, α + ω, and ο + ω all contract to which vowel?","kind":"concept","options":["ου","ει","ω","οι"],"answer":2,"explain":"In all three families, the stem vowel plus ω gives ω (φιλῶ, ἀγαπῶ, πληρῶ)."},{"q":"Which two vowel collisions in the lesson's tables both produce ου?","kind":"concept","options":["ε + ε and ο + ει","ε + ο and ο + ο","α + ο and ε + ω","α + ει and ο + ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"ε + ο → ου (φιλοῦμεν) and ο + ο → ου (πληροῦμεν)."},{"q":"What is the uncontracted form behind this contracted form?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπά + ομεν","ἀγαπά + εις","φιλέ + ω","ἀγαπά + ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαπῶ comes from ἀγαπά + ω, since α + ω → ω.","greek":"ἀγαπῶ"},{"q":"You meet a compressed-looking form with a circumflex and suspect a contract verb. What does the lesson say to ask next?","kind":"concept","options":["Where is the reduplication?","Is this a μι-verb?","Is this a second aorist?","What would the uncontracted form be?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The recognition strategy is to ask whether it is a contract verb and what the uncontracted form would be."},{"q":"The lesson gives φιλε- as the stem of this verb. What meaning does it give for that stem?","kind":"meaning","options":["fill","love/cherish","call","do/make"],"answer":1,"explain":"φιλέω has the stem φιλε-, meaning \"love/cherish\"; its stem-final ε makes it an ε-contract verb.","greek":"φιλέω"},{"q":"Which ε-contract verb does the lesson describe as very high frequency?","kind":"meaning","options":["καλέω","φιλέω","πληρόω","ποιέω"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson marks ποιέω, \"I do/make,\" as a very high frequency ε-contract verb."},{"q":"According to the lesson, contraction is the connecting vowel rule encountering what?","kind":"concept","options":["A vowel-final stem","A consonant-final stem","An augment","A tense formative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Contraction happens when the regular connecting vowel rule meets a stem that already ends in a vowel."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the frequency of contract verbs in the NT?","kind":"concept","options":["Extremely common","Rare","Found only in John","Found only in quotations"],"answer":0,"explain":"Contract verbs are extremely common in the NT, which makes recognizing them essential."},{"q":"In the lesson's tables, which contract family produces a contracted form written with an iota subscript?","kind":"form","options":["ε-contract","ο-contract","α-contract","Thematic verbs without contraction"],"answer":2,"explain":"α + ει → ᾳ gives ἀγαπᾷς, whose ᾳ carries an iota subscript. The ε- and ο-contract tables have no iota subscript."},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I call","I fill","I do/make","I love"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγαπάω means \"I love\"; its stem ends in α, so it is an α-contract verb.","greek":"ἀγαπάω"},{"q":"The lesson glosses both φιλέω and ἀγαπάω as \"I love.\" Which contract families do they belong to?","kind":"concept","options":["φιλέω α-contract; ἀγαπάω ε-contract","φιλέω ε-contract; ἀγαπάω α-contract","Both ε-contract","φιλέω ο-contract; ἀγαπάω α-contract"],"answer":1,"explain":"φιλέω has stem φιλε- (ε-contract) and ἀγαπάω has stem ἀγαπα- (α-contract)."},{"q":"Besides a circumflex accent, what other sign of a compressed form does the lesson mention?","kind":"concept","options":["An initial augment","An unexpected diphthong","A sigma before the ending","Reduplication"],"answer":1,"explain":"An unexpected diphthong, like ει, ου, or οι, can also signal a contracted form."}],"va_03":[{"q":"Which image does the lesson use to picture imperfective aspect?","kind":"concept","options":["A camera slowly panning across a scene","A single snapshot","A runner crossing a finish line","A door being closed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Imperfective aspect is like a camera slowly panning across a scene, showing the action as it unfolds."},{"q":"According to the lesson, \"imperfective\" means the action is incomplete in a theological or philosophical sense.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Imperfective describes the writer's viewpoint (the inside view), not incompleteness in a theological or philosophical sense."},{"q":"What is a historical present?","kind":"concept","options":["A past tense verb describing present events","A present tense verb describing past events","A future tense verb describing past events","A present tense used only in commands"],"answer":1,"explain":"A historical present is a present tense verb used to describe past events, common in narrative."},{"q":"Which Gospel does the lesson call the most prolific user of historical presents in the NT?","kind":"concept","options":["Matthew","Luke","Mark","John"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mark's Gospel uses historical presents more than any other NT book."},{"q":"How does the lesson gloss this verb, which Mark often uses as a historical present?","kind":"meaning","options":["he comes","he drives out","he heals","he says"],"answer":3,"explain":"λέγει is glossed \"he says\"; in Mark it often appears in clearly past narrative.","greek":"λέγει"},{"q":"How does the lesson gloss this verb, another historical present in Mark?","kind":"meaning","options":["he says","he comes","he follows","he heals"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔρχεται is glossed \"he comes\"; like λέγει, it shows up in past narrative contexts.","greek":"ἔρχεται"},{"q":"In this phrase from Mark 1:12, which word is the present tense verb?","kind":"reading","options":["εὐθὺς","πνεῦμα","ἐκβάλλει","ἔρημον"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐκβάλλει is the present tense verb, used here to narrate a past event.","greek":"καὶ εὐθὺς τὸ πνεῦμα αὐτὸν ἐκβάλλει εἰς τὴν ἔρημον"},{"q":"In this phrase from Mark 1:12, which words are the subject of ἐκβάλλει, going by the lesson's literal rendering?","kind":"reading","options":["αὐτὸν","τὸ πνεῦμα","τὴν ἔρημον","εὐθὺς"],"answer":1,"explain":"In the literal rendering the Spirit is the one who drives him out, so τὸ πνεῦμα is the subject; αὐτὸν (\"him\") is the object.","greek":"καὶ εὐθὺς τὸ πνεῦμα αὐτὸν ἐκβάλλει εἰς τὴν ἔρημον"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what discourse purposes do historical presents serve in narrative?","kind":"concept","options":["Showing future time or a condition","Expressing commands or wishes","Marking passive voice or questions","Creating vividness or marking prominence"],"answer":3,"explain":"Historical presents serve discourse purposes such as creating vividness or marking prominence."},{"q":"What does the historical present often mark in a narrative?","kind":"concept","options":["Transitions or moments of prominence","The end of a book","Only direct quotations","Conditional clauses"],"answer":0,"explain":"The historical present often marks transitions or moments of prominence in the story."},{"q":"According to the lesson, in what kind of context does Mark use this verb (and λέγει)?","kind":"concept","options":["Letters to churches","Commands to disciples","Clearly past narrative contexts","Prophecies about the future"],"answer":2,"explain":"They are present tense verbs that appear in clearly past narrative contexts.","greek":"ἔρχεται"},{"q":"The lesson treats historical presents in narrative as grammatical errors.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Historical presents are not errors; they are stylistic choices and a narrative technique."},{"q":"In letters, what does the present tense often describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing states or habitual patterns","Past events told vividly","Solemn promises","Completed past events"],"answer":0,"explain":"In letters, the present often describes ongoing states or habitual patterns."},{"q":"According to the lesson, a present imperative emphasizes continuing action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. In commands, the present imperative emphasizes continuing action."},{"q":"\"He heals the sick,\" meant as a pattern or characteristic behavior, is which kind of present?","kind":"concept","options":["Ongoing","Historical","Habitual","Imperatival"],"answer":2,"explain":"A pattern or characteristic behavior is a habitual present."},{"q":"\"He is healing the sick,\" describing a current, unfolding scene, is which kind of present?","kind":"concept","options":["Habitual","Historical","Imperatival","Ongoing"],"answer":3,"explain":"A current, unfolding scene is an ongoing present."},{"q":"How does Greek distinguish an ongoing present from a habitual present?","kind":"concept","options":["By a separate ending","By context","By adding an augment","By word order alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"Neither use needs a separate Greek form; context carries the distinction."},{"q":"A translator renders every present tense with English present time, even in Mark's past narrative. According to the lesson, what does this lead to?","kind":"concept","options":["Awkward or misleading translations","More accurate translations","Clearer aspect distinctions","Better recognition of prominence"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reducing the present to simple present time leads to awkward or misleading translations."},{"q":"In the lesson's summary of Mark 1:12, when does the narrated event take place?","kind":"reading","options":["In the future","At the moment of writing","In the past","At no particular time"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson sums it up as a past event told with a present tense verb.","greek":"καὶ εὐθὺς τὸ πνεῦμα αὐτὸν ἐκβάλλει εἰς τὴν ἔρημον"},{"q":"In a passage of Mark that is clearly set in the past, you read this verb. How does the lesson suggest you understand it?","kind":"reading","options":["As a copying error","As a future prediction","As a command","As a historical present in past narrative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Recognizing a historical present keeps you from wrongly placing a past event in the present.","greek":"λέγει"},{"q":"For which group does the lesson say the Greek present tense is one of the most commonly misunderstood tenses?","kind":"concept","options":["English speakers","Hebrew speakers","Latin scholars","Ancient scribes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the Greek present is commonly misunderstood by English speakers."},{"q":"The intro says the present tense communicates how the writer perceives the action's character, not merely what?","kind":"concept","options":["Who performs it","When it occurs","Where it happens","Why it happens"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present tense is primarily about how the action is viewed, not merely about when it happens."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what error does recognizing the historical present keep a translator from making?","kind":"concept","options":["Placing past events in the present","Treating a command as a statement","Reading a letter as a narrative","Confusing the subject with the object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Recognizing the historical present keeps the translator from placing events in the present when the narrative is clearly past."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the present tense uses the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Describing ongoing states in letters","Emphasizing continuing action in commands","Vivifying past events in narrative","Marking a completed action with a present result"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists letters, commands, and narrative vividness; it does not list completed action with a present result."},{"q":"What tense is this verb, which the lesson cites from Mark's narrative?","kind":"form","options":["Imperfect","Aorist","Present","Future"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔρχεται is a present tense form; Mark uses it as a historical present in past narrative.","greek":"ἔρχεται"},{"q":"According to the intro, the Greek present tense is primarily what kind of marker?","kind":"concept","options":["A time marker","A voice marker","A mood marker","An aspect marker"],"answer":3,"explain":"The intro says the present is primarily an aspect marker: it shows how the writer views the action, not merely when it happens."},{"q":"The lesson gives this phrase from Mark 1:12 as an example of which phenomenon?","kind":"reading","options":["A habitual present","A present imperative","A present in a letter","A historical present"],"answer":3,"explain":"Mark 1:12 appears in the section on the historical present: a past event told in the present tense.","greek":"καὶ εὐθὺς τὸ πνεῦμα αὐτὸν ἐκβάλλει εἰς τὴν ἔρημον"},{"q":"According to the intro, English trains us to associate \"present tense\" with what?","kind":"concept","options":["Completed action","Habitual action","Present time","Future expectation"],"answer":2,"explain":"English links \"present tense\" with present time, what is happening right now. The lesson says Greek works differently."},{"q":"Viewing an action from the inside means seeing it as something developing rather than as what?","kind":"concept","options":["A future event","A closed whole","A command","A grammatical error"],"answer":1,"explain":"The writer views the action as developing, not as a closed whole."},{"q":"What effect does the lesson say the present tense creates in Mark 1:12?","kind":"reading","options":["A habitual effect","A conditional effect","A vividness effect","A command effect"],"answer":2,"explain":"Using a present tense for a past event in Mark 1:12 creates vividness.","greek":"ἐκβάλλει"}],"va_04":[{"q":"What two features does the imperfect add to the present stem to signal past time?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication and primary endings","Augment and secondary endings","A sigma and a connecting vowel","A sigma and secondary endings"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfect keeps the present stem but adds the augment and uses secondary endings, locating the action in past time."},{"q":"According to the intro, how does the present tense portray ongoing action, in contrast to the imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["Only in past time","Without temporal restriction","Only in future time","As a complete whole"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present portrays ongoing action without temporal restriction; the imperfect anchors that ongoing quality in past time."},{"q":"Which stem does the imperfect share?","kind":"form","options":["The aorist stem","The perfect stem","The future stem","The present stem"],"answer":3,"explain":"The imperfect shares the same stem as the present tense."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the present and the imperfect share the same aspect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Both are imperfective in aspect; the imperfect places that view in past time."},{"q":"Which set of English renderings does the lesson call valid for the imperfect, depending on context?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"was doing,\" \"kept doing,\" \"began doing\"","\"did,\" \"had done,\" \"will do\"","\"is doing,\" \"does,\" \"will do\"","\"has done,\" \"had done,\" \"will have done\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Was doing,\" \"kept doing,\" and \"began doing\" all present the action as in progress in the past."},{"q":"Complete the lesson's summary: \"Present = imperfective now. Imperfect = imperfective ___.\"","kind":"concept","options":["later","then","always","never"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfect is imperfective aspect located in past time: imperfective then."},{"q":"In NT narrative, which tense does the lesson say tends to carry the main storyline forward, event by event?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfect","Present","Aorist","Future"],"answer":2,"explain":"Aorists tend to move the main storyline forward, while imperfects describe the surrounding circumstances."},{"q":"\"Crowds were gathering, people were asking, the disciples were watching\" illustrates the role of which tense?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Future","Present","Imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"The imperfect paints the scene with ongoing background action like this."},{"q":"What do discourse analysts call the setting against which foreground events occur?","kind":"concept","options":["Background information","The main line","The climax","Direct speech"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfect contributes background information, the setting for the foreground events."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this imperfect from Mark 1:32-34?","kind":"meaning","options":["they will bring","they have brought","they were bringing","they used to send"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson renders ἔφερον as \"they were bringing,\" an ongoing past action.","greek":"ἔφερον"},{"q":"In Mark 1:32-34, what does the lesson say this imperfect shows?","kind":"reading","options":["A decisive healing event","The ongoing flow of people coming","A future promise","A command to the crowd"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfect shows the ongoing flow of people coming, creating the scene.","greek":"ἔφερον"},{"q":"In Mark 1:32-34, what does the lesson say aorists such as this one mark?","kind":"reading","options":["The ongoing flow of people","Background scenery","Decisive healing events","Habitual past actions"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorists record decisive healing events within the scene that the imperfects create.","greek":"ἐθεράπευσεν"},{"q":"Complete the lesson's summary: \"Imperfects set the stage. Aorists ___.\"","kind":"concept","options":["build the scenery","perform the action on it","end the story","repeat the background"],"answer":1,"explain":"Imperfects set the stage; aorists perform the action on it."},{"q":"Which English rendering does the lesson say emphasizes habitual past action?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"was [verb]ing\"","\"kept [verb]ing\"","simple past","\"used to [verb]\""],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Used to [verb]\" emphasizes habitual past action."},{"q":"Which English rendering does the lesson say emphasizes repeated or continuous action?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"kept [verb]ing\"","\"used to [verb]\"","simple past","\"will [verb]\""],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Kept [verb]ing\" emphasizes repeated or continuous action."},{"q":"Which parts of the NT does the lesson especially mention for imperfects in narrative?","kind":"concept","options":["Paul's letters","Revelation","The Gospels and Acts","Hebrews and James"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says NT narrative, especially in the Gospels and Acts, uses the imperfect to develop scenes."},{"q":"When does the lesson say a simple past is acceptable for translating an imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["Never","Only in letters","When the imperfective nuance is contextually obvious","Only when the verb has no augment"],"answer":2,"explain":"A simple past is fine when context already makes the imperfective nuance obvious."},{"q":"English has a dedicated imperfect tense that matches the Greek imperfect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. English has no dedicated imperfect tense, which is why translating the imperfect is a challenge."},{"q":"The lesson says the right English rendering of an imperfect depends on what it communicates in its specific context.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. There is no one-size-fits-all rendering; context decides."},{"q":"The contrast between aorists and imperfects creates what kind of narrative texture, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A flat texture","A layered texture","A poetic texture","A random texture"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aorists for the storyline and imperfects for the scene create a layered narrative texture."},{"q":"Recognizing the aorist and imperfect contrast lets you see how Greek narrative is organized in what way?","kind":"concept","options":["Alphabetically","Randomly","Chronologically only","Architecturally"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says this contrast shows how Greek narrative is architecturally organized."},{"q":"The lesson says the imperfect's role as background is not merely a stylistic observation. What does it affect?","kind":"concept","options":["How you interpret a passage","How the verb is pronounced","How the verb is spelled","How verses are numbered"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing which actions are background and which are foreground affects how you interpret a passage."},{"q":"This imperfect begins with ε-. According to the lesson, what does that prefixed ε- signal?","kind":"form","options":["The augment, signaling past time","Reduplication, signaling the perfect","A connecting vowel","A tense formative"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfect adds the augment, the prefixed ε-, which together with secondary endings locates the action in past time.","greek":"ἔφερον"},{"q":"In Mark 1:32-34, the lesson identifies this verb as an imperfect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson identifies ἐθεράπευσεν as an aorist; ἔφερον is the imperfect.","greek":"ἐθεράπευσεν"},{"q":"What is the common thread in all the valid renderings of the imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["The action is viewed as completed in the past","The action is viewed as in progress or unfolding in the past","The action is placed in the future","The action is a command"],"answer":1,"explain":"Whatever the English rendering, the imperfect views the action as in progress or unfolding in past time."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the events that take place against the background setting?","kind":"concept","options":["Background information","Scene-setting","Foreground events","Circumstantial details"],"answer":2,"explain":"The background is the setting against which foreground events occur."},{"q":"Which passage does the lesson use to show imperfects and aorists working together?","kind":"reading","options":["Mark 1:32-34","A letter of Paul","The Ten Commandments","A psalm"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's example is Mark 1:32-34, where imperfects create the scene and aorists record events in it."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the aorist paints the scene while the imperfect moves the story forward.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. It is the reverse: the aorist moves the story forward and the imperfect paints the scene."},{"q":"The lesson glosses this verb \"they were bringing.\" Which rendering pattern from the lesson does that gloss follow?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"used to [verb]\" (habitual)","\"kept [verb]ing\" (repeated)","Simple past","\"was [verb]ing\" (ongoing process)"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"They were bringing\" uses the \"was [verb]ing\" pattern, which emphasizes ongoing process.","greek":"ἔφερον"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the English options the lesson gives for translating the imperfect?","kind":"concept","options":["\"was [verb]ing\"","\"kept [verb]ing\"","\"used to [verb]\"","\"will have [verb]ed\""],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's options are \"was [verb]ing,\" \"kept [verb]ing,\" \"used to [verb],\" and simple past; a future perfect is not one of them."}],"va_05":[{"q":"Which future function does the lesson define as \"what the speaker anticipates will happen\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Expectation","Prediction","Promise","Warning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Expectation is what the speaker anticipates will happen."},{"q":"Which future function does the lesson define as \"a confident assertion about the future\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Warning","Prediction","Expectation","Promise"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prediction is a confident assertion about what will happen."},{"q":"Which future function does the lesson define as \"a pledge from speaker to listener\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Prediction","Warning","Promise","Expectation"],"answer":2,"explain":"A promise is a pledge the speaker makes to the listener."},{"q":"Which future function does the lesson define as \"a caution about future consequences\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Promise","Expectation","Prediction","Warning"],"answer":3,"explain":"A warning cautions the listener about future consequences."},{"q":"A teacher cautions students about consequences: \"If you skip the readings, you will struggle on the exam.\" Using the lesson's categories, which function is this future?","kind":"concept","options":["Promise","Warning","Expectation","Imperatival"],"answer":1,"explain":"A caution about future consequences is a warning."},{"q":"A friend commits herself: \"I will help you move on Saturday.\" Using the lesson's categories, which kind of future is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Predictive","Imperatival","Warning","Promissory"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the speaker commits to bringing something about, the future is promissory."},{"q":"Which passage does the lesson connect with this future form?","kind":"reading","options":["Matthew 5:48","Matthew 4:19","The Ten Commandments","Peter's denial"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson cites ποιήσω from Matthew 4:19 as its example of a promise.","greek":"ποιήσω"},{"q":"Which kind of future does the lesson say this form from Matthew 4:19 is?","kind":"concept","options":["Promissory","Predictive","Imperatival","Warning"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls Matthew 4:19 promissory: a relational pledge.","greek":"ποιήσω"},{"q":"Grammatically, what kind of form does the lesson say this is?","kind":"form","options":["Present imperative","Aorist subjunctive","Future indicative","Present indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"οὐ φονεύσεις is grammatically a future indicative, even though it works as a prohibition.","greek":"οὐ φονεύσεις"},{"q":"How does the lesson say this future indicative from the Ten Commandments functions?","kind":"reading","options":["As a prediction","As a promise","As an expectation","As a prohibition"],"answer":3,"explain":"Though grammatically future indicative, it functions as a prohibition.","greek":"οὐ φονεύσεις"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe this phrase from Matthew 5:48?","kind":"reading","options":["Present imperative with continuing force","Future indicative with imperatival force","Future indicative with predictive force only","Aorist with ingressive force"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson calls it a future indicative with imperatival force.","greek":"ἔσεσθε οὖν ὑμεῖς τέλειοι"},{"q":"In what kinds of contexts does the lesson say future indicatives especially function as imperatives?","kind":"concept","options":["Legal or moral contexts","Narrative scene-setting","Greetings in letters","Parables"],"answer":0,"explain":"Imperatival futures appear especially in legal or moral contexts."},{"q":"The lesson says the imperatival use of the future has its roots in Latin legal language.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says this usage has deep roots in Hebrew-influenced Greek."},{"q":"According to the lesson, future indicatives function as imperatives in the LXX and occasionally in the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson places this use in the LXX and occasionally in the NT."},{"q":"Which text does the lesson say provides the clearest examples of the future used as a command or prohibition?","kind":"concept","options":["Matthew 4:19","The Ten Commandments","Peter's denial","Paul's letters"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Ten Commandments give the clearest examples, such as οὐ φονεύσεις."},{"q":"In a predictive future, the speaker asserts that something will occur based on what?","kind":"concept","options":["A legal requirement","A condition being met","Knowledge or foresight","A personal wish"],"answer":2,"explain":"A predictive future rests on the speaker's knowledge or foresight."},{"q":"In a promissory future, what does the speaker do?","kind":"concept","options":["Commits to bringing something about","Forbids an action","Describes a past event","Asks a question about the future"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a promissory future, the speaker commits to bringing something about."},{"q":"Which example does the lesson give of predictive futures?","kind":"concept","options":["ποιήσω in Matthew 4:19","οὐ φονεύσεις","Jesus's predictions about Peter's denial","ἔσεσθε in Matthew 5:48"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson names Jesus's predictions about Peter's denial as predictive futures."},{"q":"According to the lesson, a future verb takes a different form when it is a promise than when it is a prediction.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The form is the same; context, speaker, and relationship shape the force."},{"q":"The lesson says a promise reads differently than a mere prediction.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Distinguishing promise from prediction changes how a passage is read."},{"q":"Which question about the speaker does the lesson suggest asking when you meet a future verb?","kind":"concept","options":["How old is the speaker?","What language did the speaker grow up with?","Where was the speaker born?","What authority do they have?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson asks: Who is the speaker? What authority do they have?"},{"q":"Which list of genres does the lesson suggest checking when reading a future verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Narrative, discourse, letter, prophecy","Poetry, law, wisdom, history","Hymn, creed, prayer, proverb","Parable, miracle, genealogy, sermon"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson asks: What is the genre — narrative, discourse, letter, prophecy?"},{"q":"Besides the speaker, audience, and genre, what else does the lesson tell you to check?","kind":"concept","options":["The length of the verb","What precedes and follows the statement","The date of the manuscript","The verb's accent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The surrounding text, what precedes and follows, is part of reading a future verb's context."},{"q":"In this phrase from Matthew 5:48, which word is the future indicative verb?","kind":"reading","options":["ἔσεσθε","οὖν","ὑμεῖς","τέλειοι"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔσεσθε is the future indicative (\"you will be\") that the lesson says has imperatival force; ὑμεῖς is a pronoun and τέλειοι an adjective.","greek":"ἔσεσθε οὖν ὑμεῖς τέλειοι"},{"q":"Which question about the audience does the lesson suggest asking?","kind":"concept","options":["How large is the audience?","Does the audience read Greek?","What is their relationship to the speaker?","Where is the audience sitting?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson asks: Who is the audience? What is their relationship to the speaker?"},{"q":"Which reference does the lesson give for this phrase?","kind":"reading","options":["Matthew 4:19","The Ten Commandments","Peter's denial","Matthew 5:48"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson cites Matthew 5:48 for ἔσεσθε οὖν ὑμεῖς τέλειοι.","greek":"ἔσεσθε οὖν ὑμεῖς τέλειοι"},{"q":"According to the lesson, the distinction between predictive and promissory futures shapes how a passage is understood in which two ways?","kind":"concept","options":["Grammatically and phonetically","Historically and geographically","Theologically and pastorally","Textually and visually"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson notes these functions shape how a passage is understood theologically and pastorally."},{"q":"How does the intro describe the future compared with a simple marker of upcoming events?","kind":"concept","options":["Communicatively poorer","Exactly the same","Less important","Communicatively richer"],"answer":3,"explain":"The intro says the future is communicatively richer than a simple \"this will happen.\""},{"q":"What tense is this form, which the lesson lists as a promise in Matthew 4:19?","kind":"form","options":["Future","Perfect","Present","Imperfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"ποιήσω is a future form; the lesson gives it as an example of a promise.","greek":"ποιήσω"},{"q":"Which two examples does the lesson give of future indicatives used with the force of a command or prohibition?","kind":"reading","options":["ποιήσω and ἔσεσθε","οὐ φονεύσεις and ποιήσω","οὐ φονεύσεις and ἔσεσθε","ποιήσω and Peter's denial"],"answer":2,"explain":"οὐ φονεύσεις (a prohibition) and ἔσεσθε in Matthew 5:48 (imperatival force) are the lesson's examples. ποιήσω is a promise, and Peter's denial involves predictive futures."}],"va_06":[{"q":"Which image does the lesson use for the aorist's perfective aspect?","kind":"concept","options":["A video","A photograph","A panning camera","A diary entry"],"answer":1,"explain":"A photograph captures the event as a whole without showing how it unfolds, like the aorist."},{"q":"In the lesson's photograph-versus-video analogy, what does the video represent?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective aspect","Past time","Imperfective aspect","The indicative mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"The video, which captures movement and process, stands for imperfective aspect."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the aorist says the action was instantaneous.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The aorist does not say the action was instantaneous; it presents the action as a whole unit."},{"q":"How does the lesson classify the popular gloss \"the aorist means once-for-all action\"?","kind":"concept","options":["A precise grammatical description","A theological import, not a grammatical description","A rule of the indicative mood","A feature of the aorist ending"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson calls it a theological import rather than a description of the grammar."},{"q":"According to the lesson, \"once-for-all\" language in theology is a conclusion drawn from what?","kind":"concept","options":["Context and argument","The aorist form itself","The augment","The aorist ending"],"answer":0,"explain":"That language is a conclusion drawn from context and argument, not from the aorist form."},{"q":"Which passage does the lesson name as an example of \"once-for-all\" language in theology?","kind":"reading","options":["Mark 1:12","Hebrews 9-10","John 1:20","Matthew 4:19"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson points to Hebrews 9-10 for \"once-for-all\" language, and says that sense comes from context and argument, not from the aorist form."},{"q":"In the indicative mood, what does the aorist typically refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["Future time","Present time","No time at all","Past time"],"answer":3,"explain":"In the indicative, the aorist typically refers to past time."},{"q":"According to the lesson, where does the \"simple past\" gloss for the aorist come from?","kind":"concept","options":["From the aorist indicative's typical reference to past time","From the aorist participle","From the aorist's lack of an augment","From Hebrews 9-10"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the aorist indicative typically refers to past time, \"simple past\" became a common gloss."},{"q":"Which forms does the lesson say do NOT inherently give the aorist past time?","kind":"concept","options":["The indicative only","The indicative and subjunctive","The subjunctive, infinitive, and participle","The indicative and participle"],"answer":2,"explain":"Outside the indicative (subjunctive, infinitive, participle), the aorist communicates only aspect."},{"q":"Even in the indicative, what does the lesson say is the aorist's primary signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Time","Aspect (whole-action view)","Voice","Person and number"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aspect is primary; time is a secondary contribution of the indicative mood."},{"q":"According to the lesson, past time in the aorist indicative is a secondary contribution of what?","kind":"concept","options":["The personal ending alone","The connecting vowel","The context alone","The indicative mood in combination with the stem"],"answer":3,"explain":"Time comes from the indicative mood combined with the stem, while aspect is the aorist's primary signal."},{"q":"Complete the lesson's summary: \"Aorist indicative = past time + ___.\"","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective aspect","Imperfective aspect","Completed result","Future expectation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The aorist indicative combines past time with perfective aspect."},{"q":"According to the lesson, an aorist participle inherently communicates past time.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Outside the indicative, the aorist communicates only aspect, not inherent past time."},{"q":"What is the first question the lesson suggests asking about an aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Is it a first or second aorist?","How long did the event last?","What mood is this aorist in?","Is the verb active or passive?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's checklist starts with mood: indicative, subjunctive, or participle?"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the emphases the lesson's checklist says context might bring out in an aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Completion","Summary","Beginning","Repetition"],"answer":3,"explain":"The checklist asks whether context emphasizes completion, summary, beginning, or simply whole-action view."},{"q":"What does the last section say the goal of this lesson is NOT?","kind":"concept","options":["To replace one simplistic formula with another","To let context shape interpretation","To ask what mood an aorist is in","To test claims against context"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson does not want to swap one simplistic formula for another."},{"q":"Which simplified formula does the intro say students often encounter?","kind":"concept","options":["\"The aorist is always future\"","\"The aorist marks ongoing action\"","\"The aorist is the simple past\"","\"The aorist is always passive\""],"answer":2,"explain":"The intro names \"once-for-all action\" and \"the simple past\" as simplified formulas."},{"q":"What does the intro say these simplified formulas do?","kind":"concept","options":["Clarify the aorist perfectly","Flatten a more nuanced reality","Explain all aorist uses","Replace the need for context"],"answer":1,"explain":"The formulas flatten a more nuanced reality."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the aorist presents an action without focusing on what?","kind":"concept","options":["Its subject","Its mood","Its duration or process","Its voice"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist presents the action without focusing on its duration or process."},{"q":"According to the intro, the aorist is primarily what kind of marker?","kind":"concept","options":["A time marker","A voice marker","A mood marker","An aspect marker"],"answer":3,"explain":"The intro says the aorist is primarily an aspect marker, presenting action as a whole, and its range is broader than any single formula."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does a writer choose when using the aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["To present the action as a whole unit","To show the action happened in an instant","To stress that the action can never be repeated","To show the action is still going on"],"answer":0,"explain":"The writer chose to present the action as a whole unit, not to claim it was instantaneous."},{"q":"An aorist indicative and an aorist subjunctive of the same verb both appear in a passage. According to the lesson, what does the indicative carry that the subjunctive does not inherently carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective aspect","Past time","Imperfective aspect","A once-for-all sense"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both carry perfective aspect. The indicative adds past time, while the subjunctive communicates only aspect."},{"q":"A commentator builds a theological claim mainly on a verb being aorist. Which question does the lesson suggest asking?","kind":"concept","options":["Does the context actually support it?","Is the verb a second aorist?","How many times does the verb occur?","Is the verb in a letter?"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson asks whether a claim is being built on the tense and whether the context actually supports it."},{"q":"Which tenses does the lesson identify with imperfective aspect, the inside view?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist and future","Perfect and pluperfect","Aorist and perfect","Present and imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson ties imperfective aspect (the inside view) to the present and imperfect."},{"q":"The lesson says the \"simple past\" gloss is often adequate for translating the aorist indicative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. In the indicative, \"simple past\" is often adequate for translation, though two caveats apply."},{"q":"How does the lesson say you should hold the aorist's meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Rigidly, by a fixed formula","Loosely enough to allow context to shape interpretation","Only by its time reference","Only by its ending"],"answer":1,"explain":"Holding the meaning loosely lets context shape the interpretation."},{"q":"The intro says the aorist's communicative range is broader than what?","kind":"concept","options":["The present tense's range","The indicative mood","The imperfect's range","Any single formula can capture"],"answer":3,"explain":"No single formula captures everything the aorist can communicate."},{"q":"According to the intro, which Greek tense generates more misunderstanding than any other?","kind":"concept","options":["The present","The imperfect","The future","The aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"The intro says no tense generates more misunderstanding than the aorist."},{"q":"Which two aspects does the lesson contrast in its \"outside view\" section?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective and perfective","Active and passive","Indicative and subjunctive","Singular and plural"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson sets perfective aspect (the aorist) against imperfective aspect (present/imperfect)."},{"q":"If imperfective aspect is the inside view of an action, what view does the lesson say the aorist represents?","kind":"concept","options":["The outside view","The inside view","The background view","The future view"],"answer":0,"explain":"The aorist represents the outside view: the action seen as a complete unit, without attention to its internal development."}],"va_07":[{"q":"Which other names does the lesson give for the constative aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Inceptive or ingressive aorist","Complexive or summary aorist","Culminative or effective aorist","Historical or vivid aorist"],"answer":1,"explain":"The constative aorist is sometimes called a complexive or summary aorist."},{"q":"What other name does the lesson give for the ingressive aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Complexive","Summary","Inceptive","Constative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ingressive aorist is sometimes called inceptive."},{"q":"This aorist is the lesson's constative example. Which passage does the lesson cite it from?","kind":"reading","options":["Matthew 9:27","Philippians 4:11","Mark 1:12","John 3:16"],"answer":1,"explain":"The constative example is Paul's ἔμαθον in Philippians 4:11. Matthew 9:27 is the lesson's ingressive example.","greek":"ἔμαθον"},{"q":"Which aorist category does the lesson illustrate with this verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Constative","Ingressive","Culminative","Historical present"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἔμαθον in Philippians 4:11 is the lesson's constative example: a whole learning process viewed as one unit.","greek":"ἔμαθον"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does Paul summarize in this single verb?","kind":"reading","options":["A single moment of insight","Years of experience","A plan for the future","A command to his readers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paul bundles years of experience into one aorist verb.","greek":"ἔμαθον"},{"q":"According to the lesson, the aorist ἔμαθον means the learning happened instantly.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The aorist views the whole learning process as one completed whole; it does not mean it happened instantly."},{"q":"How does the lesson say this aorist from Matthew 9:27 is often rendered?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"they had followed\"","\"they began to follow\"","\"they will follow\"","\"I have learned\""],"answer":1,"explain":"ἠκολούθησαν is often rendered \"began to follow,\" catching the entry into following.","greek":"ἠκολούθησαν"},{"q":"Which aorist category does the lesson illustrate with this verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Constative","Culminative","Ingressive","Summary"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἠκολούθησαν in Matthew 9:27 is the lesson's ingressive example: the entry into following.","greek":"ἠκολούθησαν"},{"q":"This form is an aorist of which verb, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["ἀγαπάω","λέγω","ἔρχομαι","ἀκολουθέω"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson identifies ἠκολούθησαν as the aorist of ἀκολουθέω.","greek":"ἠκολούθησαν"},{"q":"With which kind of verbs does the lesson say the ingressive use is particularly common?","kind":"concept","options":["Stative verbs","Contract verbs","μι-verbs","Verbs of speaking"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ingressive use is particularly common with stative verbs, which describe ongoing conditions."},{"q":"How does the lesson define stative verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Verbs that describe sudden events","Verbs that describe ongoing conditions","Verbs that have no aorist","Verbs used only in commands"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stative verbs describe ongoing conditions."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the aorist of a stative verb naturally highlight?","kind":"concept","options":["The end of the condition","The whole condition as a summary","The entrance into that condition","The repetition of the condition"],"answer":2,"explain":"With stative verbs, the aorist naturally highlights the entrance into the condition."},{"q":"In the lesson's reading of Matthew 9:27, what continues while the aorist catches the beginning?","kind":"reading","options":["The ongoing following","The crowd's questions","Paul's learning","The healing"],"answer":0,"explain":"The following continues; the ingressive aorist catches its beginning.","greek":"ἠκολούθησαν"},{"q":"What does the lesson's helpful habit say to do first when analyzing an aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Choose a category label","Look up a theological commentary","Identify the aorist form","Translate it as simple past"],"answer":2,"explain":"First identify the form, then ask what the context suggests; only then is a label useful."},{"q":"Following the lesson's checklist, which category should you consider if the action involves entering a state?","kind":"concept","options":["Constative","Summary","Complexive","Ingressive"],"answer":3,"explain":"Entering a state points toward an ingressive reading."},{"q":"If neither the constative nor the ingressive pattern is clear, what does the lesson advise?","kind":"concept","options":["Stay with the basic whole-action view","Default to ingressive","Default to constative","Treat it as an imperfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"When no pattern is clear, stay with the basic whole-action view of the aorist."},{"q":"Complete the lesson's summary: \"Labels articulate interpretation. They do not ___ it.\"","kind":"concept","options":["describe","generate","support","explain"],"answer":1,"explain":"Labels express an interpretation you have reached from context; they do not create it."},{"q":"According to the lesson, Greek writers were not consulting grammar categories when they wrote.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Writers used the aorist naturally; grammarians named the patterns afterward."},{"q":"According to the intro, what do labels like constative and ingressive help articulate?","kind":"concept","options":["What a particular aorist appears to communicate","How an aorist is spelled","Which manuscript is oldest","Whether a verb is a contract verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"The labels help articulate what a particular aorist appears to communicate."},{"q":"According to the lesson, these aorist categories arise from the morphology alone.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The categories describe what the aorist communicates in context; they do not arise from the morphology alone."},{"q":"According to the intro, who developed interpretive categories such as constative and ingressive?","kind":"concept","options":["The NT writers","Ancient scribes","Grammarians","Greek poets"],"answer":2,"explain":"Grammarians developed these categories to describe how the aorist functions. The NT writers did not consult them."},{"q":"An event may have taken considerable time in reality. How does a constative aorist present it?","kind":"concept","options":["As a series of repeated acts","As still in progress","As a unit","As only its first moment"],"answer":2,"explain":"However long the event took, the constative aorist simply presents it as a unit."},{"q":"A writer uses one aorist to sum up a journey that lasted many years. Following the lesson's checklist, which category should you consider?","kind":"concept","options":["Ingressive","Inceptive","Stative","Constative"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the action spans a long period, the checklist says to consider constative."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how should you treat constative, ingressive, and other aorist categories?","kind":"concept","options":["As descriptive vocabulary for communicating your analysis","As rules the text must follow","As features marked by special endings","As labels to apply before reading the context"],"answer":0,"explain":"The categories are descriptive vocabulary for communicating your analysis, not rules imposed on the text."},{"q":"Rather than summarizing the whole event, the ingressive aorist highlights the moment of what?","kind":"concept","options":["Completion","Reflection","Repetition","Initiation"],"answer":3,"explain":"The ingressive aorist highlights the moment of initiation."},{"q":"Which aorist category does the lesson sum up as \"the whole event bundled as one unit\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Ingressive","Inceptive","Constative","Stative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Constative = the whole event bundled as one unit."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what do constative, ingressive and the other aorist categories represent?","kind":"concept","options":["Rules the NT writers followed","Meanings built into special endings","Labels found in the ancient manuscripts","Patterns observed across many texts"],"answer":3,"explain":"The categories are patterns observed across many texts, tendencies that grammarians documented and named."},{"q":"In the lesson's helpful habit, what do you ask right after identifying the aorist form?","kind":"concept","options":["What the context suggests about how the action is being presented","Which label fits the verb best","How the verb is accented","Whether the verb is first or second aorist"],"answer":0,"explain":"Identify the form first, then ask what the context suggests about how the action is being presented. Only then does a label become useful."},{"q":"According to the lesson's helpful habit, when does a label such as constative or ingressive become useful?","kind":"concept","options":["Before you look at the form","As soon as you see an augment","Only when the verb is stative","After identifying the form and asking about context"],"answer":3,"explain":"Identify the form, ask what context suggests, and only then use a label to articulate your interpretation."},{"q":"The lesson glosses this form \"I have learned.\" What tense does the lesson say it is?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect","Imperfect","Present","Aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson marks ἔμαθον as an aorist. The English \"have learned\" renders it as Paul summing up a whole learning process; the Greek form is not a perfect.","greek":"ἔμαθον"}],"va_08":[{"q":"Which other names does the lesson give for the culminative aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Complexive or summary aorist","Effective or resultative aorist","Inceptive or ingressive aorist","Historical or vivid aorist"],"answer":1,"explain":"The culminative aorist is also called the effective or resultative aorist."},{"q":"Which informal description does the lesson give for the culminative aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["The \"I finally did it\" aorist","The \"I just started\" aorist","The \"I kept doing it\" aorist","The \"I will do it\" aorist"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is sometimes described as the \"I finally did it\" aorist, because the goal was reached."},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["to persuade","to find","to conquer","to complete"],"answer":2,"explain":"νικάω means \"to conquer,\" one of the lesson's classic culminative verbs.","greek":"νικάω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["to conquer","to find","to complete","to persuade"],"answer":3,"explain":"πείθω means \"to persuade,\" a verb of persuasion often read culminatively in the aorist.","greek":"πείθω"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["to find","to persuade","to conquer","to complete"],"answer":0,"explain":"εὑρίσκω means \"to find,\" a goal-directed verb that suits a culminative reading.","greek":"εὑρίσκω"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this aorist from Luke 15:24?","kind":"meaning","options":["\"will be found\"","\"was found\"","\"is finding\"","\"was searching\""],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson renders εὑρέθη as \"was found.\"","greek":"εὑρέθη"},{"q":"In Luke 15:24, what does the lesson say this aorist emphasizes?","kind":"reading","options":["The beginning of a search","The achieved result of a search","A search that is still going on","A repeated search"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist here emphasizes the achieved result: the one who was lost has been found.","greek":"εὑρέθη"},{"q":"The lesson identifies this form from John 19:30 as an aorist.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson notes τετέλεσται is a perfect, not an aorist, and brings it in for comparison.","greek":"τετέλεσται"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the perfect of τελέω show?","kind":"form","options":["Ongoing action in the past","The beginning of an action","Completed action with present result","A future expectation"],"answer":2,"explain":"The perfect τετέλεσται shows completed action with a present result.","greek":"τετέλεσται"},{"q":"Which verb is this form the perfect of, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["νικάω","πείθω","εὑρίσκω","τελέω"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson calls τετέλεσται the perfect of τελέω.","greek":"τετέλεσται"},{"q":"Which image does the lesson use to sum up culminative force?","kind":"concept","options":["A camera panning across a scene","A finish line being crossed","A door swinging open","A photograph of a crowd"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Culminative = the action reached its goal. The finish line was crossed.\""},{"q":"Which question does the lesson say helps distinguish a culminative aorist from a constative one?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb in the indicative or the subjunctive?","Does the verb have a first or second aorist stem?","Does context emphasize the process bundled together, or the successful achievement of an outcome?","Is the subject singular or plural?"],"answer":2,"explain":"Constative bundles the process together; culminative stresses that an outcome was achieved."},{"q":"In the lesson, what does \"telic\" mean?","kind":"concept","options":["Goal-directed","Ongoing","Repeated","Unreal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Telic verbs have goal-directed meanings, such as finding or winning."},{"q":"What kind of verbs does the lesson say are most naturally read culminatively when they appear in the aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["Atelic verbs","Stative verbs","Telic verbs","Contract verbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Telic verbs, whose meanings aim at a goal, are most naturally read culminatively in the aorist."},{"q":"An aorist of a verb meaning \"walk\" appears with no strong signal from context. According to the lesson, which reading does it more naturally lend itself to?","kind":"concept","options":["Culminative","Ingressive","Effective","Constative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Atelic verbs like walk, speak, and live more naturally lend themselves to constative readings."},{"q":"The lesson says the constative, ingressive, and culminative categories are exhaustive.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. They cover the major patterns but are not exhaustive."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the aorist categories are not mutually exclusive in all cases.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says the categories are not always mutually exclusive."},{"q":"If the context does not clearly indicate constative, ingressive, or culminative force, what does the lesson advise?","kind":"concept","options":["Choose culminative by default","Describe the aorist as presenting the action as a complete whole","Treat the verb as an imperfect","Choose the label that fits your theology"],"answer":1,"explain":"Do not force a label; simply describe the aorist as presenting the action as a complete whole."},{"q":"Complete the lesson's formula: \"Lexical meaning + context = ___.\"","kind":"concept","options":["the aspect","the parse","the interpretation","the tense"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lexical meaning plus context gives the interpretation; the aorist supplies the aspect framework."},{"q":"In that formula, what does the lesson say the aorist itself provides?","kind":"concept","options":["The aspect framework","The final interpretation","The lexical meaning","The discourse purpose"],"answer":0,"explain":"The aorist provides the aspect framework; meaning and context fill in the interpretation."},{"q":"The culminative aorist is still the aorist. Which view of the action does it keep?","kind":"concept","options":["The inside view","The background view","The whole-action view","The future view"],"answer":2,"explain":"It is still the whole-action view; the weight just falls on the end point."},{"q":"Which kinds of verbs does the lesson give as the classic example of culminative force?","kind":"concept","options":["Verbs of speaking or hearing","Verbs of being or becoming","Verbs of motion or rest","Verbs of persuasion or winning"],"answer":3,"explain":"The classic example involves verbs of persuasion or winning, like πείθω and νικάω."},{"q":"In which parable does the lesson say εὑρέθη appears?","kind":"reading","options":["The sower","The prodigal son","The good Samaritan","The mustard seed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson cites εὑρέθη from the prodigal son parable in Luke 15:24."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the things the lesson says matters most when using aorist categories?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every possible category","Asking what aspect of that whole the context emphasizes","Giving every aorist a label","Ignoring the verb's lexical meaning"],"answer":1,"explain":"What matters is seeing the aorist as a whole and asking which aspect of that whole the context emphasizes."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the same aorist form could be read constatively or culminatively depending on its context.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Lexical meaning, the surrounding text, and discourse purpose decide between the readings."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what do aorists in contexts similar to John 19:30 often carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Comparable culminative weight","Ingressive force","Imperfective aspect","Future reference"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aorists in similar contexts often carry comparable culminative weight."},{"q":"Which of these does the lesson list as a telic (goal-directed) meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Walking","Speaking","Living","Saving"],"answer":3,"explain":"Saving is listed with finding, winning, and persuading as telic; walk, speak, and live are atelic."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the factors the lesson lists for deciding between a constative and a culminative reading?","kind":"concept","options":["The lexical meaning of the verb","The surrounding narrative or argument","The discourse purpose of the passage","The verb's accent mark"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists lexical meaning, the surrounding narrative or argument, and the discourse purpose. The accent mark is not a factor."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the categories the lesson says cover the major patterns of aorist interpretation?","kind":"concept","options":["Constative","Ingressive","Culminative","Historical present"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson names constative, ingressive, and culminative as the categories that cover the major patterns; the historical present is not among them."},{"q":"According to the intro, what does the ingressive aorist highlight, in contrast to the culminative?","kind":"concept","options":["The end result","The entry into an action","The background scene","The whole process bundled together"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ingressive highlights the entry into an action; the culminative shifts attention to its completion."}],"va_09":[{"q":"According to the lesson, which forms do beginners sometimes confuse second aorists with?","kind":"concept","options":["Present forms","Imperfect forms","Future forms","Perfect forms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Second aorists use imperfect-style endings, so beginners sometimes take them for imperfects."},{"q":"How does the first aorist form its stem?","kind":"form","options":["By a vowel change","By using an entirely different root","By adding a sigma (-σ-) to the verb root","By doubling the first consonant"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first aorist adds a sigma (-σ-) to the verb root."},{"q":"This is the lesson's famous second aorist example. Which present form does it belong to?","kind":"form","options":["λέγω","ὁράω","λαμβάνω","ἔρχομαι"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔρχομαι (I come) has the second aorist ἦλθον (I came); the stems look completely different.","greek":"ἦλθον"},{"q":"What does this second aorist mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I said","I saw","I came","I became"],"answer":0,"explain":"εἶπον is the second aorist of λέγω and means \"I said.\"","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"Which present form does this second aorist belong to?","kind":"form","options":["λέγω","ὁράω","φέρω","γίνομαι"],"answer":1,"explain":"εἶδον (I saw) is the second aorist of ὁράω.","greek":"εἶδον"},{"q":"What does this second aorist mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I brought","I came","I took/received","I said"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλαβον is the second aorist of λαμβάνω and means \"I took/received.\"","greek":"ἔλαβον"},{"q":"Which form does the lesson give as the aorist of φέρω?","kind":"form","options":["ἦλθον","ἔλαβον","εἶπον","ἤνεγκον"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists φέρω → ἤνεγκον (I brought).","greek":"φέρω"},{"q":"Which present form does this second aorist belong to?","kind":"form","options":["γίνομαι","ἔρχομαι","λαμβάνω","ὁράω"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐγενόμην (I became) is the second aorist of γίνομαι.","greek":"ἐγενόμην"},{"q":"How does the second aorist form its stem, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["By adding a sigma to the root","Often through a vowel change or an entirely different root","By adding reduplication","By adding a connecting vowel only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The second aorist forms its stem differently, often through a vowel change or a different root."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["I loosed","I received","I was loosing","I came"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυον is the imperfect, \"I was loosing\": augmented present stem plus secondary endings.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the make-up of an imperfect?","kind":"form","options":["Augmented present stem + secondary endings","Augmented second-aorist stem + secondary endings","Present stem + sigma + endings","Reduplicated stem + primary endings"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfect is the augmented present stem with secondary endings."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the make-up of a second aorist?","kind":"form","options":["Augmented present stem + secondary endings","Present stem + sigma + endings","Reduplicated stem + primary endings","Augmented second-aorist stem + secondary endings"],"answer":3,"explain":"A second aorist is the augmented second-aorist stem with secondary endings."},{"q":"According to the lesson, if a form's stem looks like the present tense stem, it is likely a second aorist.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. If the stem looks like the present stem, it is likely imperfect; a different-looking stem points to a second aorist."},{"q":"A form has an augment and imperfect-style endings, but you do not recognize the stem. What does the lesson say to suspect?","kind":"concept","options":["A first aorist","A present","A second aorist","A future"],"answer":2,"explain":"When a form looks like an imperfect but the stem is unfamiliar, suspect a second aorist."},{"q":"Both ἔλαβον and ἔλυον have an augment and secondary endings. What marks ἔλαβον as a second aorist?","kind":"form","options":["Its ending -ον","Its stem differs from the present stem of λαμβάνω","Its augment ε-","An added sigma"],"answer":1,"explain":"The endings and augment match the imperfect; the different-looking stem is what marks a second aorist.","greek":"ἔλαβον"},{"q":"According to the lesson, second aorists use different stems that often look quite unlike the present tense form of the same verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. That is why second aorists call for a stronger recognition skill."},{"q":"What does the lesson recommend when an unfamiliar form appears?","kind":"concept","options":["Skip it and keep reading","Guess from the ending","Use lexicons, working backward from the aorist stem to the lexical form","Treat it as a present"],"answer":2,"explain":"Use a lexicon and work backward from the aorist stem to the lexical form."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what builds stem recognition naturally over time?","kind":"concept","options":["Frequency exposure through reading","Memorizing charts only","Translating English into Greek","Studying accent rules"],"answer":0,"explain":"Frequency exposure through reading builds stem recognition naturally over time."},{"q":"Why does the lesson say recognizing second aorist forms is practically essential?","kind":"concept","options":["They appear only in the Gospels","They are among the most frequent verbs in the NT","They have a different aspect","They are always passive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Many of the most frequent NT verbs have second aorists, so recognizing them is essential for fluent reading."},{"q":"Which aorist pattern does the lesson say most beginning students learn first?","kind":"concept","options":["Second aorist","Suppletive aorist","Imperfect","First aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"The sigma pattern of the first aorist is the one most beginning students learn first."},{"q":"How does the lesson gloss this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["I was loosing","I loose","I loosed","I will loose"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυσα means \"I loosed\"; it is a first aorist.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"According to the lesson, second aorists can be recognized by a regular pattern, the way first aorists are recognized by their added sigma.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Second aorists must be recognized by stem, not by a regular pattern."},{"q":"The lesson describes ἤνεγκον, listed under φέρω, as actually a suppletive form.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson notes that φέρω → ἤνεγκον is actually a suppletive form.","greek":"ἤνεγκον"},{"q":"Which of these forms is NOT a second aorist?","kind":"form","options":["ἦλθον","εἶδον","ἔλαβον","ἔλυσα"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔλυσα is a first aorist (added sigma); ἦλθον, εἶδον, and ἔλαβον are second aorists."},{"q":"Which of these forms is an imperfect rather than an aorist?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυσα","ἔλυον","ἔλαβον","εἶπον"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυον is the augmented present stem with secondary endings, so it is an imperfect."},{"q":"How many of the most frequent NT verbs does the lesson say you should learn second aorist stems for, to cover the vast majority of occurrences?","kind":"concept","options":["3-5","15-20","100-120","All of them"],"answer":1,"explain":"Learning the second aorist stems of the 15-20 most frequent verbs covers the vast majority of occurrences."},{"q":"What does this second aorist mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I came","I saw","I took/received","I became"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐγενόμην is the second aorist of γίνομαι and means \"I became.\"","greek":"ἐγενόμην"},{"q":"Which present form does the lesson list with this second aorist?","kind":"form","options":["λέγω","ὁράω","ἔρχομαι","φέρω"],"answer":0,"explain":"λέγω → εἶπον (I said). The present and aorist stems look completely different, so the pair has to be learned by stem.","greek":"εἶπον"},{"q":"Which two features does the imperfect share with the second aorist, according to the lesson's formulas?","kind":"form","options":["Present stem and primary endings","Sigma and secondary endings","Reduplication and augment","Augment and secondary endings"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both are augmented and take secondary endings; only the stem tells them apart."},{"q":"According to the intro, in how many different ways do Greek verbs form their aorist?","kind":"concept","options":["One","Three","Two","Four"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek verbs form their aorist in two ways, labeled first aorist and second aorist."}],"va_31":[{"q":"Where is a tense formative placed in a Greek verb form?","kind":"concept","options":["At the very front, before the augment","Between the lexical stem and the personal ending","After the personal ending, at the end of the word","Between the preposition and the stem of a compound"],"answer":1,"explain":"A tense formative is a small element slotted in between the lexical stem and the personal ending. Its job is to signal which tense-voice system the form belongs to."},{"q":"Which tense formative appears in this form?","kind":"form","options":["κ","θη","σ","No tense formative"],"answer":2,"explain":"λύσω is built as λύ + σ + ω. The σ between stem and ending is the tense formative of the future.","greek":"λύσω"},{"q":"Which letter in this form is the tense formative?","kind":"form","options":["κ","λ","υ","α"],"answer":0,"explain":"λέλυκα breaks down as λε + λυ + κ + α. The κ sitting between the stem and the ending is the perfect active tense formative.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which tense formative appears in this form?","kind":"form","options":["σ","κ","σα","θη"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐλύθην is ἐλύ + θη + ν. The θη formative marks the passive systems of the aorist and future.","greek":"ἐλύθην"},{"q":"Besides the aorist passive, which other tense-voice system does the θη formative signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfect active","Present middle","Future passive","Imperfect active"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's table lists θη for both the aorist passive and the future passive. So θη points you to a passive system."},{"q":"If a verb form shows no tense formative at all, which tenses are most likely?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfect active","Present or imperfect","Future or aorist","Future passive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Present and imperfect forms are built straight from stem + connecting vowel + ending. The absence of σ, κ or θη is itself a clue."},{"q":"How many key tense formatives does the lesson's table list?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson names three key tense formatives: σ, κ and θη. Each one points to a different tense-voice system."},{"q":"This form is built as λύ + σ + ο + μεν. What is the ο?","kind":"form","options":["The tense formative","The connecting vowel","The augment","The personal ending"],"answer":1,"explain":"The future is built by inserting σ between the stem and the connecting vowel + ending. In λύ + σ + ο + μεν, the ο is that connecting vowel.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"How does this form differ from the present λύομεν?","kind":"form","options":["A σ is inserted after the stem, making it future","An augment is added, making it imperfect","A κ is inserted, making it perfect","A θη is inserted, making it passive"],"answer":0,"explain":"λύσομεν is λύ + σ + ο + μεν. Inserting σ before the connecting vowel turns the present into the future.","greek":"λύσομεν"},{"q":"Which set of pieces builds this form, according to the lesson?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication + stem + κ + ending","Stem + σ + connecting vowel + primary ending","Augment + stem + σα + secondary ending","Augment + stem + θη + ending"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐλύσαμεν is ἐ + λύ + σα + μεν. The augment plus σ and a secondary ending marks the aorist active.","greek":"ἐλύσαμεν"},{"q":"You see a σ formative in a verb. What extra feature tells you it is aorist rather than future?","kind":"concept","options":["The connecting vowel ο","An augment at the front","A κ after the σ","A liquid stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"σ by itself could be future or aorist. When the form also carries the augment, it narrows to the aorist."},{"q":"What kind of personal endings does the aorist active combine with its σ formative?","kind":"concept","options":["Primary endings","Perfect endings","Passive endings","Secondary endings"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist active joins the augment and σ with secondary endings, not primary ones. That combination separates it from the future."},{"q":"Which group lists the liquid consonants named in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["λ, μ, ν, ρ","σ, ξ, ψ, ζ","π, β, φ, ψ","κ, γ, χ, ξ"],"answer":0,"explain":"The liquid consonants are λ, μ, ν and ρ. Verbs whose stems end in one of these cannot simply attach σ in the future."},{"q":"Why does a liquid verb not form its future with a simple σ?","kind":"concept","options":["Liquid verbs have no future tense","The σ is replaced by κ in the future","A liquid followed directly by σ is phonologically unworkable","Liquid verbs always use the θη formative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Greek sound rules do not allow a liquid stem to take σ directly. So these verbs use a different route to build the future."},{"q":"What element does a liquid future use where other verbs use a plain σ?","kind":"concept","options":["κ","εσ","θη","σα"],"answer":1,"explain":"Liquid futures use εσ. The σ then drops out and the ε contracts with the connecting vowel."},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I hear","I loose","I write","I judge"],"answer":3,"explain":"κρίνω means \"I judge.\" The lesson uses it as its model liquid verb.","greek":"κρίνω"},{"q":"The lesson gives the stem of κρίνω as κριν-. Which liquid consonant does this stem end in?","kind":"form","options":["ν","ρ","λ","μ"],"answer":0,"explain":"κριν- ends in ν, one of the liquids λ, μ, ν, ρ. The ρ inside the stem does not matter: what counts is the consonant at the end of the stem.","greek":"κρίνω"},{"q":"How should this form be identified?","kind":"form","options":["Present active of κρίνω","Aorist active of κρίνω","Future of κρίνω, a liquid future","Perfect active of κρίνω"],"answer":2,"explain":"κρινῶ is the future of κρίνω. It has no visible σ and is contracted like an ε-contract form, which is the mark of a liquid future.","greek":"κρινῶ"},{"q":"In the lesson's step-by-step derivation of the liquid future, what does this stage represent?","kind":"form","options":["The present active, before any change","The form after σ drops, before ε contracts with the connecting vowel","The aorist, after compensatory lengthening","The perfect, after κ is added"],"answer":1,"explain":"κριν + εσ + ω loses its σ and becomes κρινέω. Then the ε contracts with the connecting vowel to give κρινῶ.","greek":"κρινέω"},{"q":"In a liquid future, what happens to the ε of εσ once the σ drops out?","kind":"concept","options":["It lengthens to η","It drops out as well","It moves to the front as an augment","It contracts with the connecting vowel"],"answer":3,"explain":"With the σ gone, the ε stands next to the connecting vowel and the two contract. That is why κρινέω becomes κρινῶ."},{"q":"Liquid futures end up looking like the forms of which kind of verb?","kind":"concept","options":["ε-contract verbs","Second perfect verbs","Aorist passive verbs","Verbs with a temporal augment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the ε of εσ contracts with the connecting vowel, liquid futures look just like ε-contract forms."},{"q":"True or false: the liquid future is an irregularity with no rule behind it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson stresses that the liquid future follows a systematic sound rule. It is predictable once you know the εσ pattern."},{"q":"According to the lesson, compensatory lengthening is what happens in which form?","kind":"concept","options":["The liquid future","The present active","The liquid aorist","The aorist passive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson separates the two: liquid futures contract, while compensatory lengthening belongs to the liquid aorist, as in ἔμεινα."},{"q":"True or false: λύω counts as a liquid verb because it begins with λ.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. What matters is how the stem ends, not how it begins. The stem of λύω ends in a vowel, so its future simply adds σ: λύσω."},{"q":"The lesson calls the tense formative one of the most powerful slots of what?","kind":"concept","options":["The principal parts list","The case system","The full morphological template","The accent rules"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson builds on the full morphological template of the Greek verb and zooms in on one slot of it: the tense formative between stem and ending."},{"q":"True or false: a liquid future such as κρινῶ shows no visible σ.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The σ of εσ drops out, so liquid futures lack σ even though they are futures."},{"q":"According to the lesson's table, which voices of the future are signaled by the σ formative?","kind":"concept","options":["Active and middle","Passive only","Active only","Middle and passive"],"answer":0,"explain":"σ covers the future active and middle. The future passive uses θη instead."},{"q":"When no tense formative is present, how does the lesson say a present or imperfect form is built?","kind":"concept","options":["Stem + σ + ending","Reduplication + stem + κ + ending","Stem + θη + ending","Stem + connecting vowel + ending"],"answer":3,"explain":"Present and imperfect forms are built directly from the stem, a connecting vowel and the ending, with nothing in the tense formative slot."},{"q":"Which pair of forms both carry the σ tense formative?","kind":"form","options":["λύομεν and λέλυκα","λύσομεν and ἐλύσαμεν","ἐλύθην and λύομεν","λέλυκα and ἐλύθην"],"answer":1,"explain":"λύσομεν (future) and ἐλύσαμεν (aorist) both have σ after the stem. λύομεν has no formative, λέλυκα has κ, and ἐλύθην has θη."},{"q":"True or false: a verb form with a θη formative belongs to the perfect active system.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. θη signals the aorist passive or future passive. The perfect active uses κ."}],"va_32":[{"q":"What does the augment signal in a Greek verb?","kind":"concept","options":["Past time in the indicative","Completed action with an ongoing result","Passive voice","Future time"],"answer":0,"explain":"The augment is the past-time prefix. It marks past time in the indicative mood."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the augment marks the perfect, while reduplication signals past indicative time.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. It is the other way around: the augment signals past indicative time, and reduplication is the signature of the perfect."},{"q":"When is the syllabic augment used?","kind":"concept","options":["With vowel-initial stems","With consonant-initial stems","Only with compound verbs","Only in the perfect tense"],"answer":1,"explain":"The syllabic augment adds ε- to stems that begin with a consonant. Vowel-initial stems take the temporal augment instead."},{"q":"How does the temporal augment mark past time?","kind":"concept","options":["It adds ε- before the stem","It repeats the first consonant plus ε","It lengthens the initial vowel of a vowel-initial stem","It adds κ after the stem"],"answer":2,"explain":"With vowel-initial stems there is no ε- prefix. Instead the first vowel is lengthened, and that lengthening is the augment."},{"q":"This augmented form is the lesson's example of the syllabic augment. Which verb does it come from?","kind":"form","options":["γράφω","ἀκούω","στέλλω","λύω"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔλυον is ε- plus the stem of λύω. Because λύω begins with a consonant, it takes the syllabic augment.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"Why does ἀκούω take a temporal augment rather than the syllabic ε-?","kind":"concept","options":["It is a compound verb","It is in the perfect","Its stem begins with a vowel","Its stem begins with an aspirated consonant"],"answer":2,"explain":"The syllabic ε- is added to consonant-initial stems. ἀκούω begins with a vowel, so its augment is made by lengthening that vowel instead.","greek":"ἀκούω"},{"q":"Under the temporal augment, what does an initial ο lengthen to?","kind":"concept","options":["ω","η","ου","ῳ"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's list of temporal augment lengthenings includes ο→ω."},{"q":"In this imperfect, which type of augment follows the prefix ἐξ-?","kind":"form","options":["Temporal augment","Syllabic augment","Reduplication","No augment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment goes on the verbal element βαλλ-, which begins with a consonant. Consonant-initial stems take the syllabic augment ε-, giving ἐξ-έ-βαλλον.","greek":"ἐξέβαλλον"},{"q":"Which temporal augment lengthening is listed correctly in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["ε→ει","ο→ου","α→αι","αυ→ηυ"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists αυ→ηυ among the temporal augment lengthenings. The other three changes are not on its list."},{"q":"Which statement about λέλυκα and γέγραφα is correct?","kind":"form","options":["Both have κ, but only γέγραφα is reduplicated","Neither is reduplicated; both use an augment","Both are reduplicated, but only γέγραφα has κ","Both are reduplicated, but only λέλυκα has κ"],"answer":3,"explain":"λε + λυ + κ + α and γε + γραφ + α both begin with reduplication. γράφω forms a second perfect, so γέγραφα has no κ."},{"q":"The stem of this verb is λυ-. Which syllable would serve as its reduplication prefix?","kind":"form","options":["λε","ἐ","λυ","λη"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reduplication repeats the stem's first consonant and adds ε. For λυ- that gives λε-, as in λέλυκα.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"Why is there no κ in this perfect form?","kind":"form","options":["It is an aorist passive","It is a second perfect","It is a liquid future","κ appears only in the middle voice"],"answer":1,"explain":"γράφω forms a second perfect, which has no κ. It still shows reduplication, γε-.","greek":"γέγραφα"},{"q":"What aspect does reduplication communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective: action unfolding","Perfective: action viewed as a simple whole","Future: action still to come","Stative: completed action with an ongoing result"],"answer":3,"explain":"Reduplication marks the perfect, whose stative aspect presents a completed action with an ongoing result."},{"q":"Which tense does the doubled λε- at the front of this form signal?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist","Imperfect","Perfect","Future"],"answer":2,"explain":"A doubled initial consonant plus ε at the front is reduplication, and reduplication signals the perfect.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"This form begins with πε-. What does that prefix show?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication of a φ-initial stem, using unaspirated π","A syllabic augment on a π-initial stem","A temporal augment of the vowel ε","A preposition prefix πε-"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stem begins with φ, an aspirated consonant. Reduplication uses the unaspirated π instead, so the prefix is πε-.","greek":"πεφίλημαι"},{"q":"In a compound verb, where does the augment go?","kind":"concept","options":["Before the preposition prefix","On the verbal element, after the prefix","At the end of the word","On both the preposition and the verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augment belongs to the verb, not to the preposition. So it is placed on the verbal element, after the prefix."},{"q":"This is the imperfect of ἐκβάλλω. Where is the augment?","kind":"form","options":["Before ἐξ-","On the final -ον","Between the prefix and βαλλ-","There is no augment"],"answer":2,"explain":"In ἐξ-έβαλλον the ε- augment sits after the prefix ἐξ- and right before the verbal stem βαλλ-.","greek":"ἐξέβαλλον"},{"q":"This is the perfect of στέλλω. Why does it begin with just ἐ- instead of full reduplication?","kind":"form","options":["στέλλω is a compound verb","στέλλω has an aspirated stem","The perfect never reduplicates","στέλλω begins with two consonants"],"answer":3,"explain":"Stems beginning with two consonants usually take only ε- in the perfect. στέλλω starts with στ, so its perfect is ἔσταλκα.","greek":"ἔσταλκα"},{"q":"γράφω also begins with two consonants. Why does its perfect still show full reduplication?","kind":"concept","options":["γρ is a stop plus a liquid","γράφω is a compound verb","γράφω takes a temporal augment","γράφω has an aspirated first letter"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two-consonant rule has a limit: a stop followed by a liquid still reduplicates. γρ is that kind of cluster, so the perfect is γέγραφα.","greek":"γέγραφα"},{"q":"Besides two-consonant clusters, which initial letters usually take only ε- in the perfect instead of full reduplication?","kind":"concept","options":["λ, μ, ν","ζ, ξ, ψ","π, κ, τ","α, ε, ο"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson groups ζ, ξ and ψ with two-consonant starts: in the perfect they usually take only ε-."},{"q":"What is the lesson's first step when you meet an unfamiliar verb form?","kind":"concept","options":["Look the form up in a lexicon","Count its syllables","Strip the initial syllable and ask if it could be an augment or reduplication","Check whether it has an article"],"answer":2,"explain":"Start at the front: remove the first syllable and test whether it is an augment or a reduplication prefix."},{"q":"After checking the front of an unfamiliar form for augment or reduplication, what should you identify next?","kind":"concept","options":["The stem, tense formative and ending","The accent and breathing mark","The article and its case","The preposition and its object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once the prefix is settled, the lesson says to identify the stem, the tense formative and the ending."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, compound verbs and aspirated stems follow consistent rules rather than being exceptions.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says these cases are the normal rule applied in a different context, not exceptions."},{"q":"Which of these forms shows a syllabic augment?","kind":"form","options":["ἤκουον","ἔλυον","λέλυκα","γέγραφα"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυον adds ε- to a consonant-initial stem. ἤκουον has a temporal augment, while λέλυκα and γέγραφα show reduplication."},{"q":"Which of these forms shows reduplication rather than an augment?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυον","ἤκουον","ἐξέβαλλον","γέγραφα"],"answer":3,"explain":"γέγραφα repeats the initial γ plus ε, which is reduplication. The other three carry augments."},{"q":"Which tense formative follows the reduplicated stem in this form?","kind":"form","options":["σ","θη","κ","No formative"],"answer":2,"explain":"λέλυκα is built as λε + λυ + κ + α. The κ comes after the reduplicated stem and before the ending.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"True or false: the ἐ- at the front of ἔσταλκα is the past-time augment.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. ἔσταλκα is a perfect. Because στέλλω begins with two consonants, its perfect takes only ε- in place of full reduplication; this ε- is not the past-time augment.","greek":"ἔσταλκα"},{"q":"Why can this form not be a subjunctive or a participle?","kind":"concept","options":["It ends in -ον","It carries an augment, which appears only in the indicative","It has no tense formative","It comes from a consonant-initial stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ε- at the front is an augment. Since the augment occurs only in the indicative, ἔλυον must be indicative.","greek":"ἔλυον"},{"q":"Under the temporal augment, what does an initial αι become?","kind":"concept","options":["η","ει","αι with no change","ῃ"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's list of temporal augment lengthenings gives αι→ῃ."},{"q":"Greek has two types of augment. What are they called?","kind":"concept","options":["Primary and secondary","First and second","Syllabic and temporal","Liquid and contract"],"answer":2,"explain":"The syllabic augment adds ε-, and the temporal augment lengthens an initial vowel. Both mark past time in the indicative."}],"va_10":[{"q":"In this lesson, what does \"temporal reference\" describe?","kind":"concept","options":["When the action occurs relative to the moment of speaking or writing","The speaker's perspective on the action","Who performs the action","Whether the verb gives a command"],"answer":0,"explain":"Temporal reference is about time: when the action happens compared with the moment the speaker speaks or the writer writes."},{"q":"Which aspect views an action from the inside, as unfolding?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective","Imperfective","Stative","Temporal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Imperfective aspect looks at the action from inside, as something in progress or unfolding."},{"q":"Which aspect presents an action as a whole, complete event?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective","Stative","Perfective","Indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Perfective aspect views the action as a whole, from the outside, rather than as unfolding."},{"q":"Which aspect focuses on a resultant state?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective","Perfective","Indicative","Stative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Stative aspect presents the state that results from an action."},{"q":"In which mood are both aspect and temporal reference typically present?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Indicative","Imperative","Infinitive"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indicative typically carries both kinds of information: aspect and time."},{"q":"In a subjunctive form, which kind of information is primary?","kind":"concept","options":["Temporal reference","Voice","Aspect","Person"],"answer":2,"explain":"The subjunctive is a non-indicative mood, and in non-indicative moods aspect is primary."},{"q":"You meet an infinitive in a passage. According to the lesson, how should you treat its time reference?","kind":"concept","options":["As fixed by the tense form alone","As absent, or derived from context","As always past","As always future"],"answer":1,"explain":"The infinitive is non-indicative. Its tense signals aspect, while any time reference is either absent or comes from context."},{"q":"Which group does the lesson list together as non-indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Indicative, subjunctive, imperative","Present, aorist, perfect","Active, middle, passive","Subjunctive, infinitive, imperative, participle"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson names the subjunctive, infinitive, imperative and participle as the non-indicative forms where aspect is primary."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson warns only against aspect-only positions and sees no risk in time-only positions.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson lists risks on both sides: aspect-only views can strip temporal clarity, and time-only views can miss the significance of aspect choices."},{"q":"Which position does the lesson associate with Stanley Porter?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense forms communicate only aspect, with no inherent time even in the indicative","Tense forms communicate only time, with no aspect","Aspect matters only in participles","The indicative has no aspect at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Porter argued for an aspect-only view, holding that even the indicative has no inherent temporal reference."},{"q":"What do most traditional grammarians maintain about the indicative mood?","kind":"concept","options":["It carries only aspect","It carries both aspect and temporal reference","It carries only time","It carries neither aspect nor time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Traditional grammarians hold that the indicative carries both aspect and temporal reference."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the middle position held by most working NT scholars?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect only, with no time at all","Time only, with aspect a modern invention","Aspect primary, with temporal reference a real and regular secondary feature","Neither aspect nor time carries meaning"],"answer":2,"explain":"Most working NT scholars treat aspect as primary while still seeing time as a real, regular secondary feature, especially in the indicative."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says denying time from Greek tenses entirely can create as many problems as it solves.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson warns that an aspect-only view taken to the extreme causes its own problems."},{"q":"What does the lesson say about the stakes of the scholarly debate on tense?","kind":"concept","options":["It has real exegetical stakes","It matters only for Classical Greek","It has no effect on reading the NT","It concerns only accent marks"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says the debate has real exegetical stakes, which is why a careful middle path matters."},{"q":"What guidance does this track give about temporal language?","kind":"concept","options":["Avoid all temporal language","Use temporal language only for participles","Treat every tense as pure time","Use it carefully, but do not pretend time is absent"],"answer":3,"explain":"The track asks you to recognize both dimensions: be careful with time language, but do not act as if Greek lacks it."},{"q":"A translator renders past narrative with no sense of past time at all, even where the passage clearly tells past events. Which risk from the lesson does this illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Flattening narrative texture","Missing the significance of aspect choices","Stripping temporal clarity unnecessarily","Treating all past tenses as equivalent"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is the aspect-only extreme: removing time from translations where it is clearly present. The other three options are risks of the time-only view."},{"q":"A reader treats every past-tense verb in a narrative as interchangeable. This risk comes from which extreme?","kind":"concept","options":["The aspect-only position","The time-only position","The middle position","The practical reading posture"],"answer":1,"explain":"Time-only readings tend to treat all past tenses as the same, which flattens the texture of a narrative."},{"q":"Which problem does the lesson link to aspect-only positions?","kind":"concept","options":["Missing the rhetorical significance of aspect choices","Treating all past tenses as equivalent","Flattening narrative texture","Arguments that hinge on aspect distinctions the text may not support"],"answer":3,"explain":"Aspect-only readings can lead to arguments built on aspect distinctions that the text itself may not support. The other options are time-only risks."},{"q":"Which problem does the lesson link to time-only positions?","kind":"concept","options":["Missing the rhetorical and communicative significance of aspect choices","Stripping temporal clarity from translations","Overclaiming from tense forms","Arguments that hinge on unsupported aspect distinctions"],"answer":0,"explain":"If you read only for time, you can miss why a writer chose a particular aspect. The other options are aspect-only risks."},{"q":"Which of these questions is about aspect rather than temporal reference?","kind":"concept","options":["Did the action happen before the moment of speaking?","Is the action happening at the moment of writing?","How does the speaker view the action: as unfolding, as a whole, or as a resultant state?","Will the action happen after the moment of speaking?"],"answer":2,"explain":"Aspect is the speaker's perspective on the action. The other three questions ask when the action occurs relative to the moment of speaking or writing, which is temporal reference."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how does good exegesis read the text?","kind":"concept","options":["With the grain of the text","With predetermined grammatical dogmas","Against the grain of the text","From tense forms alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Good exegesis follows what the text itself signals rather than forcing a fixed grammatical theory onto it."},{"q":"What is the first step in the lesson's practical reading posture?","kind":"concept","options":["Notice the tense and mood","Let context confirm the signals","Note the temporal reference","Build an argument from the tense"],"answer":0,"explain":"The posture begins simply: notice which tense and mood you are looking at."},{"q":"In the reading posture, which step applies specifically to the indicative?","kind":"concept","options":["Ask what aspect signal is present","Notice the tense and mood","Note the expected temporal reference","Let context confirm or qualify"],"answer":2,"explain":"Because the indicative typically carries time as well as aspect, the posture adds a step there: note the expected temporal reference."},{"q":"What role does context play in the lesson's reading posture?","kind":"concept","options":["It replaces the tense form entirely","It is ignored once the tense is known","It matters only for participles","It confirms or qualifies both the aspect and time signals"],"answer":3,"explain":"Context is the check on both signals: it can confirm them or qualify them."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson's reading posture says it is fine to build theological arguments on tense distinctions alone.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson warns against building such arguments on tense distinctions without support from context."},{"q":"In the lesson's reading posture, which step comes right after noticing the tense and mood?","kind":"concept","options":["Let context confirm or qualify both signals","Ask what aspect signal is present","Note the expected temporal reference","Avoid building arguments on tense alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The order is: notice tense and mood, ask about aspect, note time in the indicative, then let context confirm or qualify."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, most working NT scholars hold the aspect-only view.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson links the aspect-only view to Stanley Porter and some others. Most working NT scholars take a middle position: aspect is primary, but time is a real secondary feature."},{"q":"When, according to the lesson, did some scholars begin arguing that tense forms communicate only aspect?","kind":"concept","options":["In the first century","In the medieval period","In the age of Classical Greek","In recent decades"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson places the aspect-only argument in recent decades of scholarship."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson notes that modern discussion of Greek tense has sometimes generated more heat than light.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson opens by noting the debate has sometimes been more heated than helpful, and then charts a careful middle path."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, what does the most responsible reading approach avoid?","kind":"concept","options":["Noticing aspect at all","Recognizing temporal reference","Using context to check the tense","Collapsing into either extreme"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson charts a middle path: hold both aspect and temporal reference without collapsing into an aspect-only or a time-only extreme."}],"va_11":[{"q":"How does the lesson describe active voice compared with the other Greek voices?","kind":"concept","options":["The most straightforward","The most nuanced","The rarest","The most often mishandled"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls active voice the most straightforward of the three Greek voices, though it still carries real communicative depth."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says active voice carries more communicative depth than its apparent simplicity suggests.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Active voice looks simple, but it says something about agency, subject involvement and the force of the action."},{"q":"Beyond being a grammatical fact, what does active voice make a claim about?","kind":"concept","options":["When the action happened","Who or what is responsible for the action","Whether the action is completed","How many people are involved"],"answer":1,"explain":"Presenting the subject as agent is a communicative claim about responsibility: who or what is doing the action."},{"q":"When a writer chooses active voice, what is being foregrounded?","kind":"concept","options":["The recipient of the action","The time of the action","The subject's role as actor","The case of the object"],"answer":2,"explain":"Choosing active voice puts the spotlight on the subject as the one who acts."},{"q":"In an active sentence, which way does the sentence's attention move?","kind":"concept","options":["From the object back toward the subject","From the action inward toward the subject","From the time frame toward the subject","From the subject outward toward the action and its effects"],"answer":3,"explain":"Active voice starts with the actor and moves outward to what the actor does and what results."},{"q":"How does the lesson contrast active voice with the middle voice?","kind":"concept","options":["In the middle, the subject's involvement is more reflexive or participatory","In the middle, the subject never acts at all","The middle voice has no grammatical subject","The middle voice always has a causative meaning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active presents the subject acting outward. In the middle, the subject's involvement is more reflexive or participatory."},{"q":"How does active voice present the subject's involvement in the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Receiving the action from outside","Fully involved, acting outward toward an object or goal","Only allowing something to happen to itself","Acting for its own benefit"],"answer":1,"explain":"In active voice the subject is fully involved in doing the action and acts outward, toward an object or goal."},{"q":"An active verb with transitive force directs its action toward what?","kind":"concept","options":["No recipient at all","The subject itself","An explicit object in the accusative","A noun in the vocative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Transitive active verbs aim the action at an explicit object, and that object is in the accusative case."},{"q":"What marks an active verb with intransitive force?","kind":"concept","options":["It takes two accusative objects","It must be in the passive","It always has a causative meaning","The subject acts without a stated recipient"],"answer":3,"explain":"An intransitive active verb describes the subject acting with no recipient named in the sentence."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what do active voice constructions communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Directness and agency","Uncertainty and possibility","Reflexive involvement","An unnamed agent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active constructions are direct: they name the agent and present it as acting."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["he was running","he loosed the bonds","the bonds were loosed by him","he loosed himself"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson glosses it as \"he loosed the bonds\": an active verb with its action aimed at an object.","greek":"ἔλυσεν τοὺς δεσμούς"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this form?","kind":"meaning","options":["he ran the race","he was made to run","he was running","he will run"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson renders ἔτρεχεν as \"he was running,\" its example of an intransitive active verb.","greek":"ἔτρεχεν"},{"q":"Why does the lesson classify this active verb as intransitive?","kind":"concept","options":["It is in the passive voice","It has an accusative object","It is a causative verb","The action has no stated recipient"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔτρεχεν describes running with no one or nothing receiving the action, so it is intransitive.","greek":"ἔτρεχεν"},{"q":"In this transitive phrase, which words are the explicit accusative object?","kind":"concept","options":["ἔλυσεν","τοὺς δεσμούς","ἔλυσεν τοὺς","No object is stated"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἔλυσεν is the active verb, and τοὺς δεσμούς (the bonds) is the accusative object the action is directed toward.","greek":"ἔλυσεν τοὺς δεσμούς"},{"q":"When an active verb has a named agent as its subject, what communicative force does the lesson describe?","kind":"concept","options":["An unnamed agent implied by context","The full, intentional action of the named agent","An action done to oneself","An action merely permitted"],"answer":1,"explain":"With a named agent as subject, active voice presents the full, intentional action of that agent."},{"q":"When the same content is put in passive rather than active voice, what does the lesson say differs?","kind":"concept","options":["The meaning becomes the opposite","The verb loses its aspect","The communicative emphasis","The tense becomes future"],"answer":2,"explain":"Active and passive versions can say similar things, but they put the emphasis in different places."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, every active verb carries a causative nuance.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Only some active verbs, such as βαπτίζω and διδάσκω, can carry causative force, and that comes from their lexical meaning."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, active voice is always more forceful than the other voices.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Force depends on context. What active voice does reliably signal is clear agency."},{"q":"Even when active voice is not the most forceful choice, what does it reliably signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Unambiguous agency","Past time","Ongoing action","A command"],"answer":0,"explain":"Whatever its force in context, active voice makes clear who the agent is."},{"q":"What does it mean for an active verb to carry a causative nuance?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject receives the action","The subject acts on itself","The subject causes something to happen rather than directly performing it","The action is still in the future"],"answer":2,"explain":"With a causative nuance the subject brings something about, rather than doing the action directly itself."},{"q":"A reader treats every active verb as if the subject must personally perform the action directly. What does the lesson say recognizing causative nuance helps you avoid?","kind":"concept","options":["Over-literalizing active constructions","Confusing aspect with time","Reading the middle as reflexive","Missing the augment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Some active verbs mean causing an action, not performing it. Noticing that keeps you from reading active constructions too literally."},{"q":"True or false: a causative nuance comes from the verb's lexical meaning, not from the active voice itself.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says causative force is lexically determined and is not a voice distinction."},{"q":"What does this verb mean, and what causative sense does the lesson give it?","kind":"meaning","options":["To baptize: to cause someone to be immersed","To run: to cause motion","To loose: to cause release","To teach: to cause learning"],"answer":3,"explain":"διδάσκω means \"to teach,\" and the lesson notes its causative sense: to cause learning.","greek":"διδάσκω"},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the causative sense of this verb?","kind":"meaning","options":["To cause learning","To cause someone to be immersed","To cause someone to run","To cause bonds to be loosed"],"answer":1,"explain":"βαπτίζω means \"to baptize,\" which the lesson explains as causing someone to be immersed.","greek":"βαπτίζω"},{"q":"The lesson's introduction names three things active voice communicates. Which is one of them?","kind":"concept","options":["The time of the action","The mood of the verb","The number of objects","Subject involvement"],"answer":3,"explain":"The introduction names agency, subject involvement and the force of the action as what active voice communicates."},{"q":"According to the introduction, understanding what active voice communicates helps readers do what?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorize principal parts","Engage more carefully with what a writer is asserting","Identify the tense formative","Find the augment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's goal is careful reading: knowing what active voice says about agency and force helps you engage with what the writer is asserting."},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's intransitive example ἔτρεχεν, the subject is still presented as the agent.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. ἔτρεχεν has no stated recipient, but it is still active: the subject is the one performing the running.","greek":"ἔτρεχεν"},{"q":"\"The servant opened the door\" is rewritten as \"The door was opened.\" Following the lesson's active-passive comparison, what has changed?","kind":"concept","options":["The servant is now more prominent","The action is now causative","The verb now has an accusative object","The door is foregrounded and the agent is left implied"],"answer":3,"explain":"As in the lesson's comparison, the passive puts the one acted on in front and leaves the agent implied."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what determines whether an active verb's subject performs the action directly or causes it?","kind":"concept","options":["The voice of the verb","The case of the subject","The verb's lexical meaning and context","The tense formative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The subject acts in either case. Whether that means direct performance or causation depends on the verb's lexical meaning and the context."},{"q":"How many voices does Greek have, according to the lesson's opening?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson refers to the three Greek voices, of which active is the most straightforward."}],"va_12":[{"q":"The lesson uses this middle form as an example where the reflexive gloss fits. What does it mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["I wash myself","I buy for myself","I come","I answer"],"answer":0,"explain":"λούομαι can mean \"I wash myself.\" It is one of the minority of middle forms where a reflexive meaning really fits.","greek":"λούομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to come","to become","to answer","to pray"],"answer":1,"explain":"γίνομαι means \"to become.\" It is middle in form but has no reflexive meaning.","greek":"γίνομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to touch","to receive","to answer","to become"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἀποκρίνομαι means \"to answer.\" Like γίνομαι and ἔρχομαι, it is middle in form without a reflexive sense.","greek":"ἀποκρίνομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to pray","to buy","to wash","to come/go"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔρχομαι means \"to come\" or \"to go.\" It is one of the common NT verbs found in middle forms.","greek":"ἔρχομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to pray","to touch","to welcome","to become"],"answer":0,"explain":"προσεύχομαι means \"to pray,\" an activity the lesson treats as naturally subject-involved.","greek":"προσεύχομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to touch","to receive/welcome","to answer","to buy"],"answer":1,"explain":"δέχομαι means \"to receive\" or \"to welcome.\"","greek":"δέχομαι"},{"q":"What does this verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to come","to receive","to touch","to pray"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἅπτομαι means \"to touch.\"","greek":"ἅπτομαι"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this middle form?","kind":"meaning","options":["I am bought","I sell","I wash myself","I buy for myself"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀγοράζομαι is glossed \"I buy for myself\": the subject acts for its own benefit.","greek":"ἀγοράζομαι"},{"q":"Which type of middle does the lesson say this form illustrates?","kind":"concept","options":["Permissive middle","Natural middle","Indirect reflexive","Passive of agency"],"answer":2,"explain":"Buying for oneself is the lesson's example of the indirect reflexive, where the subject acts for its own benefit.","greek":"ἀγοράζομαι"},{"q":"Which type of middle describes the subject allowing or permitting something to happen to itself?","kind":"concept","options":["Natural middle","Permissive middle","Indirect reflexive","Participatory middle"],"answer":1,"explain":"The permissive middle presents the subject as letting something be done to itself."},{"q":"Which type of middle covers verbs that naturally describe subject-involved activities?","kind":"concept","options":["Natural middle","Permissive middle","Indirect reflexive","Reflexive middle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The natural middle is for activities that by nature involve the subject, like praying, coming and becoming."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the vast majority of middle voice verbs in the NT carry a reflexive meaning.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Some middle forms are reflexive, but the vast majority of NT middle verbs are not."},{"q":"According to the lesson's checklist, when is a reflexive reading of a middle form appropriate?","kind":"concept","options":["Whenever the verb ends in -ομαι","Whenever the subject is singular","Never, in any context","When a reflexive meaning actually fits the context"],"answer":3,"explain":"The checklist asks whether a reflexive meaning actually fits the context. Reflexive is one real use of the middle, but only where the context supports it."},{"q":"True or false: reflexive meaning is one use of the middle voice, not its definition.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson's key point is that reflexive is just one of the ways the middle can be used."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the middle form of this verb signal?","kind":"concept","options":["That the subject prays to itself","That the subject is prayed for by others","The subject's engagement in personal communication","That the prayer is only permitted"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson explains the middle of προσεύχομαι as the subject engaged in personal communication, not a reflexive action.","greek":"προσεύχομαι"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the middle form of this verb highlight?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject touching itself","The subject being touched by another","The subject allowing itself to be touched","The subject's direct, personal contact"],"answer":3,"explain":"For ἅπτομαι, the middle points to the subject's own direct, personal contact with something.","greek":"ἅπτομαι"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the middle form of this verb signal about the subject?","kind":"concept","options":["Personal acceptance","Being received by others","Receiving itself","Causing others to receive"],"answer":0,"explain":"The middle of δέχομαι is explained as the subject's personal acceptance: receiving or welcoming.","greek":"δέχομαι"},{"q":"In the lesson's list of NT middles, what does the middle form of this verb point to?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject being sent","The subject's own movement","The subject bringing others","Permission to come"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson links the middle of ἔρχομαι to the subject's own movement.","greek":"ἔρχομαι"},{"q":"True or false: some of the verbs that appear only or primarily in middle forms are among the most common verbs in the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Many NT verbs appear mainly in the middle, and they include some of the most common verbs in the text."},{"q":"For verbs found mainly in the middle, what does the middle form signal, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Reflexive action","A passive meaning","A future action","The natural or characteristic way Greek expresses that kind of activity"],"answer":3,"explain":"For these verbs the middle is simply how Greek normally expresses that kind of activity, not a sign of reflexive action."},{"q":"Which question appears on the lesson's checklist for reading a middle form?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb in the optative?","Does the verb have an augment?","Is this a deponent verb?","Is the subject plural?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The checklist begins by asking whether the verb is a deponent: middle in form but active in translation."},{"q":"Which question about the subject's stake in the action is on the lesson's checklist?","kind":"concept","options":["Does the middle signal the subject's own interest or benefit?","Is the subject in the genitive case?","Is the subject a pronoun?","Does the subject come first in the clause?"],"answer":0,"explain":"One checklist question asks whether the middle shows the subject acting with its own interest or benefit in view."},{"q":"A student translates this verb as \"I answer myself.\" What is the error?","kind":"concept","options":["Reading an active form as passive","Missing the augment","Confusing aspect with time","Forcing a reflexive meaning onto a middle that carries none"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἀποκρίνομαι is middle in form but simply means \"to answer.\" Adding \"myself\" applies a reflexive meaning that is not there.","greek":"ἀποκρίνομαι"},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, what does a more careful understanding of the middle voice reward?","kind":"concept","options":["Quick memorization","Patient study","Ignoring context","Translating every middle reflexively"],"answer":1,"explain":"The introduction ends by saying that a more careful understanding of the middle voice rewards patient study."},{"q":"The lesson says to work from context outward. What should a reader not work inward from?","kind":"concept","options":["The context","The verb's lexical meaning","The reflexive definition","The subject of the sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"Start with the context and ask what the middle is doing there, instead of starting from the reflexive definition and forcing it onto the verb."},{"q":"How does the indirect reflexive ἀγοράζομαι differ from the reflexive λούομαι?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject acts for its own benefit rather than on itself","The subject receives the action from someone else","The subject allows something to happen to itself","The subject causes someone else to act"],"answer":0,"explain":"λούομαι (I wash myself) has the subject doing the action to itself. ἀγοράζομαι (I buy for myself) has the subject acting for its own benefit.","greek":"ἀγοράζομαι"},{"q":"Which type of middle describes the subject as personally involved in the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Permissive","Participatory","Indirect reflexive","Natural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The participatory middle presents the subject as personally involved in the action."},{"q":"Which activities does the lesson give as examples of the natural middle?","kind":"concept","options":["Buying, selling, trading","Washing, loosing, judging","Running, writing, hearing","Praying, coming, becoming"],"answer":3,"explain":"The natural middle covers verbs that by nature describe subject-involved activities. The lesson's examples are praying, coming and becoming."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does relying on the reflexive gloss lead to in most cases?","kind":"concept","options":["Accent errors","Spelling errors","Interpretive errors","No errors at all"],"answer":2,"explain":"Because most middles are not reflexive, using the reflexive gloss as the rule leads to interpretive errors in the majority of cases."},{"q":"A reader finds that no reflexive meaning fits a middle verb. Which checklist question should the reader consider next?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb in the indicative mood?","Does the verb have a tense formative?","Is the subject participating in or personally connected to the action?","Is the subject plural?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The checklist moves on from reflexive meaning to ask whether the subject is participating in or personally connected to the action."}],"va_13":[{"q":"In the lesson's active example \"God healed him,\" what is foregrounded?","kind":"concept","options":["The person who was healed","No one; the agent is unstated","God, as the agent","The result of the healing"],"answer":2,"explain":"In the active sentence the agent stands in the subject position, so God is foregrounded. The passive \"He was healed\" foregrounds the healed person instead."},{"q":"What does a passive construction let a writer put in the subject position?","kind":"concept","options":["The agent","The time of the action","The affected party","The preposition ὑπό"],"answer":2,"explain":"The passive puts the one acted upon, the affected party, in the subject slot, and that changes where the sentence's emphasis falls."},{"q":"Which construction expresses an explicit agent with a passive verb?","kind":"concept","options":["ὑπό + genitive","ἵνα + subjunctive","εἰς + accusative","ἐν + dative"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a passive names who did the action, the agent is introduced by ὑπό with the genitive."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["Jesus was healed by him","he healed Jesus","he will be healed by Jesus","he was healed by Jesus"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐθεραπεύθη is passive (\"he was healed\"), and ὑπὸ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ names the agent: \"by Jesus.\"","greek":"ἐθεραπεύθη ὑπὸ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ"},{"q":"Which words in this phrase name the agent explicitly?","kind":"concept","options":["ἐθεραπεύθη","ὑπὸ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ","τοῦ by itself","No agent is named"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ὑπό + genitive phrase ὑπὸ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ supplies the agent. ἐθεραπεύθη is the passive verb.","greek":"ἐθεραπεύθη ὑπὸ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ"},{"q":"A passive verb appears with no ὑπό phrase. What does the lesson say about its agent?","kind":"concept","options":["The agent is the grammatical subject","There can be no agent at all","The agent is implied or unknown","The agent must be the speaker"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without ὑπό + genitive, the agent is not stated, so it is either implied or unknown."},{"q":"True or false: in the NT, passive constructions more often leave the agent unstated than name it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Explicit agents occur, but more often NT passives leave the agent unstated for the reader to infer."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson treats a passive with no stated agent as a weakness in the writing.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Leaving the agent unstated is not a deficiency. It is a communicative choice."},{"q":"When a writer leaves the agent of a passive unstated, where is the writer's focus, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["On the result or the recipient","On the agent's identity","On the time of the action","On the form of the verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leaving out the agent keeps attention on what happened and to whom, not on who did it."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how clear is the implied agent of a passive?","kind":"concept","options":["Always obvious from the verb ending","Never knowable in any case","Always identified by a ὑπό phrase","Sometimes clear from context, sometimes deliberately ambiguous"],"answer":3,"explain":"Context sometimes makes the actor clear. Other times the writer leaves it deliberately ambiguous."},{"q":"What Latin term does the lesson give for the divine passive?","kind":"concept","options":["deponere","passivum divinum","verbum medium","modus irrealis"],"answer":1,"explain":"The divine passive is also called the passivum divinum."},{"q":"According to the lesson, in what background is the divine passive convention rooted?","kind":"concept","options":["Hebrew background","Latin grammar","Classical Greek poetry","Egyptian papyri"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson describes the divine passive as a convention rooted in Hebrew background."},{"q":"In which writings does the lesson say the divine passive is a recognized convention?","kind":"concept","options":["Classical Athenian drama","Latin legal texts","Jewish and early Christian writing","Modern Greek novels"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson places the divine passive convention in Jewish and early Christian writing."},{"q":"What does the divine passive convention allow writers to do, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Name God explicitly with ὑπό","Turn a statement into a question","Mark an action as past","Avoid using God's name while still asserting divine action"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson explains that the convention lets writers refer to divine action without using God's name."},{"q":"What does the lesson say is essential for identifying a divine passive?","kind":"concept","options":["Context and background knowledge","The passive ending alone","A ὑπό phrase naming God","The augment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the agent is unnamed, the form alone cannot show a divine passive. Context and background knowledge are needed."},{"q":"A passive verb is followed by ὑπό + genitive that names God. Is this a divine passive in the lesson's sense?","kind":"concept","options":["Yes: any passive involving God is a divine passive","Yes: ὑπό always marks a divine passive","No: the divine passive leaves God unnamed","No: divine passives never use passive forms"],"answer":2,"explain":"The defining feature of the divine passive is that God is the agent but is not named. An explicit ὑπό phrase naming God does not fit that pattern."},{"q":"Besides Matthew, which NT book does the lesson draw a classic divine passive example from?","kind":"concept","options":["John","Luke","Romans","Acts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two of the lesson's examples come from Matthew 5, and the third comes from Luke 6:38."},{"q":"Beyond the divine passive, what broader discourse function does the lesson give to passive constructions?","kind":"concept","options":["Marking past time","Expressing purpose","Signaling commands","Shifting focus"],"answer":3,"explain":"Passive constructions serve a broader discourse function: they shift focus within the discourse."},{"q":"A writer uses a passive where an active would be expected. What does the lesson say about this choice?","kind":"concept","options":["It carries communicative weight","It is likely a copying error","It has no significance","It always marks a divine passive"],"answer":0,"explain":"Switching to the passive, or using it where active is expected, is a meaningful choice worth noticing."},{"q":"Which question is on the lesson's checklist for any passive construction?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb in the optative?","How many syllables does it have?","Who is in the subject position?","Does it have an augment?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The checklist starts by asking who is in the subject position and what foregrounding them communicates."},{"q":"Which checklist question asks you to test a passive by comparing it with another voice?","kind":"concept","options":["Is an agent named?","Who is in the subject position?","Is this a divine passive context?","What would be lost if this were rephrased in active voice?"],"answer":3,"explain":"Imagining the active version shows what the passive choice adds, such as its focus on the recipient."},{"q":"In the lesson's English comparison, which sentence foregrounds the person who was healed?","kind":"concept","options":["God healed him","He was healed","He healed others","God heals"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"He was healed\" is passive, so the healed person stands in front and the agent is left implied."},{"q":"Which sentence is passive with an unstated agent?","kind":"concept","options":["The crowd followed him","The door was opened","She opened the door","The door was opened by the servant"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"The door was opened\" is passive and does not say who opened it. The last option is passive too, but it names the agent."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, putting the affected party in the subject position significantly affects the communicative emphasis of a sentence.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Foregrounding the one acted upon changes what the sentence emphasizes."},{"q":"True or false: when a passive has no stated agent, the lesson says the reader can never infer who acted.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Unstated agents often have to be inferred from context, and sometimes context makes them clear."},{"q":"When a passive has no explicit agent, what question does the lesson say readers must ask?","kind":"concept","options":["Who is the implied actor?","What is the tense formative?","Is the verb deponent?","Is the subject plural?"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no agent stated, the reader's job is to ask who the implied actor is."},{"q":"How many classic NT examples of the divine passive does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["One","Two","Four","Three"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists three: Matthew 5:4, Matthew 5:7 and Luke 6:38."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, what can a passive construction do beyond reversing the direction of action?","kind":"concept","options":["Always name its agent","Change the tense of the verb","Imply an unstated agent and shift communicative focus","Turn a statement into a command"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says the passive can imply an unstated agent, shift focus, and in some contexts signal the divine passive."},{"q":"In the lesson's example of explicit agency, what voice is this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Active","Middle","Passive","Causative active"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐθεραπεύθη is passive: \"he was healed.\" That is why it can take an agent with ὑπό.","greek":"ἐθεραπεύθη"},{"q":"A reader meets a passive in a context where God is plausibly the unnamed actor. Which checklist question does this prompt?","kind":"concept","options":["Is the verb deponent?","Is there an augment?","Is the verb in the imperative?","Is this a divine passive context?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The checklist includes asking whether the passive sits in a divine passive context."}],"va_14":[{"q":"What does the Latin verb deponere mean?","kind":"concept","options":["to lay aside","to take up","to reflect","to receive"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deponere means \"to lay aside,\" which is where the grammatical term \"deponent\" comes from."},{"q":"According to the traditional idea behind the term, what have deponent verbs laid aside?","kind":"concept","options":["Their middle endings","Their active forms","Their augments","Their passive meanings"],"answer":1,"explain":"The traditional picture is that these verbs have laid aside their active forms, while keeping an active meaning."},{"q":"From which language is the term \"deponent\" borrowed?","kind":"concept","options":["Hebrew","Aramaic","Latin","Coptic"],"answer":2,"explain":"The term comes from Latin, and the lesson notes it brings Latin-based assumptions with it."},{"q":"What does this traditionally deponent verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to become","to answer","to come","to pray"],"answer":3,"explain":"προσεύχομαι means \"to pray.\" It appears in middle forms.","greek":"προσεύχομαι"},{"q":"What does this traditionally deponent verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to answer","to pray","to become","to go"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἀποκρίνομαι means \"to answer.\" It appears in middle/passive forms.","greek":"ἀποκρίνομαι"},{"q":"This is the aorist of ἔρχομαι. What kind of form does the lesson say it is?","kind":"form","options":["Middle","Active","Passive","Middle/passive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Although ἔρχομαι is middle in the present and future, its aorist ἦλθον is active.","greek":"ἦλθον"},{"q":"Which tense of ἔρχομαι is this, as the lesson lists it?","kind":"form","options":["Present","Future","Perfect","Aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐλήλυθα is the perfect of ἔρχομαι, and the lesson notes it is active in form.","greek":"ἐλήλυθα"},{"q":"In which tenses does the lesson say this verb is middle?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist and perfect","Present and perfect","Future and aorist","Present and future"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἔρχομαι is middle in the present and future, but its aorist and perfect are active.","greek":"ἔρχομαι"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says the term \"deponent\" has been used for centuries.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The label is centuries old, which is part of why the modern debate about it matters."},{"q":"What do some modern scholars argue the \"deponent\" label does?","kind":"concept","options":["It obscures rather than explains what is happening","It explains these verbs perfectly","It applies only to Latin verbs","It applies only to passive verbs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Their concern is that the label hides what the middle form is doing instead of explaining it."},{"q":"On the alternative view, why are verbs like ἔρχομαι and γίνομαι middle in form?","kind":"concept","options":["Because they lost their active forms","Because middle voice accurately describes their meaning","Because of a copying error","Because they are passive in meaning"],"answer":1,"explain":"The alternative view says the middle form fits these verbs' meaning; it is not a leftover after losing an active."},{"q":"On the alternative view, what kind of activities do verbs of coming, becoming, praying and answering describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Actions done by an unnamed agent","Commands to others","Subject-involved, personally engaged activities","Completed states"],"answer":2,"explain":"These are activities that naturally involve the subject personally, which is exactly what middle voice communicates."},{"q":"Taken to its conclusion, what does the alternative view say about true deponents?","kind":"concept","options":["Every middle verb is a true deponent","Only aorists can be deponent","All deponents are passive in meaning","There are no true deponents"],"answer":3,"explain":"On this view there are no true deponents, only middle verbs that have been misread."},{"q":"On the alternative view, what has led readers to misread these middle forms?","kind":"concept","options":["Latin-influenced assumptions","Hebrew background","Textual corruption","Modern punctuation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The alternative view blames Latin-influenced assumptions for treating these middle forms as empty."},{"q":"Which problem does the lesson link to the reject-all-deponents position?","kind":"concept","options":["It forces every verb into an active translation","Some middle forms genuinely appear to have no semantic middle value","It treats every middle form as meaningless","It depends on Latin terminology"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rejecting all deponents runs into verbs whose middle forms really do seem to lack middle meaning."},{"q":"According to the lesson, which distinction is blurred in some tenses?","kind":"concept","options":["Present/future","Singular/plural","Middle/passive","Indicative/subjunctive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson notes that in some tenses the middle/passive distinction is blurred, which complicates the debate."},{"q":"Why might some verbs use middle endings without any middle meaning, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Because they are commands","Because of the divine passive","Because they are infinitives","For historical or morphological reasons"],"answer":3,"explain":"Some verbs may carry middle endings for historical or morphological reasons that have nothing to do with active voice meaning."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the safest approach to these verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["Ignore the middle form completely","Recognize them, translate actively, and stay open to middle nuance in context","Translate them as passives","Reject the category and stop translating actively"],"answer":1,"explain":"The safest path holds both sides: read them actively, but watch for middle nuance in specific contexts."},{"q":"Even with the label under debate, why does the lesson say recognizing these verbs still matters?","kind":"concept","options":["It is required only for Latin study","It replaces the need to learn the middle voice","It is still a necessary skill for practical reading","It proves the traditional view correct"],"answer":2,"explain":"Whatever the theory, readers still need to recognize these verbs and know to translate them actively."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says you should wait until the scholarly debate is settled before practicing basic reading.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Engage the discussion as you advance, but do not let it stop basic reading practice."},{"q":"According to the lesson's practical guidance, what should you be aware of about some traditionally \"deponent\" verbs?","kind":"concept","options":["They are always passive in meaning","They have no forms in the present","They appear only in Paul","They carry genuine middle voice nuance"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson asks you to notice that some of these verbs carry real middle nuance, such as subject involvement."},{"q":"What does this traditionally deponent verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to pray","to become","to answer","to come/go"],"answer":1,"explain":"γίνομαι means \"to become.\" It appears in middle/passive forms.","greek":"γίνομαι"},{"q":"What does this traditionally deponent verb mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["to become","to pray","to come/go","to answer"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔρχομαι means \"to come\" or \"to go.\" It is middle in the present and future, but its aorist and perfect are active.","greek":"ἔρχομαι"},{"q":"A student says, \"This verb is deponent, so its middle ending means nothing.\" Which position's risk does this illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["The traditional deponent label","The reject-all-deponents position","The divine passive","The aspect-only view"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson warns that the traditional label can lead students to treat middle forms as meaningless.","greek":"ἀποκρίνομαι"},{"q":"According to the scholars' concern, what does the deponent label imply happened to the verb's active counterpart?","kind":"concept","options":["It became a passive","It became a future","It merged with the infinitive","It was simply lost"],"answer":3,"explain":"The label treats the middle ending as empty, as if the verb had simply lost its active counterpart."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson names Jonathan Pennington as a scholar who questions the deponent label.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Pennington and others argue the term obscures more than it explains."},{"q":"The practical approach warns against dismissing a middle form as meaningless simply because of what?","kind":"concept","options":["A traditional grammar calls the verb deponent","The verb appears in a Gospel","The verb is in the present tense","The verb has an augment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's advice is not to treat a middle form as empty just because a traditional grammar labels the verb deponent, since some of these verbs carry real middle nuance."},{"q":"What does the lesson say about both extreme positions on deponents?","kind":"concept","options":["Both are fully correct","Both have problems","Both come from Hebrew grammar","Both deny the middle voice exists"],"answer":1,"explain":"Neither the traditional label nor the reject-all view is free of problems, so the lesson takes a balanced line."},{"q":"What kind of forms does the lesson list for this verb?","kind":"form","options":["Active forms","Passive forms only","Middle forms","Perfect forms only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists προσεύχομαι (to pray) with middle forms.","greek":"προσεύχομαι"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson presents the deponent question as fully settled among scholars.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson presents both the traditional framework and an ongoing scholarly conversation."}],"va_15":[{"q":"According to the lesson, what is the imperative mood used for?","kind":"concept","options":["To direct the behavior of another person","To state facts about the past","To express purpose","To describe a resultant state"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperative is the mood of directives: it is used to direct what another person does."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the contrast between present and aorist imperatives is a distinction of what?","kind":"concept","options":["Aspect","Time","Voice","Person"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls it the most discussed distinction in imperative aspect: imperfective for the present, perfective for the aorist."},{"q":"The lesson cites Matthew 15:25 as an example of which use of the imperative?","kind":"concept","options":["Direct command","Request or entreaty","Permission","Condition"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists Matthew 15:25 under requests and entreaties, showing that an imperative need not be a harsh order."},{"q":"Which use of the imperative does the lesson describe as appearing in conditional-like contexts?","kind":"concept","options":["Permissions","Direct commands","Conditions","Entreaties"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's fourth category is conditions: imperatives used in conditional-like contexts."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the imperative form itself distinguishes urgency from politeness.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The form issues a directive, but it does not show whether it is urgent or polite. Context carries that."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the uses the lesson lists for the imperative mood?","kind":"concept","options":["Permissions","Requests and entreaties","Statements of past fact","Direct commands"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists direct commands, requests and entreaties, permissions and conditions. Stating past facts is not a use of the imperative, which directs another person's behavior."},{"q":"Which aspect does the present imperative carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective","Imperfective","Stative","No aspect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present imperative has imperfective aspect, which leans toward ongoing or habitual action."},{"q":"Which aspect does the aorist imperative carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective","Stative","Perfective","No aspect"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist imperative has perfective aspect, viewing the action as a specific act or as a whole."},{"q":"A teacher wants to tell students, \"Keep practicing every day.\" Following the lesson's default tendencies, which imperative tense fits best?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Perfect","Future","Present"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ongoing, habitual action is the default tendency of the present imperative."},{"q":"Which aspect do χαίρετε and προσεύχεσθε carry in the lesson's 1 Thessalonians example?","kind":"form","options":["Imperfective","Perfective","Stative","No aspect"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson labels both as present imperatives, and the present imperative has imperfective aspect."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says every aorist imperative is a once-for-all command.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson warns that not every aorist imperative is a once-for-all command."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says a present imperative does not necessarily mean ongoing action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Present imperatives tend toward ongoing action, but context can qualify that tendency."},{"q":"In this phrase from 1 Thessalonians 5:16-18, what tense does the lesson give for the imperative χαίρετε?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist","Present","Perfect","Future"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson labels χαίρετε a present imperative, fitting a command about ongoing practice.","greek":"πάντοτε χαίρετε"},{"q":"True or false: in the lesson's 1 Thessalonians 5:16-18 example, πάντοτε χαίρετε is an aorist imperative.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson marks πάντοτε χαίρετε as present: \"Rejoice at all times\" is presented as ongoing activity.","greek":"πάντοτε χαίρετε"},{"q":"Which pairing of passage and Greek imperative matches the lesson?","kind":"reading","options":["Matthew 7:7: πάντοτε χαίρετε","Matthew 15:25: αἰτεῖτε... ζητεῖτε... κρούετε","1 Thessalonians 5:16-18: πάντοτε χαίρετε","1 Thessalonians 5:16-18: αἰτεῖτε... ζητεῖτε... κρούετε"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson cites πάντοτε χαίρετε from 1 Thessalonians 5:16-18 and αἰτεῖτε... ζητεῖτε... κρούετε from Matthew 7:7. Matthew 15:25 is cited for a request."},{"q":"Why does the present aspect fit πάντοτε χαίρετε and ἀδιαλείπτως προσεύχεσθε, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["They are single, one-time commands","They present the commands as ongoing or repeated activity rather than a single act","They are polite requests to a superior","They grant permission"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says the present aspect presents these commands as ongoing or repeated activity, which is where present imperatives tend to appear."},{"q":"In the lesson's Matthew 7:7 example, which tense are these imperatives?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist","Perfect","Future","Present"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson labels αἰτεῖτε, ζητεῖτε and κρούετε as present imperatives: ongoing or repeated asking, seeking and knocking.","greek":"αἰτεῖτε... ζητεῖτε... κρούετε"},{"q":"How many uses of the imperative mood does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Two","Five"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists four: direct commands, requests and entreaties, permissions, and conditions."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT among the lesson's examples of speakers whose identical imperatives can carry different force?","kind":"concept","options":["A parent speaking to a child","A commander in battle","A friend making a polite request","A scribe copying a manuscript"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's list is a parent speaking to a child, a commander in battle, a friend making a polite request, and Jesus addressing a storm."},{"q":"The lesson lists Jesus addressing a storm alongside a parent and a commander. What is this list meant to show?","kind":"concept","options":["That imperatives are used only by people in authority","That identical imperative forms can carry completely different force","That the aorist is always urgent","That commands to nature use a special mood"],"answer":1,"explain":"The list shows that the same form can sound very different depending on who speaks and in what situation."},{"q":"According to the lesson, which pairs of qualities do contextual factors produce in imperatives?","kind":"concept","options":["Past or future, singular or plural","Active or passive, middle or deponent","Purpose or result, cause or effect","Urgency or gentleness, authority or request"],"answer":3,"explain":"Who is speaking, the relationship and the stakes produce urgency or gentleness, authority or request."},{"q":"Which imperative use does the lesson illustrate with \"Go!\" \"Heal!\" \"Believe!\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Requests","Permissions","Conditions","Direct commands"],"answer":3,"explain":"Short, plain directives like these are the lesson's examples of direct commands."},{"q":"The lesson's example \"If you are willing, you may go\" illustrates which use of the imperative?","kind":"concept","options":["Direct command","Entreaty","Permission","Condition"],"answer":2,"explain":"Here the imperative grants permission rather than demanding action."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson calls the present vs. aorist contrast the most discussed distinction in imperative aspect.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The present/aorist contrast is the most discussed distinction, though the lesson cautions against overpressing it."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the aspect signal of an imperative is a default tendency that context shapes.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Present and aorist imperatives each have a default tendency, but context shapes how it plays out."},{"q":"Which statement about imperatives is supported by the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The same imperative form can serve as a command, a request or a permission","Requests use a special polite imperative ending","Permissions cannot be expressed with an imperative","Imperatives are never used for requests"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists commands, requests, permissions and conditions as uses of one mood, with context deciding the force."},{"q":"Which English rendering does the lesson give as typical of the present imperative?","kind":"concept","options":["pray (now)","take (this)","believe (and be saved)","go on forgiving"],"answer":3,"explain":"\"Go on forgiving\" expresses continuing action, the present imperative's tendency. The other three are the lesson's aorist examples."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, Greek imperatives span a wide range of what?","kind":"concept","options":["Urgency, tone and intent","Cases and numbers","Tenses and moods","Accents and breathings"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson opens by noting that imperatives vary widely in urgency, tone and intent."},{"q":"The lesson renders \"pray\" two ways: \"keep praying\" and \"pray (now).\" What accounts for the difference?","kind":"concept","options":["Present vs. aorist imperative","Active vs. passive voice","Singular vs. plural","Command vs. permission"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"Keep praying\" reflects the present imperative's tendency toward ongoing action, while \"pray (now)\" reflects the aorist imperative's tendency toward a specific act."},{"q":"Which English rendering does the lesson give as typical of the aorist imperative?","kind":"concept","options":["keep praying","continue following","pray (now)","go on forgiving"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Pray (now)\" points to a specific act, the aorist imperative's tendency. The others express continuing action."}],"va_16":[{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, using the subjunctive means the action will not happen.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The subjunctive does not deny the action. It simply does not present it as a direct, unqualified assertion."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says some subjunctive constructions communicate high confidence or near-certainty.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. A subjunctive can still express strong expectation. What it does not do is assert the action outright."},{"q":"When a speaker uses the subjunctive, what is the speaker not doing, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Referring to an action at all","Using a finite verb","Presenting the action as a direct, unqualified assertion of reality","Addressing another person"],"answer":2,"explain":"The subjunctive steps back from flat assertion. The action is framed as potential, contingent or expected."},{"q":"In this phrase from John 20:31, what does the subjunctive after ἵνα communicate, according to the lesson?","kind":"reading","options":["A direct assertion of fact","The goal or intended outcome","A first-person exhortation","A prohibition"],"answer":1,"explain":"After ἵνα the subjunctive presents something aimed at, hoped for or expected: the goal or intended outcome.","greek":"ἵνα πιστεύητε"},{"q":"This phrase from John 3:16 contains μή, yet the lesson does not list it as a prohibition. Why?","kind":"reading","options":["Because the verb is indicative","Because prohibitions use ἐάν","Because the clause is introduced by ἵνα, a purpose/result construction","Because the verb is first person plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"The construction identifies the function. This clause opens with ἵνα, so the lesson lists it under ἵνα + subjunctive (purpose or result), not as a prohibition.","greek":"ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτὸν μὴ ἀπόληται"},{"q":"In this phrase from John 3:16, the verb ἀπόληται follows ἵνα. What mood is it?","kind":"reading","options":["Indicative","Imperative","Infinitive","Subjunctive"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson gives this as an example of ἵνα + subjunctive, so ἀπόληται is subjunctive.","greek":"ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτὸν μὴ ἀπόληται"},{"q":"What kind of condition does ἐάν + subjunctive form, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Third class condition","First class condition","Second class condition","Fourth class condition"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἐάν with the subjunctive forms third class conditions, which are contingent.","greek":"ἐάν"},{"q":"How does the lesson gloss the ἐάν + subjunctive condition?","kind":"concept","options":["\"since this happened...\"","\"let this happen...\"","\"in order that this happens...\"","\"if (ever) this happens...\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Third class conditions are contingent, so the lesson renders them \"if (ever) this happens...\""},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this subjunctive form?","kind":"meaning","options":["we went","let us go","go!","if we go"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἄγωμεν is the lesson's hortatory example: \"let us go.\"","greek":"ἄγωμεν"},{"q":"Which type of subjunctive is this form?","kind":"form","options":["Hortatory","Deliberative","Prohibitive","Purpose"],"answer":0,"explain":"A first-person exhortation (\"let us go\") is the hortatory subjunctive.","greek":"ἄγωμεν"},{"q":"Which person does the hortatory subjunctive use, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Second person","Third person","First person","Any person, with no difference"],"answer":2,"explain":"The hortatory subjunctive is a first-person exhortation: \"Let us...\""},{"q":"Which type of subjunctive does the lesson identify in this question?","kind":"reading","options":["Hortatory","Prohibitive","Third class condition","Deliberative"],"answer":3,"explain":"A question about what one should do uses the deliberative subjunctive.","greek":"τί ποιήσωμεν;"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson takes its deliberative example τί ποιήσωμεν; from Acts 2:37.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson cites τί ποιήσωμεν; from Acts 2:37 as its example of a deliberative subjunctive."},{"q":"What kind of question does a deliberative subjunctive ask?","kind":"concept","options":["A question about past facts","A rhetorical question about what one should do","A request for permission","A question about someone's identity"],"answer":1,"explain":"The deliberative subjunctive weighs what to do, often as a rhetorical question."},{"q":"Which tense of the subjunctive does the lesson specify for prohibitions with μή?","kind":"form","options":["Present","Aorist","Perfect","Future"],"answer":1,"explain":"The prohibitive subjunctive is μή with the aorist subjunctive.","greek":"μή"},{"q":"Which English gloss does the lesson give for the prohibitive subjunctive?","kind":"concept","options":["\"let us...\"","\"if ever...\"","\"do not begin to...\"","\"so that...\""],"answer":2,"explain":"μή + aorist subjunctive forbids starting an action: \"do not begin to...\""},{"q":"According to the lesson's reading checklist, what does a subjunctive after this word signal?","kind":"concept","options":["Purpose/result","Conditional","Hortatory","Prohibition"],"answer":1,"explain":"The checklist links ἐάν + subjunctive to a conditional sense.","greek":"ἐάν"},{"q":"A first-person plural subjunctive appears with no conjunction. According to the checklist, what is it most likely?","kind":"concept","options":["Deliberative","Prohibitive","Hortatory","Purpose"],"answer":2,"explain":"With no conjunction, a first-person plural subjunctive is read as hortatory: \"let us...\""},{"q":"A subjunctive appears after μή in an aorist form. According to the checklist, what is its function?","kind":"concept","options":["Prohibition","Purpose","Condition","Exhortation"],"answer":0,"explain":"μή with an aorist subjunctive is the prohibitive pattern."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what identifies the function of a subjunctive form?","kind":"concept","options":["The accent on the verb","The person ending alone","The construction it appears in","The verb's dictionary meaning"],"answer":2,"explain":"The subjunctive's job depends on its setting: ἵνα, ἐάν, μή, a question, and so on."},{"q":"If the construction identifies a subjunctive's function, what does the subjunctive mood itself provide?","kind":"concept","options":["The time of the action","The subject of the clause","The voice of the verb","The modal frame of contingency or expectation"],"answer":3,"explain":"The construction supplies the specific function, and the mood supplies the frame of contingency or expectation."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the subjunctive is one of the most important moods for understanding what?","kind":"concept","options":["Complex NT sentences","Greek accent rules","Noun declensions","The pronunciation of the alphabet"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because it appears in purpose clauses, conditions and more, the subjunctive is key to reading complex NT sentences."},{"q":"Each subjunctive construction uses which quality of the mood to express its meaning?","kind":"concept","options":["Its past-time reference","Its non-assertive quality","Its passive meaning","Its stative aspect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every construction builds on the fact that the subjunctive does not assert the action directly."},{"q":"In this phrase from John 3:16, which word introduces the subjunctive clause?","kind":"reading","options":["πᾶς","πιστεύων","αὐτόν","ἵνα"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἵνα opens the clause, and the subjunctive that follows expresses the intended outcome.","greek":"ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτὸν μὴ ἀπόληται"},{"q":"\"She studied so that she might pass.\" Which Greek subjunctive construction from the lesson matches the \"so that\" clause?","kind":"concept","options":["ἵνα + subjunctive","ἐάν + subjunctive","μή + aorist subjunctive","A hortatory subjunctive"],"answer":0,"explain":"\"So that\" expresses purpose, and purpose is the job of ἵνα + subjunctive."},{"q":"\"Do not begin to worry.\" Which subjunctive construction from the lesson matches this kind of prohibition?","kind":"concept","options":["ἵνα + subjunctive","ἐάν + subjunctive","μή + aorist subjunctive","A deliberative subjunctive"],"answer":2,"explain":"Prohibitions of this kind use μή with the aorist subjunctive, glossed \"do not begin to...\""},{"q":"\"If ever it rains, we will stay inside.\" Which Greek construction from the lesson matches the \"if ever\" clause?","kind":"concept","options":["ἵνα + subjunctive","ἐάν + subjunctive","A hortatory subjunctive","A deliberative subjunctive"],"answer":1,"explain":"A contingent \"if ever\" condition is a third class condition, built with ἐάν + subjunctive."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, the indicative can present a future action as real.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The indicative presents action as actual: something that is, was, or will be real. The subjunctive presents action as potential, contingent or expected."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson's two examples of ἵνα + subjunctive both come from Acts.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Both ἵνα examples come from John: John 3:16 and John 20:31. Acts 2:37 is the deliberative example."},{"q":"Which question in the reading checklist leads to the label \"deliberative\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Is it after ἵνα?","Is it after ἐάν?","Is it after μή in an aorist form?","Is it a question about what to do?"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the subjunctive sits in a question about what to do, the checklist identifies it as deliberative."}],"va_17":[{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["I want to believe","I want you to believe","believing is salvation","he came to believe"],"answer":0,"explain":"θέλω means \"I want,\" and the infinitive πιστεύειν completes it: \"I want to believe.\"","greek":"θέλω πιστεύειν"},{"q":"What function does the infinitive πιστεύειν have in this phrase?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject","Object","Purpose","Temporal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson uses θέλω πιστεύειν as its example of an infinitive in the object slot: what I want is to believe.","greek":"θέλω πιστεύειν"},{"q":"What function does τὸ πιστεύειν have in this sentence?","kind":"concept","options":["Object","Purpose","Subject","Temporal"],"answer":2,"explain":"τὸ πιστεύειν is the subject of ἐστίν. The infinitive fills a noun slot, the way a verbal noun can.","greek":"τὸ πιστεύειν σωτηρία ἐστίν"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this sentence?","kind":"meaning","options":["I want to believe","he came to save","in order to believe","believing is salvation"],"answer":3,"explain":"The articular infinitive τὸ πιστεύειν works as the subject \"believing,\" so the sentence reads \"believing is salvation.\"","greek":"τὸ πιστεύειν σωτηρία ἐστίν"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["he was saving","he came to save","he wants to save","saving has come"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἦλθεν is \"he came,\" and the infinitive σῴζειν gives the purpose: \"he came to save.\"","greek":"ἦλθεν σῴζειν"},{"q":"What function does the infinitive σῴζειν have in this phrase?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject","Object","Purpose (adverbial)","Temporal"],"answer":2,"explain":"σῴζειν explains why he came, so it is an adverbial infinitive of purpose.","greek":"ἦλθεν σῴζειν"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the tense of an infinitive communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Time","Aspect","Mood","Person"],"answer":1,"explain":"An infinitive's tense signals aspect, not time."},{"q":"Which aspect does a present infinitive carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective","Stative","Imperfective","No aspect"],"answer":2,"explain":"A present infinitive is imperfective."},{"q":"Which aspect does an aorist infinitive carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective","Stative","No aspect","Perfective"],"answer":3,"explain":"An aorist infinitive is perfective."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["he came to heal","he was healed","he healed them","he wants to heal"],"answer":0,"explain":"ἦλθεν (he came) followed by the infinitive ἰάσασθαι gives \"he came to heal.\"","greek":"ἦλθεν ἰάσασθαι"},{"q":"Which way of expressing purpose does this phrase illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["εἰς τό + infinitive","Simple infinitive after a verb of motion","πρός + articular infinitive","ἐν τῷ + infinitive"],"answer":1,"explain":"ἦλθεν is a verb of motion, and a plain infinitive follows it to state the purpose.","greek":"ἦλθεν ἰάσασθαι"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["while being","of being","being is","in order to be"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰς τό + infinitive expresses purpose, so εἰς τὸ εἶναι means \"in order to be.\"","greek":"εἰς τὸ εἶναι"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["in order to see","while seeing","in order to be seen","having been seen"],"answer":2,"explain":"πρός with the articular infinitive expresses purpose. The lesson renders πρὸς τὸ θεαθῆναι \"in order to be seen.\"","greek":"πρὸς τὸ θεαθῆναι"},{"q":"Which purpose pattern does this phrase illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Simple infinitive after a verb of motion","εἰς τό + infinitive","πρός + articular infinitive","ἐν τῷ + infinitive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The phrase has πρός followed by the article τό and an infinitive: the πρός + articular infinitive pattern.","greek":"πρὸς τὸ θεαθῆναι"},{"q":"True or false: the lesson calls the infinitive of purpose one of the most common infinitive uses in the NT.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Purpose infinitives are common, and recognizing them helps you follow the direction of an argument or narrative."},{"q":"Which article precedes an articular infinitive, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The neuter article","The masculine article","The feminine article","The plural article"],"answer":0,"explain":"The articular infinitive takes the neuter article (τό, τοῦ, τῷ), which lets it act like a noun in different cases."},{"q":"What signals the syntactical role of an articular infinitive?","kind":"concept","options":["The case of the article","The tense of the infinitive","The voice of the infinitive","Word order alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the article can be put in different cases, its case tells you what job the infinitive is doing."},{"q":"According to the lesson's table, what function does nominative τό + infinitive have?","kind":"concept","options":["Object or purpose","Result","Subject","Temporal"],"answer":2,"explain":"With the article in the nominative, the infinitive functions as the subject."},{"q":"What does genitive τοῦ + infinitive express, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject function","Result or purpose","Temporal: \"while\"","Direct object only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The genitive article τοῦ with an infinitive expresses result or purpose (\"so as to\").","greek":"τοῦ"},{"q":"What does dative τῷ + infinitive express, according to the lesson's table?","kind":"concept","options":["Subject function","Result only","Various adverbial functions","Direct object only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists the dative τῷ + infinitive as covering various adverbial functions.","greek":"τῷ"},{"q":"What does ἐν + τῷ + infinitive express?","kind":"concept","options":["Purpose","Result","Subject function","Time: \"while,\" \"when\""],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐν τῷ with an infinitive is temporal, translated \"while\" or \"when.\"","greek":"ἐν τῷ"},{"q":"Which functions does the lesson give for accusative τό + infinitive?","kind":"concept","options":["Object or purpose","Subject only","Temporal","Result only"],"answer":0,"explain":"With the article in the accusative, the infinitive serves as an object or expresses purpose."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this sentence?","kind":"meaning","options":["I want to believe","I want you to believe","you want me to believe","believing is wanted"],"answer":1,"explain":"σε (you) is the accusative subject of the infinitive, so the sentence means \"I want you to believe.\"","greek":"θέλω σε πιστεύειν"},{"q":"In this sentence, who is the subject of the infinitive πιστεύειν?","kind":"concept","options":["\"I,\" the subject of θέλω","There is no subject","πιστεύειν itself","σε, \"you\""],"answer":3,"explain":"θέλω has \"I\" as its subject, but the infinitive has its own subject: σε, \"you,\" in the accusative.","greek":"θέλω σε πιστεύειν"},{"q":"Why does the lesson say recognizing accusative-subject infinitives matters?","kind":"concept","options":["It helps untangle sentences where multiple actors are in view","It reveals the tense of the main verb","It marks the infinitive as passive","It shows the action is in the past"],"answer":0,"explain":"An infinitive with its own accusative subject brings a second actor into the sentence. Spotting it keeps the actors straight."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson's table gives εἰς τό + infinitive and ἐν τῷ + infinitive the same function.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. εἰς + τό + infinitive expresses purpose, while ἐν + τῷ + infinitive is temporal (\"while,\" \"when\")."},{"q":"In what case is an infinitive's own subject expressed?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Genitive","Dative","Accusative"],"answer":3,"explain":"An infinitive can have its own subject, and that subject goes in the accusative case."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson describes the articular infinitive as a uniquely Greek construction.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson calls the articular infinitive a uniquely Greek construction."},{"q":"According to the lesson's introduction, understanding infinitives is essential for parsing which sentences?","kind":"concept","options":["Complex Pauline and Johannine sentences","Only Gospel narratives","Only Septuagint poetry","Only short commands"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson stresses that infinitives in larger structures are key to parsing complex Pauline and Johannine sentences."},{"q":"Which of these phrases from the lesson uses an articular infinitive?","kind":"form","options":["θέλω πιστεύειν","ἦλθεν σῴζειν","ἦλθεν ἰάσασθαι","εἰς τὸ εἶναι"],"answer":3,"explain":"Only εἰς τὸ εἶναι has the article τό before the infinitive. The other three use infinitives without an article."}],"va_18":[{"q":"How does the lesson's opening describe participles?","kind":"concept","options":["The workhorses of Greek prose","A rare ornament of style","A feature found only in poetry","A construction borrowed from Latin"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls participles the workhorses of Greek prose because they do so much of the work in NT sentences."},{"q":"Which aspect does a present participle carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Perfective","Imperfective","Stative","No aspect"],"answer":1,"explain":"A present participle is imperfective."},{"q":"Which aspect does a perfect participle carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Imperfective","Perfective","Stative","No aspect"],"answer":2,"explain":"A perfect participle is stative."},{"q":"Which aspect does an aorist participle carry?","kind":"concept","options":["Stative","Imperfective","No aspect","Perfective"],"answer":3,"explain":"An aorist participle is perfective."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, attributive participles always appear with the article.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says attributive participles often appear with the article, in the article-participle pattern, not always."},{"q":"In the lesson's parsing scheme, tense and voice belong to which kind of analysis?","kind":"concept","options":["Adjectival analysis","Verbal analysis","Syntactical analysis","Lexical analysis"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tense and voice are the verbal side of the participle, so they belong to the verbal analysis."},{"q":"In the lesson's parsing scheme, gender, case and number belong to which kind of analysis?","kind":"concept","options":["Verbal analysis","Syntactical analysis","Adjectival analysis","Lexical analysis"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gender, case and number are the adjectival side of the participle."},{"q":"After the verbal and adjectival analysis of a participle, what does the lesson say comes next?","kind":"concept","options":["Checking its accent","Tracing its etymology","Finding its augment","Analyzing how it functions in the sentence"],"answer":3,"explain":"Once you have parsed it both ways, you identify its syntactical role in the sentence."},{"q":"Besides attributively, how else can a participle be used as an adjective, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Predicatively","Vocatively","Imperatively","Deponently"],"answer":0,"explain":"Like other adjectives, a participle can be used attributively or predicatively."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says Paul's letters are dense with participial constructions.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson singles out Paul's letters as especially full of participles."},{"q":"What does the lesson say about Ephesians 1:3-14 in the Greek text?","kind":"concept","options":["It contains no participles","It is a single sentence","It has no main verb","It is written entirely in the imperative"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ephesians 1:3-14 is one long sentence in Greek, held together largely by participial phrases."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how is the long sentence in Ephesians 1:3-14 built?","kind":"concept","options":["Through a chain of imperatives","Through repeated ἵνα clauses","Largely through participial phrases that elaborate the main assertion","Through a list of nouns with no verbs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Participial phrases expand and elaborate one main assertion, which is how the sentence can run so long."},{"q":"According to the lesson, mastering participles is perhaps what?","kind":"concept","options":["Optional for beginning readers","Useful only for reading Paul","Less important than learning accents","The single most important step toward fluent NT reading"],"answer":3,"explain":"Because participles are everywhere, the lesson calls mastering them perhaps the single most important step toward fluent reading."},{"q":"Which category of participle functions adjectivally, modifying a specific noun?","kind":"concept","options":["Circumstantial","Attributive","Imperatival","Infinitival"],"answer":1,"explain":"Attributive participles act like adjectives and modify a particular noun."},{"q":"Which category of participle describes the circumstances surrounding the main clause action?","kind":"concept","options":["Attributive","Relative","Circumstantial","Articular"],"answer":2,"explain":"Circumstantial participles fill in the circumstances around the main action, such as when or why."},{"q":"How does the lesson suggest translating an attributive participle?","kind":"concept","options":["With an adverbial clause such as \"while he was...\"","As a command","As a purpose clause such as \"in order to...\"","With a relative clause such as \"the one who...\""],"answer":3,"explain":"Since attributive participles modify a noun, a relative clause like \"the one who...\" fits them."},{"q":"How does the lesson suggest translating a circumstantial participle?","kind":"concept","options":["With a relative clause such as \"the man who...\"","With an adverbial clause such as \"while he was...\"","As a noun in the subject slot","As a question"],"answer":1,"explain":"Circumstantial participles work adverbially, so they are rendered with clauses like \"while...,\" \"because...\" or \"having done...\""},{"q":"Which pattern do attributive participles often appear in?","kind":"concept","options":["The article-participle pattern","The ἵνα + subjunctive pattern","The ἐάν + subjunctive pattern","The μή + aorist pattern"],"answer":0,"explain":"Attributive participles often come with the article, in what the lesson calls the article-participle pattern."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does the tense of a participle communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Time only","Mood","Aspect, not time","Person"],"answer":2,"explain":"As with infinitives, a participle's tense signals aspect, not time."},{"q":"If a participle's tense does not signal time, how can its relative time be known?","kind":"concept","options":["From an augment","From the case ending","From the article","By inference from context"],"answer":3,"explain":"Relative time is not built into the tense. It is inferred from context."},{"q":"A present participle typically describes action that relates to the main verb how?","kind":"concept","options":["As prior to it","As ongoing or simultaneous with it","As following it in the future","As a state resulting from it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present participle's imperfective aspect usually fits action going on at the same time as the main verb."},{"q":"An aorist participle typically describes action that relates to the main verb how?","kind":"concept","options":["As simultaneous with it","As later than it","As prior to it","As unrelated to it"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist participle's default is action that happens before the main verb's action."},{"q":"What does a perfect participle typically present?","kind":"concept","options":["Action prior to the main verb","Ongoing action","A command","A completed state from which something results"],"answer":3,"explain":"The perfect participle is stative: it presents a completed state with results."},{"q":"True or false: according to the lesson, an aorist participle always describes action prior to the main verb.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Prior action is the usual tendency, but an aorist participle of identical action sometimes describes simultaneous events."},{"q":"According to the lesson, the relative time inferred for a participle is time relative to what?","kind":"concept","options":["The main verb's action","The moment of writing","The noun it agrees with","The article before it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Present participles tend to be simultaneous with the main verb and aorist participles prior to it, so the time in view is relative to the main verb's action."},{"q":"What are the two main categories of participle use named in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Articular and anarthrous","Active and passive","Attributive and circumstantial","Present and aorist"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson names attributive participles, which modify a noun, and circumstantial participles, which describe the circumstances of the main action."},{"q":"True or false: the lesson says participles are foundational to how Greek communicates complex ideas.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson stresses that participles are not occasional; they are foundational."},{"q":"Which kind of aorist participle does the lesson say sometimes describes simultaneous events?","kind":"concept","options":["An aorist participle with the article","An aorist participle in the genitive","An aorist participle after a verb of motion","The aorist participle of identical action"],"answer":3,"explain":"Prior action is only the aorist participle's usual tendency. The lesson notes that an aorist participle of identical action sometimes describes events simultaneous with the main verb."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what will students who master participles find?","kind":"concept","options":["That they no longer need context","That the NT opens up significantly","That aspect no longer matters","That they can skip the main verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson contrasts two outcomes: students who struggle with participles will struggle with the NT, while those who master them will find it opens up significantly."},{"q":"Which statement about Ephesians 1:3-14 is supported by the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Reading it well requires confident handling of participles","It can be read well without attention to participles","It is a collection of short, separate sentences","It is mainly built from subjunctive clauses"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the passage is built largely from participial phrases, reading it well depends on handling participles confidently."}],"va_19":[{"q":"Which attributive pattern does this phrase follow?","kind":"form","options":["Article — Participle — Noun","Article — Noun — Article — Participle","Noun — Participle, with no article","Participle — Article — Noun"],"answer":0,"explain":"The article ὁ comes first, then the participle πιστεύων, then the noun ἄνθρωπος. The participle sits between the article and its noun.","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων ἄνθρωπος"},{"q":"Which attributive pattern does this phrase follow?","kind":"form","options":["Article — Participle — Noun","Noun — Participle, with no article","Participle — Noun — Article","Article — Noun — Article — Participle"],"answer":3,"explain":"Here the article-noun unit ὁ ἄνθρωπος comes first, and the article ὁ is repeated before the participle πιστεύων.","greek":"ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὁ πιστεύων"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["because the man believes","the man will believe","the believing man","the men who believed"],"answer":2,"explain":"The participle πιστεύων describes the noun ἄνθρωπος like an adjective: \"the believing man.\"","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων ἄνθρωπος"},{"q":"What does this phrase mean?","kind":"meaning","options":["those who love God","the one who believes in him","the word which is being spoken","the one who comes to me"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson renders οἱ ἀγαπῶντες τὸν θεόν with a relative clause: \"those who love God.\"","greek":"οἱ ἀγαπῶντες τὸν θεόν"},{"q":"What does this phrase from John 6:35 mean?","kind":"reading","options":["the one who hears my word","the one who comes to me","those who love God","the believing man"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὁ ἐρχόμενος is \"the one who comes,\" and πρός με adds \"to me.\"","greek":"ὁ ἐρχόμενος πρός με"},{"q":"What does this phrase from John 5:24 mean?","kind":"reading","options":["the one who comes to me","the one who hears my word","the word which is being spoken","those who love God"],"answer":1,"explain":"The article ὁ and the participle ἀκούων frame the phrase \"the one who hears,\" with τὸν λόγον μου (\"my word\") tucked in between.","greek":"ὁ τὸν λόγον μου ἀκούων"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["the one who hears my word","the believing man","the word which is being spoken","those who love God"],"answer":2,"explain":"The repeated article τό ties λαλούμενον to ῥῆμα, so it becomes a relative clause: \"the word which is being spoken.\"","greek":"τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον"},{"q":"In John 3:16, what role does the lesson say this phrase plays?","kind":"reading","options":["Object of the main verb","A circumstantial \"while\" clause","A description of a following noun","Subject of the purpose clause"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson notes that ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν (\"the one who believes in him\") is the subject of the purpose clause.","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν"},{"q":"What is a substantival participle?","kind":"concept","options":["A participle with no article that describes the circumstances","A participle placed between an article and its noun","An article + participle working as a complete noun phrase","A participle that serves as the main verb of the clause"],"answer":2,"explain":"A substantival participle has no separate noun to modify; the article-participle unit itself acts as the noun."},{"q":"How does the lesson classify the use of the participle in this phrase?","kind":"form","options":["Circumstantial: it tells when the main action happens","Attributive, describing a following noun","Attributive, in the pattern Article — Noun — Article — Participle","Substantival: the article-participle unit acts as a noun"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson lists ὁ ἐρχόμενος πρός με under substantival participles: \"the one who comes to me\" works as a full noun phrase.","greek":"ὁ ἐρχόμενος πρός με"},{"q":"In a substantival participle, the participle modifies a separate noun that follows it.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A substantival participle has no separate noun it modifies; the article and participle together carry the noun meaning."},{"q":"According to the lesson, this phrase can be translated simply as \"the believer.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. ὁ πιστεύων can be \"the one who believes\" or simply \"the believer.\"","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what is the key feature that tells an attributive participle apart from a circumstantial one?","kind":"concept","options":["The tense of the participle","The voice of the main verb","The article","The length of the sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says the key distinction is the article: attributive participles are preceded by one, circumstantial ones are not."},{"q":"A participle stands in a clause with no article before it. According to the lesson, what should you think?","kind":"concept","options":["Circumstantial","Attributive","Substantival","A relative clause with \"who\""],"answer":0,"explain":"No article before the participle points to a circumstantial participle, which describes the circumstances of the main clause."},{"q":"According to this lesson, what does a circumstantial participle describe?","kind":"concept","options":["A quality of the noun right after the article","The full noun phrase, with no noun stated","The circumstances of the main clause action","The identity of the noun it follows"],"answer":2,"explain":"A circumstantial participle has no article and describes the circumstances of the main clause action, not a noun."},{"q":"What do both attributive patterns do to the noun?","kind":"concept","options":["They restrict or define the noun","They turn the noun into a verb","They give the time of the main verb","They make the noun the object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both patterns mark the participle as attributive, and it restricts or defines the noun."},{"q":"Why does the article τό appear right before λαλούμενον?","kind":"form","options":["It shows the participle is circumstantial","It matches the noun ῥῆμα and marks the participle as attributive","It shows λαλούμενον is the main verb","It makes λαλούμενον a separate subject"],"answer":1,"explain":"An attributive participle is preceded by an article matching its noun. τό matches ῥῆμα, so λαλούμενον modifies ῥῆμα directly.","greek":"τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον"},{"q":"How does the lesson render this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["the man, the believing one","while believing, the man","the man who will believe","because the man believes"],"answer":0,"explain":"With the repeated article, the lesson renders it \"the man, the believing one.\"","greek":"ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὁ πιστεύων"},{"q":"Where does the lesson say attributive participles appear with striking frequency?","kind":"concept","options":["The book of Acts alone","The genealogies of Matthew","The Gospel of John and the Johannine letters","The book of Revelation alone"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson points to John's Gospel and the Johannine letters as places where attributive participles appear with striking frequency."},{"q":"A participle follows an article-noun unit, and the article is repeated right before the participle. What is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Attributive position","Circumstantial use","Substantival use with no stated noun","A participle used as the main verb"],"answer":0,"explain":"Following the article-noun unit with a repeated article is one of the two classic attributive positions."},{"q":"In this phrase, which word does the article ὁ go with?","kind":"form","options":["λόγον","ἀκούων","μου","τὸν"],"answer":1,"explain":"ὁ ... ἀκούων is the article-participle unit, \"the one who hears.\" The words τὸν λόγον μου (\"my word\") sit inside it.","greek":"ὁ τὸν λόγον μου ἀκούων"},{"q":"In this phrase, which part means \"in him\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["ὁ πιστεύων","πιστεύων","ὁ","εἰς αὐτόν"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὁ πιστεύων is \"the one who believes,\" so εἰς αὐτόν supplies \"in him.\"","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does an attributive participle describe?","kind":"concept","options":["The time of the main clause action","A quality or characteristic of the noun it agrees with","The reason the main verb happens","The speaker who uses the verb"],"answer":1,"explain":"An attributive participle works like an adjective, describing a quality or characteristic of the noun it agrees with."},{"q":"In this phrase, the participle is circumstantial because it comes after the noun.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The repeated article ὁ before πιστεύων makes this the Article — Noun — Article — Participle pattern, which is attributive.","greek":"ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὁ πιστεύων"},{"q":"Which of these phrases is substantival, with no stated noun?","kind":"form","options":["ὁ πιστεύων ἄνθρωπος","ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὁ πιστεύων","τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον","ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν"],"answer":3,"explain":"ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν has no noun for the participle to modify; the other three each contain a noun (ἄνθρωπος or ῥῆμα) that the participle describes."},{"q":"The lesson renders this phrase with a relative clause. Which relative word does it use?","kind":"meaning","options":["who","when","which","because"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson translates it \"the word which is being spoken.\" The standard strategy uses \"who\" or \"which\"; \"which\" fits a thing like a word.","greek":"τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον"},{"q":"The lesson says substantival participles are rare in John.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says the substantival participle is extremely common in John, as in John 3:16, 6:35 and 5:24."},{"q":"In both classic attributive patterns, an article stands directly before the participle.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. In Article — Participle — Noun and in Article — Noun — Article — Participle, the participle comes right after an article."},{"q":"Which phrase from the lesson follows the Article — Noun — Article — Participle pattern?","kind":"form","options":["ὁ ἐρχόμενος πρός με","οἱ ἀγαπῶντες τὸν θεόν","ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτόν","τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον"],"answer":3,"explain":"In τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ λαλούμενον the article-noun unit τὸ ῥῆμα is followed by a repeated article τό and the participle λαλούμενον."},{"q":"Which English rendering uses the standard relative-clause strategy for this phrase?","kind":"meaning","options":["the one who believes","while believing","because he believes","if he believes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The standard strategy for an attributive participle is a relative clause with \"who\" or \"which\": \"the one who believes.\"","greek":"ὁ πιστεύων"}],"va_20":[{"q":"Which relationship does the lesson use this example to illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Temporal","Causal","Concessive","Means"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson renders it \"while going, he saw,\" a temporal relationship that tells when the main action happened.","greek":"πορευόμενος εἶδεν"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this example?","kind":"meaning","options":["although he believes, he rejoices","by rejoicing, he believes","he rejoiced after believing","because he believes, he rejoices"],"answer":3,"explain":"This is the lesson's causal example: the believing is the reason for the rejoicing.","greek":"πιστεύων χαίρει"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this phrase from 2 Cor 8:9?","kind":"reading","options":["although he was rich, he became poor","because he was rich, he became poor","after he became poor, he was rich","if he is rich, he will become poor"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson reads the participle here as concessive: \"although he was rich, he became poor.\"","greek":"ἐπτώχευσεν πλούσιος ὤν"},{"q":"In this example, which word is the circumstantial participle?","kind":"form","options":["ἐπορεύθη","εἶδεν","χαίρων","σωθήσεται"],"answer":2,"explain":"χαίρων (\"rejoicing\") is the participle; ἐπορεύθη (\"he went\") is the main verb. Together: \"he went rejoicing.\"","greek":"χαίρων ἐπορεύθη"},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this example?","kind":"meaning","options":["while he was believing, he was saved","if he believes, he will be saved","although he believes, he will be saved","he went on believing"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is the lesson's conditional example, glossed with \"if.\"","greek":"πιστεύων σωθήσεται"},{"q":"The lesson reads πιστεύων as causal in πιστεύων χαίρει but conditional in πιστεύων σωθήσεται. What decides the difference?","kind":"concept","options":["A different ending on the participle","Context","An article before the participle","A conjunction placed before the participle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek uses the same participial construction for every relationship, so context determines which one is in view.","greek":"πιστεύων"},{"q":"Which relationship does the lesson use this example to illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["Manner","Concessive","Means","Temporal"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson glosses it \"by doing this...,\" which expresses means: how the main action is accomplished.","greek":"τοῦτο ποιῶν..."},{"q":"How does the lesson translate this example?","kind":"meaning","options":["because he went, he rejoiced","if he goes, he will rejoice","he went rejoicing","although he rejoiced, he went"],"answer":2,"explain":"The participle χαίρων describes the way he went: \"he went rejoicing.\"","greek":"χαίρων ἐπορεύθη"},{"q":"After identifying the noun a circumstantial participle agrees with, what does the lesson say to ask next?","kind":"concept","options":["Whether an article stands before the participle","What the logical relationship is between the participial action and the main clause action","Which English word the participle sounds like","Whether the main verb is in the future tense"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step two is to ask how the participial action relates logically to the main clause action, since that decides the English conjunction."},{"q":"Like English, Greek uses a different conjunction with the participle for each relationship (when, because, although, if).","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. English uses different conjunctions, but Greek uses the same participial construction for all of them."},{"q":"In which features does a circumstantial participle agree with its noun?","kind":"concept","options":["Gender, case, and number","Tense, voice, and mood","Person and number only","Tense and aspect only"],"answer":0,"explain":"It agrees with its noun (often the subject) in gender, case, and number, even though no article introduces it."},{"q":"The lesson says the participle's subject must always be the same as the main clause subject.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Its subject is typically, but not always, the same as the main clause subject."},{"q":"How does a circumstantial participle relate to the main clause predicate?","kind":"concept","options":["It replaces the main verb","It is the main clause predicate","It modifies a noun inside the predicate","It supplements it but is not part of it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says the circumstantial participle is not part of the main clause predicate; it supplements it."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the circumstantial participle among NT participles?","kind":"concept","options":["The most flexible and frequently occurring type","The rarest type, found mainly in poetry","A type used only in the Gospels","A type that always needs an article"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson calls it the most flexible and frequently occurring type of participle in the NT."},{"q":"What kind of information does a circumstantial participle add to the main clause?","kind":"concept","options":["Which noun the article points to","When, why, how, or under what conditions the action occurs","Who owns the object of the verb","How the main verb should be spelled"],"answer":1,"explain":"It provides adverbial information: when, why, how, or under what conditions the main action occurs."},{"q":"Greek uses one participial form, while English needs a specific conjunction. What fills that gap?","kind":"concept","options":["A fixed rule that every participle is temporal","The article placed before the participle","The translator's interpretive judgment","The accent on the participle"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says translation requires interpretation: the translator's interpretive judgment fills the gap."},{"q":"What does the lesson say mechanical translations often do with circumstantial participles?","kind":"concept","options":["Default to concessive glosses in every case","Leave the participle untranslated","Turn every participle into a noun","Default to temporal glosses when another relationship may fit better"],"answer":3,"explain":"Mechanical translations often fall back on temporal glosses, even when another relationship may be more accurate."},{"q":"Which of these does the lesson list as part of the context that determines the relationship?","kind":"concept","options":["The length of the participle","The discourse genre","The first letter of the participle","The number of words in the sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Context includes the logical relationship between the actions, the discourse genre, and surrounding syntax."},{"q":"What is the first step in the lesson's translation strategy for a circumstantial participle?","kind":"concept","options":["Choose the conjunction \"although\"","Look for an article before it","Translate it as a relative clause","Identify what noun it agrees with"],"answer":3,"explain":"Step one is to identify the noun the participle agrees with, which is usually the main clause subject."},{"q":"If no other relationship is obvious, which relationship does the lesson say to try first?","kind":"concept","options":["Concessive","Conditional","Temporal","Means"],"answer":2,"explain":"Try temporal first when nothing else is obvious; \"while\" or \"after\" are safe default glosses."},{"q":"You try a temporal gloss, but it does not fit well. Which relationships does the lesson say to consider next?","kind":"concept","options":["Attributive or substantival","Only the conditional","Only the manner","Causal, concessive, or means"],"answer":3,"explain":"If temporal does not fit well, the lesson points you to causal, concessive, or means."},{"q":"Which feature of the participle does the lesson suggest using as a clue to the relationship?","kind":"concept","options":["Its accent","Its aspect","Its first letter","Its position at the end of the sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says to use the aspect of the participle as a clue when choosing the relationship."},{"q":"The lesson calls \"while\" and \"after\" default safe glosses.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. When no other relationship is obvious, \"while\" or \"after\" are the safe default glosses."},{"q":"Which English glosses go with the concessive relationship?","kind":"concept","options":["\"although,\" \"even though\"","\"because,\" \"since\"","\"while,\" \"when,\" \"after\"","\"by [doing]\""],"answer":0,"explain":"Concessive participles are glossed \"although\" or \"even though.\""},{"q":"The lesson glosses this example with \"while.\" What does that say about the two actions?","kind":"meaning","options":["The seeing happened before the going began","The going and the seeing happen at the same time","The going is the reason for the seeing","The seeing happened in spite of the going"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"While going, he saw\" puts the two actions side by side in time. The lesson notes that simultaneous action often suits a temporal \"while.\"","greek":"πορευόμενος εἶδεν"},{"q":"In this phrase, which word is the main verb, meaning \"he became poor\"?","kind":"form","options":["πλούσιος","ὤν","εἶδεν","ἐπτώχευσεν"],"answer":3,"explain":"ἐπτώχευσεν is the main verb, \"he became poor\"; the participial phrase πλούσιος ὤν supplies the concessive \"although he was rich.\"","greek":"ἐπτώχευσεν πλούσιος ὤν"},{"q":"How many adverbial relationships does the lesson list for circumstantial participles?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Six","Four","Eight"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists six: temporal, causal, concessive, conditional, means, and manner."},{"q":"Which summary does the lesson give for identifying a circumstantial participle?","kind":"concept","options":["Article + agrees with a noun + adjective-like description","No article + agrees with a noun + adverbial information","No article + agrees with the main verb + noun function","Article + no stated noun + full noun function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's formula: no article, agreement with a noun, and adverbial information."},{"q":"A participle has no article, agrees with the subject, and tells why the main action happens. Which relationship is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Temporal","Manner","Concessive","Causal"],"answer":3,"explain":"No article plus adverbial information marks it as circumstantial, and a participle that gives the reason (\"because,\" \"since\") is causal."},{"q":"Which of these glosses belongs to the causal relationship?","kind":"concept","options":["although","while","since","if"],"answer":2,"explain":"Causal participles give a reason and are glossed \"because\" or \"since.\""}],"va_21":[{"q":"In a genitive absolute, what case is the participle's subject in?","kind":"concept","options":["Nominative","Accusative","Dative","Genitive"],"answer":3,"explain":"As the name says, both the participle and its subject are in the genitive case."},{"q":"In a genitive absolute, only the subject is genitive; the participle takes the case of the main clause subject.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The participle and its subject are both in the genitive case."},{"q":"From which Latin word does the term \"absolute\" come?","kind":"concept","options":["abstractus","absolutus","absentia","obsoletus"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Absolute\" comes from the Latin absolutus."},{"q":"What does the Latin absolutus mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["\"joined\" or \"bound to\"","\"owned\" or \"possessed\"","\"freed\" or \"loosened from\"","\"repeated\" or \"doubled\""],"answer":2,"explain":"absolutus means \"freed\" or \"loosened from,\" which fits a construction that is free from the main clause's grammar."},{"q":"Any two genitive nouns standing side by side form a genitive absolute.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. A genitive absolute is not simply two genitive nouns together; it is a participle and its subject, independent from the main clause."},{"q":"What separates a genitive absolute from an ordinary phrase that just happens to contain genitives?","kind":"concept","options":["Its grammatical independence from the main clause","Its position at the very end of the sentence","Its use of the nominative case","Its appearance only in letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says independence from the main clause is what defines a genitive absolute."},{"q":"Which three of the lesson's common translation patterns express time (a temporal circumstance)?","kind":"concept","options":["\"Because,\" \"When,\" and \"While\"","\"After,\" \"Because,\" and \"While\"","\"After,\" \"When,\" and \"While\"","\"After,\" \"When,\" and \"Because\""],"answer":2,"explain":"\"After,\" \"When,\" and \"While\" all place the circumstance in time. \"Because\" gives a reason, so it is causal."},{"q":"\"After [subject] [verb-ing]…\" is one of the lesson's common translations of a genitive absolute.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. It is the first of the four common translation patterns the lesson lists."},{"q":"Which of the lesson's translation patterns expresses a cause?","kind":"concept","options":["\"After [subject] [verb-ing]…\"","\"Because [subject] [verb-ed]…\"","\"When [subject] [verb-ed]…\"","\"While [subject] was [verb-ing]…\""],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Because\" gives a reason, so this pattern suits a causal genitive absolute."},{"q":"In Gospel and Acts narratives, what do genitive absolutes often introduce?","kind":"concept","options":["The closing greeting of a letter","New narrative scenes or background circumstances","Direct commands to the reader","Lists of names"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says they often introduce new narrative scenes or background circumstances."},{"q":"Take the sentence \"While the teacher was speaking, the crowd listened.\" If Greek expressed this with a genitive absolute, which part would be the genitive absolute?","kind":"concept","options":["the crowd listened","the crowd","listened","While the teacher was speaking"],"answer":3,"explain":"The genitive absolute is the participle and its subject giving the background circumstance: \"While the teacher was speaking.\""},{"q":"Why does each of the lesson's translation patterns, such as \"When [subject] [verb-ed]…\", include its own [subject]?","kind":"concept","options":["Because the main clause subject is always repeated in English","Because the participle is in the nominative case","Because the genitive absolute is a command with an implied \"you\"","Because the genitive absolute has its own subject, in the genitive with the participle"],"answer":3,"explain":"A genitive absolute pairs a participle with its own subject, both genitive. The [subject] slot in each pattern translates that genitive subject."},{"q":"In the term \"genitive absolute,\" what does the word \"genitive\" refer to?","kind":"concept","options":["The case of the participle and its subject","The tense of the main verb","The position of the phrase in the sentence","The number of words in the phrase"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both the participle and its subject are in the genitive case, which gives the construction the first half of its name."},{"q":"\"Because the rain was falling, they stayed inside.\" Which of the lesson's functions does the first clause express?","kind":"concept","options":["Temporal","Concessive","Causal","Possessive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first clause gives the reason for staying inside, so it is causal, one of the three typical functions."},{"q":"\"Although the night was dark, the shepherds kept watch.\" If Greek expressed the first clause with a genitive absolute, which of the lesson's typical functions would it show?","kind":"concept","options":["Temporal","Causal","Concessive","Possessive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The dark night is a circumstance that might have prevented the watch but did not, so the clause is concessive (\"although\")."},{"q":"Why is a phrase like \"the house of the king\" not a genitive absolute, even in Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["It has no participle forming a clause independent of the main clause","It is in the nominative case","It could only appear in Acts","It must be translated with \"after\""],"answer":0,"explain":"A genitive absolute is not just two genitive nouns together; it needs a participle and its subject, independent from the main clause."},{"q":"A genitive participle describes a genitive noun that the main clause itself needs. Is this a genitive absolute?","kind":"concept","options":["No, because it is not grammatically independent from the main clause","Yes, because any genitive participle is absolute","Yes, as long as it is translated with \"when\"","No, because genitive participles do not exist"],"answer":0,"explain":"Being genitive is not enough. A genitive absolute must be grammatically independent from the main clause."},{"q":"How many typical functions of the genitive absolute does the lesson name?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Five","Three","Six"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson names three: temporal, causal, and concessive."},{"q":"What kind of construction is the genitive absolute?","kind":"concept","options":["A participial construction","An infinitive construction","An imperative command","A list of genitive nouns"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson defines the genitive absolute as a participial construction."},{"q":"The lesson says the participle in a genitive absolute is always translated with \"because.\"","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson gives several patterns: After, When, While, and Because, depending on the circumstance."},{"q":"Which English example has the shape of a genitive absolute: a participle and its subject giving a circumstance, apart from the main clause?","kind":"concept","options":["The host's guests ate.","The host spoke to the guests.","The guests of the host.","After the guests had eaten, the host spoke."],"answer":3,"explain":"\"After the guests had eaten\" is a background circumstance with its own subject, standing apart from the main clause \"the host spoke.\""},{"q":"If you removed a genitive absolute from its sentence, what would happen to the main clause's grammar?","kind":"concept","options":["It would lose its subject","It would lose its main verb","It would have to change to the genitive","It would still be complete, because the genitive absolute is independent"],"answer":3,"explain":"The genitive absolute is free from the main clause's grammar, so the main clause stands on its own."},{"q":"If Greek expressed \"Because the storm was rising, the sailors turned back\" with a genitive absolute, which word would be the genitive participle?","kind":"concept","options":["storm","rising","sailors","turned"],"answer":1,"explain":"The participle of the genitive absolute is the verb of the circumstance, \"rising\"; its subject \"storm\" would be genitive too."},{"q":"Which of these words does NOT begin one of the lesson's four common translation patterns?","kind":"concept","options":["After","If","When","Because"],"answer":1,"explain":"The four patterns begin with After, When, While, and Because. \"If\" is not among them."},{"q":"The circumstances a genitive absolute describes surround what?","kind":"concept","options":["A noun inside the main clause","The writer's opinion","The next paragraph","The main clause action"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says genitive absolutes describe circumstances surrounding the main clause action."},{"q":"Which pattern fits a background action that was still in progress?","kind":"concept","options":["\"After [subject] [verb-ing]…\"","\"Because [subject] [verb-ed]…\"","\"When [subject] [verb-ed]…\"","\"While [subject] was [verb-ing]…\""],"answer":3,"explain":"\"While [subject] was [verb-ing]\" pictures an action going on in the background."},{"q":"Explaining absolutus, the lesson says the construction is free from what?","kind":"concept","options":["The genitive case","The main clause's grammar","The participle's subject","The narrative scene"],"answer":1,"explain":"Absolutus means \"freed\" or \"loosened from.\" The genitive absolute is free from the main clause's grammar; it still keeps its genitive case and its own subject."},{"q":"In the pattern \"When [subject] [verb-ed]…\", what does [verb-ed] stand for in a genitive absolute?","kind":"concept","options":["The genitive participle","The main verb of the sentence","A genitive noun with no verb","The main clause subject"],"answer":0,"explain":"The patterns translate the genitive absolute itself: [subject] is its genitive subject and the verb slot is its genitive participle."},{"q":"In \"After the guests had eaten, the host spoke,\" which word would be the genitive subject if Greek used a genitive absolute?","kind":"concept","options":["host","eaten","guests","spoke"],"answer":2,"explain":"The subject of the background circumstance is \"guests,\" so it would be genitive along with the participle."},{"q":"Which word in the term \"genitive absolute\" points to its independence from the main clause?","kind":"concept","options":["genitive","participle","absolute","construction"],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Absolute\" comes from absolutus, \"freed\" or \"loosened from\": the construction is free from the main clause's grammar."}],"va_22":[{"q":"According to the lesson's chart, which two classes use the indicative mood?","kind":"form","options":["Second and third","First and third","First and second","Only the third"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first class is εἰ + indicative and the second is εἰ + secondary indicative. The third class uses the subjunctive."},{"q":"In \"If it rains, the road will flood,\" what is the apodosis?","kind":"concept","options":["the road will flood","If it rains","If it","rains, the road"],"answer":0,"explain":"The apodosis is the \"then\" clause giving the result: \"the road will flood.\""},{"q":"What is the protasis of a conditional statement?","kind":"concept","options":["The \"then\" clause that gives the result","The main verb of the sentence","The \"if\" clause that states the condition","The particle that ends the sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"The protasis is the \"if\" clause: the condition stated."},{"q":"The apodosis is the \"then\" clause, giving the result or conclusion.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The apodosis is the \"then\" clause, the result or conclusion of the condition."},{"q":"How does the form of a third class protasis differ from a first class protasis?","kind":"form","options":["Same particle, different mood","Different particle and different mood","Different particle, same mood","Same particle and mood; only word order differs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first class uses εἰ + indicative; the third uses ἐάν + subjunctive. Both the particle and the mood change."},{"q":"A reader sees this particle at the start of a protasis and decides the condition must be contrary to fact. What has the reader overlooked?","kind":"concept","options":["εἰ never appears in conditional sentences","εἰ always takes the subjunctive","εἰ also marks the first class, which assumes the condition for argument's sake","εἰ marks only the probable future condition"],"answer":2,"explain":"εἰ appears in both the first class (εἰ + indicative) and the second class (εἰ + secondary indicative), so the particle alone does not make a condition contrary to fact.","greek":"εἰ"},{"q":"What kind of condition does this form mark?","kind":"form","options":["A contrary-to-fact condition","One assumed for argument's sake","A probable future condition","A command with no condition"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐάν + subjunctive is the third class conditional: a probable future condition.","greek":"ἐάν + subjunctive"},{"q":"Which construction marks a contrary-to-fact condition?","kind":"form","options":["εἰ + secondary indicative","ἐάν + subjunctive","εἰ + indicative","A participle with no article"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second class, εἰ + secondary indicative, is the contrary-to-fact condition."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the class patterns the lesson lists?","kind":"form","options":["εἰ + indicative","ἐάν + secondary indicative","ἐάν + subjunctive","εἰ + secondary indicative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists εἰ + indicative (first), εἰ + secondary indicative (second) and ἐάν + subjunctive (third). It pairs ἐάν only with the subjunctive."},{"q":"Both the first class and the second class conditionals use this particle.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The first class is εἰ + indicative and the second class is εἰ + secondary indicative.","greek":"εἰ"},{"q":"The first and second classes both use εἰ with the indicative. What detail does the lesson's chart add for the second class?","kind":"form","options":["The verb is subjunctive","ἐάν follows εἰ","The apodosis comes first","The indicative is a secondary indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The chart lists the second class as εἰ + secondary indicative. Since the classes describe form, context remains essential in confirming a contrary-to-fact reading.","greek":"εἰ"},{"q":"According to the lesson, what do the conditional classes describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Whether the condition is actually true","The speaker's tone of voice","The grammatical form of the condition","The length of the sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"The classes describe the grammatical form, not certainty about whether the condition is true. Context is still needed to interpret them."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the conditional classes?","kind":"concept","options":["Rigid rules that settle what is true","Tools for analysis, not rigid rules about reality","Labels that replace the need for context","Patterns found only in the Gospels"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson calls the classes tools for analysis, not rigid rules about reality."},{"q":"What does the lesson say remains essential in interpreting what each conditional communicates?","kind":"concept","options":["The length of the protasis","Whether the apodosis comes first","Context","The number of particles"],"answer":2,"explain":"Context remains essential; the classes are tools, not rigid rules."},{"q":"A first class conditional, built with this form, shows that the speaker is certain the condition is true.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The first class assumes the condition for argument's sake, and the classes describe form, not certainty.","greek":"εἰ + indicative"},{"q":"Which English sentence expresses a contrary-to-fact condition?","kind":"concept","options":["If you study, you will pass.","Study, and you will pass.","If I were a bird, I would fly home.","Because you studied, you passed."],"answer":2,"explain":"\"If I were a bird\" states a condition contrary to fact, the kind the second class expresses."},{"q":"The lesson presents its three classes as the major ones students should know, not as every possible conditional pattern in Greek.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson introduces them as \"the major conditional classes students should know\" within a sophisticated system."},{"q":"Which Greek conditional class matches the kind of condition in \"If you had come, we would have eaten\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Second class (εἰ + secondary indicative)","First class (εἰ + indicative)","Third class (ἐάν + subjunctive)","It is not a conditional"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sentence describes something that did not happen, a contrary-to-fact condition, which is the second class."},{"q":"Greek has only one kind of conditional sentence.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek has a sophisticated conditional system with several classes."},{"q":"Why does each Greek conditional class use different forms?","kind":"concept","options":["To show which word is the subject","To mark the end of a paragraph","To show who is speaking","To communicate different kinds of conditionality"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each class uses different forms to communicate a different kind of conditionality."},{"q":"How many major conditional classes does this lesson present?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Five","Six","Three"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson presents three: first class, second class, and third class."},{"q":"Which pairing of class and form is correct?","kind":"form","options":["First class: ἐάν + subjunctive","Second class: ἐάν + indicative","Third class: ἐάν + subjunctive","Third class: εἰ + secondary indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson pairs the third class with ἐάν + subjunctive. The first and second classes use εἰ."},{"q":"\"If you are a teacher (and let's assume you are), then teach well.\" Which class matches this kind of condition?","kind":"concept","options":["First class (εἰ + indicative)","Second class (εἰ + secondary indicative)","Third class (ἐάν + subjunctive)","It is not a conditional"],"answer":0,"explain":"The speaker assumes the condition for argument's sake, which is what the first class does."},{"q":"A conditional uses ἐάν + subjunctive. What should a careful reader still do?","kind":"concept","options":["Assume the condition will certainly happen","Assume the condition is contrary to fact","Read it in context, since the class describes form, not certainty","Ignore the apodosis"],"answer":2,"explain":"The class is a tool for analysis. Context remains essential, because the classes describe form, not certainty.","greek":"ἐάν"},{"q":"In \"They will listen if he speaks,\" which clause is the protasis?","kind":"concept","options":["if he speaks","They will listen","They will","listen if"],"answer":0,"explain":"The protasis is the \"if\" clause, wherever it sits: here \"if he speaks\" comes second."},{"q":"Which particle introduces a third class conditional?","kind":"form","options":["εἰ","ἐάν","εἰ or ἐάν, interchangeably","No particle at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"The third class is ἐάν + subjunctive; εἰ belongs to the first and second classes."},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"If he speaks, they will listen,\" what is the apodosis?","kind":"concept","options":["If he speaks","If he","speaks, they","they will listen"],"answer":3,"explain":"The apodosis is the \"then\" clause, the result: \"they will listen.\""},{"q":"In the lesson's example \"If he speaks, they will listen,\" which part would begin with εἰ or ἐάν in Greek?","kind":"concept","options":["they will listen","Both clauses equally","Neither clause","If he speaks"],"answer":3,"explain":"εἰ and ἐάν introduce the protasis, the \"if\" clause. Here that is \"If he speaks\"; \"they will listen\" is the apodosis."},{"q":"If Greek presented \"If he speaks, they will listen\" as a probable future condition, which construction would its protasis use?","kind":"concept","options":["ἐάν + subjunctive","εἰ + secondary indicative","εἰ + indicative","ἐάν + secondary indicative"],"answer":0,"explain":"A probable future condition is the third class, ἐάν + subjunctive."},{"q":"\"If you come tomorrow, I will show you the book.\" Which class matches this kind of condition?","kind":"concept","options":["Second class (εἰ + secondary indicative)","Third class (ἐάν + subjunctive)","First class (εἰ + indicative)","It is not a conditional"],"answer":1,"explain":"It looks to a likely future event, a probable future condition, which is the third class."}],"va_23":[{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist active","Present active","Perfect active","Future active"],"answer":3,"explain":"λύσω adds the σ tense formative to the stem, marking the future active.","greek":"λύσω"},{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Future active","Aorist passive","Aorist active","Perfect active"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἔλυσα has the augment and σα, the signals of the aorist active.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Perfect middle/passive","Perfect active","Aorist active","Future active"],"answer":1,"explain":"λέλυκα shows reduplication (λε-) plus κ, the signals of the perfect active.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"This form is the perfect active principal part of λύω.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. λέλυμαι is the perfect middle/passive: it has reduplication with a middle/passive ending. The perfect active is λέλυκα.","greek":"λέλυμαι"},{"q":"Which principal part of λύω is this?","kind":"form","options":["Aorist active","Perfect middle/passive","Aorist passive","Future active"],"answer":2,"explain":"ἐλύθην has the augment plus the θη formative, marking the aorist passive.","greek":"ἐλύθην"},{"q":"What does this verb mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["I believe","I loose","I hear","I speak"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson glosses λύω as \"I loose.\"","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"What signal marks this form as future active?","kind":"form","options":["An augment","Reduplication","The θη formative","The σ tense formative"],"answer":3,"explain":"The future active adds the σ tense formative to the stem: λύ-σ-ω.","greek":"λύσω"},{"q":"Which signals mark this form as perfect active?","kind":"form","options":["Augment + σα","Augment + θη","The σ tense formative alone","Reduplication + κ"],"answer":3,"explain":"The perfect active shows reduplication (λε-) and κ: λέ-λυ-κ-α.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"Which signals mark this form as aorist active?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication + κ","Augment + σα","Augment + θη","No tense formative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist active has the augment ἐ- and σα: ἔ-λυ-σ-α.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"What signals mark this form as perfect middle/passive?","kind":"form","options":["Reduplication with a middle/passive ending","Augment + θη formative","Reduplication + κ","The σ tense formative"],"answer":0,"explain":"λέλυμαι is reduplicated like the perfect active, but it takes a middle/passive ending instead of κ.","greek":"λέλυμαι"},{"q":"Which principal part is the basis for future passive forms?","kind":"form","options":["λύσω (future active)","λέλυμαι (perfect middle/passive)","ἔλυσα (aorist active)","ἐλύθην (aorist passive)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The aorist passive principal part is the basis for both aorist and future passive forms."},{"q":"Which principal part is the basis for imperfect forms?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυσα (aorist active)","λύω (present active)","λύσω (future active)","λέλυκα (perfect active)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present active principal part is the basis for both present and imperfect forms."},{"q":"Which principal part is the basis for aorist middle forms?","kind":"form","options":["ἔλυσα (aorist active)","ἐλύθην (aorist passive)","λέλυμαι (perfect middle/passive)","λύσω (future active)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The aorist active principal part is the basis for aorist active and middle forms."},{"q":"Which two principal parts of λύω both show reduplication?","kind":"form","options":["λέλυκα and λέλυμαι","ἔλυσα and ἐλύθην","λύω and λύσω","λύσω and ἔλυσα"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two perfect forms, λέλυκα and λέλυμαι, both begin with reduplication (λε-)."},{"q":"Both of these forms begin with an augment.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The aorist active ἔλυσα (augment + σα) and the aorist passive ἐλύθην (augment + θη) both carry the augment.","greek":"ἔλυσα, ἐλύθην"},{"q":"Which principal part of λύω has no tense formative?","kind":"form","options":["λύσω","λύω","ἔλυσα","λέλυκα"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present active λύω has no tense formative: just stem + CV + ending."},{"q":"In what person and number are principal parts given?","kind":"concept","options":["First person singular","Third person singular","First person plural","Second person singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each principal part is listed in the 1st singular, like λύω, \"I loose.\""},{"q":"In which mood are all six principal parts listed?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Indicative","Imperative","Optative"],"answer":1,"explain":"All six principal parts are indicative forms, such as the present active indicative and the aorist passive indicative."},{"q":"Which verbs does the lesson say to prioritize first when learning principal parts?","kind":"concept","options":["The longest verbs","Verbs that appear only once","The most frequent NT verbs","Verbs with no irregular parts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson advises starting with the most frequent NT verbs."},{"q":"The lesson advises noticing which verbs have irregular or missing principal parts.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Watching for irregular or missing parts is one of the lesson's strategies."},{"q":"Which practice method does the lesson recommend for recognition?","kind":"concept","options":["Memorizing every form of every verb separately","Skipping verbs with irregular parts","Reading only English translations","Working through verb forms backwards from principal parts"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson suggests practicing recognition by working backwards from the principal parts."},{"q":"What do students who invest in principal parts find, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Reading speed and confidence increase significantly","They no longer need vocabulary","They can skip noun endings","They stop needing context"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reading speed and confidence rise, and recognition becomes more instinctive over time."},{"q":"The perfect active principal part (λέλυκα) is also the basis for perfect middle/passive forms.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Perfect middle/passive forms have their own principal part, λέλυμαι. λέλυκα is the basis for perfect active forms only.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"What does each principal part bundle, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A complete vocabulary list","All of a noun's case endings","The verb's English meanings","A tense system's signals"],"answer":3,"explain":"Each principal part bundles the signals of one tense system, such as augment, reduplication, or a formative."},{"q":"Which two principal parts of λύω use σ as part of their signal?","kind":"form","options":["λύσω and ἔλυσα","λέλυκα and λέλυμαι","λύω and ἐλύθην","ἐλύθην and λέλυκα"],"answer":0,"explain":"The future λύσω has the σ tense formative, and the aorist ἔλυσα has σα."},{"q":"ἔλυσα and ἐλύθην both have an augment. Which signal sets ἐλύθην apart as the aorist passive?","kind":"form","options":["The σα ending","Reduplication","The θη formative","The κ"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist active has augment + σα; the aorist passive has augment + θη.","greek":"ἐλύθην"},{"q":"In this form, which part is the augment?","kind":"form","options":["-λυ-","-σ-","ἔ-","-α"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson highlights ἔ- at the front of ἔλυσα as the augment; σα follows the stem λυ.","greek":"ἔλυσα"},{"q":"In this form, which part is the reduplication?","kind":"form","options":["λέ-","-λυ-","-κ-","-α"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson marks λέ- as the reduplication; κ after the stem is the other perfect active signal.","greek":"λέλυκα"},{"q":"The lesson describes this form as \"stem + CV + ending.\" Which part is the stem?","kind":"form","options":["-ω","λυ-","λ-","-ύω"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stem is λυ-, the part that carries through the principal parts (λύσω, ἔλυσα, λέλυκα). The -ω follows it.","greek":"λύω"},{"q":"How many of the six principal parts of λύω are middle/passive or passive forms?","kind":"form","options":["One","Three","Two","Four"],"answer":2,"explain":"Two: λέλυμαι (perfect middle/passive) and ἐλύθην (aorist passive). The other four are active."}],"va_24":[{"q":"Which of these is one of the tenses the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Middle","Plural","Pluperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"Pluperfect is a tense. Subjunctive is a mood, middle is a voice, and plural is a number."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the moods the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Subjunctive","Aorist","Passive","Third"],"answer":0,"explain":"Subjunctive is a mood. Aorist is a tense, passive is a voice, and third is a person."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the voices the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Middle","Imperfect","Imperative","Singular"],"answer":0,"explain":"Middle is a voice. Imperfect is a tense, imperative is a mood, and singular is a number."},{"q":"How many tenses does the lesson list for a complete parse?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Six","Four","Five"],"answer":1,"explain":"Six: present, imperfect, future, aorist, perfect, and pluperfect."},{"q":"Which list gives the four moods named in the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["present, future, aorist, perfect","active, middle, passive, indicative","indicative, subjunctive, optative, imperative","first, second, third, plural"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's moods are indicative, subjunctive, optative, and imperative."},{"q":"In a parse, which element does the label \"third\" answer?","kind":"concept","options":["Number","Person","Tense","Voice"],"answer":1,"explain":"First, second, and third are the values for person."},{"q":"Which parse element would you check to tell \"I loose\" from \"I am loosed\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Number","Voice","Mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both are first person singular statements; the difference is whether the subject acts or is acted on, which is voice (active vs. passive)."},{"q":"A participle parse, like a finite verb parse, includes tense, voice, and number.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Tense, voice, and number appear in both. What changes for a participle is that gender takes the place of person, and case is added."},{"q":"Which question does the lesson connect with voice?","kind":"concept","options":["What does it communicate about the kind of statement being made?","Who is the subject?","What does it communicate about the subject's relationship to the action?","Which noun does it modify?"],"answer":2,"explain":"Voice is about the subject's relationship to the action."},{"q":"Which question does the lesson connect with mood?","kind":"concept","options":["What does it communicate about the kind of statement being made?","What does it communicate about the subject's relationship to the action?","Who is the subject?","Which noun does it modify?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood tells you what kind of statement is being made."},{"q":"What do person and number tell you in a parse?","kind":"concept","options":["Who the subject is","What kind of statement is made","The subject's relationship to the action","When the action happens"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson pairs person/number with the question \"Who is the subject?\""},{"q":"Which question does the lesson connect with tense?","kind":"concept","options":["What does the tense communicate in this context?","Who is the subject?","What kind of statement is being made?","What is the subject's relationship to the action?"],"answer":0,"explain":"For tense, the lesson asks what it communicates in this particular context."},{"q":"\"He looses\" and \"they loose\" differ in person.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Both are third person; they differ in number (singular vs. plural)."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the goal of parsing is?","kind":"concept","options":["To collect as many grammatical labels as possible","To understand how the verb communicates meaning in its sentence","To replace reading with label lists","To memorize every possible parse"],"answer":1,"explain":"The goal is not the labels but understanding how this verb communicates meaning in this sentence."},{"q":"According to the lesson, parsing serves reading.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says plainly: parsing serves reading."},{"q":"A writer could state a fact (\"you loose\") or give a command (\"loose!\"). Which parse element marks that difference?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Tense","Number","Person"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood communicates the kind of statement being made: indicative for a statement, imperative for a command."},{"q":"Which element do \"singular\" and \"plural\" describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Voice","Number","Mood"],"answer":2,"explain":"Singular and plural are the values for number."},{"q":"A verb is parsed \"aorist, active, indicative, third, singular.\" Which label tells you the kind of statement being made?","kind":"concept","options":["aorist","active","singular","indicative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Indicative is the mood, and mood tells you the kind of statement being made."},{"q":"In the parse \"aorist, active, indicative, third, singular,\" which labels tell you who the subject is?","kind":"concept","options":["aorist, active","active, indicative","third, singular","aorist, indicative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Person and number (third, singular) answer the question \"Who is the subject?\""},{"q":"A student parses a finite verb as \"present, active, indicative, singular.\" Which element is missing?","kind":"concept","options":["Person","Tense","Voice","Mood"],"answer":0,"explain":"A complete finite parse has tense, voice, mood, person, and number; this one lacks person."},{"q":"The lesson lists the imperfect as one of the moods.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Imperfect is a tense. The moods are indicative, subjunctive, optative, and imperative."},{"q":"Which set lists only tenses?","kind":"concept","options":["active, middle, aorist","indicative, imperative, future","first, plural, perfect","present, imperfect, aorist"],"answer":3,"explain":"Present, imperfect, and aorist are all tenses; the other sets mix in voices, moods, persons, or numbers."},{"q":"\"I loosed\" and \"I was loosing\" are both first person singular, active, indicative. Which parse element differs?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Person","Number","Tense"],"answer":3,"explain":"The difference is tense: \"I loosed\" is aorist and \"I was loosing\" is imperfect. Everything else matches."},{"q":"In the parse \"aorist, passive, subjunctive, second, plural,\" which label is the voice?","kind":"concept","options":["passive","aorist","subjunctive","second"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive is the voice; aorist is the tense, subjunctive the mood, and second the person."},{"q":"Which of these labels would NOT appear in a complete parse of a finite verb?","kind":"concept","options":["pluperfect","optative","masculine","second"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gender belongs to a participle parse, which replaces person with gender. A finite verb parse has tense, voice, mood, person, and number."},{"q":"Which element is added to a participle parse but is not part of a finite verb parse?","kind":"concept","options":["Tense","Voice","Case","Number"],"answer":2,"explain":"A participle parse replaces person with gender and adds case."},{"q":"The five-element parse (tense, voice, mood, person, number) is for which kind of verb form?","kind":"concept","options":["A participle","A noun","An article","A finite verb"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson gives the five elements for a finite verb; a participle parse swaps person for gender and adds case."},{"q":"Which of these is a complete parse for a finite verb?","kind":"concept","options":["aorist, passive, third, singular","aorist, passive, indicative, third, singular","passive, indicative, third, singular","aorist, indicative, third, singular, masculine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only \"aorist, passive, indicative, third, singular\" gives all five: tense, voice, mood, person, and number. The others leave one out or add gender."},{"q":"Which pair contains exactly one tense and one mood?","kind":"concept","options":["aorist and perfect","imperfect and optative","middle and passive","first and singular"],"answer":1,"explain":"Imperfect is a tense and optative is a mood. The others pair two tenses, two voices, or a person with a number."},{"q":"In the parse \"perfect, passive, indicative, first, plural,\" which label is the tense?","kind":"concept","options":["passive","indicative","first","perfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"Perfect is the tense; passive is the voice, indicative the mood, and first the person."}],"va_25":[{"q":"What is another name for literal translation?","kind":"concept","options":["Functional equivalence","Paraphrase","Interpretive translation","Formal equivalence"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson labels literal translation as formal equivalence."},{"q":"What is another name for dynamic translation?","kind":"concept","options":["Formal equivalence","Functional equivalence","Paraphrase","Word-for-word translation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson labels dynamic translation as functional equivalence."},{"q":"Besides \"paraphrase,\" what other label does the lesson give this approach?","kind":"concept","options":["Formal","Interpretive","Functional","Literal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson names the third philosophy \"Paraphrase / Interpretive,\" because it often interprets rather than just translating."},{"q":"A translator recasts a Greek sentence into natural English idiom, changing its structure so the meaning comes through clearly. Which philosophy is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Literal (formal equivalence)","Dynamic (functional equivalence)","Paraphrase that adds new content","Word-for-word interlinear"],"answer":1,"explain":"Willingness to rephrase, restructure, and use natural English idiom marks dynamic equivalence."},{"q":"What does paraphrase / interpretive translation prioritize?","kind":"concept","options":["Readability and clarity","Structural accuracy","Keeping Greek word order","Reproducing every Greek construction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Paraphrase prioritizes readability and clarity, often by interpreting."},{"q":"What does literal (formal equivalence) translation prioritize?","kind":"concept","options":["Natural English idiom","Adding explanatory content","Structural accuracy","Readability above all"],"answer":2,"explain":"Literal translation follows Greek grammatical structure closely, prioritizing structural accuracy."},{"q":"How many major translation philosophies does the lesson describe?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson describes three: literal, dynamic, and paraphrase / interpretive."},{"q":"The lesson says each translation philosophy has genuine strengths and genuine tradeoffs.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. No approach is flawless; each has real strengths and real tradeoffs."},{"q":"Which order runs from closest to the Greek structure to most interpretive?","kind":"concept","options":["Dynamic, literal, paraphrase","Paraphrase, dynamic, literal","Literal, paraphrase, dynamic","Literal, dynamic, paraphrase"],"answer":3,"explain":"Literal follows Greek structure closely, dynamic is willing to restructure for clear meaning, and paraphrase often interprets and adds explanatory content."},{"q":"Two English translations render the same Greek sentence differently. What does comparing them reveal?","kind":"concept","options":["That one of them must contain no interpretation","That the Greek has no clear meaning","That only the longer one is accurate","Where translators made different choices"],"answer":3,"explain":"Comparing multiple translations shows where translators made different choices."},{"q":"A reader wants to see how a Greek construction is built. Which kind of translation will usually show this more clearly?","kind":"concept","options":["A dynamic translation","A paraphrase","A literal translation","An interpretive retelling"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson says a literal translation can show Greek structures more clearly."},{"q":"A reader wants the meaning to come through in natural English. Which kind of translation does the lesson say may serve this better?","kind":"concept","options":["A dynamic translation","A literal translation","A word-for-word interlinear","A transliteration of the Greek"],"answer":0,"explain":"A dynamic translation may communicate the meaning more naturally in English."},{"q":"What does knowing Greek allow you to do with English translations?","kind":"concept","options":["Evaluate all of them more critically","Ignore them completely","Treat one as identical to the original","Avoid ever comparing them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Knowing Greek lets you evaluate all translations more critically."},{"q":"A careful enough translation can be treated as identical to the original.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson warns against treating any translation as identical to the original."},{"q":"Even a literal translation involves interpretive choices.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says all translations involve interpretive choices, the literal ones included."},{"q":"What does the ability to work with the Greek text directly provide, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["A guarantee that one translation is perfect","Independence that no translation can fully replace","Freedom from ever reading translations","A single correct English wording"],"answer":1,"explain":"Working with the Greek text, even partially, gives an independence no translation can fully replace."},{"q":"According to the lesson, only full mastery of Greek gives any independence from translations.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson says working with the Greek text directly, even partially, provides that independence."},{"q":"Which three things does literal translation try to keep as close to the original as possible?","kind":"concept","options":["Word order, constructions, and wording","Idiom, readability, and explanation","Tone, rhythm, and rhyme","Chapter titles, headings, and notes"],"answer":0,"explain":"A literal translation retains word order, constructions, and wording as close to the original as possible."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does paraphrase often do rather than just translating?","kind":"concept","options":["Interpret","Copy the Greek word order","Leave words untranslated","Shorten the text"],"answer":0,"explain":"Paraphrase often interprets rather than just translating."},{"q":"Dynamic equivalence aims at the \"receptor language audience.\" Who is that?","kind":"concept","options":["The original Greek-speaking readers","The readers of the language the text is translated into","The translators themselves","Scholars who read only Greek"],"answer":1,"explain":"The receptor language is the language being translated into; for an English version, that audience is English readers."},{"q":"According to the lesson, how does dynamic translation differ from literal translation?","kind":"concept","options":["It keeps Greek word order more strictly than a literal translation","It is willing to restructure to communicate meaning clearly, while literal follows Greek structure closely","It avoids interpretive choices, while a literal translation involves them","It adds explanatory content as its main goal, while literal never does"],"answer":1,"explain":"Literal translation prioritizes structural accuracy; dynamic translation will rephrase and restructure so the meaning comes through clearly in natural English."},{"q":"A version adds a phrase explaining a custom that the original text never mentions. Which philosophy does this fit?","kind":"concept","options":["Literal (formal equivalence)","Dynamic (functional equivalence)","Word-for-word interlinear","Paraphrase / interpretive"],"answer":3,"explain":"Adding explanatory content not in the original is a mark of paraphrase / interpretive translation."},{"q":"Translation A reads \"having believed, he rejoiced\"; translation B reads \"He believed and was full of joy.\" If A keeps the Greek construction, which philosophy is A closer to?","kind":"concept","options":["Dynamic (functional equivalence)","Paraphrase / interpretive","Both are equally paraphrase","Literal (formal equivalence)"],"answer":3,"explain":"A literal translation retains constructions as close to the original as possible, while B restructures into natural English."},{"q":"Why might a Greek student read both a literal and a dynamic translation of the same passage?","kind":"concept","options":["Because one of them is always identical to the Greek","The literal one shows structure, the dynamic one shows meaning naturally, and comparing reveals choices","Because dynamic translations never interpret","Because literal translations add explanatory notes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson notes each type's strength, and comparing translations reveals where translators made different choices."},{"q":"Based on the lesson's descriptions, which would you expect to read most smoothly while straying furthest from the original's wording?","kind":"concept","options":["Literal (formal equivalence)","An interlinear word-for-word gloss","Dynamic (functional equivalence)","Paraphrase / interpretive"],"answer":3,"explain":"Paraphrase prioritizes readability and may interpret and add content, so it moves furthest from the original wording."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the three philosophies the lesson describes?","kind":"concept","options":["Grammatical-historical","Textual criticism","Dynamic (functional equivalence)","Allegorical reading"],"answer":2,"explain":"The three philosophies are literal (formal), dynamic (functional), and paraphrase / interpretive."},{"q":"In the name \"formal equivalence,\" what does \"formal\" point to?","kind":"concept","options":["Using formal, dignified English","Matching only the meaning, not the structure","Matching the form, or grammatical structure, of the Greek","Adding formal explanatory notes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Formal equivalence follows Greek grammatical structure closely, keeping word order, constructions, and wording near the original form."},{"q":"Into which language does this lesson discuss rendering Greek texts?","kind":"concept","options":["Latin","Hebrew","Aramaic","English"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson discusses philosophies for rendering Greek texts into English."},{"q":"Based on the lesson, which tradeoff fits a literal translation?","kind":"concept","options":["It adds content that is not in the original","It ignores Greek structure entirely","Its English may read less naturally than a dynamic translation","It cannot show any Greek constructions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Literal translation keeps Greek structure, while the lesson credits dynamic translation with communicating meaning more naturally in English."},{"q":"In its \"Implications for Students\" section, which two kinds of translation does the lesson set side by side?","kind":"concept","options":["Literal and dynamic","Paraphrase and interlinear","Dynamic and allegorical","Literal and devotional"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson notes that a literal translation can show Greek structures, while a dynamic one may communicate meaning more naturally."}],"va_26":[{"q":"According to the lesson's descriptions, how does \"simplistic aspect claims\" differ from \"over-reading tense\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Simplistic aspect claims ignore grammar completely, while over-reading tense uses it carefully","Simplistic aspect claims apply aspect rules mechanically without context, while over-reading tense claims more than a form communicates","They are two names for the same pitfall","Over-reading tense is about the aorist only, while simplistic aspect claims are about the present only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson lists them separately: over-reading tense claims more than the form communicates, and simplistic aspect claims apply aspect rules mechanically, without considering context."},{"q":"What does the lesson call the claim that the aorist proves \"once-for-all\" action?","kind":"concept","options":["Using grammar as one tool among many","Noting what grammar cannot say alone","Letting context shape how forms are interpreted","The aorist fallacy"],"answer":3,"explain":"That claim is the aorist fallacy; the aorist simply presents action as a whole."},{"q":"A writer says, \"The present tense here proves continuous habitual action.\" Which pitfall does the lesson use this claim to illustrate?","kind":"concept","options":["The aorist fallacy","Over-reading tense","Letting context shape forms","Using grammar as one tool among many"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is the lesson's own example of over-reading tense: claiming more than the form actually communicates."},{"q":"What does the pitfall called \"simplistic aspect claims\" involve?","kind":"concept","options":["Refusing to mention aspect at all","Applying aspect rules mechanically without considering context","Checking aspect against the context","Using aspect as one tool among many"],"answer":1,"explain":"Simplistic aspect claims apply aspect rules mechanically, without considering context."},{"q":"A reader says, \"This verb form, by itself, proves my doctrinal position.\" Which pitfall is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Forcing theology into grammar","Simplistic aspect claims","Using grammar as one tool among many","Letting context shape forms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Making a grammatical form carry a theological conclusion it cannot support alone is forcing theology into grammar."},{"q":"According to the lesson, what does grammar provide?","kind":"concept","options":["Final interpretive conclusions","Proof of theological points","A replacement for context","Data for interpretation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Grammar provides data for interpretation; it does not provide interpretive conclusions by itself."},{"q":"Grammar provides interpretive conclusions by itself.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Grammar provides data for interpretation, not interpretive conclusions by itself."},{"q":"Which three things does the lesson say shape how grammatical signals communicate?","kind":"concept","options":["Spelling, accent, and breathing","Tense, voice, and mood alone","Context, genre, and discourse","Commentaries, sermons, and notes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Context, genre, and discourse shape how grammatical signals communicate."},{"q":"How should grammar be used, according to the lesson's list of responsible practices?","kind":"concept","options":["As the only tool that matters","As a way to settle debates","As one tool among many","As a replacement for context"],"answer":2,"explain":"Responsible exegesis uses grammar as one tool among many."},{"q":"What should shape how grammatical forms are interpreted?","kind":"concept","options":["The reader's preferred conclusion","The length of the verb","A fixed rule for each tense","Context"],"answer":3,"explain":"Responsible exegesis lets context shape how forms are interpreted."},{"q":"Responsible exegesis notes what grammar can say. What else does it note?","kind":"concept","options":["What grammar cannot say alone","What the reader wants it to say","How to win the argument","Which commentary agrees"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson asks you to note what grammar can say, and what it cannot say alone."},{"q":"Which kind of claim does responsible exegesis avoid?","kind":"concept","options":["That context shapes how forms are interpreted","That grammar is one tool among many","That grammar \"proves\" theological points without additional support","That the aorist presents action as a whole"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson warns against claiming grammar \"proves\" theological points without additional support."},{"q":"What does the lesson say the goal is?","kind":"concept","options":["To use Greek grammar to win arguments","To find a proof text for every claim","To read what the Greek text communicates","To make every sentence say as much as possible"],"answer":2,"explain":"The goal is to read what the Greek text communicates, not to use grammar to win arguments."},{"q":"A reader explains a verb's force by looking at the kind of writing it appears in and the flow of the passage around it. What is the reader recognizing?","kind":"concept","options":["That grammar provides conclusions by itself","That the aorist proves once-for-all action","That every tense has one fixed meaning","That context, genre, and discourse shape how grammatical signals communicate"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson says context, genre, and discourse shape how grammatical signals communicate, so a careful reader looks beyond the form itself."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe the best exegetes?","kind":"concept","options":["Bold rather than cautious with grammatical claims","Cautious rather than bold with grammatical claims","Focused on winning debates","Unwilling to use grammar at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"The best exegetes are cautious rather than bold with grammatical claims."},{"q":"According to the lesson, nuanced reading is a virtue, not a weakness.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The lesson says nuanced reading is a virtue, not a weakness."},{"q":"How many grammar-based pitfalls does the lesson list?","kind":"concept","options":["Two","Four","Three","Six"],"answer":1,"explain":"Four: over-reading tense, the aorist fallacy, forcing theology into grammar, and simplistic aspect claims."},{"q":"A student writes, \"The aorist here presents the action as a whole; the context will tell us more.\" How does this fit the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["It is the aorist fallacy","It matches the lesson's description of the aorist and lets context speak","It is over-reading tense","It forces theology into grammar"],"answer":1,"explain":"The aorist simply presents action as a whole, and the student leaves the rest to context, as the lesson recommends."},{"q":"A reader says, \"This present tense presents the action as ongoing, and the surrounding context supports that reading here.\" Which responsible practice does this follow?","kind":"concept","options":["Applying aspect rules mechanically","Making the form carry a conclusion alone","Using grammar to win an argument","Letting context shape how forms are interpreted"],"answer":3,"explain":"The reader checks the form against context instead of treating the tense as proof on its own."},{"q":"Which statement shows responsible use of grammar, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["\"This form fits the reading, but context and other evidence are needed too.\"","\"The tense alone proves the point.\"","\"The aorist proves this happened once-for-all.\"","\"Grammar settles the question, so context does not matter.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"Responsible exegesis notes what grammar can say and what it cannot say alone, and lets context shape the reading."},{"q":"Someone concludes, \"Since grammar cannot prove conclusions alone, it tells us nothing at all.\" How does this compare with the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["The lesson agrees that grammar tells us nothing","The lesson says grammar alone settles every question","The lesson says grammar does provide data; it is one tool among many","The lesson says only aspect matters"],"answer":2,"explain":"Grammar provides data for interpretation. It simply does not provide conclusions by itself."},{"q":"According to the lesson, a present tense form by itself proves continuous habitual action.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The lesson gives that claim as an example of over-reading tense: claiming more than the form actually communicates."},{"q":"Where do the pitfalls in this lesson come from?","kind":"concept","options":["Misusing Greek grammar","Reading Greek aloud","Comparing translations","Learning vocabulary"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says these common exegetical pitfalls arise from misusing Greek grammar."},{"q":"Which tense appears in the lesson's example of over-reading tense?","kind":"concept","options":["Aorist","Perfect","Future","Present"],"answer":3,"explain":"The example is \"The present tense proves continuous habitual action.\""},{"q":"What problem do all four pitfalls share?","kind":"concept","options":["Ignoring grammar completely","Reading too slowly","Claiming more than a grammatical form can support on its own","Relying too much on context"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each pitfall makes a form say more than it can carry by itself, whether about tense, aspect, or theology."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the pitfalls the lesson lists?","kind":"concept","options":["Simplistic aspect claims","Reading aloud too often","Comparing too many translations","Using paragraph divisions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The four pitfalls are over-reading tense, the aorist fallacy, forcing theology into grammar, and simplistic aspect claims."},{"q":"Which pitfall in the lesson's list is named after one particular tense?","kind":"concept","options":["Over-reading tense","Forcing theology into grammar","The aorist fallacy","Simplistic aspect claims"],"answer":2,"explain":"The aorist fallacy is named for the aorist tense. Over-reading tense concerns tense in general, not one tense."},{"q":"A reader insists that one aspect rule applies the same way in every passage, whatever the context. Which pitfall is this?","kind":"concept","options":["Simplistic aspect claims","The aorist fallacy","Forcing theology into grammar","Letting context shape how forms are interpreted"],"answer":0,"explain":"Applying aspect rules mechanically without considering context is a simplistic aspect claim."},{"q":"Someone cites a Greek tense mainly to defeat an opponent in a debate. What does the lesson say the aim should be instead?","kind":"concept","options":["To read what the Greek text communicates","To find a stronger grammatical proof","To avoid Greek altogether","To use more technical terms"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's goal is to read what the text communicates, not to use grammar to win arguments."},{"q":"A student writes, \"The Greek proves my view, so no other evidence is needed.\" Which responsible practice does this skip?","kind":"concept","options":["Reading Greek aloud","Avoiding claims that grammar proves points without additional support","Comparing English translations","Learning principal parts"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson asks for additional support rather than claiming grammar alone proves a point."}],"va_27":[{"q":"What does this discourse marker mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["for/because","but/and","and","therefore"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson glosses οὖν as \"therefore.\"","greek":"οὖν"},{"q":"What does this discourse marker mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["therefore","but/and","for/because","and"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson glosses γάρ as \"for/because.\"","greek":"γάρ"},{"q":"What does this discourse marker mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["but/and","therefore","for/because","if"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson glosses δέ as \"but/and.\"","greek":"δέ"},{"q":"What does this discourse marker mean, according to the lesson?","kind":"meaning","options":["therefore","for/because","and","but only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson glosses καί simply as \"and.\"","greek":"καί"},{"q":"A clause gives the reason for the statement just before it. Which marker from the lesson would you expect?","kind":"meaning","options":["οὖν","καί","δέ","γάρ"],"answer":3,"explain":"γάρ means \"for/because,\" so it fits a clause that supplies a reason.","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"A sentence turns to a contrasting point. Which marker from the lesson can carry \"but\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["δέ","οὖν","γάρ","καί"],"answer":0,"explain":"δέ is glossed \"but/and,\" so it can mark a turn or contrast.","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"A paragraph opens with this word. What relationship to the earlier material does it signal?","kind":"meaning","options":["A reason for what came before (\"for\")","A simple addition (\"and\")","A condition (\"if\")","A conclusion drawn from what came before (\"therefore\")"],"answer":3,"explain":"οὖν means \"therefore,\" so it introduces a conclusion that follows from what was said earlier.","greek":"οὖν"},{"q":"What does the lesson call words like these?","kind":"concept","options":["Principal parts","Tense formatives","Discourse markers","Augments"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson calls οὖν, δέ, γάρ, and καί discourse markers: they show how ideas connect.","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"Which tense shift does the lesson tell you to notice?","kind":"concept","options":["From future to perfect only","From subjunctive to indicative","From singular to plural","From aorist to present or imperfect"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson asks where the writer switches from aorist to present or imperfect."},{"q":"What does the lesson say paragraph structure shows?","kind":"concept","options":["The argument's shape","The spelling of each word","The author's age","The number of verbs in each sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"Let paragraph structure show the argument's shape."},{"q":"Why does the lesson recommend reading Greek aloud?","kind":"concept","options":["Greek was written for oral performance","It replaces the need for vocabulary","It makes every form easy to parse","It lets you skip paragraph structure"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reading aloud fits the text, since Greek was written for oral performance."},{"q":"Treating each sentence in isolation is a habit that helps extended reading.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Treating every sentence in isolation is one of the habits the lesson says hinders extended reading."},{"q":"According to the lesson, not every form needs immediate detailed analysis.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. Extended reading trains fluency, and not every form needs detailed analysis right away."},{"q":"Why does stopping to fully analyze every form hinder extended reading?","kind":"concept","options":["It breaks discourse flow","It makes the text shorter","It skips the main verbs","It removes the discourse markers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson says stopping to fully analyze every form breaks discourse flow."},{"q":"What does extended reading train, according to the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Handwriting","Fluency","Memorizing paradigms","Accent placement"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says extended reading trains fluency."},{"q":"The lesson says the goal of building reading fluency is perfection.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. The goal is not perfection but increasing confidence with the text."},{"q":"How does the lesson view slow progress in reading fluency?","kind":"concept","options":["Slow progress means the method is failing","Only fast progress counts","Even slow progress is real progress","Slow progress should be avoided by skipping hard texts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson encourages you: even slow progress is real progress."},{"q":"You reach a long Greek sentence full of extra phrases. Following the lesson, what do you do first?","kind":"concept","options":["Parse every participle before anything else","Look up every word in order","Find the main clause, then fill in the surrounding details","Skip to the next paragraph"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's helpful habit is to identify the main clause first, then fill in surrounding details."},{"q":"You meet an unfamiliar word in the middle of a paragraph. Which response fits the lesson's advice best?","kind":"concept","options":["Stop and look it up before reading on","Fully analyze every form around it first","Treat that sentence on its own and move on","Keep following the flow instead of looking it up immediately"],"answer":3,"explain":"Looking up every unknown word immediately is a hindering habit; the lesson wants you to keep the flow going."},{"q":"Which of these does the lesson list as a habit that helps extended reading?","kind":"concept","options":["Looking up every unknown word immediately","Using paragraph divisions as guides to discourse structure","Stopping to fully analyze every form","Treating every sentence in isolation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paragraph divisions guide you to the structure of the discourse; the other three are hindering habits."},{"q":"Student A stops at every form to parse it fully. Student B finds each main clause and keeps reading. Whose approach matches the lesson?","kind":"concept","options":["Student B","Student A","Both equally","Neither, since parsing should be skipped entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stopping to fully analyze every form breaks discourse flow; identifying the main clause first is a helpful habit."},{"q":"What does the lesson say building reading fluency takes?","kind":"concept","options":["One careful reading","Time and practice","A complete vocabulary list","Perfect parsing"],"answer":1,"explain":"Building reading fluency takes time and practice."},{"q":"A student tracks vocabulary in a passage but ignores which verbs are main verbs and who their subjects are. What will be hardest to follow?","kind":"concept","options":["Who is doing what","How each word is spelled","Which words are Greek","How long the passage is"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tracking main verbs and their subjects is how you follow who is doing what."},{"q":"Which of these words is NOT in the lesson's list of discourse markers?","kind":"meaning","options":["οὖν","γάρ","ἵνα","δέ"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists οὖν, δέ, γάρ, and καί. ἵνα is not among them."},{"q":"Two items are simply joined together in a list. Which marker from the lesson fits best?","kind":"meaning","options":["γάρ","καί","οὖν","δέ"],"answer":1,"explain":"καί is glossed simply \"and,\" which fits joining items.","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"Which two of the lesson's habits both use paragraphs to follow the text?","kind":"concept","options":["Reading aloud, and looking up every word","Letting paragraph structure show the argument's shape, and using paragraph divisions as guides","Stopping to analyze every form, and treating sentences in isolation","Tracking main verbs, and noticing tense shifts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both habits use paragraphs: one lets the structure show the argument's shape; the other uses divisions as guides to discourse structure."},{"q":"In the lesson's glosses, which two discourse markers can both be rendered \"and\"?","kind":"meaning","options":["οὖν and γάρ","γάρ and καί","οὖν and δέ","δέ and καί"],"answer":3,"explain":"δέ is glossed \"but/and\" and καί is glossed \"and.\" οὖν is \"therefore\" and γάρ is \"for/because.\"","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"Which two markers in the lesson's list tie ideas together by reasoning, one giving a reason and one drawing a conclusion?","kind":"meaning","options":["γάρ and οὖν","δέ and καί","καί and γάρ","οὖν and δέ"],"answer":0,"explain":"γάρ (\"for/because\") gives a reason, and οὖν (\"therefore\") draws a conclusion. δέ and καί mainly join or contrast.","greek":"οὖν, δέ, γάρ, καί"},{"q":"A writer narrates events in the aorist, then switches to the imperfect. According to the lesson, what should you do?","kind":"concept","options":["Ignore it, since tense does not matter in extended reading","Notice the tense shift","Stop and fully analyze every form in the paragraph","Treat the new sentence in isolation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Noticing where the writer switches from aorist to present or imperfect is one of the lesson's reading habits."},{"q":"The lesson says Greek was written mainly for silent reading.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Greek was written for oral performance, which is why the lesson recommends reading aloud."}],"va_28":[{"q":"Which system concerns how the action is viewed?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Mood","Tense and aspect","Syntax"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tense and aspect describe how the action is viewed: ongoing, complete, or undefined."},{"q":"Which system concerns the subject's relationship to the action?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Voice","Tense and aspect","Nuance"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voice describes the subject's relationship to the action: active, middle, or passive."},{"q":"Which system does the lesson describe as \"the communicative force\"?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Mood","Syntax","Tense and aspect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mood is the communicative force: assertion, possibility, command, or contingency."},{"q":"What does syntax cover in the lesson's list?","kind":"concept","options":["How the action is viewed","The subject's relationship to the action","The spelling of verb endings","How verbs relate to their larger sentences and clauses"],"answer":3,"explain":"Syntax is about how verbs relate to their larger sentences and clauses."},{"q":"How does the lesson define nuance?","kind":"concept","options":["The subject's relationship to the action","What the form communicates given the specific discourse context","The communicative force of the verb","How the action is viewed, apart from context"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nuance is what the form communicates in the specific discourse context."},{"q":"Which list gives the ways tense and aspect can view an action?","kind":"concept","options":["active, middle, passive","assertion, possibility, command","ongoing, complete, undefined","first, second, third"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson lists ongoing, complete, and undefined under tense and aspect."},{"q":"Which list gives the communicative forces the lesson names under mood?","kind":"concept","options":["ongoing, complete, undefined","active, middle, passive","assertion, possibility, command, contingency","first, second, third"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mood covers assertion, possibility, command, and contingency."},{"q":"In real reading, the five systems work one at a time, in isolation.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. They do not work in isolation; all five interact simultaneously."},{"q":"A reader labels a verb \"aorist\" and stops there. Which system in the lesson's list pushes the reader to ask what that form communicates in this particular passage?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice","Nuance","Mood","Syntax"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nuance is what the form communicates given the specific discourse context, so it moves the reader from the label to the passage."},{"q":"According to the list of topics covered, what does verbal aspect show about how Greek communicates?","kind":"concept","options":["Time and nothing else","Only who the subject is","Viewpoint, not just time","Only whether the verb is a command"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson sums up aspect as how Greek communicates viewpoint, not just time."},{"q":"Which of these does the lesson list among the topics you have covered?","kind":"concept","options":["Noun declension tables","Greek accent rules","The history of Greek manuscripts","Conditionals, genitive absolutes, and complex syntax"],"answer":3,"explain":"Conditionals, genitive absolutes, and complex syntax appear in the list of topics covered."},{"q":"Which three voices does the lesson name?","kind":"concept","options":["ongoing, complete, undefined","active, middle, passive","assertion, command, contingency","present, aorist, perfect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voice is active, middle, or passive, and the lesson says you have covered all three."},{"q":"How does the lesson describe every system in this track?","kind":"concept","options":["As a set of labels to memorize","As a tool for following Greek communication","As a replacement for reading","As a rule that works in isolation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every system is a tool for following Greek communication."},{"q":"What does the lesson encourage you to do as you finish?","kind":"concept","options":["Stop reading until every form is memorized","Read only English translations","Focus only on vocabulary lists","Keep reading and keep noticing"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson closes: keep reading, keep noticing, and the text will open to you more and more."},{"q":"According to the lesson, mood is the system that describes how the action is viewed.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explain":"False. Tense and aspect describe how the action is viewed (ongoing, complete, undefined). Mood is the communicative force."},{"q":"While reading, you ask whether a verb makes an assertion or gives a command. Which system are you using?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Voice","Syntax","Tense and aspect"],"answer":0,"explain":"Assertion and command are communicative forces, which belong to mood."},{"q":"While reading, you ask how a participle relates to the main clause of its sentence. Which system are you using?","kind":"concept","options":["Mood","Voice","Tense and aspect","Syntax"],"answer":3,"explain":"Syntax covers how verbs relate to their larger sentences and clauses."},{"q":"How many systems does final integration bring together in the lesson's list?","kind":"concept","options":["Three","Four","Six","Five"],"answer":3,"explain":"Five: tense and aspect, voice, mood, syntax, and nuance."},{"q":"Which topic in the lesson's covered list ties most directly to the voice system?","kind":"concept","options":["Strategies for extended NT reading","All three voices and their nuanced contributions","Translation philosophy and the responsibility of exegesis","Conditionals, genitive absolutes, and complex syntax"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voice is the subject's relationship to the action (active, middle, passive), and the list says you covered all three voices."},{"q":"How does the list of covered topics describe your study of participles?","kind":"concept","options":["The full range of participle constructions","Only attributive participles","Participles in the Gospels only","Participle spelling rules"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson lists imperatives, infinitives, and the full range of participle constructions."},{"q":"Mood is the communicative force. Which English sentence shows the force of command?","kind":"concept","options":["He loosed the colt.","Perhaps he may loose the colt.","Loose the colt!","He was loosing the colt."],"answer":2,"explain":"\"Loose the colt!\" gives a command. The others make an assertion, express a possibility, or describe an ongoing action."},{"q":"Tense and aspect concern how the action is viewed. Which English sentence pictures the action as ongoing?","kind":"concept","options":["He was loosing the colt.","Loose the colt!","The colt was loosed.","Perhaps he may loose the colt."],"answer":0,"explain":"\"He was loosing the colt\" shows the action in progress. The others give a command, a passive statement, or a possibility."},{"q":"Which pairing of system and values is correct?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice: active, middle, passive","Mood: ongoing, complete, undefined","Tense and aspect: assertion, command","Syntax: active, passive"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voice is active, middle, or passive. Ongoing, complete, and undefined belong to tense and aspect; assertion and command belong to mood."},{"q":"Which two systems in the lesson's list look beyond the verb form itself to its surroundings (sentence, clause, or discourse)?","kind":"concept","options":["Voice and mood","Tense and aspect, and voice","Mood, and tense and aspect","Syntax and nuance"],"answer":3,"explain":"Syntax is how verbs relate to their larger sentences and clauses, and nuance is what the form communicates in the specific discourse context."},{"q":"Strategies for extended NT reading are one of the topics the lesson says you have covered.","kind":"truefalse","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"True. The list of covered topics ends with strategies for extended NT reading."},{"q":"A reader labels every verb correctly but cannot follow what the passage is saying. According to the lesson, what is still missing?","kind":"concept","options":["More grammatical labels","A longer list of paradigms","Thinking through how the Greek communicates, with the systems working together","Fewer systems to keep track of"],"answer":2,"explain":"The goal was never just to name labels, but to think through how Greek communicates and follow it naturally."},{"q":"Which of these is one of the five systems in the lesson's list?","kind":"concept","options":["Syntax","Vocabulary","Accentuation","Declension"],"answer":0,"explain":"The five systems are tense and aspect, voice, mood, syntax, and nuance."},{"q":"Voice is the subject's relationship to the action. In which sentence is the subject acted upon rather than acting?","kind":"concept","options":["He loosed the colt.","The colt was loosed.","Loose the colt!","He was loosing the colt."],"answer":1,"explain":"In \"The colt was loosed,\" the subject (the colt) receives the action, which is passive voice. In the others the subject acts or is told to act."},{"q":"You read a sentence and, at the same moment, weigh how the action is viewed, how the subject relates to it, and how the clause fits the sentence. What does the lesson say this reflects?","kind":"concept","options":["A pitfall to avoid","Reading each system in isolation","The systems interacting simultaneously in real reading","A step you should skip"],"answer":2,"explain":"In real reading, all five systems interact simultaneously; that is what final integration means."},{"q":"Which topic in the lesson's covered list ties most directly to the syntax system?","kind":"concept","options":["All three voices and their nuanced contributions","Verbal aspect and viewpoint","Conditionals, genitive absolutes, and complex syntax","Translation philosophy"],"answer":2,"explain":"That item names complex syntax directly, and syntax is how verbs relate to their larger sentences and clauses."}]};
